    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), and what are its core advantages over Procedural Programming?",
    "answer": "Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a software design paradigm organized around 'objects' rather than functions and sequential logic:\n\n1. Concept:\n   - An object binds together internal state (data fields/attributes) with behavior (procedures/methods) into a cohesive unit.\n   - Follows a bottom-up architectural design modeling real-world domain entities.\n2. Key Advantages over Procedural Programming:\n   - Data Security & Encapsulation: In procedural programming, global data flows openly and can be mutated by any function. OOP restricts direct data access using access modifiers.\n   - Maintainability & Modularity: Classes act as independent modules; changing internal implementation does not break external callers.\n   - Code Reusability: Inheritance and polymorphism eliminate code duplication.\n   - Scalability: Essential for large enterprise software architectures.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "OOP models software as interacting objects encapsulating state and behavior.",
      "Provides data hiding via access specifiers; procedural programming has open global data.",
      "Bottom-up architectural approach optimizing modularity, maintainability, and code reuse."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// OOP: Encapsulating state and behavior together\npublic class BankAccount {\n    private double balance; // Protected state\n\n    public void deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount > 0) balance += amount; // Validated behavior\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "When asked for the difference in one sentence: 'Procedural programming tells computers what steps to execute on data; OOP organizes data into objects that manage their own behavior.'",
