    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the difference between Abstraction and Encapsulation?",
    "answer": "Though complementary, Abstraction and Encapsulation solve fundamentally different design problems:\n\n1. Abstraction (Hiding Complexity):\n   - Focus: 'WHAT' an entity does, rather than 'HOW' it does it.\n   - Conceals complex internal implementation details and presents only a clean, minimal interface to the outside world.\n   - Implemented using: Interfaces and Abstract classes.\n   - Real-World Analogy: A car's accelerator pedal. You press it to speed up without knowing the mechanical engine fuel injection and combustion details.\n2. Encapsulation (Data Hiding / Binding):\n   - Focus: 'HOW' to protect data from unauthorized external modification.\n   - Bundles data and the methods operating on that data into a single unit (class), restricting direct access via private/protected modifiers and exposing validated getters/setters.\n   - Implemented using: Access specifiers (private, protected, public).\n   - Real-World Analogy: A medical capsule enclosing pharmaceutical chemicals inside a protective coating.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Abstraction hides internal implementation complexity (Interface / Abstract class).",
      "Encapsulation binds data with methods and hides state from direct modification (private fields).",
      "Abstraction is implemented at the design level; Encapsulation is implemented at the code level."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Encapsulation: Hiding fields behind validated methods\npublic class Employee {\n    private double salary; // Encapsulated\n    public void setSalary(double s) {\n        if (s > 0) this.salary = s;\n    }\n}\n\n// Abstraction: Hiding implementation behind an interface\npublic interface PaymentGateway {\n    void processPayment(double amount); // Abstract contract\n}"
    },
