    "id": "int-oops-003",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is Polymorphism? What is the difference between Compile-Time and Runtime Polymorphism?",
    "answer": "Polymorphism (Greek: 'many forms') allows a single entity (method, operator, or object reference) to behave differently based on context:\n\n1. Compile-Time Polymorphism (Static Binding / Early Binding):\n   - Resolved at compile time by the compiler based on method signatures.\n   - Primary Forms: Method Overloading (same method name, different parameters in the same class) and Operator Overloading (e.g. '+' adding numbers vs concatenating strings).\n   - Faster execution because method addresses are linked directly at compile time (zero runtime dispatch overhead).\n2. Runtime Polymorphism (Dynamic Binding / Late Binding):\n   - Resolved at runtime based on the actual object instance residing in memory.\n   - Primary Form: Method Overriding (subclass provides a specific implementation of a method declared in its superclass).\n   - Dispatched dynamically via Virtual Method Tables (vtables). Enables the Liskov Substitution Principle.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Compile-time: Method Overloading; resolved at build time based on reference types.",
      "Runtime: Method Overriding; resolved at runtime based on actual heap object.",
      "Runtime polymorphism uses dynamic dispatch via vtables."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Compile-Time (Overloading):\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\ndouble add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }\n\n// Runtime (Overriding):\nAnimal a = new Dog();\na.makeSound(); // Dispatches dynamically to Dog's makeSound() at runtime!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Remember: A method cannot be overloaded based solely on a different return type because the compiler cannot resolve which method to invoke when the return value is ignored.",
