    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What are the strict rules for Method Overriding in Object-Oriented languages?",
    "answer": "Method Overriding must preserve substitutability and contract safety across class hierarchies:\n\nStrict Rules:\n1. Signature Match: The method name and parameter list must be identical to the superclass method.\n2. Return Type: Must be identical or a Covariant Return Type (a subtype of the superclass method's return type).\n3. Access Modifier Visibility: The overriding method CANNOT reduce visibility (e.g. cannot override a public method as protected or private). It can expand visibility (e.g. protected -> public).\n4. Exception Handling Rules: The overriding method cannot throw new or broader checked exceptions, but can throw fewer, narrower, or unchecked exceptions.\n5. Non-Overridable Methods: Static methods (method hiding), private methods (invisible to subclasses), and final methods (explicitly locked) cannot be overridden.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Method name and parameter types must match exactly.",
      "Cannot reduce access visibility (public cannot become protected).",
      "Cannot declare broader checked exceptions.",
      "Private, static, and final methods cannot be overridden."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "class SuperClass {\n    public Object process() throws IOException { return null; }\n}\nclass SubClass extends SuperClass {\n    // Valid: Widened return type (String is Object) and narrower exception\n    @Override\n    public String process() throws FileNotFoundException { return \"ok\"; }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If an interviewer asks 'Can static methods be overridden?', answer: 'No, static methods belong to the class and are resolved at compile time via static binding. Re-declaring a static method is Method Hiding.'",
