    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "How does Dynamic Method Dispatch work under the hood (Virtual Method Table / vtable)?",
    "answer": "Dynamic Method Dispatch is the internal mechanism by which a call to an overridden method is resolved at runtime rather than compile time:\n\nUnder-the-Hood Mechanics:\n1. vtable (Virtual Table): For every class with virtual/overridden methods, the compiler creates a static array of function pointers during compilation. Each entry points to the most derived implementation of that method.\n2. vptr (Virtual Pointer): The compiler inserts a hidden pointer (vptr) inside each object instance pointing to its class's vtable.\n3. Runtime Resolution: When ptr.method() is invoked, the CPU fetches the object's vptr, looks up the function pointer at the known index in the vtable, and jumps to that address.\n4. Overhead: Incurs an indirect memory dereference (~2-3 CPU instructions) and prevents compiler inlining.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Compiler builds a static vtable containing function pointers for polymorphic classes.",
      "Each object holds a hidden vptr pointing to its class's vtable.",
      "Dynamic dispatch looks up method addresses at runtime via vptr -> vtable[index]."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "// Assembly sequence for dynamic dispatch:\n// 1. mov rax, [rcx]       ; Load vptr from object\n// 2. call [rax + 8]       ; Indirect call to method at vtable offset 8"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Highlight the trade-off: Dynamic dispatch enables runtime flexibility and clean polymorphism at the cost of indirect memory lookups and disabling compiler method inlining.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Apple",
      "NVIDIA"
    ],
