    "id": "int-oops-008",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Diamond Problem in Multiple Inheritance? How do languages resolve it?",
    "answer": "The Diamond Problem occurs when class D inherits from two classes B and C, which both inherit from a common base class A:\n\nAmbiguity Scenario:\n- If class A defines method display(), and both B and C override display(), which version should class D inherit?\n- Calling d.display() creates fatal ambiguity.\n\nLanguage Resolutions:\n1. Java: Disallows multiple inheritance of classes entirely. Allowed with interfaces, but if two interfaces share identical default methods, the compiler forces class D to explicitly override and disambiguate (InterfaceName.super.display()).\n2. C++: Supports Virtual Inheritance (class B : virtual public A). Ensures only ONE shared instance of class A exists in class D.\n3. Python: Uses C3 Linearization to compute a deterministic Method Resolution Order (MRO).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Occurs when a class inherits conflicting implementations from two parent classes.",
      "Java disallows multiple class inheritance and forces explicit default method disambiguation.",
      "C++ uses Virtual Base Classes; Python uses C3 Linearization (MRO)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "interface A { default void show() { System.out.println(\"A\"); } }\ninterface B { default void show() { System.out.println(\"B\"); } }\n\nclass C implements A, B {\n    @Override\n    public void show() {\n        A.super.show(); // Explicit disambiguation\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Draw the diamond diagram (A at top, B and C in middle, D at bottom) during whiteboard interviews.",
