    "id": "int-oops-010",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Why is Composition preferred over Inheritance ('Favor composition over inheritance')?",
    "answer": "Inheritance creates tight compile-time coupling and breaks encapsulation, whereas Composition achieves loose coupling:\n\nProblems with Inheritance:\n1. Fragile Base Class Problem: Subclasses depend intimately on the internal implementation details of superclasses. If a superclass method implementation changes, subclasses can silently break.\n2. White-Box Reuse: Breaks encapsulation because subclasses are exposed to parent implementation details.\n3. Static Hierarchy: Inheritance relationships are fixed at compile time and cannot be altered at runtime.\n\nAdvantages of Composition (HAS-A):\n1. Black-Box Reuse: Components interact purely through defined public interfaces without exposing internal state.\n2. Dynamic Runtime Swapping: Behaviors can be swapped at runtime via dependency injection (Strategy Pattern).\n3. Testability: Easy to mock individual components in unit test suites.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Inheritance creates tight compile-time coupling and breaks encapsulation (white-box reuse).",
      "Composition enables loose coupling and dynamic runtime behavior swapping (black-box reuse).",
      "Prevents the Fragile Base Class problem."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Composition: Engine is injected and encapsulated\npublic class Car {\n    private final Engine engine; // HAS-A relationship\n    public Car(Engine engine) { this.engine = engine; }\n    public void start() { engine.ignite(); }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Cite the famous example from Effective Java where subclassing HashSet to count elements broke because addAll() internally called add(), doubling the count.",
