    "id": "int-oops-011",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the difference between Association, Aggregation, and Composition?",
    "answer": "These represent different degrees of relationship coupling between objects:\n\n1. Association (General Relationship):\n   - A structural relationship indicating objects know about each other.\n   - Example: Teacher and Student. Both exist independently; deleting Teacher has zero impact on Student.\n2. Aggregation (Weak HAS-A Relationship):\n   - A specialized association representing a whole-part relationship where parts can exist INDEPENDENTLY of the whole.\n   - Ownership: Non-exclusive / Weak.\n   - Lifetime: The lifecycle of the part is NOT tied to the whole.\n   - Example: Department and Professor. If the Department is closed, the Professors still exist and can join another department.\n3. Composition (Strong HAS-A Relationship):\n   - A specialized aggregation representing a strict whole-part relationship where parts CANNOT exist without the whole.\n   - Ownership: Exclusive / Strong.\n   - Lifetime: The lifecycle of the part is strictly bound to the whole. Destroying the whole DESTROYS all its parts.\n   - Example: House and Rooms, or Human and Heart.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Association: General loose relationship between independent objects.",
      "Aggregation: Weak HAS-A; parts survive the destruction of the container (Department & Teacher).",
      "Composition: Strong HAS-A; parts are destroyed when the container is destroyed (House & Rooms)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Aggregation: Professor exists outside Department\nclass Department {\n    private List<Professor> professors; // Injected from outside\n}\n\n// Composition: Engine lifecycle is bound to Car\nclass Car {\n    private final Engine engine = new Engine(); // Instantiated and destroyed with Car\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "UML representation tip: Aggregation uses an open/hollow diamond; Composition uses a filled/solid diamond.",
