    "id": "int-oops-012",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Fragile Base Class problem, and how does it compromise OOP hierarchies?",
    "answer": "The Fragile Base Class problem is a fundamental architectural hazard where seemingly safe modifications to a base class unintentionally break derived subclasses:\n\nClassic Scenario:\n- Base class defines add(item) and addAll(items).\n- Subclass overrides add() to maintain an added_count counter, and calls super.add().\n- Initially, the base class implements addAll() by directly inserting elements.\n- Later, a base class maintainer refactors addAll() to call add() in a loop for clean code.\n- Catastrophe: Subclass calling addAll() now has its count DOUBLED because each element increments the count twice!\n\nRoot Cause & Solution:\n- Caused by white-box inheritance breaking encapsulation.\n- Solution: Favor composition over inheritance, or declare non-overridable methods 'final'.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Modifications to superclasses can silently corrupt subclass behaviors.",
      "Stemming from white-box inheritance exposing internal coupling.",
      "Prevent using composition (wrapping base class) or marking methods 'final'."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Broken subclass due to fragile base class:\npublic class InstrumentedSet<E> extends HashSet<E> {\n    private int addCount = 0;\n    @Override public boolean add(E e) { addCount++; return super.add(e); }\n    @Override public boolean addAll(Collection<? extends E> c) {\n        addCount += c.size(); // Corrupted if super.addAll calls add()!\n        return super.addAll(c);\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "This is the exact example written by Joshua Bloch in Effective Java Item 18.",
