    "title": "What is Delegation in OOP? How does the Delegation Pattern work?",
    "answer": "Delegation is an object-oriented technique where an object handles a request by delegating the actual work to a secondary helper object:\n\nMechanics:\n- The delegator class exposes a public interface matching the requested action.\n- Instead of implementing the algorithm itself or inheriting, it holds a reference to a delegate and invokes delegate.doWork().\n- Achieves dynamic polymorphism at runtime without class inheritance.\n- Foundational to State, Strategy, and Decorator design patterns.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Delegator passes operations to an internal helper object rather than executing them directly.",
      "Provides runtime behavior swapping without class inheritance.",
      "Foundation of Strategy, Decorator, and Proxy patterns."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "interface Printer { void print(String msg); }\nclass RealPrinter implements Printer { public void print(String msg) { System.out.println(msg); } }\n\nclass PrinterController implements Printer { // Delegator\n    private final Printer delegate = new RealPrinter();\n    public void print(String msg) { delegate.print(msg); } // Delegating work\n}"
    },
