    "title": "What is High Cohesion vs Low Cohesion?",
    "answer": "Cohesion measures how closely related and focused the responsibilities of a single class or module are:\n\n1. High Cohesion (Best Practice):\n   - A class has a single, well-defined purpose and all its fields and methods are tightly aligned toward fulfilling that purpose.\n   - Code is easy to understand, test, maintain, and reuse.\n   - Aligns directly with the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP).\n2. Low Cohesion (Anti-Pattern):\n   - A class attempts to do multiple unrelated tasks (e.g. User class managing user data, parsing JSON, validating credit cards, and sending emails).\n   - Results in 'God Classes' or 'Spaghetti Code' that are brittle, difficult to debug, and prone to breaking.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "High cohesion: A class has a single, tightly focused responsibility.",
      "Low cohesion: A class performs multiple unrelated responsibilities (God Object).",
      "High cohesion makes code modular, maintainable, and readable."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Low Cohesion (God Class Anti-pattern):\nclass Order {\n    void calculateTotal() {}\n    void printInvoice() {}\n    void sendEmailNotification() {}\n    void connectToDatabase() {}\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Pair with Loose Coupling: The golden architectural standard is 'High Cohesion, Loose Coupling'.",
