    "id": "int-oops-017",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the difference between Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI)?",
    "answer": "Comparison of architectural principle vs specific design pattern:\n\n1. Inversion of Control (IoC) (The Broad Architectural Principle):\n   - Inverts the flow of control compared to traditional procedural programming.\n   - Traditional: Your custom code controls the execution flow and instantiates libraries.\n   - IoC ('Hollywood Principle': 'Don't call us, we'll call you'): A framework or runtime container controls the execution flow and invokes your custom code when needed.\n2. Dependency Injection (DI) (The Concrete Design Pattern):\n   - A specific subtype/implementation of IoC.\n   - An object does NOT instantiate its dependencies (using new); instead, an external assembler (DI container like Spring, Guice, or Dagger) 'injects' dependencies into the object via constructors, setters, or fields.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "IoC is the broad principle ('Don't call us, we'll call you'); DI is a specific pattern implementing IoC.",
      "In DI, dependencies are supplied from the outside rather than created internally.",
      "Eliminates hardcoded dependencies, making unit testing and mocking trivial."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Dependency Injection via Constructor (Best Practice):\npublic class NotificationService {\n    private final MessageSender sender;\n    // Injected from outside container!\n    public NotificationService(MessageSender sender) {\n        this.sender = sender;\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewers frequently ask: 'What are the 3 types of Dependency Injection?' Answer: Constructor Injection (preferred), Setter Injection, and Field Injection.",
