    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the difference between an Anemic Domain Model and a Rich Domain Model?",
    "answer": "Architectural debate in Domain-Driven Design (DDD) popularized by Martin Fowler:\n\n1. Anemic Domain Model (Anti-Pattern):\n   - Domain entities contain ONLY data fields with getters and setters (DUMMY POJOs / DTOs).\n   - Zero business logic inside entities; all validation and logic are extracted into external Service classes (OrderService).\n   - Procedural programming disguised as OOP! Encapsulation is broken because any service can mutate entity state directly.\n2. Rich Domain Model (True OOP):\n   - Domain entities encapsulate BOTH data state AND the business rules/invariants operating on that data.\n   - State is protected (private setters); entities validate their own state transitions (e.g. order.cancel()).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Anemic model: Entities hold only data (getters/setters); logic lives in service layers (procedural).",
      "Rich domain model: Entities encapsulate state and business logic invariants together (true OOP).",
      "Rich models preserve encapsulation and enforce business rules at the domain entity boundary."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Rich Domain Model: Entity enforces its own business rules\npublic class Order {\n    private OrderStatus status = OrderStatus.DRAFT;\n    \n    public void submit() {\n        if (status != OrderStatus.DRAFT) throw new IllegalStateException(\"Cannot submit non-draft order!\");\n        this.status = OrderStatus.SUBMITTED;\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Martin Fowler coined 'Anemic Domain Model' as an anti-pattern because it negates the primary benefit of object-oriented design.",
