    "id": "int-oops-019",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the difference between Value Objects and Entities in Domain-Driven Design?",
    "answer": "Core modeling concepts in Object-Oriented Domain Architecture:\n\n1. Entity:\n   - Defined by its unique IDENTITY (ID) that persists over time, rather than its attributes.\n   - Two entities with identical attribute values but different IDs are distinct entities.\n   - Mutable: Attributes can change, but the entity remains the same object.\n   - Examples: User (id=101), Order (id=\"ORD-99\").\n2. Value Object:\n   - Defined purely by its VALUE, with NO conceptual identity.\n   - Immutable: Once created, cannot be changed. If you want a different value, instantiate a new Value Object.\n   - Equality: Two value objects with identical attributes are completely interchangeable (equals checks values).\n   - Examples: Money (amount, currency), Address, Color, DateRange.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Entity has a unique persistent identity (ID); tracked across state mutations.",
      "Value Object has no identity; defined strictly by its immutable attributes.",
      "Value objects are compared by value equality, not reference or ID."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Value Object: Immutable and compared by value\npublic record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {\n    public Money add(Money other) {\n        if (!currency.equals(other.currency)) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Mismatched currency\");\n        return new Money(amount.add(other.amount), currency);\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Java 14+ Records are the ideal implementation construct for Value Objects.",
