    "id": "int-oops-020",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is Method Hiding vs Method Overriding?",
    "answer": "Comparison of static method hiding vs instance method overriding:\n\n1. Method Overriding (Instance Methods):\n   - Dynamic Binding (Late Binding).\n   - Dispatched at runtime based on the actual object on the heap.\n   - Enables polymorphism.\n2. Method Hiding (Static Methods):\n   - Static Binding (Early Binding).\n   - If a subclass defines a static method with the exact same signature as a superclass static method, it HIDES the superclass method, but does NOT override it!\n   - The method invoked is determined at compile time strictly by the declared REFERENCE TYPE, not the underlying object!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Overriding applies to instance methods; resolved at runtime based on object.",
      "Hiding applies to static methods; resolved at compile time based on reference type.",
      "Calling static methods via references is bad practice; use ClassName.method()."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "class Parent { static void print() { System.out.println(\"Parent\"); } }\nclass Child extends Parent { static void print() { System.out.println(\"Child\"); } }\n\nParent p = new Child();\np.print(); // Prints \"Parent\"! (Resolved statically based on Parent reference)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "A standard classic trick question in Java and C++ technical interviews.",
