    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Explain the Open/Closed Principle (OCP) with a code violation and refactored fix.",
    "answer": "Open/Closed Principle: 'Software entities should be open for extension, but closed for modification.'\n\nOCP Violation:\n- A PaymentProcessor class with a process(type) method containing an ever-growing switch-case or if-else ladder (if 'CREDIT_CARD', else if 'PAYPAL', else if 'CRYPTO').\n- Every time a new payment method is added, you MUST MODIFY the existing, tested PaymentProcessor class, introducing regression bug risks!\n\nRefactored Fix via Polymorphism / Strategy Pattern:\n- Define a PaymentMethod interface with process(amount).\n- Create concrete implementations (CreditCardPayment, PayPalPayment, CryptoPayment).\n- PaymentProcessor now depends on the PaymentMethod interface. Adding new payment methods requires writing NEW classes without modifying a single line of existing code!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Open for extension (add new features), closed for modification (don't touch working code).",
      "Violated by cascading switch/if-else type checks.",
      "Solved using Interfaces and the Strategy Design Pattern."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// OCP Compliant Interface:\npublic interface PaymentMethod {\n    void pay(double amount);\n}\npublic class PayPalPayment implements PaymentMethod {\n    public void pay(double amount) { /* PayPal logic */ }\n}\npublic class PaymentProcessor {\n    public void executePayment(PaymentMethod method, double amount) {\n        method.pay(amount); // Open to new methods with ZERO modifications!\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Highlight how OCP enables plug-in architectures where third-party developers can extend your system without touching your source code.",
