    "id": "int-oops-024",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Explain the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP). Why does a Square inheriting from Rectangle violate LSP?",
    "answer": "Liskov Substitution Principle: 'Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without altering the correctness of the program.' (Introduced by Barbara Liskov in 1987).\n\nThe Classic Square-Rectangle Violation:\n- Mathematically, a Square is a Rectangle. So a junior engineer writes: class Square extends Rectangle.\n- A Rectangle has independent setters: setWidth(w) and setHeight(h).\n- To maintain square invariant, Square overrides setWidth(w) to set BOTH width and height to 'w'.\n- Catastrophe: A client function assumes a Rectangle behaves like a rectangle:\n  void test(Rectangle r) { r.setWidth(5); r.setHeight(4); assert(r.getArea() == 20); }\n- If passed a Square, r.setHeight(4) sets width to 4 as well! Area is 16, crashing the assertion!\n- The subtype Square broke client assumptions and invariants, directly violating LSP.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Subtypes must honor all behavioral contracts and invariants of their base type.",
      "Mathematical 'IS-A' does not always equal Object-Oriented 'BEHAVES-AS'.",
      "Square inheriting from Rectangle violates LSP because modifying width independently fails."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// LSP Violation:\nclass Rectangle {\n    protected int w, h;\n    public void setWidth(int w) { this.w = w; }\n    public void setHeight(int h) { this.h = h; }\n    public int getArea() { return w * h; }\n}\nclass Square extends Rectangle {\n    @Override public void setWidth(int w) { this.w = this.h = w; } // Breaks contract!\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Golden Rule: In OOP, inheritance is based on BEHAVIOR, not real-world taxonomy. If behavior differs, do not inherit.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Meta"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "is_hidden": false,
