    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Explain the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP). How does it differ from Dependency Injection?",
    "answer": "Dependency Inversion Principle dictates two rules:\n1. High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions.\n2. Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions.\n\nDIP vs Dependency Injection (DI):\n- DIP is the high-level design principle stating that code should depend on interfaces rather than concrete classes.\n- Dependency Injection is the concrete mechanism/pattern by which dependencies are passed to an object (via constructor or setter) rather than created with 'new'.\n\nViolation Scenario:\n- A high-level NotificationManager class directly instantiates a low-level SendGridEmailService class (new SendGridEmailService()).\n- If SendGrid is replaced by AWS SES, the high-level business logic must be rewritten!\n- Fix: Introduce a MessageService interface.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "High-level business logic must depend on abstractions (interfaces), not concrete implementations.",
      "DIP is the architectural goal; Dependency Injection (DI) is the tool used to achieve it.",
      "Enables effortless swapping of database drivers, cloud providers, and mock test fixtures."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Abstraction:\npublic interface MessageSender { void send(String msg); }\n\n// High-level module depends on abstraction:\npublic class AlertService {\n    private final MessageSender sender;\n    public AlertService(MessageSender sender) { this.sender = sender; }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Mentioning that DIP prevents architecture from turning into an inverted pyramid of brittle dependencies shows deep architectural maturity.",
