    "id": "int-oops-027",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "How do you implement a Thread-Safe Singleton in Java? What are the 4 common approaches?",
    "answer": "The Singleton pattern ensures a class has only ONE instance and provides a global access point to it:\n\n4 Approaches:\n1. Eager Initialization: private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();. Thread-safe, simple, but instantiates at startup even if never used.\n2. Synchronized Method: public static synchronized Singleton getInstance(). Thread-safe and lazy, but incurs 99% unnecessary locking overhead on every read after initialization.\n3. Double-Checked Locking (DCL): Checks null before and after acquiring lock. Requires 'private static volatile Singleton instance;' to prevent CPU instruction reordering.\n4. Bill Pugh Singleton (Static Inner Helper Class - Best Practice): Relies on JVM ClassLoader guarantees. Lazy, thread-safe, and zero lock synchronization overhead!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Singleton guarantees exactly one class instance in memory.",
      "Double-Checked Locking requires 'volatile' to prevent CPU instruction reordering bugs.",
      "Bill Pugh approach (static inner helper) provides lazy, thread-safe initialization without locks."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Bill Pugh Singleton (Industry Best Practice):\npublic class Singleton {\n    private Singleton() {}\n    private static class Helper {\n        private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();\n    }\n    public static Singleton getInstance() {\n        return Helper.INSTANCE; // Class loaded lazily on first call!\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Josh Bloch's Effective Java recommendation: A single-element Enum (enum Singleton { INSTANCE; }) is the most bulletproof singleton, immune to reflection and serialization attacks.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Goldman Sachs"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
