    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "How can a Singleton be broken via Reflection, Serialization, and Cloning? How do you defend against them?",
    "answer": "Three ways standard singletons can be broken, and their defenses:\n\n1. Reflection Attack:\n   - Adversary calls constructor.setAccessible(true) and instantiates a second object.\n   - Defense: Inside the private constructor, throw an exception if instance already exists: if (instance != null) throw new RuntimeException(\"Use getInstance()!\");.\n2. Serialization Attack:\n   - Deserializing a serialized singleton creates a brand-new distinct object in memory!\n   - Defense: Implement protected Object readResolve() { return getInstance(); }.\n3. Cloning Attack:\n   - Calling clone() duplicates the object.\n   - Defense: Override clone() and throw new CloneNotSupportedException().\n4. The Ultimate Defense: Use a Java 'enum' (enum Singleton { INSTANCE; }). Enums are guaranteed by the JVM to be 100% immune to reflection, serialization, and cloning attacks!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Reflection bypasses private constructors (defend by throwing exception in constructor).",
      "Deserialization creates duplicate instances (defend via readResolve() method).",
      "Java Enum singletons provide JVM-enforced immunity against all 3 attacks."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// Bulletproof Enum Singleton:\npublic enum SafeSingleton {\n    INSTANCE;\n    public void doWork() { /* logic */ }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "This is a favorite senior Java technical interview question testing JVM-level security.",
