    "id": "int-oops-037",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Composite Pattern, and how does it treat individual objects and compositions uniformly?",
    "answer": "The Composite Pattern composes objects into Tree Structures to represent Part-Whole hierarchies, allowing clients to treat individual leaf objects and composite groups of objects uniformly:\n\n3 Core Roles:\n1. Component: Common interface declaring operations (e.g. getSize(), display()).\n2. Leaf: Represents terminal nodes with no children (e.g. File).\n3. Composite: Represents container nodes holding a collection of child Components (e.g. Directory / Folder).\n\nUniform Treatment: Calling directory.getSize() recursively sums the sizes of all contained files and nested subdirectories without the caller needing to know whether an item is a file or a folder!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Treats individual leaf objects and composite tree structures uniformly.",
      "Organizes objects into hierarchical Part-Whole tree structures.",
      "Standard for file systems, GUI widget trees, and graphics scene graphs."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "interface FileSystemItem { int getSize(); }\nclass File implements FileSystemItem { public int getSize() { return 100; } }\nclass Directory implements FileSystemItem {\n    private List<FileSystemItem> items = new ArrayList<>();\n    public int getSize() {\n        return items.stream().mapToInt(FileSystemItem::getSize).sum(); // Recursive calculation\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Canonical example: GUI DOM trees where a Container (composite) contains Buttons (leaf) and Panels (composite).",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Adobe",
      "Microsoft"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
    "is_hidden": false,
