    "id": "int-oops-038",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Observer Pattern (Publish-Subscribe), and how is it implemented in OOP?",
    "answer": "The Observer Pattern defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object (the Subject/Publisher) changes state, all its dependents (Observers/Subscribers) are notified and updated automatically:\n\nCore Components:\n1. Subject (Publisher): Maintains a list of registered observers. Provides attach(observer), detach(observer), and notifyObservers().\n2. Observer (Subscriber): Interface declaring update(state).\n3. Push vs Pull Model:\n   - Push: Subject passes detailed state data inside the update() call.\n   - Pull: Subject merely notifies observers, and observers query the subject for needed data.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "One-to-many event notification system.",
      "Subject notifies all registered observers upon state changes.",
      "Loose coupling: Subject knows only the Observer interface, not concrete subscriber classes."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "public interface Observer { void update(double price); }\npublic class Stock {\n    private List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();\n    private double price;\n    public void setPrice(double p) {\n        this.price = p;\n        for (Observer o : observers) o.update(price); // Notify all\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Standard throughout reactive programming (RxJava, Redux, Event Listeners in UI frameworks).",
