    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Strategy Pattern, and how does it eliminate conditional if-else statements?",
    "answer": "The Strategy Pattern defines a family of interchangeable algorithms, encapsulates each one into a separate class, and makes them swappable at runtime:\n\nMechanics:\n- The Context class holds a reference to a Strategy interface.\n- Clients inject whichever concrete strategy algorithm is needed.\n- Eliminates Massive Switch / If-Else ladders: Instead of switching on discount types, inject PercentageDiscountStrategy or FlatDiscountStrategy.\n- Adheres directly to the Open/Closed Principle (OCP).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Encapsulates interchangeable algorithms behind a common Strategy interface.",
      "Enables dynamic runtime swapping of algorithms.",
      "Eliminates complex conditional if-else ladders and satisfies OCP."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "public interface SortingStrategy { void sort(int[] arr); }\npublic class QuickSortStrategy implements SortingStrategy { /* ... */ }\npublic class MergeSortStrategy implements SortingStrategy { /* ... */ }\n\npublic class Sorter {\n    private SortingStrategy strategy;\n    public Sorter(SortingStrategy s) { this.strategy = s; }\n    public void executeSort(int[] arr) { strategy.sort(arr); }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Difference between Strategy and State: Strategy algorithms are chosen independently by the client; State transitions are triggered internally as object state changes.",
