    "id": "int-oops-040",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Command Pattern, and how does it enable Undo/Redo operations?",
    "answer": "The Command Pattern encapsulates a request as an object, thereby letting you parameterize clients with different requests, queue or log requests, and support undoable operations:\n\n4 Core Roles:\n1. Command Interface: Declares execute() and undo().\n2. Concrete Command: Binds an action with its Receiver (e.g. LightOnCommand).\n3. Receiver: Performs the actual work (Light).\n4. Invoker: Triggers the command (RemoteControl).\n\nImplementing Undo / Redo:\n- The Invoker maintains an internal Stack<Command> history.\n- When execute() is called, the command is pushed onto the history stack.\n- Calling undo() pops the top command and invokes command.undo()!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Encapsulates requests as standalone Command objects.",
      "Decouples the invoker (caller) from the receiver (executor).",
      "Maintains command history stacks to provide seamless Undo/Redo functionality."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "public interface Command {\n    void execute();\n    void undo();\n}\npublic class CopyCommand implements Command {\n    private TextEditor editor; private String backup;\n    public void execute() { backup = editor.getText(); editor.copy(); }\n    public void undo() { editor.setText(backup); }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Ubiquitous in text editors, transaction rollbacks, and task scheduling queues.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Adobe"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
