    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the State Pattern, and how does it differ from the Strategy Pattern?",
    "answer": "The State Pattern allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes, appearing as if the object changed its class:\n\nMechanics:\n- The Context class delegates requests to an internal State interface pointer.\n- Each concrete state class implements behavior specific to that state and controls transitions to subsequent states (e.g. VendingMachine: NoCoinState -> HasCoinState -> SoldState).\n\nState vs Strategy Difference:\n- Strategy: The client explicitly selects and injects which strategy algorithm to use; strategies typically don't know about each other.\n- State: State transitions are automatic and internal; concrete states know about other states and trigger transitions on the context object.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Encapsulates state-dependent behavior into discrete state classes.",
      "Transitions between states happen dynamically and automatically.",
      "Eliminates messy switch-case state machines."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "public interface State { void insertCoin(VendingMachine vm); }\npublic class NoCoinState implements State {\n    public void insertCoin(VendingMachine vm) {\n        System.out.println(\"Coin inserted\");\n        vm.setState(new HasCoinState()); // State transition!\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Standard pattern for order lifecycles (Draft -> Paid -> Shipped -> Delivered).",
