    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Template Method Pattern, and how does it use the Hollywood Principle?",
    "answer": "The Template Method Pattern defines the skeleton of an algorithm in an abstract base class method, deferring specific steps to subclasses without altering the algorithm's overall structure:\n\nHollywood Principle ('Don't call us, we'll call you'):\n- The base class template method (often marked final) calls abstract or hook methods.\n- Subclasses override individual step methods, but the BASE CLASS controls the overarching execution sequence!\n- Inverts control compared to standard inheritance.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Defines algorithm skeleton in a base class template method.",
      "Subclasses override individual steps without changing execution sequence.",
      "Template method is declared final to prevent altering algorithm structure."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "public abstract class DataMiner {\n    public final void mine() { // Template method (final)\n        openFile(); extractData(); parseData(); closeFile();\n    }\n    abstract void extractData(); // Subclasses implement this\n    abstract void parseData();\n    void openFile() { System.out.println(\"File opened\"); }\n    void closeFile() { System.out.println(\"File closed\"); }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Contrast Template Method vs Strategy: Template Method uses inheritance (compile time); Strategy uses composition (runtime).",
