    "title": "What is the Iterator Pattern, and why does it decouple collections from traversal algorithms?",
    "answer": "The Iterator Pattern provides a way to access elements of an aggregate collection sequentially without exposing its underlying internal representation (array, linked list, tree, hash map):\n\nWhy It Matters:\n- Client code can traverse ANY data structure using the exact same uniform interface (hasNext(), next()).\n- Multiple concurrent iterators can traverse the same collection independently, each maintaining its own cursor position.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Provides uniform sequential traversal across arbitrary data structures.",
      "Encapsulates internal collection implementation (list, tree, graph).",
      "Enables multiple independent concurrent traversals of the same collection."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "public interface Iterator<T> {\n    boolean hasNext();\n    T next();\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Underpins the foreach loop across almost all modern languages (Java Iterable, Python __iter__).",
