    "title": "What is the Flyweight Pattern, and how does it minimize memory in large-scale systems?",
    "answer": "The Flyweight Pattern minimizes memory consumption by sharing common, immutable state across thousands of fine-grained objects:\n\nState Segmentation:\n1. Intrinsic State: Context-independent, immutable data shared across all instances (e.g. character font, texture bitmap, 3D model mesh). Stored inside the shared Flyweight object.\n2. Extrinsic State: Context-dependent data unique to each instance (e.g. coordinates x, y, color). Passed to flyweight methods from the outside.\n\nApplications: Word processors (sharing glyph character objects), Game engines (rendering 100,000 forest trees sharing one tree mesh/texture).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Shares common immutable intrinsic state to drastically reduce memory usage.",
      "Separates shared intrinsic state from unique extrinsic state.",
      "Used for rendering text glyphs, particle effects, and game foliage."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "// TreeType is the shared Flyweight (one instance for 100,000 trees)\npublic class TreeType {\n    private final String name, color, texture;\n    public void draw(int x, int y) { /* Render at extrinsic coordinates */ }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Java's String Constant Pool and Integer Cache ([-128, 127]) are classic implementations of the Flyweight pattern.",
