    "id": "int-oops-048",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Visitor Pattern, and how does it achieve Double Dispatch?",
    "answer": "The Visitor Pattern lets you separate algorithms from the objects on which they operate by adding new virtual operations to existing class hierarchies without modifying them:\n\nDouble Dispatch Mechanics:\n- Regular single dispatch resolves methods based solely on the runtime type of the receiver (object.method()).\n- Visitor achieves Double Dispatch: Resolves execution based on the runtime types of TWO objects (the Receiver element AND the Visitor)!\n  1. element.accept(visitor) dispatches dynamically on the element type.\n  2. Inside accept(), it calls visitor.visit(this), dispatching dynamically on the visitor type!\n- Use Case: Compiler Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) traversals (e.g. TypeCheckerVisitor, CodeGeneratorVisitor).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Separates algorithms from object structure, adding operations without modifying classes.",
      "Uses Double Dispatch: element.accept(visitor) -> visitor.visit(this).",
      "Standard pattern for AST compiler traversals and export operations."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "interface ShapeVisitor { void visit(Circle c); void visit(Rectangle r); }\ninterface Shape { void accept(ShapeVisitor v); }\nclass Circle implements Shape {\n    public void accept(ShapeVisitor v) { v.visit(this); } // Double dispatch\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Trade-off: Visitor makes adding new operations trivial, but makes adding new concrete element classes very difficult because all visitors must be updated.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle"
    ],
