    "title": "What is the Null Object Pattern, and how does it eliminate NullPointerExceptions?",
    "answer": "The Null Object Pattern replaces null references with a neutral, do-nothing object that conforms to the expected interface:\n\nBenefits:\n- Eliminates defensive null checks: Removes repetitive 'if (obj != null)' guard conditions throughout codebases.\n- Safe Defaults: Calling methods on a Null Object performs safe default actions (or no-op) without throwing NullPointerException.\n\nStandard Example: A NullLogger that does nothing when log() is called, allowing systems to run without loggers without checking for null.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Provides a neutral do-nothing object implementing the expected interface.",
      "Eliminates repetitive 'if (obj != null)' boilerplate checks.",
      "Prevents runtime NullPointerExceptions cleanly."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "interface Logger { void log(String msg); }\nclass ConsoleLogger implements Logger { public void log(String msg) { System.out.println(msg); } }\nclass NullLogger implements Logger { public void log(String msg) { /* do nothing */ } }\n\n// Client runs safely with zero null checks:\nLogger logger = getLogger(); // Returns NullLogger if logging disabled\nlogger.log(\"System event\");"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Mention Java 8 Optional as the modern functional alternative to the Null Object pattern.",
