    "id": "int-oops-053",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) and what problems does a 'Fat Interface' cause?",
    "answer": "The Interface Segregation Principle states: 'Clients should not be forced to depend upon interfaces that they do not use.'\n\nA 'Fat Interface' (or polluted interface) groups multiple unrelated responsibilities together. When a class implements a fat interface, it is forced to provide empty, throw `UnsupportedOperationException`, or dummy implementations for methods it does not need.\n\nNegative consequences of violating ISP:\n1. Fragile coupling: Changes to method signatures required by client A force recompilation and redeployment of client B that never called those methods.\n2. Polluted API: IDE autocompletion exposes irrelevant and dangerous methods to callers.\n3. Testing friction: Creating mock implementations requires mocking dozens of irrelevant methods.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "ISP advises splitting fat, monolithic interfaces into smaller, cohesive, role-specific interfaces.",
      "Classes can implement multiple focused interfaces (e.g., `Readable`, `Writable`, `Closeable`).",
      "Prevents classes from having dummy methods throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// VIOLATION: Fat Interface\ninterface MultiFunctionPrinter {\n    void print(Document doc);\n    void scan(Document doc);\n    void fax(Document doc);\n}\n\nclass SimpleInkjetPrinter implements MultiFunctionPrinter {\n    public void print(Document doc) { /* prints */ }\n    public void scan(Document doc) { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); } // Polluted!\n    public void fax(Document doc) { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }  // Polluted!\n}\n\n// ISP Compliant: Role-based interfaces\ninterface Printer { void print(Document doc); }\ninterface Scanner { void scan(Document doc); }\ninterface Fax { void fax(Document doc); }\n\nclass SimplePrinter implements Printer {\n    public void print(Document doc) { /* prints */ }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In Java, `AutoCloseable`, `Comparable`, and `Runnable` are classic examples of single-purpose, ISP-compliant role interfaces.",
