    "id": "int-oops-054",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Explain the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP). How does it differ from Dependency Injection (DI) and Inversion of Control (IoC)?",
    "answer": "These three terms represent different conceptual levels:\n1. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP): A high-level design principle stating:\n   - High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions (interfaces/abstract classes).\n   - Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions.\n2. Inversion of Control (IoC): A broad architectural pattern where the control flow of a program is inverted compared to traditional procedural programming. Instead of user code calling library functions, a framework or runtime container calls your code (the 'Hollywood Principle': Don't call us, we'll call you).\n3. Dependency Injection (DI): A concrete implementation technique and pattern used to realize DIP and IoC. Dependencies are supplied (injected) to a class from the outside (via constructor, setter, or field) rather than the class instantiating them internally via `new`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "DIP is the architectural principle (reversing traditional top-down coupling).",
      "IoC is the broad design pattern (inverting control flow to a framework/container).",
      "DI is the concrete design pattern (injecting dependencies into constructors/setters)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// VIOLATION: High-level OrderProcessor tightly coupled to low-level MySQLDatabase\nclass OrderProcessor {\n    private MySQLDatabase db = new MySQLDatabase(); // Tight coupling!\n}\n\n// DIP Compliant: Both depend on Database interface\ninterface Database {\n    void save(Order order);\n}\n\nclass OrderProcessor {\n    private final Database db; // Depends on abstraction\n\n    // Dependency Injection via constructor\n    public OrderProcessor(Database db) {\n        this.db = db;\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "DIP enables effortless unit testing: you can inject an in-memory mock or stub Database into OrderProcessor during testing without touching a live database.",
