    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the difference between Factory Method and Abstract Factory design patterns?",
    "answer": "Key differences:\n1. Level of Abstraction:\n   - Factory Method uses inheritance and subclassing to create a single product. A creator class defines an abstract method (`createProduct()`), and subclasses decide which concrete class to instantiate.\n   - Abstract Factory uses object composition to produce families of related or dependent products (e.g., `createButton()`, `createCheckbox()`) without specifying their concrete classes.\n2. Scope:\n   - Factory Method: Creates ONE product family or variant.\n   - Abstract Factory: Creates ENTIRE SUITES of complementary products that must match (e.g., MacUIFactory creates MacButton + MacCheckbox; WindowsUIFactory creates WinButton + WinCheckbox).\n3. Extension Mechanism:\n   - Factory Method modifies product creation by overriding a single method in a subclass.\n   - Abstract Factory modifies creation by swapping out the entire factory object instance.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Factory Method: A single method overridden by subclasses to produce one product.",
      "Abstract Factory: An interface with multiple factory methods creating families of related products.",
      "Abstract Factory is often implemented using a set of Factory Methods."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// Abstract Factory: creates families of related objects\ninterface GUIFactory {\n    Button createButton();\n    Checkbox createCheckbox();\n}\n\nclass MacFactory implements GUIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() { return new MacButton(); }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() { return new MacCheckbox(); }\n}\n\nclass WinFactory implements GUIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() { return new WinButton(); }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() { return new WinCheckbox(); }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Use Abstract Factory when your system must be independent of how its products are created, and products from the same family must be used together without mixing incompatible variants.",
