    "id": "int-oops-058",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "When should you use the Builder Pattern, and how does it prevent the 'Telescoping Constructor' anti-pattern?",
    "answer": "The Telescoping Constructor anti-pattern occurs when a class has multiple overloaded constructors with increasing parameter lists (e.g., `User(name)`, `User(name, email)`, `User(name, email, age)`, `User(name, email, age, phone)`).\n\nDrawbacks of Telescoping Constructors:\n1. Error-prone: Callers easily swap adjacent parameters of the same type (e.g., passing address in phone parameter).\n2. Readability: Hard to discern which arguments represent what without looking at class definitions.\n\nWhy not JavaBeans (getters/setters)?\nJavaBeans allow instantiating with default constructor and setting fields individually. However, JavaBeans allow an object to be in an inconsistent, partially constructed state midway through setters, and make the class completely mutable.\n\nBuilder Pattern solves this by providing fluent method chaining, step-by-step construction, mandatory vs optional parameter enforcement, and producing an immutable, fully validated object on `.build()`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Eliminates telescoping constructors with multiple confusing arguments.",
      "Preserves immutability unlike JavaBeans with setter methods.",
      "Allows parameter validation inside `.build()` before instantiating the object."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\npublic class HttpRequest {\n    private final String url;       // required\n    private final String method;    // required\n    private final int timeout;      // optional\n    private final String body;      // optional\n\n    private HttpRequest(Builder b) {\n        this.url = b.url;\n        this.method = b.method;\n        this.timeout = b.timeout;\n        this.body = b.body;\n    }\n\n    public static class Builder {\n        private final String url, method;\n        private int timeout = 5000; // default\n        private String body = \"\";\n\n        public Builder(String url, String method) {\n            this.url = url; this.method = method;\n        }\n        public Builder timeout(int t) { this.timeout = t; return this; }\n        public Builder body(String b) { this.body = b; return this; }\n        public HttpRequest build() {\n            if (url == null || method == null) throw new IllegalStateException();\n            return new HttpRequest(this);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In Java libraries like Lombok, `@Builder` generates this entire boilerplate builder pattern automatically at compile time.",
