    "id": "int-oops-059",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Prototype Pattern and what is the difference between Shallow Copy and Deep Copy?",
    "answer": "The Prototype Pattern is a creational design pattern used to clone or duplicate existing objects without making code dependent on their concrete classes.\n\nKey distinction in copying:\n1. Shallow Copy:\n   - Copies all primitive values directly.\n   - For reference fields (pointers/objects), it copies only the memory reference, not the underlying object. Both original and clone share the same referenced instances! Mutating an inner object in the clone mutates it in the original.\n2. Deep Copy:\n   - Copies all primitive fields AND recursively allocates new memory and duplicates every referenced child object.\n   - Complete independence: changes to the clone's internal nested structures have zero side effects on the original object.\n\nIn Java, `Object.clone()` performs a shallow field-by-field copy by default.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Prototype pattern creates new instances by cloning a prototype instance.",
      "Shallow copy shares referenced objects; deep copy clones entire object graphs recursively.",
      "Deep copies can be implemented via copy constructors, deep clone methods, or serialization libraries."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\nclass Address implements Cloneable {\n    String city;\n    public Address(String city) { this.city = city; }\n    public Address clone() { return new Address(this.city); } // deep copy helper\n}\n\nclass User implements Cloneable {\n    String name;\n    Address address;\n\n    // Shallow copy: shares address reference\n    public User shallowCopy() {\n        User u = new User();\n        u.name = this.name;\n        u.address = this.address; // SHARED!\n        return u;\n    }\n\n    // Deep copy: independent address\n    public User deepCopy() {\n        User u = new User();\n        u.name = this.name;\n        u.address = this.address.clone(); // NEW OBJECT!\n        return u;\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Avoid implementing Java's `Cloneable` interface if possible; Joshua Bloch famously noted it is broken because it does not define a public `clone()` method. Use copy constructors or factory methods instead.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Microsoft",
      "Amazon",
      "VMware",
      "Oracle"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
