    "id": "int-oops-060",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Adapter Pattern, and what is the difference between Class Adapter and Object Adapter?",
    "answer": "The Adapter Pattern (also known as a Wrapper) converts the interface of a class into another interface that clients expect, letting classes work together that couldn't otherwise because of incompatible interfaces.\n\nTwo variants:\n1. Object Adapter (Composition-based):\n   - The Adapter implements the Target interface and contains a reference (composition) to the Adaptee instance.\n   - Highly flexible: Works with the Adaptee and any of its subclasses.\n   - Preferred in accordance with 'Favor composition over inheritance'.\n2. Class Adapter (Inheritance-based):\n   - The Adapter inherits simultaneously from the Target class (or implements Target interface) and the Adaptee class using multiple inheritance (e.g., in C++ or Java interfaces).\n   - Less flexible because it commits statically to one specific Adaptee class.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Adapter reconciles incompatible interfaces between two legacy or external libraries.",
      "Object Adapter uses composition (holds an instance of the Adaptee).",
      "Class Adapter uses inheritance (subclasses the Adaptee)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// Target expected by client\ninterface LightningPort {\n    void chargePhone();\n}\n\n// Adaptee (incompatible interface)\nclass USBCTypeCharger {\n    public void powerWithUSBC() { System.out.println(\"Charging via USB-C...\"); }\n}\n\n// Object Adapter: Implements target, delegates to adaptee\nclass USBCToLightningAdapter implements LightningPort {\n    private final USBCTypeCharger usbcCharger;\n\n    public USBCToLightningAdapter(USBCTypeCharger charger) {\n        this.usbcCharger = charger;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void chargePhone() {\n        usbcCharger.powerWithUSBC();\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "`Arrays.asList(T... a)` in Java is a classic adapter wrapping a raw array into a `List` interface.",
