    "id": "int-oops-064",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Strategy Pattern and how does it eliminate sprawling `if-else` or `switch` statements?",
    "answer": "The Strategy Pattern defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one in a separate class, and makes them interchangeable at runtime. Strategy lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it.\n\nProblem it solves:\nCode with monolithic `switch(paymentType)` or chained `if (type == CREDIT_CARD) ... else if (type == PAYPAL) ...` violates the Open/Closed Principle. Adding a new payment type requires modifying existing code, risking regressions.\n\nWith Strategy:\n1. Define a `PaymentStrategy` interface with `pay(amount)`.\n2. Create concrete strategy classes: `CreditCardPayment`, `PayPalPayment`, `CryptoPayment`.\n3. The Context class (`ShoppingCart`) holds a reference to `PaymentStrategy` and executes `strategy.pay(amount)`.\n4. New strategies can be added anytime without touching existing classes.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Encapsulates algorithms into standalone interchangeable strategy classes.",
      "Eliminates conditional branching (`switch`/`if-else`) for behavior selection.",
      "Enforces the Open/Closed Principle and Dependency Inversion Principle."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\ninterface PaymentStrategy {\n    void pay(int amount);\n}\n\nclass CreditCardStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {\n    public void pay(int amount) { System.out.println(\"Paid \" + amount + \" via Card\"); }\n}\n\nclass PayPalStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {\n    public void pay(int amount) { System.out.println(\"Paid \" + amount + \" via PayPal\"); }\n}\n\nclass ShoppingCart {\n    private PaymentStrategy strategy;\n    public void setPaymentStrategy(PaymentStrategy s) { this.strategy = s; }\n    public void checkout(int total) {\n        strategy.pay(total); // No if-else branching!\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In modern Java 8+, if a strategy has only one method, you can pass lambdas directly: `cart.setPaymentStrategy(amt -> System.out.println(amt));`.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Uber",
      "Paypal"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
