    "id": "int-oops-069",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Explain the Chain of Responsibility Pattern. How is it implemented in Servlet Filters and Spring Security?",
    "answer": "The Chain of Responsibility pattern decouples the sender of a request from its receivers by giving more than one object a chance to handle the request. Receivers are linked into a chain; the request passes along the chain until an object handles it or the end of the chain is reached.\n\nKey Mechanics:\nEach handler contains a reference to the `nextHandler` in the chain. When receiving a request, the handler:\n1. Decides whether to process the request.\n2. Performs its logic (e.g., authentication, logging, rate limiting).\n3. Decides whether to short-circuit (abort) or pass execution to `nextHandler.handle(request)`.\n\nIn Web Architectures:\n- Java Servlet `FilterChain`: Intercepts HTTP requests for logging, CORS, CSRF, and compression.\n- Spring Security `SecurityFilterChain`: Sequential filters verifying JWT tokens, checking roles, and authenticating credentials before hitting controller endpoints.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Avoids coupling the sender to a specific receiver by passing requests down a pipeline.",
      "Supports dynamic reordering or addition of handling steps.",
      "Found in web middleware, HTTP filter pipelines, and event dispatchers."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\nabstract class Middleware {\n    private Middleware next;\n    public Middleware linkWith(Middleware next) { this.next = next; return next; }\n    public abstract boolean check(String user, String role);\n    protected boolean checkNext(String user, String role) {\n        if (next == null) return true;\n        return next.check(user, role);\n    }\n}\n\nclass AuthMiddleware extends Middleware {\n    public boolean check(String user, String role) {\n        if (user == null) return false; // reject\n        return checkNext(user, role);   // pass downstream\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Chain of Responsibility is ideal for request validation pipelines where failing any single check immediately rejects the request without running the remaining checks.",
