    "id": "int-oops-070",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Composite Pattern, and when is it necessary to treat individual objects and compositions of objects uniformly?",
    "answer": "The Composite Pattern is a structural design pattern that lets you compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies. Composite allows clients to treat individual objects and compositions of objects uniformly.\n\nReal-World Use Cases:\n1. File Systems: A File is an individual leaf node; a Directory is a composite node that contains both Files and other Directories. Both implement a `FileSystemItem` interface with `getSize()` or `delete()`.\n2. UI Component Hierarchies: A Button is a leaf; a Panel is a composite containing Buttons, TextBoxes, and other Panels. Calling `render()` on the root window recursively renders all children.\n3. Organization Charts: An Employee is a leaf; a Department/Manager is a composite managing multiple Employees and sub-departments.\n\nClient code does not need to know whether it is dealing with a single leaf or a massive composite tree; it calls `operation()` on the root.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Represents part-whole hierarchical tree structures.",
      "Leaf and Composite share a common component interface.",
      "Recursively propagates operations down the entire hierarchy."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\ninterface FileSystemComponent {\n    int getSize();\n}\n\nclass FileItem implements FileSystemComponent {\n    private int size;\n    public FileItem(int size) { this.size = size; }\n    public int getSize() { return size; }\n}\n\nclass Directory implements FileSystemComponent {\n    private List<FileSystemComponent> children = new ArrayList<>();\n    public void add(FileSystemComponent c) { children.add(c); }\n\n    public int getSize() {\n        int total = 0;\n        for (FileSystemComponent c : children) total += c.getSize(); // recursive\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "When implementing Composite, consider whether client code should add children via the common interface (transparent) or only on the Composite class (type-safe). Type-safe is usually preferred in strongly-typed languages.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Adobe",
      "Microsoft",
      "Apple"
    ],
