    "id": "int-oops-071",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Flyweight Pattern? Distinguish between Intrinsic State and Extrinsic State with a concrete example.",
    "answer": "The Flyweight Pattern minimizes memory usage by sharing as much data as possible with similar objects. It is crucial when an application needs to spawn millions of fine-grained objects (e.g., rendering a forest of 1,000,000 trees in a game or formatting every character in a document editor).\n\nState Separation:\n1. Intrinsic State:\n   - Invariant, immutable, and context-independent data.\n   - Shared across all instances.\n   - Stored directly inside the Flyweight object.\n   - Example: Tree model mesh, high-res texture image, color palette (`TreeType`).\n2. Extrinsic State:\n   - Variant, context-dependent data that changes per instance.\n   - Passed into the flyweight's methods by client code from the outside.\n   - Example: Specific (x, y, z) coordinates, height, and health of a tree in the game world.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Reduces memory footprint by sharing immutable intrinsic state among millions of objects.",
      "Intrinsic state: Shared, stored inside flyweight.",
      "Extrinsic state: Unique, maintained outside by the client and passed in as parameters."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// Flyweight: Shared Intrinsic State\nclass TreeType {\n    private final String name, color, textureData; // Heavy data\n    public TreeType(String n, String c, String t) {\n        this.name = n; this.color = c; this.textureData = t;\n    }\n    // Extrinsic state (x, y) passed dynamically\n    public void draw(int x, int y) {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing \" + name + \" at \" + x + \",\" + y);\n    }\n}\n\n// Client manages Extrinsic coordinates, referencing shared TreeType\nclass Tree {\n    private int x, y; // Extrinsic\n    private TreeType type; // Shared reference (Intrinsic)\n    public Tree(int x, int y, TreeType type) {\n        this.x = x; this.y = y; this.type = type;\n    }\n    public void draw() { type.draw(x, y); }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Java's `Integer.valueOf(int)` caches numbers between -128 and 127 using the Flyweight Pattern to conserve heap memory.",
