    "id": "int-oops-076",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization), and how does it compare to Garbage Collection in Java/Python?",
    "answer": "RAII is a C++ programming idiom where resource lifecycle (file handles, mutex locks, network sockets, heap memory) is tied directly to the lifetime of a stack-allocated object.\n\nMechanics:\n- The constructor acquires the resource.\n- The destructor automatically releases the resource when the object goes out of scope (stack unwinding), even during exceptions.\n- Deterministic cleanup: Resources are released the exact microsecond the scope exits.\n\nComparison with Garbage Collection (Java, Python, C#):\n- Garbage collection only manages heap memory, not non-memory system resources (file descriptors, sockets, DB connections).\n- GC is non-deterministic: you cannot know when finalizers/garbage collectors will run.\n- Java introduced `try-with-resources` (`AutoCloseable`) and Python introduced `with` statements (context managers) to mimic RAII's deterministic scope-bound cleanup for non-memory resources.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "RAII guarantees deterministic, automatic resource cleanup upon scope exit in C++.",
      "Garbage collection manages memory non-deterministically; it does not reliably manage OS handles.",
      "Java's try-with-resources and Python's context managers emulate RAII cleanup."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```cpp\n// C++ RAII: File closed deterministically on scope exit\nvoid processFile() {\n    std::ofstream file(\"log.txt\"); // acquired in constructor\n    file << \"Hello World\";\n    if (errorCondition) return; // Destructor called automatically! No leak!\n}\n\n// Java Equivalent (try-with-resources):\n// try (FileWriter fw = new FileWriter(\"log.txt\")) {\n//     fw.write(\"Hello\");\n// } // AutoCloseable.close() called automatically\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Never rely on `finalize()` in Java or `__del__` in Python for closing critical files or sockets; use `try-with-resources` or context managers instead.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Microsoft",
      "Bloomberg",
      "Google",
      "Apple"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
