    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Explain the Virtual Method Table (vtable) and vptr. What is the exact performance and memory overhead of dynamic dispatch?",
    "answer": "Dynamic dispatch (runtime polymorphism) is powered internally by a Virtual Method Table (vtable):\n\n1. Vtable structure:\n   - For every class containing at least one virtual method, the compiler generates a static table of function pointers (vtable).\n   - Each virtual method occupies a fixed slot offset in this table.\n2. Vptr (Virtual Pointer):\n   - Every instance of the class contains an invisible pointer (`vptr`) pointing to the vtable of its runtime type.\n   - The vptr is initialized during the constructor execution.\n3. Resolution Step at Runtime:\n   - When executing `ptr->speak()`, the program dereferences the object's `vptr`, looks up the function pointer at the known fixed slot offset for `speak()`, and jumps to that instruction address.\n\nOverheads:\n- Memory: An extra 8 bytes (on 64-bit OS) per object instance for the `vptr`, plus one vtable per polymorphic class.\n- Execution: Extra pointer indirection (dereference `vptr` -> read slot -> jump) plus CPU branch misprediction penalties.\n- Inlining inhibition: Compilers cannot inline virtual method calls unless Devirtualization optimizations succeed.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Vtable is a static array of function pointers per polymorphic class.",
      "Vptr is a hidden pointer inside each object instance referencing its class vtable.",
      "Causes pointer dereference overhead and prevents aggressive compiler inlining."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```cpp\nclass Animal {\npublic:\n    virtual void speak() { std::cout << \"Animal\"; } // slot 0 in vtable\n};\n\nclass Dog : public Animal {\npublic:\n    void speak() override { std::cout << \"Bark\"; } // overrides slot 0\n};\n\nAnimal* a = new Dog();\n// Under the hood:\n// 1. Fetch Dog's vptr from *a\n// 2. Fetch function pointer at slot 0: vptr[0]\n// 3. Call function pointer: (*vptr[0])(a)\na->speak();\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In C++, declaring a destructor as `virtual` in a polymorphic base class is mandatory; otherwise, deleting a derived object through a base pointer causes undefined behavior and memory leaks.",
