    "id": "int-oops-080",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Why must base class destructors be declared `virtual` in C++ when deleting through a base pointer?",
    "answer": "In C++, if you delete a derived class object through a pointer to its base class and the base class destructor is non-virtual, the behavior is undefined according to the C++ standard.\n\nIn practice:\n- The compiler statically binds the destructor call to the type of the pointer (`Base`), not the runtime object (`Derived`).\n- Only the `Base` destructor executes.\n- The `Derived` destructor is completely skipped!\n\nConsequence:\nAny dynamically allocated resources, files, network sockets, or memory allocated inside `Derived` are never cleaned up, resulting in severe resource leaks and potential memory corruption.\n\nDeclaring `virtual ~Base()` ensures dynamic dispatch is used to invoke the derived destructor first, followed sequentially by the base destructor.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Deleting through a base pointer without a virtual destructor causes undefined behavior.",
      "Derived destructor will never be called, leaking resources.",
      "Rule of thumb: Any class with at least one virtual method should have a virtual destructor."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```cpp\nclass Base {\npublic:\n    // BUG: Missing virtual destructor!\n    // virtual ~Base() {} // Correct fix\n    ~Base() { std::cout << \"Base destroyed\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass Derived : public Base {\n    int* buffer;\npublic:\n    Derived() { buffer = new int[1000]; }\n    ~Derived() {\n        delete[] buffer; // SKIPPED IF BASE DESTRUCTOR IS NOT VIRTUAL!\n        std::cout << \"Derived destroyed\\n\";\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Base* p = new Derived();\n    delete p; // Only prints \"Base destroyed\"! buffer is LEAKED!\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If a class is not meant to be a base class, avoid virtual destructors because adding a vptr wastes 8 bytes per object instance.",
