    "id": "int-oops-081",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is Covariance, Contravariance, and Invariance? How does PECS (Producer Extends, Consumer Super) apply to OOP generics?",
    "answer": "Variance defines how subtyping between complex types (like `List<Dog>`) relates to subtyping between their component types (`Dog` is a subtype of `Animal`):\n\n1. Invariance: No subtyping relationship is preserved. Even though `Dog` is an `Animal`, `List<Dog>` is NOT a subtype of `List<Animal>`. (Java generics are invariant by default to prevent type corruption: adding a `Cat` to a `List<Animal>` that actually points to `List<Dog>`).\n2. Covariance (`? extends T`): Subtyping is preserved. `List<Dog>` is a subtype of `List<? extends Animal>`. You can READ animals out of this list (it produces `Animal`), but you CANNOT write to it (except `null`).\n3. Contravariance (`? super T`): Subtyping is reversed. `List<Animal>` is a subtype of `List<? super Dog>`. You can safely WRITE `Dog` instances into this list (it consumes `Dog`), but reading only yields `Object`.\n\nPECS Rule (Joshua Bloch):\n- 'Producer Extends': If your parameterized type represents a producer that only yields items, use `<? extends T>`.\n- 'Consumer Super': If your parameterized type represents a consumer that absorbs items, use `<? super T>`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Invariance: Default in Java generics; prevents invalid object insertion across hierarchies.",
      "Covariance (? extends T): Safe for reading; objects produce data of type T.",
      "Contravariance (? super T): Safe for writing; objects consume data of type T."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// PECS in Collections.copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src)\npublic static <T> void copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < src.size(); i++) {\n        // src PRODUCES T (extends), dest CONSUMES T (super)\n        dest.set(i, src.get(i));\n    }\n}\n\n// Covariant return types in Java method overriding:\nclass AnimalShelter {\n    public Animal adopt() { return new Animal(); }\n}\nclass DogShelter extends AnimalShelter {\n    // Covariant return: Dog is subtype of Animal (Legal in Java 5+)\n    @Override public Dog adopt() { return new Dog(); }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Covariant return types are permitted in method overriding, but method parameter types cannot be covariant (they would violate LSP by narrowing preconditions).",
