    "id": "int-oops-083",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What are the Rule of Three, Rule of Five, and Rule of Zero in C++ object lifecycle management?",
    "answer": "In C++, these rules govern resource management when a class owns raw pointers or OS handles:\n\n1. Rule of Three (C++98):\nIf a class needs to explicitly define any of the following three, it almost certainly must define all three:\n   - Destructor (to release memory/resource)\n   - Copy Constructor (to perform deep copy)\n   - Copy Assignment Operator (to free old memory and deep copy)\n\n2. Rule of Five (C++11):\nWith the introduction of move semantics and rvalue references (`&&`), two additional special functions are needed for performance (transferring ownership without copying):\n   - Move Constructor (`T(T&& other)`)\n   - Move Assignment Operator (`T& operator=(T&& other)`)\n\n3. Rule of Zero (Modern C++ Best Practice):\nClasses should avoid manually managing raw resources. Instead, rely on modern smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) or containers (`std::vector`, `std::string`). Because these RAII wrappers manage their own cleanup and copying, the enclosing class defines ZERO custom destructors or copy/move operators.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Rule of Three: Destructor, Copy Constructor, Copy Assignment Operator.",
      "Rule of Five: Adds Move Constructor and Move Assignment Operator for rvalues.",
      "Rule of Zero: Use RAII types (smart pointers) so the class defines no special lifecycle members."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```cpp\n// Rule of Five Example:\nclass Buffer {\n    size_t size;\n    int* data;\npublic:\n    Buffer(size_t s) : size(s), data(new int[s]) {}\n    ~Buffer() { delete[] data; } // 1. Destructor\n    Buffer(const Buffer& o) : size(o.size), data(new int[o.size]) { // 2. Copy Ctor\n        std::copy(o.data, o.data + size, data);\n    }\n    Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& o) { /* 3. Copy Assign */ return *this; }\n    Buffer(Buffer&& o) noexcept : size(o.size), data(o.data) { // 4. Move Ctor\n        o.data = nullptr; o.size = 0;\n    }\n    Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& o) noexcept { /* 5. Move Assign */ return *this; }\n};\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Always mark move constructors and move assignment operators with `noexcept`; otherwise, standard containers like `std::vector` will fall back to expensive copying during reallocations.",
