    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "How does the Memento Pattern capture an object's internal state without violating encapsulation?",
    "answer": "The Memento Pattern captures and externalizes an object's internal state so that the object can be restored to this state later, without violating encapsulation.\n\nThree Core Roles:\n1. Originator: The object whose state needs to be saved (e.g., `TextEditor`). It creates a memento containing a snapshot of its current state and uses the memento to restore state.\n2. Memento: A passive value object storing the state snapshot. It provides a 'narrow' interface to the outside world (no getters/setters) and a 'wide' interface accessible only to the Originator.\n3. Caretaker: Responsible for keeping the memento safe (e.g., `HistoryStack`). The Caretaker never inspects, modifies, or reads the contents of the memento; it simply stores it and hands it back to the Originator for restoration.\n\nEncapsulation is preserved because the Caretaker and client code cannot inspect the private fields inside the Memento.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Captures snapshot of an object's state without exposing internal fields.",
      "Separates Originator (state creator), Memento (snapshot), and Caretaker (history manager).",
      "Essential for Undo/Redo mechanisms and database savepoints."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// Memento: Immutable snapshot\nclass EditorMemento {\n    private final String content;\n    public EditorMemento(String content) { this.content = content; }\n    public String getContent() { return content; } // package-private or inner class\n}\n\n// Originator: Creates and restores from memento\nclass TextEditor {\n    private String content = \"\";\n    public void type(String words) { content += words; }\n    public EditorMemento save() { return new EditorMemento(content); }\n    public void restore(EditorMemento m) { this.content = m.getContent(); }\n    public String getContent() { return content; }\n}\n\n// Caretaker manages history stack without inspecting memento state\nStack<EditorMemento> history = new Stack<>();\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In languages like Java, declare `Memento` as a private inner class inside `Originator` so only the originator can access its private state.",
