    "id": "int-oops-087",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Mediator Pattern and how does it prevent an exponential web of dependencies between UI components?",
    "answer": "In complex interactive systems (like a dialog box with 10 UI elements: Checkboxes, Buttons, TextBoxes, Dropdowns), each element needs to react to state changes in other elements (e.g., checking a box disables a textbox, activates a button, and filters a dropdown). If components talk directly to each other, you get an unmaintainable $O(N^2)$ web of tight coupling.\n\nThe Mediator Pattern defines an object that encapsulates how a set of objects interact. Mediator promotes loose coupling by keeping objects from referring to each other explicitly, and it lets you vary their interaction independently.\n\nMechanics:\n- Components (Colleagues) communicate solely with the Mediator (`mediator.notify(this, event)`).\n- The Mediator handles the complex coordination logic and directs other colleagues accordingly.\n- Reduces $O(N^2)$ cross-dependencies to $O(N)$ star topology centered on the mediator.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Centralizes complex communication between multiple collaborating objects into a single hub.",
      "Replaces many-to-many relationships with manageable one-to-many relationships.",
      "Air Traffic Control tower is the standard real-world metaphor for a Mediator."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\ninterface Mediator {\n    void notify(Component sender, String event);\n}\n\nclass AuthenticationDialog implements Mediator {\n    private Button submitBtn;\n    private Checkbox agreeTerms;\n\n    public void notify(Component sender, String event) {\n        if (sender == agreeTerms && event.equals(\"checked\")) {\n            submitBtn.setEnabled(true); // Mediator coordinates UI interaction!\n        }\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
