    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Explain the Iterator Pattern. What is the difference between External (Passive) and Internal (Active) Iterators?",
    "answer": "The Iterator Pattern provides a way to access the elements of an aggregate object sequentially without exposing its underlying representation (array, linked list, tree, hash table).\n\n1. External (Passive) Iterator:\n   - The client controls the iteration sequence. The client manually advances the iterator using methods like `hasNext()` and `next()`.\n   - Offers high control: Client can break early, skip elements, compare adjacent elements, or iterate two collections simultaneously.\n   - Standard in Java `java.util.Iterator`, C++ STL iterators, Python `iter()`.\n\n2. Internal (Active) Iterator:\n   - The collection itself controls the iteration loop; the client passes a callback/function/lambda that is applied to each element.\n   - Client cannot easily pause or interleave iterations.\n   - Examples: `list.forEach(element -> process(element))` in Java 8+, Python list comprehensions, JavaScript `Array.prototype.forEach`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Decouples traversal algorithms from underlying data structure storage representations.",
      "External iterators grant iteration control to the client (hasNext/next).",
      "Internal iterators delegate traversal to the collection and accept functional lambda callbacks."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// External Iterator:\nIterator<String> it = names.iterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\n    String name = it.next();\n    if (name.equals(\"Target\")) break; // Client controls loop termination\n}\n\n// Internal Iterator:\nnames.forEach(name -> {\n    System.out.println(name); // Collection controls the traversal loop\n});\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In concurrent programming, fail-fast iterators throw `ConcurrentModificationException` if the underlying collection is modified during external iteration without using the iterator's own `remove()` method.",
