    "id": "int-oops-089",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "How does the Null Object Pattern eliminate defensive `if (obj != null)` checks throughout a codebase?",
    "answer": "Sir Tony Hoare called `null` his 'billion-dollar mistake'. Throughout procedural and poorly designed OOP code, developers scatter defensive null checks (`if (user != null && user.getAddress() != null ...)`).\n\nThe Null Object Pattern provides an object that conforms to the expected interface but provides a do-nothing (no-op) or neutral default behavior instead of returning `null`.\n\nImplementation:\n1. Define common interface (`Logger`).\n2. Implement real behavior (`ConsoleLogger`, `FileLogger`).\n3. Implement `NullLogger` where all methods do nothing (`public void log(String msg) { /* no-op */ }`).\n4. Clients can safely call `logger.log(...)` without any null checks.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Replaces null references with a polymorphically safe no-op object.",
      "Eliminates NullPointerException crashes and removes clutter of repetitive null checks.",
      "Examples: NullLogger, Optional.empty(), Collections.emptyList()."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\ninterface Logger {\n    void log(String message);\n}\n\nclass ConsoleLogger implements Logger {\n    public void log(String msg) { System.out.println(msg); }\n}\n\nclass NullLogger implements Logger {\n    public void log(String msg) { /* do nothing safely */ }\n}\n\nclass OrderService {\n    private final Logger logger;\n    public OrderService(Logger logger) {\n        // Fallback to NullLogger if null passed\n        this.logger = (logger != null) ? logger : new NullLogger();\n    }\n\n    public void completeOrder() {\n        logger.log(\"Order completed\"); // ZERO null checks needed!\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
