    "id": "int-oops-090",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What rules must an OOP class follow to be completely Immutable and inherently Thread-Safe?",
    "answer": "An immutable object is an object whose state cannot be modified after it is constructed. Immutable objects are inherently thread-safe and can be shared freely across threads without synchronization.\n\nRules to make a class completely Immutable (Joshua Bloch):\n1. Do not provide any 'mutator' methods (no setters).\n2. Ensure the class cannot be extended: Mark the class `final` (or make constructors private and provide public static factories) so malicious subclasses cannot override methods to mutate state.\n3. Make all fields `final`: Enforces immutability at compile-time and guarantees memory visibility across threads according to the Java Memory Model.\n4. Make all fields `private`: Prevents clients from obtaining direct references to mutate state.\n5. Ensure exclusive access to any mutable components: If the class contains fields pointing to mutable objects (e.g., `Date`, `ArrayList`), make defensive copies in constructors AND defensive copies in getter methods! Never return the direct internal reference.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Immutable objects are completely thread-safe with zero synchronization locking overhead.",
      "Make class final, fields private and final, and provide no mutator methods.",
      "Defensively copy all mutable parameters in constructors and getters."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\npublic final class ImmutableUser {\n    private final String username;\n    private final List<String> roles; // Mutable collection!\n\n    public ImmutableUser(String username, List<String> roles) {\n        this.username = username;\n        // Defensive copy to protect against external mutation!\n        this.roles = new ArrayList<>(roles);\n    }\n\n    public String getUsername() { return username; }\n\n    // Defensive copy or unmodifiable view on getter!\n    public List<String> getRoles() {\n        return Collections.unmodifiableList(roles);\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "`String`, `Integer`, `BigDecimal`, and modern `java.time.LocalDate` in Java are standard immutable, thread-safe classes.",
