    "id": "int-oops-091",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "What is the Object Pool Pattern and when does it outperform standard garbage collection?",
    "answer": "The Object Pool Pattern manages a cache of initialized, reusable objects ready to be checked out, used, and checked back in, rather than creating and destroying objects on demand.\n\nWhen to Use:\n1. Allocation cost is exceptionally high: Initializing the object requires heavyweight OS socket handshakes, TLS negotiation, authentication, or physical memory allocation (e.g., Database Connection Pools like HikariCP, Thread Pools like `ExecutorService`).\n2. Real-time / Low-latency Systems: In high-frequency trading (HFT) and game engines, continuous object creation causes GC pause spikes (Stop-the-World pauses). Reusing pooled objects completely avoids triggering GC collections.\n\nWhen NOT to Use:\nFor lightweight, short-lived objects (e.g., simple strings or DTOs). Modern JVMs can allocate 1,000,000 small objects on the Eden generation in milliseconds; an object pool adds synchronization contention that is slower than the garbage collector.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Reuses heavyweight, expensive objects rather than continually creating and destroying them.",
      "Standard for Database Connections (HikariCP) and Thread Pools (ExecutorService).",
      "Prevents garbage collection Stop-The-World latency spikes in real-time gaming and HFT."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\npublic class ConnectionPool {\n    private BlockingQueue<DBConnection> pool = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(10);\n\n    public ConnectionPool() {\n        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) pool.add(new DBConnection());\n    }\n\n    public DBConnection acquire() throws InterruptedException {\n        return pool.take(); // Wait until a connection is available\n    }\n\n    public void release(DBConnection conn) {\n        conn.resetState();\n        pool.offer(conn); // Return to pool for reuse\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Always wrap pooled objects with an `AutoCloseable` proxy so that `conn.close()` returns the object to the pool rather than terminating the underlying physical connection.",
