    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Explain Low Coupling and High Cohesion from the GRASP patterns. How do they balance each other?",
    "answer": "GRASP (General Responsibility Assignment Software Patterns) provides foundational principles for assigning responsibilities to classes:\n\n1. High Cohesion:\n   - Measures how strongly related and focused the responsibilities of a single class are.\n   - A highly cohesive class does one clear job and does it well. All its methods operate on its internal fields to achieve that purpose.\n   - Benefit: Easy to maintain, understand, reuse, and test.\n\n2. Low Coupling:\n   - Measures how dependent a class is on other classes.\n   - Tightly coupled classes break whenever their dependencies change, are impossible to unit test in isolation, and cannot be reused without dragging along their dependency graph.\n   - Benefit: Changes to one module do not ripple across the codebase.\n\nBalancing Both:\nExtreme low coupling would mean putting ALL application logic in one single giant class (so there are 0 external dependencies!). However, this annihilates High Cohesion (creating a God Object). Good architecture strikes a balance: highly cohesive modules interacting via clean, minimal, abstract interfaces (low coupling).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "High Cohesion: A class has a single, well-defined focus with related methods.",
      "Low Coupling: Minimal dependencies between distinct modules.",
      "Achieved by assigning responsibilities to classes based on information locality and role clarity."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// POOR COHESION & HIGH COUPLING:\nclass MonolithManager {\n    void authenticateUser() { /*...*/ }\n    void renderHTML() { /*...*/ }\n    void executeRawSQL() { /*...*/ }\n    void sendSMSToTwilio() { /*...*/ }\n}\n\n// HIGH COHESION & LOW COUPLING:\nclass AuthService { void authenticate() { /*...*/ } }\nclass UserRepository { void findUser() { /*...*/ } }\nclass NotificationService { void sendAlert() { /*...*/ } }\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In system design interviews, describe microservice boundary definition as maximizing internal service cohesion while minimizing cross-service coupling.",
