    "id": "int-oops-100",
    "topic_id": "topic-oops",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OOPS",
    "subject_label": "OOPs",
    "title": "Summarize the Top 5 Tricky OOP Pitfalls frequently targeted by FAANG interviewers.",
    "answer": "Top 5 Tricky Pitfalls in OOP Technical Interviews:\n\n1. Calling Virtual/Overridable Methods inside Constructors:\n   - The derived object's constructor has NOT finished running its field initializers. Invoking an overridden method will access uninitialized derived fields, causing subtle null pointer exceptions or zeros.\n2. Violating LSP with Downcasting (`instanceof` cascades):\n   - Using chains of `if (obj instanceof Subclass)` indicates poor polymorphism and tightly couples callers to concrete implementations.\n3. Breaking Hash-Based Collections by Overriding `equals()` without `hashCode()`:\n   - Equal objects hash to different buckets in `HashSet`/`HashMap`, making retrieved objects vanish.\n4. Leaking Internal References to Mutable Fields:\n   - Returning raw mutable arrays, dates, or collections from getters breaks encapsulation and allows external callers to corrupt private state.\n5. Subclassing when Composition was Needed (Fragile Base Class):\n   - Inheritance couples derived classes to parent implementation details that can change without warning.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Never invoke overridable virtual methods inside constructors.",
      "Always override hashCode when overriding equals.",
      "Defensively copy mutable objects in constructors and getters to guarantee encapsulation.",
      "Avoid instanceof chains; rely on dynamic polymorphism instead."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "java",
      "code": "```java\n// Pitfall 1: Calling overridable method in constructor\nclass Parent {\n    public Parent() {\n        printValue(); // DANGEROUS! Calls child method before child fields are initialized!\n    }\n    public void printValue() { System.out.println(\"Parent\"); }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    private String name = \"Antigravity\"; // Not yet initialized when Parent() runs!\n    @Override\n    public void printValue() {\n        System.out.println(\"Child: \" + name.toUpperCase()); // CRASHES: NullPointerException!\n    }\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Memorize the constructor pitfall: In Java, base constructor runs BEFORE derived field initializers, so calling an overridden method will see uninitialized fields as null or 0.",
