    "id": "int-dbms-004",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What are Entity Integrity, Referential Integrity, and Domain Integrity? What happens during ON DELETE CASCADE vs RESTRICT vs SET NULL?",
    "answer": "Relational integrity constraints enforce database consistency:\n\n1. Domain Integrity: Every attribute value must belong to the predefined atomic domain (data type, range, format, `CHECK` constraints, `NOT NULL`).\n2. Entity Integrity: No attribute of a Primary Key can be `NULL`. Primary keys serve as row addresses; a null identifier makes tuple differentiation impossible.\n3. Referential Integrity: A Foreign Key in referencing relation $R_1$ referencing Primary Key in $R_2$ must either:\n   - Match an existing valid Primary Key value in $R_2$, OR\n   - Be completely `NULL` (unless explicitly marked `NOT NULL`).\n\nForeign Key Actions on Parent Row Deletion (`ON DELETE`):\n- `CASCADE`: Deleting a parent row automatically deletes all corresponding child rows referencing it.\n- `RESTRICT` / `NO ACTION`: Aborts and rejects the deletion with a foreign key violation error if any child row references it.\n- `SET NULL`: Sets the foreign key column in all matching child rows to `NULL` (requires child column to be nullable).\n- `SET DEFAULT`: Sets the child foreign key column to its declared default value.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Entity Integrity: Primary key columns cannot contain NULL.",
      "Referential Integrity: Foreign keys must match a valid parent key or be NULL.",
      "ON DELETE CASCADE deletes children; RESTRICT aborts the operation if children exist."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\nCREATE TABLE Orders (\n    order_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    customer_id INT,\n    FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES Customers(customer_id)\n        ON DELETE CASCADE       -- Deleting customer deletes all their orders\n        ON UPDATE RESTRICT      -- Rejects customer_id modification if orders exist\n);\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In PostgreSQL, `NO ACTION` checks constraints at the end of the transaction if marked `DEFERRABLE`, whereas `RESTRICT` checks immediately.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle",
      "Amazon",
      "Uber"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
