    "id": "int-dbms-005",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "How does Three-Valued Logic (3VL) handle NULL in SQL? Why does `WHERE col = NULL` always return zero rows?",
    "answer": "In SQL and the Relational Model, `NULL` does NOT represent zero, empty string `\"\"`, or any specific value; it represents 'Unknown', 'Missing', or 'Not Applicable'.\n\nBecause NULL represents an unknown value, SQL evaluates boolean expressions using Three-Valued Logic (3VL) with truth values: `TRUE`, `FALSE`, and `UNKNOWN`.\n\nWhy `col = NULL` fails:\nComparing anything with an unknown value yields `UNKNOWN`. Even `NULL = NULL` evaluates to `UNKNOWN`, because one unknown value may not equal another unknown value!\n\nIn SQL queries, `WHERE` clauses filter rows where the predicate evaluates strictly to `TRUE`. Because `UNKNOWN` is not `TRUE`, `WHERE col = NULL` evaluates to `UNKNOWN` for every single row, returning 0 rows!\n\nTo check for nullity, SQL provides the special unary predicates `IS NULL` and `IS NOT NULL`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "NULL represents unknown or missing data, evaluated using Three-Valued Logic (TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN).",
      "`x = NULL` evaluates to UNKNOWN for all values of x, including when x is NULL.",
      "WHERE filters require truth value strictly equal to TRUE (UNKNOWN is discarded).",
      "Always use `IS NULL` and `IS NOT NULL` instead of equality operators."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Truth Table for 3VL:\n-- TRUE AND UNKNOWN  -> UNKNOWN\n-- FALSE AND UNKNOWN -> FALSE\n-- TRUE OR UNKNOWN   -> TRUE\n-- NOT UNKNOWN       -> UNKNOWN\n\nSELECT * FROM Employees WHERE bonus = NULL;    -- Returns EMPTY result set!\nSELECT * FROM Employees WHERE bonus IS NULL;   -- Correctly finds employees without bonus\n\n-- Tricky trap: NOT IN with NULL\n-- If subquery returns ANY NULL value, NOT IN evaluates to UNKNOWN for all rows!\nSELECT * FROM Dept WHERE dept_id NOT IN (10, 20, NULL); -- Returns EMPTY! Always use NOT EXISTS!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Remember the `NOT IN (..., NULL)` interview trap: If a subquery returns even a single NULL, `NOT IN` evaluates to UNKNOWN for all rows, returning zero results. Always prefer `NOT EXISTS`.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Goldman Sachs"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
