    "id": "int-dbms-011",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Compare DELETE, TRUNCATE, and DROP across Transactionality, Performance, Triggers, and Storage Deallocation.",
    "answer": "Detailed technical comparison:\n\n1. DELETE:\n   - Command Type: DML.\n   - Operation: Deletes rows one by one, writing an Undo/Redo log entry for every individual row.\n   - WHERE clause: Supported (can delete specific rows).\n   - Triggers: Fires `BEFORE/AFTER DELETE` row-level triggers.\n   - Rollback: Fully rollback-capable.\n   - Storage: Does NOT release allocated disk pages back to the OS/tablespace (retains high-water mark).\n   - Speed: Slowest on large tables.\n\n2. TRUNCATE:\n   - Command Type: DDL.\n   - Operation: Deallocates the underlying data pages directly by resetting page allocation pointers.\n   - WHERE clause: Not supported (empties entire table).\n   - Triggers: Does NOT fire row-level delete triggers.\n   - Rollback: In SQL Server and Postgres, TRUNCATE can be rolled back inside a transaction; in MySQL/Oracle, it causes implicit commit.\n   - Identity/Auto-increment: Resets identity counter back to seed value.\n   - Storage: Deallocates data pages immediately.\n   - Speed: Extremely fast ($O(1)$ page unlinking).\n\n3. DROP:\n   - Command Type: DDL.\n   - Operation: Completely destroys the table definition, data, indexes, constraints, and triggers from the data dictionary.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "DELETE is DML, row-by-row logged, supports WHERE, fires triggers, retains high-water mark.",
      "TRUNCATE is DDL, deallocates pages instantly, resets auto-increment, no WHERE clause.",
      "DROP is DDL, permanently removes table schema and all associated metadata."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- TRUNCATE is rollback-capable in PostgreSQL:\nBEGIN;\nTRUNCATE TABLE active_sessions;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM active_sessions; -- Returns 0\nROLLBACK;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM active_sessions; -- Restored completely!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If asked: 'Can TRUNCATE be rolled back?', state that it depends on the RDBMS: In PostgreSQL and SQL Server, TRUNCATE within a transaction can be rolled back; in MySQL and Oracle, it auto-commits and cannot.",
