    "id": "int-dbms-012",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What is a Functional Dependency? State Armstrong's Axioms and the secondary inference rules.",
    "answer": "A Functional Dependency (FD) $X \to Y$ over relation $R$ is a constraint between two sets of attributes $X, Y \\subseteq R$. It states that for any two tuples $t_1, t_2 \\in R$, if $t_1[X] = t_2[X]$, then $t_1[Y] = t_2[Y]$. (The values of $X$ uniquely determine the values of $Y$).\n\nArmstrong's Axioms (Sound and Complete inference rules):\n1. Axiom of Reflexivity: If $Y \\subseteq X$, then $X \to Y$ (Trivial FD, e.g., $AB \to A$).\n2. Axiom of Augmentation: If $X \to Y$, then $XZ \to YZ$ for any attribute set $Z$.\n3. Axiom of Transitivity: If $X \to Y$ and $Y \to Z$, then $X \to Z$.\n\nSecondary Inference Rules (derived from Armstrong's axioms):\n- Union / Additive Rule: If $X \to Y$ and $X \to Z$, then $X \to YZ$.\n- Decomposition / Projective Rule: If $X \to YZ$, then $X \to Y$ and $X \to Z$. (Notice: $XY \to Z$ does NOT imply $X \to Z$!).\n- Pseudotransitivity: If $X \to Y$ and $WY \to Z$, then $WX \to Z$.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Functional Dependency $X \to Y$: Knowing $X$ uniquely determines $Y$.",
      "Armstrong's Axioms are Sound (generate only valid FDs) and Complete (generate all valid FDs).",
      "Three primary axioms: Reflexivity, Augmentation, Transitivity."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Decomposition Trap:\n-- Correct:   X -> YZ  implies  X -> Y and X -> Z\n-- INCORRECT: XY -> Z  DOES NOT IMPLY  X -> Z!\n-- Example:   (StudentID, CourseID) -> Grade does NOT mean StudentID -> Grade!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Armstrong's Axioms are called 'sound' because they never infer a false dependency, and 'complete' because their repeated application computes the full closure $F^+$ of any FD set.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle",
      "Amazon"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
