    "id": "int-dbms-021",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Describe the 5 Transaction States (Active, Partially Committed, Committed, Failed, Aborted) and their transition conditions.",
    "answer": "A database transaction transitions through five formalized states during its lifecycle:\n\n1. Active: The initial state. The transaction enters this state upon `BEGIN TRANSACTION` and remains here while executing read/write operations.\n2. Partially Committed: The transaction enters this state after the final operation is executed, but before changes are guaranteed durable on non-volatile disk. (In-memory updates completed; WAL buffer flush is in progress).\n3. Committed: Entered after the transaction successfully commits. Its updates are permanently written to WAL/disk. There is no turning back; committed transactions cannot be undone.\n4. Failed: Entered if the transaction encounters an error (hardware failure, deadlock abort, integrity constraint violation) while in the Active or Partially Committed state.\n5. Aborted: Entered after the transaction fails and the DBMS completes the rollback/undo process, restoring all mutated data back to the pre-transaction state.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Active: Executing read/write statements in memory.",
      "Partially Committed: Final query executed; WAL disk flush pending.",
      "Committed: Redo log flushed to disk; changes are permanent.",
      "Failed -> Aborted: Rollback executed via Undo log; locks released."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nState Transition Diagram:\n     [Active]\n       |           |     \\---> [Failed]\n       v             |\n [Partially Comm]    |\n    /        \\       v\n   v          \\-> [Aborted] (Restart or Kill)\n[Committed]\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Crucial distinction: Partially Committed means code execution is complete, but system failure before the disk commit log flush pushes the transaction into the Failed state, triggering rollback.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle",
      "Cisco"
    ],
