    "id": "int-dbms-023",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Distinguish between Non-Repeatable Read (Fuzzy Read) and Phantom Read. Why do row-level locks prevent one but fail to prevent the other?",
    "answer": "Both anomalies involve a transaction reading data twice and seeing different results, but they operate on fundamentally different scopes:\n\n1. Non-Repeatable Read (Fuzzy Read):\n   - Scope: Individual existing rows.\n   - Mechanism: $T_1$ reads row $R$. $T_2$ modifies (or deletes) row $R$ and commits. $T_1$ reads row $R$ again and finds its column values have changed (or row disappeared).\n   - Prevented by: Row-level shared locks (`S-locks`) held until the end of $T_1$, or MVCC snapshot read views.\n\n2. Phantom Read:\n   - Scope: Range queries (predicates like `WHERE age > 30`).\n   - Mechanism: $T_1$ reads a set of rows satisfying a range predicate (`SELECT * FROM users WHERE salary > 50000` -> returns 10 rows). $T_2$ executes an `INSERT` of a brand-new user with salary = 60,000 and commits. $T_1$ re-executes the exact same query and sees 11 rows! The 11th row appeared like a 'phantom'.\n\nWhy row-level locks fail to prevent Phantoms:\nRow-level locks can only lock rows that ALREADY EXIST in the table. $T_1$ cannot put a lock on a row that hasn't been inserted yet! Preventing Phantoms requires Gap Locks / Next-Key Locks (in InnoDB) or Predicate Locks (in Serializable isolation).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Non-Repeatable Read: Existing rows are modified or deleted by another transaction.",
      "Phantom Read: New matching rows are inserted by another transaction into a range query.",
      "Row-level locks prevent non-repeatable reads but cannot prevent phantom inserts without Gap Locking."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Non-Repeatable Read:\n-- T1: SELECT salary FROM emp WHERE id = 1; (sees 5000)\n-- T2: UPDATE emp SET salary = 9000 WHERE id = 1; COMMIT;\n-- T1: SELECT salary FROM emp WHERE id = 1; (sees 9000! Changed!)\n\n-- Phantom Read:\n-- T1: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM emp WHERE dept = 'Eng'; (returns 5)\n-- T2: INSERT INTO emp (id, dept) VALUES (99, 'Eng'); COMMIT;\n-- T1: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM emp WHERE dept = 'Eng'; (returns 6! Phantom row appeared!)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In MySQL InnoDB, `REPEATABLE READ` prevents Phantom Reads during standard `SELECT` using MVCC snapshots, and during locking reads (`SELECT FOR UPDATE`) using Next-Key Locks.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Meta",
      "Oracle"
    ],
