    "id": "int-dbms-034",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "How does Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) ensure that 'Readers never block Writers and Writers never block Readers'?",
    "answer": "Traditional lock-based protocols (2PL) require readers to acquire Shared ($S$) locks, which block concurrent Writers requesting Exclusive ($X$) locks. At scale, long-running read queries (reports) starve writers, and writes block readers.\n\nMVCC Mechanism:\nInstead of overwriting data in-place, the DBMS maintains multiple historical versions of each tuple:\n1. Non-destructive Writes: When $T$ updates or deletes a row, the old row is NOT overwritten. A new row version is created with metadata marking the transaction that created it (`xmin` / `created_by_tx`) and expired it (`xmax` / `deleted_by_tx`).\n2. Snapshot Read Views: When a reader starts, the engine assigns it a Read View (a snapshot of currently active transaction IDs).\n3. Version Visibility: The reader scans rows and evaluates visibility rules: it sees only row versions committed BEFORE its snapshot was taken, ignoring versions created by currently active or younger transactions.\n\nResult:\n- Readers read historical immutable snapshots from Undo logs or old row versions without acquiring any locks.\n- Writers modify new versions concurrently.\n- Zero read-write lock contention!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "MVCC stores multiple row versions instead of performing in-place destructive updates.",
      "Readers read historical snapshots matching their transaction start time without locking.",
      "Writers create new row versions; readers never block writers and writers never block readers."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Postgres Row Header Metadata for MVCC:\n-- Every PostgreSQL tuple contains hidden system attributes:\n-- xmin: Transaction ID that inserted the row\n-- xmax: Transaction ID that updated/deleted the row (or 0 if alive)\n\nSELECT xmin, xmax, id, balance FROM accounts;\n-- id | balance | xmin | xmax\n-- 1  | 1000    | 501  | 505 (updated by tx 505)\n-- 1  | 1200    | 505  | 0   (active row created by tx 505)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "PostgreSQL stores new versions directly in the table heap and uses `VACUUM` to clean dead rows; MySQL InnoDB stores old versions in the Undo Log tablespace.",
