    "id": "int-dbms-035",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Compare the internal implementation of MVCC in PostgreSQL (Heap + VACUUM) versus MySQL InnoDB (Undo Log Segments).",
    "answer": "Although both PostgreSQL and MySQL InnoDB implement MVCC, their underlying physical architectures are radically different:\n\n1. PostgreSQL (Append-Only Heap + VACUUM):\n   - Updates: `UPDATE` is physically implemented as `INSERT` (new row version) + `DELETE` (marking old row's `xmax`). Both versions reside directly in the main heap table data pages!\n   - Bloat & VACUUM: Old dead versions accumulate in table pages, causing 'table bloat'. PostgreSQL requires a background worker (`AUTOVACUUM`) to freeze transaction IDs and reclaim dead tuple space.\n   - Secondary Indexes: Updating any column requires updating all secondary indexes to point to the new heap tuple pointer (unless HOT - Heap-Only Tuple optimization applies).\n\n2. MySQL InnoDB (In-Place Update + Undo Log Segments):\n   - Updates: InnoDB modifies the row IN-PLACE inside the clustered index leaf page.\n   - Historical Versions: The old values are written to an Undo Log segment. The row in the clustered index contains a roll pointer (`DB_ROLL_PTR`) pointing to the undo chain.\n   - Readers: Readers traversing old snapshots follow `DB_ROLL_PTR` to reconstruct old values on the fly from the Undo Log.\n   - Secondary Indexes: Updating non-indexed columns does not require updating secondary indexes; secondary indexes point to the Primary Key value.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "PostgreSQL stores all versions in table heap pages; requires VACUUM to reclaim dead row bloat.",
      "InnoDB updates in-place in clustered index; stores old versions in rollback Undo Log segments.",
      "PostgreSQL secondary indexes point to heap tuple TIDs; InnoDB secondary indexes point to Primary Key values."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nArchitecture Comparison:\nPostgres Heap Page:\n[ Row v1 (xmax=50) ] -> [ Row v2 (xmin=50, xmax=0) ]  <- Bloats heap until VACUUM\n\nInnoDB Clustered Index Page:\n[ Row (Current Value) | DB_TRX_ID=50 | DB_ROLL_PTR ]\n                                          |\n                                          v (Undo Log Segment)\n                                    [ Undo v1 (Old Value) ]\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "A frequent senior DBA question: 'Why does PostgreSQL suffer from table bloat while MySQL InnoDB does not?' The answer lies in Postgres heap versioning vs InnoDB Undo Log versioning.",
