    "id": "int-dbms-037",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What is the difference between a Clustered Index and a Non-Clustered (Secondary) Index? Why can a table have only ONE Clustered Index?",
    "answer": "1. Clustered Index (Primary Index):\n   - Physical Organization: The leaf pages of a clustered index DO NOT contain pointers to data; the leaf pages ARE THE ACTUAL TABLE DATA ROWS sorted in physical order of the indexed key.\n   - Cardinality: A table can have ONE AND ONLY ONE clustered index because physical rows can only be arranged in one physical sorting order on disk.\n   - In MySQL InnoDB: The Primary Key is always the Clustered Index. If no PK is defined, InnoDB picks the first non-null `UNIQUE` index; if none exists, it generates an invisible 6-byte row ID (`GEN_CLUST_INDEX`).\n\n2. Non-Clustered Index (Secondary Index):\n   - Physical Organization: Stored in a completely separate B+ tree structure.\n   - Leaf Nodes: Leaf pages contain the index key column value + a row locator (pointer):\n     - In Heap tables (PostgreSQL, SQL Server heap): The pointer is the physical Tuple ID (`TID` = `PageID:SlotOffset`).\n     - In Clustered tables (MySQL InnoDB): The pointer is the Primary Key value! Looking up via secondary index requires a secondary index scan followed by a 'Bookmark Lookup' (Clustered Index traversal).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Clustered Index: Leaf pages store the actual table rows sorted physically by the key.",
      "A table can have only ONE clustered index because physical data can only be sorted in one order.",
      "Non-Clustered Index: Leaf pages store index keys and pointers (TID or Primary Key value) back to the clustered table."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- In MySQL InnoDB:\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,         -- Clustered Index: Data rows physically stored on disk sorted by 'id'\n    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE,  -- Non-Clustered Index: Leaf node stores 'email' + 'id'\n    name VARCHAR(100)\n);\n\n-- Query: SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'alice@example.com';\n-- Step 1: Traverse email secondary B+ Tree -> finds id = 42\n-- Step 2: Traverse primary clustered B+ Tree with id = 42 -> retrieves full row (Bookmark Lookup)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Explain the overhead of secondary indexes in InnoDB: because secondary leaves store the primary key, large primary keys (e.g., UUID v4 strings) dramatically bloat every single secondary index on the table.",
