    "id": "int-dbms-039",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Calculate the maximum number of records a 3-level B+ Tree can index given a 4KB disk page size, 8-byte search key, 8-byte pointer, and 100-byte data record.",
    "answer": "Step-by-step mathematical derivation:\n\n1. Given Parameters:\n   - Disk Block / Page Size = 4,096 bytes\n   - Search Key Size ($K$) = 8 bytes\n   - Child Pointer Size ($P$) = 8 bytes\n   - Data Record / Leaf Pointer Size ($R$) = 100 bytes (for clustered or leaf entry)\n\n2. Order of Internal Node ($m$):\n   - An internal node with $m$ child pointers has $(m - 1)$ keys.\n   - Inequality: $m \\cdot P + (m - 1) \\cdot K \\le 4096$\n   - $m \\cdot 8 + (m - 1) \\cdot 8 \\le 4096 \\implies 16m - 8 \\le 4096 \\implies 16m \\le 4104 \\implies m \\le 256.5$\n   - Maximum fan-out / order $m = 256$.\n\n3. Capacity of Leaf Node ($L$):\n   - Leaf nodes contain key-record pairs + one sibling pointer (8 bytes):\n   - $L \\cdot (Key + Record) + P_{sibling} \\le 4096$\n   - $L \\cdot (8 + 100) + 8 \\le 4096 \\implies 108L \\le 4088 \\implies L \\le 37.85$\n   - Maximum records per leaf page $L = 37$.\n\n4. Total Records in a 3-Level B+ Tree:\n   - Level 1 (Root): 1 page with 256 child pointers.\n   - Level 2: 256 internal pages, each with 256 child pointers ($256 \\times 256 = 65,536$ leaf pointers).\n   - Level 3 (Leaf Level): 65,536 leaf pages, each holding 37 records.\n   - Total Capacity: $65,536 \\times 37 = 2,424,832$ records (approx. 2.42 million records!).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Internal node order formula: $m \\cdot P + (m - 1) \\cdot K \\le BlockSize$.",
      "Leaf node capacity formula: $L \\cdot (K + RecordSize) + P_{sibling} \\le BlockSize$.",
      "Total records = (Fanout at level 1) $\\times$ (Fanout at level 2) $\\times$ (Leaf capacity)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nCalculation Summary:\n- Block Size: 4096 bytes\n- Internal Node Fan-out m: 256\n- Leaf Node Capacity L: 37 records\n- Total records in 3 levels: 256 * 256 * 37 = 2,424,832 records\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If the tree is 4 levels, capacity becomes $256^3 \\times 37 = 620,756,992$ (over 620 million rows) all accessible in just 4 I/O reads (with root and level 2 permanently cached in RAM).",
