    "id": "int-dbms-042",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Compare B+ Trees with Log-Structured Merge-Trees (LSM Trees). Why do write-intensive systems (Cassandra, RocksDB) choose LSM Trees?",
    "answer": "1. B+ Trees:\n   - Updates: In-place random updates. Modifying a row requires reading the target page into memory and writing it back to disk.\n   - Write Amplification: Modifying an 80-byte record forces a full 4KB or 16KB page write to disk. Random writes saturate disk write throughput.\n   - Read Latency: Outstanding $O(\\log N)$ read performance.\n\n2. LSM Trees (Log-Structured Merge-Trees):\n   - Updates: Append-only sequential writes. Writes append to an in-memory sorted buffer (`MemTable`) and a sequential write-ahead log (`CommitLog`).\n   - SSTables: When MemTable fills, it flushes sequentially to disk as an immutable Sorted String Table (`SSTable`). Never updates existing files!\n   - Compaction: Background threads merge sorted SSTables (Leveled or Size-Tiered compaction), discarding obsolete and deleted tombstones.\n   - Read Latency: Reads must check MemTable and multiple SSTables (mitigated using Bloom Filters to avoid searching SSTables that do not contain the key).\n\nWhy LSM Wins for Writes:\nSequential disk writes are 100x-1000x faster than random disk writes (especially on spinning disks and flash wear-leveling SSDs). LSM trees convert random writes into sequential bursts.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "B+ Trees perform in-place random writes; optimal for read-heavy workloads.",
      "LSM Trees perform append-only sequential writes to MemTable and immutable SSTables.",
      "LSM uses Bloom Filters and Compaction to optimize reads and reclaim disk space.",
      "Adopted by Cassandra, RocksDB, Bigtable, ScyllaDB, and LevelDB."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nLSM Tree Architecture:\nWrites ----> [ CommitLog ] (Sequential WAL)\n        \\\n         --> [ MemTable (RAM) ] (SkipList / Red-Black Tree)\n                  | (Flush when full)\n                  v\n             [ SSTable L0 ] (Immutable Sorted Disk Files)\n             [ SSTable L1 ] (Compacted & Merged sequentially)\nReads check: MemTable -> Bloom Filter -> SSTables\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In system design interviews, whenever write throughput exceeds 50,000 writes/sec (time-series, metrics, logs, chat messages), propose an LSM-tree based engine (Cassandra/RocksDB) over a B+ tree engine.",
