    "id": "int-dbms-043",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "How do Bloom Filters optimize read operations in LSM-Tree databases? What are False Positives and False Negatives?",
    "answer": "A Bloom Filter is a space-efficient probabilistic data structure used to test whether an element is a member of a set.\n\nMechanics:\n- Consists of a bit array of $m$ bits (initially all 0) and $k$ independent hash functions.\n- Insertion: Hash the key with all $k$ hash functions and set the corresponding bit indices to 1.\n- Lookup: Hash the query key with all $k$ hash functions. If ANY bit at the calculated indices is 0, the element is DEFINITELY NOT in the set!\n- If all bits are 1, the element MIGHT be in the set (or bits were set by collisions of other keys).\n\nKey Guarantees:\n- False Negatives: IMPOSSIBLE (0% probability). If the filter says 'No', the key is 100% guaranteed not to exist.\n- False Positives: Possible (configurable probability, e.g., 1%). If the filter says 'Yes', the key might exist or might be a collision.\n\nRole in LSM Databases:\nBefore reading an SSTable from disk, the engine checks its in-memory Bloom filter. If it returns False, the database skips reading that SSTable entirely, saving expensive disk seeks!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Space-efficient probabilistic data structure for set membership testing.",
      "Zero False Negatives: Never claims an existing item is absent.",
      "Small False Positive rate: May occasionally claim an absent item is present.",
      "Saves disk I/O in LSM databases (RocksDB, Cassandra) by skipping irrelevant SSTables."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```python\n# Conceptual Bloom Filter in Python:\nclass BloomFilter:\n    def __init__(self, size=1000):\n        self.size = size\n        self.bits = 0\n\n    def add(self, key):\n        for seed in [31, 37, 41]:\n            idx = hash(key + str(seed)) % self.size\n            self.bits |= (1 << idx)\n\n    def might_contain(self, key):\n        for seed in [31, 37, 41]:\n            idx = hash(key + str(seed)) % self.size\n            if not (self.bits & (1 << idx)):\n                return False # DEFINITELY NOT PRESENT! (0% False Negative)\n        return True # PROBABLY PRESENT (May be False Positive)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Bloom filters do NOT support element deletion in their standard form because clearing a bit would inadvertently delete all other keys that hashed to that same bit. Counting Bloom Filters solve this.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Meta",
      "Amazon",
      "Netflix"
    ],
