    "id": "int-dbms-044",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Explain the Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) Protocol. Why must log records reach disk before dirty pages are flushed?",
    "answer": "The Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) protocol is the fundamental law of database durability and atomicity:\n\nFormal WAL Rules:\n1. Rule 1 (Atomicity / Undo rule): Before any dirty page in the buffer pool is written to disk, all log records describing the update (including undo records) must be written to stable storage on disk. (If the system crashes while writing the page, the undo log on disk is guaranteed to exist to revert it!).\n2. Rule 2 (Durability / Redo rule): A transaction cannot be declared committed until all log records describing its changes (up to the commit record) are safely flushed to stable disk storage (`fsync`).\n\nWhy Not Flush Dirty Pages Directly at Commit?\n1. Random I/O vs Sequential I/O: Modifying 10 rows in different tables requires 10 random page writes across disk. WAL appends log entries sequentially to a single append-only log file, running 100x faster.\n2. Incomplete State: Flashing dirty pages without a log makes crash recovery impossible if power cuts midway through writing page 5 of 10.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "WAL Rule 1: Log records must reach disk before dirty data pages are flushed.",
      "WAL Rule 2: Transaction commits only after its redo log records are flushed to disk.",
      "Converts slow random disk writes into high-speed sequential append writes."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- WAL In Action (PostgreSQL write sequence):\n-- 1. Client: UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 1;\n-- 2. Engine updates in-memory buffer page; marks it DIRTY.\n-- 3. Engine creates WAL record: [LSN=1054, Tx=42, Page=12, Undo=old_val, Redo=new_val].\n-- 4. Client: COMMIT;\n-- 5. Engine issues fsync() on WAL file (LSN 1054 hits disk).\n-- 6. Engine returns SUCCESS to client.\n-- 7. Dirty buffer page 12 remains in RAM and is lazily written to disk minutes later!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "LSN (Log Sequence Number) is the monotonically increasing integer assigned to every WAL record that connects data pages to their corresponding log records.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle",
      "Meta"
    ],
