    "id": "int-dbms-047",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What is Database Checkpointing? Why does Naive Checkpointing cause latency spikes, and how does Fuzzy Checkpointing solve it?",
    "answer": "Without checkpointing, crash recovery would require scanning and replaying the WAL from the very beginning of the database creation (days or years of logs!). A Checkpoint creates a known sync point to truncate obsolete WAL logs.\n\n1. Naive (Stop-The-World) Checkpoint:\n   - Pauses all transaction processing.\n   - Flushes every dirty buffer page to disk.\n   - Writes a checkpoint record to the WAL.\n   - Flaw: In a 128GB buffer pool, flushing tens of thousands of dirty pages freezes the database for seconds or minutes, causing catastrophic latency spikes.\n\n2. Fuzzy Checkpoint (Non-blocking Standard):\n   - Does NOT flush dirty pages synchronously and does NOT stop transactions!\n   - Writes a `CHECKPOINT_BEGIN` record.\n   - Takes an in-memory snapshot of the Dirty Page Table (DPT) and Transaction Table (TT).\n   - Writes a `CHECKPOINT_END` record containing these tables to the log.\n   - Background flusher threads continuously push dirty pages to disk at an even pace.\n   - Recovery only needs to scan back to the smallest `RecLSN` present in the checkpoint's Dirty Page Table.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Checkpointing truncates WAL logs and bounds recovery time.",
      "Naive checkpointing halts all transactions to flush all dirty pages, causing severe latency spikes.",
      "Fuzzy checkpointing writes current DPT and active transactions without pausing writes."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nFuzzy Checkpointing:\n1. Log [CHECKPOINT_BEGIN]\n2. Snapshot current in-memory tables:\n   - Active Transactions: { T1, T3 }\n   - Dirty Pages: { Page 5: RecLSN=1020, Page 8: RecLSN=1045 }\n3. Log [CHECKPOINT_END with snapshot tables]\nTransactions run continuously with ZERO pauses!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In PostgreSQL, checkpoint frequency is controlled by `checkpoint_timeout` and `max_wal_size`. Tuning these is crucial to prevent I/O spikes in high-write databases.",
