    "id": "int-dbms-048",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Explain the CAP Theorem and why 'Pick Two' is a misconception. What does the PACELC Theorem add?",
    "answer": "1. CAP Theorem (Eric Brewer):\nIn an asynchronous network, a distributed data store can guarantee at most two of three properties:\n- Consistency (Linearizability): Every read receives the most recent write or an error.\n- Availability: Every non-failing node returns a non-error response for every request (no guarantee it is the most recent write).\n- Partition Tolerance: The system continues to operate despite arbitrary packet drops or network partitions.\n\nWhy 'Pick Two' is a Misconception:\nYou CANNOT choose 'CA'! In distributed systems, network cables get cut, routers reboot, and partitions are unavoidable physical realities ($P$ is non-negotiable). Therefore, the actual choice is: During a network partition ($P$), do you choose Consistency ($CP$) or Availability ($AP$)?\n- $CP$ (e.g., HBase, Spanner, ZooKeeper): Rejects writes on isolated minority partitions to preserve consistency.\n- $AP$ (e.g., Cassandra, DynamoDB, CouchDB): Accepts writes on all partitions, resulting in temporary divergence.\n\n2. PACELC Theorem (Daniel Abadi):\nCAP only describes behavior during rare network partitions ($P$). PACELC covers normal operations ($E$ for Else):\nIf Partition ($P$): choose Availability ($A$) or Consistency ($C$).\nElse ($E$): choose Latency ($L$) or Consistency ($C$).\n- Example: MongoDB is $PC/EC$ (chooses consistency in both).\n- Example: Cassandra is $PA/EL$ (chooses availability during partition, low latency normally).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Network partitions (P) are unavoidable; the true CAP choice is CP vs AP during partitions.",
      "CP systems reject reads/writes on partitioned nodes to prevent stale/divergent data.",
      "AP systems remain available on all nodes, settling for eventual consistency.",
      "PACELC theorem extends CAP by evaluating Latency vs Consistency during normal execution."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nPACELC Matrix:\nIf Partition (P) -> Availability (A) OR Consistency (C)\nElse (E)         -> Latency (L)      OR Consistency (C)\n\nSystems Classification:\n- DynamoDB / Cassandra: PA / EL\n- Spanner / CockroachDB: PC / EC\n- MongoDB: PC / EC\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Reciting PACELC when asked about CAP in FAANG interviews immediately signals deep distributed systems maturity.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Meta",
      "Netflix",
      "Microsoft"
    ],
