    "id": "int-dbms-050",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Distinguish between Horizontal Sharding, Vertical Partitioning, and Consistent Hashing in database scaling.",
    "answer": "When a database outgrows a single machine's CPU, RAM, or disk capacity, partitioning is required:\n\n1. Vertical Partitioning:\n   - Splits a single table's columns across different storage tables or physical servers based on access patterns.\n   - Example: Move infrequently accessed `user_biography` and `profile_photo_blob` to a secondary table, keeping frequently accessed `id, username, password_hash` in the primary table to fit more rows in the buffer pool.\n\n2. Horizontal Partitioning / Sharding:\n   - Splits rows of a table across multiple database instances (shards). Each shard possesses the identical schema but holds a mutually exclusive subset of rows.\n   - Sharding Key: The column used to route queries to a shard (e.g., `user_id`).\n   - Challenges: Cross-shard joins become slow, distributed transactions require 2PC, and schema migrations become complex.\n\n3. Consistent Hashing:\n   - Traditional modulus sharding (`hash(key) % N`) forces remapping almost every single key when adding an $(N+1)$-th server.\n   - Consistent Hashing maps nodes and keys onto a circular $2^{32}$ hash ring. Adding or removing a server only migrates $K/N$ keys from adjacent nodes without reshuffling the entire cluster.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Vertical partitioning splits columns by access patterns to optimize buffer pool density.",
      "Horizontal sharding splits rows across multiple independent physical database servers.",
      "Consistent hashing minimizes data rebalancing when scaling shards dynamically."
    ],
