    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Describe the architecture of a Relational Query Optimizer. How does the Cost-Based Optimizer (CBO) choose a physical plan?",
    "answer": "SQL is a declarative language: the user specifies WHAT data to retrieve, not HOW to retrieve it. The Query Optimizer transforms the SQL text into an optimal physical execution plan:\n\nPipeline:\n1. Parser: Verifies SQL syntax and semantic grammar, translating query text into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).\n2. Rewriter / Preprocessor: Resolves table and column names against system catalogs. Expands views, flattens subqueries, applies heuristic algebraic rewrites (e.g., pushing selections down before joins: Predicate Pushdown).\n3. Cost-Based Optimizer (CBO):\n   - Generates multiple candidate physical execution plans.\n   - Assigns a cost estimate to each plan: $\\text{Cost} = (N_{page\\_reads} \\times C_{I/O}) + (N_{tuples\\_processed} \\times C_{CPU})$.\n   - Uses table statistics (row counts, histograms, distinct values) to estimate intermediate result sizes (cardinality).\n4. Plan Executor: Executes the lowest-cost physical plan (e.g., Index Scan + Hash Join) using the Volcano iterator model (`open()`, `next()`, `close()`).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SQL query pipeline: Parser -> Query Rewriter -> Cost-Based Optimizer -> Plan Executor.",
      "Predicate pushdown pushes WHERE filters below JOINs to reduce intermediate row volume.",
      "Cost model balances disk I/O cost (page fetches) and CPU cost (tuple comparisons and hash computations)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Logical rewrite: Predicate Pushdown\n-- Query as written:\nSELECT * FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE c.country = 'India';\n\n-- Optimizer pushes filter down BEFORE join:\n-- Plan: (orders) JOIN (customers WHERE country = 'India')\n-- Reduces join inputs from 1,000,000 customers to only 50,000!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If the query optimizer produces a terrible plan, 95% of the time it is due to stale or missing table statistics. Running `ANALYZE table_name;` updates statistics and fixes the plan.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle"
    ],
