    "id": "int-dbms-053",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What is Cardinality Estimation? How do Equi-Depth Histograms and stale statistics cause disastrous query execution plans?",
    "answer": "Cardinality Estimation is the optimizer's process of calculating the estimated number of rows that will satisfy each filter or join operation in a query.\n\nHow it works:\n- Database collects statistics using background sampling (`ANALYZE`).\n- Uses Equi-Depth (or Equi-Width) Histograms to track column value distribution and data skew.\n- Calculates 'Selectivity' ($S \\in [0, 1]$): Fraction of rows satisfying condition. Estimated rows = $N \\times S$.\n\nHow Stale Statistics Cause Disastrous Plans:\n1. Suppose table `orders` grew from 10,000 rows to 50,000,000 rows, but statistics were not updated.\n2. The optimizer believes the table has only 10,000 rows.\n3. It chooses a Nested Loop Join (believing the outer loop has few iterations) instead of a Hash Join.\n4. At runtime, the nested loop performs 50,000,000 inner scans, causing a query that should take 2 seconds to run for 8 hours and lock the database!\n\nFix: Automated statistics gathering (`autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor` in Postgres).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Cardinality estimation determines intermediate result row counts to choose physical join and scan methods.",
      "Equi-depth histograms represent data skew across column value buckets.",
      "Stale statistics deceive the optimizer into choosing catastrophic Nested Loop Joins over Hash Joins."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- In PostgreSQL: Inspect table statistics\nSELECT relname, reltuples, relpages FROM pg_class WHERE relname = 'orders';\n\n-- Update statistics manually after massive data bulk loads:\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- In MySQL:\nANALYZE TABLE orders;\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In execution plans (`EXPLAIN`), compare `estimated rows` with `actual rows`. A 100x+ discrepancy is proof of stale statistics or skewed correlated columns.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Uber"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
