    "id": "int-dbms-055",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Compare Natural Keys, Surrogate Auto-Increment Keys, and UUIDs (v4 vs v7). Why do UUID v4s cause B+ tree index fragmentation?",
    "answer": "1. Natural Key: An existing real-world attribute (e.g., SSN, Email). Drawback: Business requirements change (e.g., user wants to change email or SSN format alters), breaking foreign keys across tables.\n2. Surrogate Auto-Increment Key (`BIGSERIAL` / `AUTO_INCREMENT`):\n   - Monotonically increasing 64-bit integer.\n   - Fast B+ tree appends: New keys append sequentially to the rightmost leaf of the B+ tree, causing zero page splits.\n   - Drawbacks: Predictable (vulnerable to enumeration attacks `api/users/1054`), single-point of failure in distributed multi-master writes.\n3. UUID v4 (Random UUID):\n   - 128-bit completely random number. Perfect for distributed systems with zero coordinator.\n   - CATASTROPHIC B+ TREE FRAGMENTATION: Because UUID v4 is completely random, inserts hit random leaf pages throughout the entire B+ tree. When a page fills, it forces a 50/50 'B+ Tree Page Split', fragmenting disk pages, wasting 50% buffer pool RAM, and thrashing disk I/O!\n4. UUID v7 / ULID (Time-Ordered UUID):\n   - Combines a 48-bit UNIX timestamp prefix + 74 bits of randomness.\n   - Monotonically increasing over time: Preserves distributed uniqueness while appending sequentially to B+ tree leaves with ZERO page splits!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Auto-increment keys provide compact, sequential B+ tree appends but leak business volume in URLs.",
      "UUID v4 causes random B+ tree page splits, bloating indexes and thrashing RAM.",
      "UUID v7 / ULID combines time-ordered sequential indexing with distributed global uniqueness."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Modern Database Best Practice: UUID v7 in PostgreSQL 17+\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid_v7(), -- Time-ordered 128-bit UUID!\n    customer_id BIGINT,\n    total NUMERIC(10, 2)\n);\n-- Appends sequentially to B+ tree leaf right-edge: 0 page splits!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "When designing distributed systems, recommend UUID v7 or ULID over UUID v4 to show you understand low-level B+ tree storage mechanics and cache efficiency.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Stripe",
      "Amazon",
      "Uber",
      "Google",
      "Shopify"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
