    "id": "int-dbms-059",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "How does SQL Injection work under the hood? Why do Prepared Statements (Parameterized Queries) guarantee 100% immunity?",
    "answer": "SQL Injection occurs when untrusted user input is concatenated directly into a SQL query string, allowing an attacker to manipulate the syntactic structure of the query executed by the database engine.\n\nWhy String Concatenation Fails:\nWhen application code executes `\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '\" + input + \"'\"` and the attacker passes `admin' --`, the query becomes:\n`SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = 'admin' --'`\nThe database parser treats `'` as a string delimiter, turning the remainder of the SQL logic into a comment (`--`), completely bypassing password verification!\n\nWhy Prepared Statements Provide 100% Immunity:\nPrepared statements separate query CODE from query DATA into two completely distinct phases:\n1. Prepare Phase: The application sends the raw SQL template containing parameter placeholders (`?` or `$1`) to the database. The database parser parses the query, checks semantics, builds the AST, and compiles the execution plan.\n2. Execute Phase: The application sends the user input values separately. The database treats the input strictly as literal scalar values, NEVER as executable SQL instructions! Even if the user passes `admin' OR '1'='1`, the engine searches for a user whose literal name is the entire string `admin' OR '1'='1`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SQL injection exploits string concatenation to alter the query's Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).",
      "Prepared statements pre-compile the SQL grammar AST before parameter values are bound.",
      "User inputs are treated strictly as data literals; they can never alter the query structure."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```java\n// VULNERABLE TO SQL INJECTION:\nString query = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '\" + userInput + \"' AND pass = '\" + pass + \"'\";\nStatement stmt = connection.createStatement();\nResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(query); // ATTACKER OWNS THE DATABASE!\n\n// 100% IMMUNE (Parameterized Prepared Statement):\nString sql = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? AND pass = ?\";\nPreparedStatement pstmt = connection.prepareStatement(sql);\npstmt.setString(1, userInput); // Bound as pure literal data\npstmt.setString(2, pass);\nResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "ORMs (like Hibernate, Prisma) use prepared statements under the hood, but developers can still introduce SQL injection if they construct raw HQL/SQL strings via concatenation.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Meta",
      "Apple"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
