    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Categorize the 4 primary NoSQL data models (Document, Key-Value, Wide-Column, Graph) with their underlying data structures and ideal use cases.",
    "answer": "NoSQL engines optimize for specific data shapes and horizontal scalability by shedding relational constraints:\n\n1. Key-Value Stores (Redis, AWS DynamoDB):\n   - Data Structure: Giant distributed Hash Table.\n   - Model: $O(1)$ lookup by primary key (`GET key`, `SET key value`). Value is an opaque blob or lightweight collection.\n   - Use Cases: Session storage, real-time leaderboards, rate limiting, shopping carts.\n\n2. Document Stores (MongoDB, Couchbase):\n   - Data Structure: BSON / JSON trees indexed by B-trees.\n   - Model: Semi-structured documents with nested arrays and sub-documents. Flexible, polymorphic schema per record.\n   - Use Cases: Content management, e-commerce product catalogs, user profiles.\n\n3. Wide-Column / Column-Family Stores (Apache Cassandra, ScyllaDB, HBase):\n   - Data Structure: LSM-Trees + SSTables.\n   - Model: Two-dimensional multidimensional map: `(RowKey, ColumnKey, Timestamp) -> Value`. Rows can have billions of sparse columns.\n   - Use Cases: Time-series metrics, IoT sensor data, chat messaging histories, activity feeds.\n\n4. Graph Databases (Neo4j, AWS Neptune):\n   - Data Structure: Adjacency Lists / Index-Free Adjacency (each node directly holds raw pointers to its neighboring nodes).\n   - Model: Nodes, Edges (Relationships), and Properties.\n   - Use Cases: Social networks, fraud detection networks, knowledge graphs, recommendation engines.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Key-Value (Redis): Hash table; sub-millisecond key lookups.",
      "Document (MongoDB): JSON/BSON trees; semi-structured and nested records.",
      "Wide-Column (Cassandra): LSM trees; high write throughput for time-series and event logs.",
      "Graph (Neo4j): Index-free adjacency; traversing complex many-to-many relationship webs."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nNoSQL Landscape:\nModel       | Core Engine         | Primary Index Structure | Traversal Complexity\n------------+---------------------+-------------------------+---------------------\nKey-Value   | Redis, DynamoDB     | Hash Table              | O(1)\nDocument    | MongoDB, Couchbase  | B+ Tree on JSON         | O(log N)\nWide-Column | Cassandra, ScyllaDB | LSM-Tree                | O(1) append writes\nGraph       | Neo4j, Neptune      | Index-Free Adjacency    | O(k) pointer hops\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Index-Free Adjacency in Graph DBs means traversing relationships doesn't use index lookups ($O(\\log N)$); it directly dereferences memory pointers ($O(1)$) to jump from node to node.",
