    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Provide an architectural decision framework for choosing between Relational (SQL) and NoSQL databases.",
    "answer": "A senior engineering decision framework evaluates five core vectors:\n\n1. Data Relationships & Joins:\n   - Choose SQL: If data has complex relational entities requiring multi-table joins, foreign key integrity, and relational algebra.\n   - Choose NoSQL: If data is aggregate-oriented (can be embedded in a single document) or independent key-value lookups.\n\n2. Transactional Guarantees (ACID vs BASE):\n   - Choose SQL: Financial transfers, order checkout, inventory reservation requiring multi-record strict ACID transactions.\n   - Choose NoSQL: Analytics, activity logs, social likes requiring high availability and eventual consistency (BASE).\n\n3. Schema Flexibility vs Strictness:\n   - Choose SQL: Known, stable schema where data uniformity is required.\n   - Choose NoSQL: Highly dynamic, rapidly evolving schemas with sparse or polymorphic attributes.\n\n4. Scaling Dimensions:\n   - Choose SQL: Read-heavy workloads that scale vertically (or with read replicas), up to ~50,000 queries/sec.\n   - Choose NoSQL: Massive write throughput (>100,000 writes/sec) and petabyte-scale datasets requiring linear horizontal partitioning across hundreds of commodity nodes.\n\n5. Modern Hybrid Reality: Modern PostgreSQL handles JSONB with functional indexes, bridging the gap between SQL and Document stores.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SQL: Complex relational joins, strict ACID transactions, structured financial data.",
      "NoSQL: Massive horizontal scale, append-only high write throughput, polymorphic documents.",
      "PostgreSQL with JSONB combines relational ACID guarantees with NoSQL document flexibility."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nDecision Matrix:\nNeed ACID & Complex Joins? ---> YES ---> SQL (PostgreSQL, MySQL)\n             |\n             NO\n             v\nWrite-heavy time-series/logs? -> YES ---> Cassandra / TimescaleDB\nFast Key-Value Cache/Session? -> YES ---> Redis / DynamoDB\nHierarchical polymorphic doc? -> YES ---> MongoDB / Postgres JSONB\nConnected Social Graph?       -> YES ---> Neo4j\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Never frame NoSQL as 'better than SQL'; explain that SQL prioritizes data integrity and query flexibility, while NoSQL trades schema rigidity and ad-hoc joins for horizontal partitionability.",
