    "id": "int-dbms-075",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What is a Bitmap Index? Why is it extraordinarily fast for OLAP analytical queries but devastating for OLTP write performance?",
    "answer": "A Bitmap Index builds a separate bit array (bitmap) for every distinct value in a column. Each bit corresponds to a row in the table (1 = match, 0 = no match).\n\nStructure:\nSuppose table has 1,000,000 rows and column `gender` has distinct values `{'M', 'F'}`:\n- Bitmap for 'M': `10110010...` (1 million bits = ~122 KB)\n- Bitmap for 'F': `01001101...`\n\nWhy It Excels in OLAP (Data Warehouses):\n1. Bitwise Boolean Operations at CPU Speed: Queries with multiple conditions (`WHERE gender = 'F' AND region = 'East' AND status = 'Active'`) evaluate by performing hardware bitwise `AND` / `OR` instructions on the bitmaps directly in CPU registers without touching table rows!\n2. Ultra-Compact: Highly compressible using Run-Length Encoding (Roaring Bitmaps).\n\nWhy It Fails in OLTP (Write-Heavy Systems):\nRow-level locking is impossible! Updating a single row modifies a bit in the bitmap. Because locking an individual bit is not supported by operating systems, the database must place an exclusive lock on the ENTIRE BITMAP BLOCK (thousands of rows)! Any concurrent write to that table stalls completely.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Bitmap index maps distinct column values to bit arrays where bit position = row ID.",
      "OLAP superpower: Combines multiple filter predicates via hardware bitwise AND/OR operations.",
      "OLTP nightmare: Updating a single row locks entire bitmap chunks, crippling concurrent writes."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- In Oracle Data Warehouse:\nCREATE BITMAP INDEX idx_customers_gender ON customers(gender);\nCREATE BITMAP INDEX idx_customers_region ON customers(region);\n\n-- Query evaluates via bitwise AND:\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers\nWHERE gender = 'F' AND region = 'North';\n-- CPU executes: (Bitmap_F & Bitmap_North) -> counts '1' bits in nanoseconds!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "PostgreSQL does not support persistent on-disk bitmap indexes; instead, it generates in-memory 'Bitmap Index Scans' dynamically by combining B+ trees.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Oracle",
      "Snowflake",
      "Amazon",
      "Google"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
    "is_hidden": false,
    "is_deleted": false,
    "sort_order": 75
