    "id": "int-dbms-076",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "How do R-Trees and Quadtrees index spatial and geographic data (lat/lng coordinates)?",
    "answer": "B+ trees fail on geographic coordinates because spatial data is multi-dimensional (latitude, longitude): sorting points along a 1D line destroys 2D spatial locality.\n\n1. R-Tree (Region Tree / B-Tree for Space):\n   - Hierarchical tree where leaf nodes store spatial objects (points, polygons) and internal nodes store Minimum Bounding Boxes (MBR - rectangles enclosing all child elements).\n   - Bounding boxes can overlap. Range queries (`ST_Within(point, polygon)`) check if the query bounding box intersects parent MBRs, pruning branches that do not overlap.\n   - Used in PostgreSQL PostGIS (`GIST` index) and SQLite SpatiaLite.\n\n2. Quadtree:\n   - Recursively decomposes a 2D bounding box into 4 equal quadrants: NW, NE, SW, SE.\n   - Decomposition continues until each quadrant contains fewer than $N$ points.\n   - Used in Google Maps tiles, Uber H3 hexagonal spatial indexing, and spatial partitioning.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Spatial data cannot be sorted linearly in standard 1D B+ trees.",
      "R-Tree organizes spatial objects into hierarchical Minimum Bounding Rectangles (MBRs).",
      "Quadtree recursively subdivides 2D space into 4 quadrants (NW, NE, SW, SE).",
      "Powers PostGIS, Uber ride-matching, and Google Maps tile lookup."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- PostGIS Spatial Indexing via R-Tree (GiST):\nCREATE TABLE restaurants (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(100),\n    location GEOMETRY(Point, 4326) -- WGS 84 lat/lng coordinates\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_restaurants_location ON restaurants USING GIST (location);\n\n-- O(log N) KNN (K-Nearest Neighbors) search using R-Tree distance operator <->:\nSELECT name, ST_Distance(location, ST_MakePoint(77.59, 12.97)::geography) AS dist_meters\nFROM restaurants\nORDER BY location <-> ST_MakePoint(77.59, 12.97)::geometry\nLIMIT 10;\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Uber's H3 library maps Earth onto a hexagonal grid; each hexagon has a 64-bit integer ID, allowing spatial lookups to be transformed back into fast B+ tree integer range scans.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Uber",
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "PostGIS Core"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
