    "id": "int-dbms-078",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Compare Row-Oriented (OLTP) and Column-Oriented (OLAP) database storage. Why are Columnar formats 100x faster for analytical aggregations?",
    "answer": "1. Row-Oriented Storage (PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle):\n   - Physical Layout: Entire tuples are stored sequentially on disk pages: `[R1_col1, R1_col2, R1_col3], [R2_col1, R2_col2, R2_col3]`.\n   - Best for: OLTP (Online Transaction Processing). Inserting, updating, or fetching an entire single record (`SELECT * WHERE id = 1`) reads just one page.\n   - Catastrophic for OLAP: Running `SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees` forces the engine to read the ENTIRE 100GB table into memory just to extract one single column!\n\n2. Column-Oriented Storage (ClickHouse, Snowflake, BigQuery, Apache Parquet):\n   - Physical Layout: Each column is stored in contiguous, dedicated disk blocks: `Col1: [R1, R2, R3...], Col2: [R1, R2, R3...]`.\n   - Why It Excels in OLAP:\n     - Extreme I/O Reduction: `AVG(salary)` reads ONLY the disk blocks holding salary numbers; 95% of columns are never touched!\n     - Unmatched Compression: Similar data types stored adjacently compress by 90%+ using Delta Encoding, Run-Length Encoding (RLE), and Dictionary Encoding.\n     - Vectorized SIMD Execution: Modern CPUs use SIMD registers (AVX-512) to sum 16 numbers in a single clock cycle!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Row stores (OLTP) pack all columns of a row together; optimal for single-record CRUD.",
      "Column stores (OLAP) pack all values of a column together; optimal for aggregations.",
      "Columnar advantages: 90%+ disk compression, reads only queried columns, and SIMD CPU vectorization."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nData Layout on Disk:\nRow-Store (OLTP):\nPage 1: [ID: 1, Name: 'Alice', Age: 30, Salary: 5000]\nPage 2: [ID: 2, Name: 'Bob',   Age: 25, Salary: 6000]\n\nColumn-Store (OLAP):\nFile ID:     [1, 2, 3, 4, 5...]\nFile Name:   ['Alice', 'Bob'...]\nFile Salary: [5000, 6000, 7500...] <-- Query reads ONLY this file for SUM/AVG!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Whenever analytical queries involve `SUM`, `AVG`, `GROUP BY` over millions of rows, route them to a Columnar database (ClickHouse, BigQuery) rather than an OLTP RDBMS.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Snowflake",
      "ClickHouse",
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Meta"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
