    "id": "int-dbms-082",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What are Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD)? Contrast SCD Type 1, Type 2, and Type 3 with practical examples.",
    "answer": "In data warehouses, dimension attributes change slowly over time (e.g., a customer moves to a new city, an employee changes departments). Managing this evolution is handled via Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD):\n\n1. SCD Type 1 (Overwrite):\n   - Action: Overwrite the old value directly with the new value in-place.\n   - History: Zero historical tracking. Past sales will now retroactively be attributed to the customer's new city.\n   - Best for: Correcting spelling errors or typos where history is irrelevant.\n\n2. SCD Type 2 (Add New Row - Industry Standard):\n   - Action: Never update existing rows! Insert a brand-new row for the changed dimension, with tracking columns: `start_date`, `end_date`, and `is_current` flag.\n   - History: 100% full historical tracking. Sales made before 2026 reflect the old city; sales made after reflect the new city.\n\n3. SCD Type 3 (Add Previous Column):\n   - Action: Add a new column to the existing row to store the previous value (`current_city`, `previous_city`).\n   - History: Tracks only the single most recent previous value. Cannot track transitions across 3+ changes.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SCD Type 1: Overwrites data; retains zero historical context.",
      "SCD Type 2: Inserts new row with validity dates (start_date, end_date, is_current); full history preserved.",
      "SCD Type 3: Stores previous value in a dedicated column; tracks only 1 historical state."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- SCD Type 2 Customer Dimension Table:\nCREATE TABLE dim_customers (\n    customer_key BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, -- Surrogate dimension key\n    customer_id INT,                    -- Natural business ID\n    name VARCHAR(100),\n    city VARCHAR(100),\n    start_date DATE NOT NULL,\n    end_date DATE,                      -- NULL or '9999-12-31' if currently active\n    is_current BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE\n);\n\n-- When Alice moves from 'Chicago' to 'New York':\n-- Step 1: Expire old active row\nUPDATE dim_customers\nSET end_date = CURRENT_DATE, is_current = FALSE\nWHERE customer_id = 101 AND is_current = TRUE;\n\n-- Step 2: Insert new active row\nINSERT INTO dim_customers (customer_id, name, city, start_date, end_date, is_current)\nVALUES (101, 'Alice', 'New York', CURRENT_DATE, NULL, TRUE);\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In dimensional modeling, Fact tables must always link to the surrogate `customer_key` of the SCD Type 2 table, not the natural `customer_id`, to preserve point-in-time reporting integrity.",
