    "id": "int-dbms-084",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Compare the 3 Multi-Tenancy database architectures: Database-per-Tenant, Schema-per-Tenant, and Shared-Database Shared-Schema.",
    "answer": "SaaS applications serving thousands of corporate customers (tenants) must balance security isolation against operational cost:\n\n1. Database-per-Tenant (Highest Isolation):\n   - Architecture: Every customer has an entirely independent physical database instance.\n   - Pros: Maximum security and regulatory compliance (HIPAA/GDPR); tenant noisy-neighbor issues cannot affect others; backup/restore per tenant.\n   - Cons: Huge operational overhead; managing 5,000 databases drives infrastructure costs sky-high; complex cross-tenant schema migrations.\n\n2. Schema-per-Tenant (Intermediate):\n   - Architecture: Single database instance, but each tenant has a separate schema (namespace).\n   - Pros: Logical data separation; simpler connection pooling than separate DBs.\n   - Cons: RDBMS engines struggle with catalog bloat when schemas exceed a few hundred.\n\n3. Shared-Database Shared-Schema (Highest Density / Cost-Effective):\n   - Architecture: All tenants share the identical tables, with a `tenant_id` column on every table.\n   - Pros: Lowest infrastructure cost; trivial centralized migrations.\n   - Cons: High risk of data leaks if developer forgets `WHERE tenant_id = :id`; noisy neighbors can saturate buffer pool; requires Row-Level Security (RLS) enforcement.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Database-per-Tenant: Complete physical isolation; high cost and maintenance overhead.",
      "Schema-per-Tenant: Logical isolation via DB schemas; bounded scalability.",
      "Shared-Schema: Lowest cost and highest density; requires strict Row-Level Security (RLS) guards."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Shared-Schema Multi-Tenancy with PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS):\nCREATE TABLE tenant_documents (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    tenant_id INT NOT NULL,\n    title VARCHAR(255),\n    content TEXT\n);\n\n-- Enable RLS:\nALTER TABLE tenant_documents ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n\n-- Create policy restricting access to tenant set in current session:\nCREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_policy ON tenant_documents\n    USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant')::INT);\n\n-- Application sets session variable upon acquiring connection:\nSET app.current_tenant = '42';\nSELECT * FROM tenant_documents; -- Automatically filters strictly to tenant 42!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS) is the gold standard for Shared-Schema multi-tenancy: it enforces tenant isolation directly in the database kernel, preventing accidental data leaks.",
