    "id": "int-dbms-085",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "How do Vector Databases (pgvector, Milvus, Pinecone) index AI embeddings? Explain HNSW and IVFFlat.",
    "answer": "AI models (LLMs, CLIP) convert text, images, and audio into high-dimensional vector embeddings (e.g., 1536 floats). Traditional B+ trees cannot index high-dimensional vectors. Vector databases perform Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search using distance metrics (Cosine Similarity, Euclidean Distance).\n\nTwo Core Vector Index Algorithms:\n\n1. IVFFlat (Inverted File Flat):\n   - Clustering: Uses k-means clustering to divide vector space into $C$ Voronoi cells.\n   - Query: The query vector finds the nearest centroid(s) and searches only the vectors inside those specific cells (`nlist` / `nprobes`).\n   - Pros: Low memory footprint; fast index build time.\n   - Cons: Lower recall accuracy for complex distributions.\n\n2. HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World):\n   - Graph Structure: Multi-layer graph. The top layers have long-distance links for fast global routing (like skip-lists); bottom layers have dense local links for fine-grained clustering.\n   - Query: Greedily traverses down graph layers to find the nearest neighbors in $O(\\log N)$.\n   - Pros: Outstanding recall accuracy and ultra-fast query latency.\n   - Cons: Heavy RAM consumption to store graph edge lists.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Vector databases index high-dimensional embeddings for AI semantic similarity search.",
      "IVFFlat clusters vectors into Voronoi cells; low memory, medium recall.",
      "HNSW builds a multi-layer hierarchical skip-graph; high memory, state-of-the-art speed and recall.",
      "Distance metrics: Cosine Distance, Inner Product (Dot Product), L2 Euclidean Distance."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Vector Search using PostgreSQL 'pgvector' extension:\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\n\nCREATE TABLE knowledge_base (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    content TEXT,\n    embedding vector(1536) -- OpenAI text-embedding-3-small dimensions\n);\n\n-- Build HNSW index with Cosine Distance (<=>):\nCREATE INDEX ON knowledge_base USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)\nWITH (m = 16, ef_construction = 64);\n\n-- Perform Sub-Second Semantic Search:\nSELECT content, 1 - (embedding <=> '[0.012, -0.045, ...]') AS similarity\nFROM knowledge_base\nORDER BY embedding <=> '[0.012, -0.045, ...]'\nLIMIT 5;\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "For production workloads with under 50 million vectors, `pgvector` inside standard PostgreSQL eliminates the need for a separate standalone vector database.",
    "company_tags": [
      "OpenAI",
      "Google",
      "Pinecone",
      "Meta",
      "Amazon"
    ],
