    "id": "int-dbms-086",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Why is `SELECT COUNT(*)` notoriously slow on large PostgreSQL tables? How do you optimize it?",
    "answer": "Why `COUNT(*)` is Slow in PostgreSQL:\nUnlike MySQL MyISAM (which stored a single row counter in table headers), PostgreSQL's MVCC design means every row version has visibility metadata (`xmin`, `xmax`).\n- What is alive for Transaction A may be dead or invisible for Transaction B!\n- Therefore, there is NO SINGLE global row count! PostgreSQL must perform a full sequential scan or full index scan to evaluate row visibility for the current transaction snapshot, taking seconds on a 50M-row table!\n\nOptimization Strategies:\n1. Fast Approximation ($O(1)$ Milliseconds): Read table statistics from system catalogs: `SELECT reltuples::BIGINT FROM pg_class WHERE relname = 'large_table';` (accurate to ~99% after autovacuum).\n2. Summary Counter Table (Exact): Maintain a counter table updated via triggers on insert/delete.\n3. Redis Counter: Atomically increment/decrement a Redis key on writes.\n4. HyperLogLog: For distinct counts (`COUNT(DISTINCT user_id)`), use Redis HLL or PostgreSQL `pg_similarity` for 99% accuracy in constant 12KB memory.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "PostgreSQL MVCC requires evaluating row visibility per transaction snapshot; no global row counter exists.",
      "For fast approximations: Query system catalog `pg_class.reltuples` in 1 millisecond.",
      "For exact real-time counts: Maintain a trigger-based summary counter table.",
      "For unique distinct counts: Use HyperLogLog to estimate cardinality with 0.81% standard error."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Method 1: Instant O(1) row count approximation (99% accurate):\nSELECT reltuples::BIGINT AS estimated_count\nFROM pg_class\nWHERE relname = 'orders';\n\n-- Method 2: Redis HyperLogLog for COUNT(DISTINCT visitor_id):\n-- In Redis:\n-- PFADD site_visitors \"user_101\"\n-- PFADD site_visitors \"user_102\"\n-- PFCOUNT site_visitors -> Returns cardinality using only 12KB RAM!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In UI pagination, never run `COUNT(*)` on millions of rows to show total page numbers; use 'Next' / 'Previous' infinite scroll or approximate counts.",
    "company_tags": [
      "PostgreSQL Core",
      "Amazon",
      "Reddit",
      "StackOverflow"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
