    "id": "int-dbms-087",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What is the N+1 Query Problem in Object-Relational Mappers (Hibernate, Prisma, Django)? How do Eager Fetching and JOIN FETCH solve it?",
    "answer": "The N+1 Query Problem is the most common database performance bug in web applications using ORMs:\n\nHow It Happens:\nSuppose you fetch 100 Blog Posts and want to display each post's Author:\n1. The ORM executes 1 query to fetch posts: `SELECT * FROM posts LIMIT 100;` (1 query).\n2. By default, ORMs use Lazy Loading for associations (`fetch = FetchType.LAZY`).\n3. In application code, iterating over posts (`post.getAuthor().getName()`) triggers a separate SQL query for EACH individual post author!\n4. Result: $1 + 100 = 101$ queries sent across the network to the database, exhausting connection pools and causing severe latency spikes!\n\nSolutions:\n1. JOIN FETCH / Eager Fetching: Instruct the ORM to fetch the relationship in the initial query using a SQL `JOIN`: `SELECT p FROM Post p JOIN FETCH p.author` (1 single query!).\n2. Batch Fetching / In-Clause: Configure the ORM to fetch missing associations in batches using an `IN` clause: `SELECT * FROM authors WHERE id IN (1, 2, 3... 100)` ($1 + 1 = 2$ queries).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "N+1 query problem executes 1 query for parents + N separate queries for each child's relationship.",
      "Caused by unmonitored Lazy Loading inside application loops.",
      "Resolved using JOIN FETCH (single join query) or Batch Fetching (IN clause)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```java\n// VULNERABLE TO N+1 (101 queries executed!):\nList<Post> posts = session.createQuery(\"FROM Post\", Post.class).getResultList();\nfor (Post p : posts) {\n    System.out.println(p.getAuthor().getName()); // Fires a SQL query on EVERY iteration!\n}\n\n// RESOLVED VIA JOIN FETCH (1 single query!):\nList<Post> posts = session.createQuery(\n    \"SELECT p FROM Post p JOIN FETCH p.author\", Post.class\n).getResultList();\n// SQL Executed: SELECT * FROM posts p INNER JOIN authors a ON p.author_id = a.id;\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Always install query counters in unit tests (e.g., Hibernate's `QuickPerf` or Django's `assertNumQueries`) to automatically catch N+1 regressions in CI/CD pipelines.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Uber",
      "Shopify"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
