    "id": "int-dbms-088",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What makes a SQL query 'SARGable' (Search Argument Able)? Identify non-SARGable anti-patterns and their fixes.",
    "answer": "A query is SARGable (Search Argument Able) if the SQL engine can utilize a B+ tree index directly to perform an efficient Index Seek, rather than having to evaluate an expression across every row via a Full Table Scan.\n\nCommon Non-SARGable Anti-Patterns and Fixes:\n\n1. Wrapping Indexed Columns in Functions:\n   - Bad: `WHERE YEAR(created_at) = 2026` (Engine must execute `YEAR()` on all 10M rows).\n   - SARGable Fix: `WHERE created_at >= '2026-01-01' AND created_at < '2027-01-01'`.\n\n2. Arithmetic on Indexed Columns:\n   - Bad: `WHERE salary * 12 > 120000`.\n   - SARGable Fix: Move arithmetic to the literal side: `WHERE salary > 120000 / 12`.\n\n3. Leading Wildcard in LIKE Queries:\n   - Bad: `WHERE username LIKE '%smith'` (Cannot traverse B+ tree; requires full table scan).\n   - SARGable Fix: `WHERE username LIKE 'smith%'` (Can traverse B+ tree!). For suffix searches, index the reversed string: `REVERSE(username)`.\n\n4. Implicit Type Conversion:\n   - Bad: `WHERE string_id = 12345` (forces engine to convert all string IDs to integers).\n   - SARGable Fix: `WHERE string_id = '12345'`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SARGable queries allow the engine to navigate B+ tree index seek branches directly.",
      "Never wrap indexed columns in functions (`YEAR(col)`, `LOWER(col)`) or arithmetic operations.",
      "Leading wildcards (`LIKE '%abc'`) negate B+ tree indexing; trailing wildcards (`LIKE 'abc%'`) are SARGable."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Non-SARGable: Full Table Scan!\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE DATE(order_date) = '2026-09-14';\n\n-- SARGable: Index Seek in O(log N)!\nSELECT * FROM orders\nWHERE order_date >= '2026-09-14 00:00:00' AND order_date < '2026-09-15 00:00:00';\n\n-- Non-SARGable:\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE salary + 5000 > 60000;\n-- SARGable:\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE salary > 55000;\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Golden Rule: Keep the indexed column completely isolated and naked on one side of the operator: `column_name OPERATOR literal_expression`.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Google",
      "Oracle"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
