    "id": "int-dbms-089",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Define RPO (Recovery Point Objective) and RTO (Recovery Time Objective) in Database Disaster Recovery planning.",
    "answer": "RPO and RTO are the two foundational business metrics defining disaster recovery SLAs:\n\n1. Recovery Point Objective (RPO) - 'How much data can we afford to lose?':\n   - Measures the maximum acceptable time interval of lost transactions caused by a disaster.\n   - Example: If a system takes nightly backups at midnight and crashes at 3:00 PM, 15 hours of data are lost. If the business SLA specifies $RPO \\le 1 \\text{ hour}$, this setup violates the SLA!\n   - To achieve $RPO = 0$: Requires synchronous multi-region replication.\n\n2. Recovery Time Objective (RTO) - 'How long can we afford to be down?':\n   - Measures the maximum acceptable duration of system downtime required to restore service and resume business operations after a failure.\n   - Example: If restoring a 10TB backup from S3 takes 8 hours, but the business SLA specifies $RTO \\le 15 \\text{ minutes}$, the disaster recovery architecture fails!\n   - To achieve low RTO: Automated multi-region failover with standby hot replicas.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "RPO: Maximum acceptable data loss measured backwards in time from failure moment.",
      "RTO: Maximum acceptable downtime duration required to restore service.",
      "Zero RPO requires synchronous replication; near-zero RTO requires automated hot standby failover."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nDisaster Recovery Metrics:\n<--- Past (RPO) -------- [ Disaster Occurs ] -------- Future (RTO) --->\n[ Data Loss Duration ]                       [ Downtime Duration ]\n(Measured in Time/Data)                      (Measured in Time to Recovery)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Executive trade-off: As RPO and RTO approach zero, infrastructure costs increase exponentially due to cross-region bandwidth, synchronous replication latency, and redundant hot clusters.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Goldman Sachs"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
    "is_hidden": false,
    "is_deleted": false,
    "sort_order": 89
