    "title": "How does Point-In-Time Recovery (PITR) work? How do base backups and archived WAL logs restore a database to 1 second before disaster?",
    "answer": "Point-In-Time Recovery (PITR) enables restoring a database to the exact state it was at any arbitrary microsecond in the past (e.g., at 02:44:59 PM, precisely 1 second before a junior developer accidentally executed `DROP TABLE users;`).\n\nPrerequisites:\n1. Continuous WAL Archiving: As soon as a WAL segment fills, the DBMS pushes it to durable object storage (AWS S3 / GCS).\n2. Periodic Base Backups: A physical snapshot of all data files taken weekly or daily (`pg_basebackup`).\n\nRecovery Steps:\n1. Stop the database and restore the latest Base Backup taken BEFORE the target recovery time.\n2. Create a recovery configuration specifying target time: `recovery_target_time = '2026-09-14 14:44:59'`. Specify `recovery_target_action = 'promote'`.\n3. Start the database in recovery mode.\n4. Replay Phase: The engine replays archived WAL logs sequentially from S3, applying all committed changes up to the exact millisecond requested, and halts right before the catastrophic drop statement!\n5. Database promotes to a primary with zero data loss!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "PITR restores a database to any historical timestamp down to the microsecond.",
      "Combines physical base backups with continuous sequential WAL log archiving.",
      "Replays WAL logs up to the exact second before a human or hardware catastrophe."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nPITR Timeline:\nBase Backup (Sunday 00:00) -------- Archived WAL Logs ---------> Disaster (Tue 14:45:00)\n    |                                                             ^\n    +--- Restored to disk ---> Replays WAL up to 14:44:59 --------+ (Halt 1 sec prior!)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Tools like `pgBackRest` and `WAL-G` automate continuous compression, encryption, and streaming of PostgreSQL WAL archives directly to S3.",
