    "id": "int-dbms-093",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What is Transparent Data Encryption (TDE)? How does Envelope Encryption secure database keys?",
    "answer": "1. Transparent Data Encryption (TDE):\n   - Encrypts database data files, WAL logs, undo logs, and temporary tables at rest on disk without requiring application code changes.\n   - Encryption is 'transparent': when an authorized user executes a query, data pages are decrypted in RAM; when dirty pages are written to disk, they are automatically encrypted using AES-256.\n   - Protects against physical disk theft, unencrypted backup leaks, and decommissioned server snooping.\n\n2. Envelope Encryption (Key Hierarchy):\n   - Encrypting gigabytes of data directly with a master key stored in an external KMS (AWS KMS, HashiCorp Vault) would be terribly slow (network latency on every disk write).\n   - Envelope encryption uses a two-tier key hierarchy:\n     - Data Encryption Key (DEK): Fast local AES symmetric key that encrypts data pages on disk.\n     - Key Encryption Key (KEK) / Master Key: Stored securely in the remote Hardware Security Module (HSM/KMS). It encrypts the DEK!\n     - Only the encrypted DEK is stored on disk alongside the database.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "TDE encrypts data pages and WAL logs at rest on disk using AES-256 transparently.",
      "Envelope encryption uses a local Data Encryption Key (DEK) encrypted by a remote Master Key (KEK).",
      "Prevents data exposure if physical SSDs, snapshots, or raw backup files are stolen."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nEnvelope Encryption Architecture:\n[ KMS / HSM ] ----> Master Key (KEK)\n                          | (Encrypts)\n                          v\nDatabase Server:    [ Encrypted DEK ] -> Decrypted in RAM -> Encrypts Data Pages (AES-256)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Remember: TDE protects data AT REST on disk; it does NOT protect data in-flight over the network (requires TLS) or against SQL injection attacks.",
