    "id": "int-dbms-095",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "What causes a Database Connection Leak in application servers? How do you diagnose and eliminate it?",
    "answer": "A Database Connection Leak occurs when an application thread borrows a physical connection from the connection pool but fails to close/return it (usually due to unhandled runtime exceptions, early returns, or missing `finally` blocks).\n\nFailure Progression:\n1. Leaked connections remain in 'active/idle-in-transaction' state indefinitely.\n2. Over time, all available connections in the pool (`max_pool_size = 30`) are drained.\n3. Subsequent incoming HTTP requests block waiting for a free connection until `connectionTimeout` (30s) expires.\n4. Entire application server freezes with `ConnectionTimeoutException: Connection is not available`!\n\nDiagnosis and Elimination:\n1. Leak Detection Threshold: Set `leakDetectionThreshold = 5000` in HikariCP. If a connection is held longer than 5 seconds, HikariCP logs a stack trace identifying the exact line of code that leaked it.\n2. Inspect Active Transactions in SQL: Query `pg_stat_activity` for `state = 'idle in transaction'` with high duration.\n3. Java `try-with-resources`: Always wrap database connections and result sets in `try (Connection conn = ds.getConnection())` so they auto-close on scope exit.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Connection leaks occur when borrowed connections are not returned to the pool after execution.",
      "Leads to connection pool exhaustion and application-wide timeouts.",
      "Diagnosed using HikariCP leak detection thresholds and pg_stat_activity inspection.",
      "Prevented using try-with-resources or framework-managed transactions (@Transactional)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- In PostgreSQL: Find connection leaks (connections idle in transaction > 5 minutes):\nSELECT pid, usename, client_addr, state,\n       now() - state_change AS idle_duration,\n       query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE state = 'idle in transaction'\n  AND (now() - state_change) > interval '5 minutes';\n\n-- Terminate rogue leaking connection:\nSELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid);\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Always configure `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '30s'` in PostgreSQL to automatically kill leaking connections on the database side.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Uber",
      "Netflix"
    ],
