    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "How do you implement Bi-Directional Keyset Pagination (Seek Method) for high-performance mobile infinite scrolling?",
    "answer": "Mobile feeds require bi-directional pagination: scrolling down (fetching older items) and pulling down to refresh (fetching newer items).\n\nKeyset Pagination Implementation:\nUse a composite unique cursor `(created_at, id)`.\n\n1. Scrolling Down (Fetch Next 20 Older Posts):\n   - Predicate: `(created_at, id) < (:last_created_at, :last_id)`\n   - SQL: `SELECT * FROM posts WHERE (created_at, id) < (:last_created_at, :last_id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 20;`\n   - Uses composite B+ tree index `(created_at DESC, id DESC)` for instant seek.\n\n2. Scrolling Up / Pull to Refresh (Fetch 20 Newer Posts):\n   - Predicate: `(created_at, id) > (:first_created_at, :first_id)`\n   - SQL: `SELECT * FROM posts WHERE (created_at, id) > (:first_created_at, :first_id) ORDER BY created_at ASC, id ASC LIMIT 20;`\n\nCursor Encoding:\nEncode the tuple `(created_at, id)` into an opaque Base64 cursor string returned in the API response: `\"cursor\": \"MjAyNi0wOS0xNFQxNDowMDowMFpfMTA1NA==\"`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Bi-directional keyset pagination uses a composite cursor `(timestamp, unique_id)`.",
      "Eliminates duplicate or skipped rows caused by new items inserted between page clicks.",
      "Encode cursors as opaque Base64 strings to decouple client from schema details."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- High-performance composite index:\nCREATE INDEX idx_posts_feed ON posts (created_at DESC, id DESC);\n\n-- Query fetching next page of feed:\nSELECT id, title, created_at\nFROM posts\nWHERE (created_at, id) < ('2026-09-14 12:00:00', 1054)\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 20;\n-- Executes as a single B+ Tree Index Seek; zero disk rows discarded!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Never expose raw database primary keys directly in client API pagination links; always serialize them into opaque encrypted or Base64 cursors.",
