    "id": "int-dbms-097",
    "topic_id": "topic-dbms",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Compare the architectural scaling techniques for Read-Heavy versus Write-Heavy database workloads.",
    "answer": "Database scaling depends fundamentally on workload access profiles:\n\n1. Scaling Read-Heavy Workloads (e.g., News sites, E-commerce browsing: 95% Read / 5% Write):\n   - Read Caching: Place Redis/Memcached in front of DB (Cache-Aside) for 90%+ hit ratios.\n   - Read Replicas: Deploy 3-5 asynchronous read replicas behind a load balancer; route `SELECT` queries to replicas.\n   - Covering Indexes: Add covering indexes to enable Index-Only Scans.\n   - Materialized Views: Precompute expensive analytical aggregations.\n   - CDN Caching: Cache static query outputs at the edge (Cloudflare/CloudFront).\n\n2. Scaling Write-Heavy Workloads (e.g., IoT telemetry, Uber driver GPS locations: 80% Write / 20% Read):\n   - LSM-Tree Storage: Migrate from B+ trees to LSM engines (Cassandra, RocksDB, ScyllaDB).\n   - Message Queues (Write Buffering): Buffer high-frequency writes in Kafka/RabbitMQ; batch insert to DB.\n   - Horizontal Sharding: Shard database across machines by hash key (`device_id`).\n   - Micro-Batching: Group single-row inserts into multi-row batches (`INSERT INTO t VALUES (1), (2), (3)...`).\n   - Disable Unnecessary Indexes: Every secondary index doubles write I/O overhead.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Read-heavy scaling: Caching (Redis/CDN), Read Replicas, Covering Indexes, Materialized Views.",
      "Write-heavy scaling: LSM-tree engines (Cassandra), Message Queue write buffers (Kafka), Batching, Sharding.",
      "Secondary indexes drastically reduce write throughput; prune unused indexes on write-heavy tables."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```text\nWorkload Scaling Blueprint:\nRead-Heavy Architecture:\nClients ---> CDN ---> Read-Aside Cache (Redis) ---> Read Replicas ---> Primary DB\n\nWrite-Heavy Architecture:\nClients ---> Message Queue (Kafka) ---> Worker Batcher ---> Sharded DB Cluster (LSM-Tree)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In interviews, always ask for the Read:Write ratio upfront before proposing database architecture: a design optimized for reads is the complete opposite of a design optimized for writes.",
