    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Why are Logical Dumps (`pg_dump` / `mysqldump`) unsuitable for Disaster Recovery of multi-terabyte production databases?",
    "answer": "1. Logical Dumps (`pg_dump`, `mysqldump`):\n   - Mechanics: Queries the database row by row, converting tables into SQL text files (`CREATE TABLE... INSERT INTO...`).\n   - Why They Fail on Large Databases (>500 GB):\n     - Crushing Backup Time: Generating text SQL statements for a 5TB database takes 18+ hours, locking tables or exhausting buffer pools.\n     - Catastrophic Recovery Time (Violates RTO): Restoring a 5TB SQL text dump requires executing billions of SQL statements, re-building all B+ tree indexes from scratch, and re-validating foreign keys! Restoration takes 48 to 72 hours, resulting in days of downtime!\n\n2. Physical Backups (`pg_basebackup`, Percona XtraBackup):\n   - Mechanics: Copies raw physical data blocks directly from disk at the block layer without parsing SQL.\n   - Recovery: Simply copies the pre-built, pre-indexed disk blocks back into the data directory and replays WAL. A 5TB database can be restored in under 30 minutes (bounded only by disk I/O / network bandwidth!).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Logical dumps export text SQL statements; restoring forces slow row-by-row reinsertion and index rebuilding.",
      "Physical backups copy raw binary disk blocks; restoration is 100x faster and bounded only by network/SSD I/O.",
      "Use logical dumps for minor schema migrations or test data; use physical backups + WAL for production disaster recovery."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```bash\n# SLOW Logical Dump (Unsuitable for large production DR):\npg_dump -Fc mydb > backup.dump # Takes 12 hours, restore takes 2 days!\n\n# FAST Physical Block Backup (Industry Standard):\npg_basebackup -h master_db -D /data/postgres -Fp -Xs -P -R # Restores in minutes!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Never use `mysqldump` or `pg_dump` as the primary disaster recovery plan for databases larger than 100GB; physical block backups with WAL archiving are mandatory.",
