    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "subject_label": "DBMS",
    "title": "Summarize the Top 5 Tricky DBMS Interview Traps and Performance Misconceptions.",
    "answer": "Top 5 DBMS Interview Traps Frequently Targeted by FAANG Interviewers:\n\n1. The `NOT IN (..., NULL)` Trap:\n   - If a subquery contains even a single `NULL` value, `NOT IN` evaluates to `UNKNOWN` for all rows and returns an empty result set! Always use `NOT EXISTS` or `WHERE col IS NOT NULL`.\n2. The 'Index on Every Column' Fallacy:\n   - Indexes accelerate reads but degrade writes: every `INSERT` or `UPDATE` must update every secondary B+ tree index synchronously. Maintain only indexes supported by query patterns.\n3. Confusing B-Tree with B+ Tree:\n   - Interviewees mistakenly state that B-Trees store data only in leaves. In B-Trees, data is stored in all nodes; only B+ Trees store data exclusively in leaves with doubly-linked leaf chaining.\n4. Believing `REPEATABLE READ` Prevents All Anomalies:\n   - `REPEATABLE READ` permits Write Skew in Snapshot Isolation! Only true `SERIALIZABLE` isolation prevents Write Skew.\n5. The Missing Foreign Key Index Trap:\n   - In most RDBMS (including Postgres and MySQL), declaring a `FOREIGN KEY` does NOT automatically create an index on the child table's foreign key column! Deleting parent rows or joining child tables forces full table scans unless you manually create an index.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "`NOT IN` with a NULL in the subquery returns 0 rows; always use `NOT EXISTS`.",
      "Foreign keys do NOT create indexes on child tables automatically; manual indexing is required.",
      "B+ trees store data only in leaves; B-trees store data in all nodes.",
      "Repeatable Read permits Write Skew; only Serializable guarantees serial equivalence."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "```sql\n-- Trap 1: NOT IN with NULL returns EMPTY SET!\nSELECT * FROM departments WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT manager_id FROM employees);\n-- If ANY manager_id is NULL, returns 0 rows!\n-- FIX:\nSELECT * FROM departments d\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM employees e WHERE e.manager_id = d.id);\n\n-- Trap 5: Foreign keys are NOT automatically indexed on child tables:\nCREATE TABLE order_items (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    order_id INT REFERENCES orders(id) -- DOES NOT CREATE INDEX!\n);\n-- MUST manually create index to prevent table lock on parent deletion:\nCREATE INDEX idx_order_items_order_id ON order_items(order_id);\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Always highlight the Foreign Key Index Trap: failing to index foreign key columns on child tables causes massive table-level locks during parent deletions and ruins join performance.",
