    "id": "int-os-002",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Distinguish between Hardware Interrupts, Traps (Software Interrupts), and CPU Exceptions/Faults.",
    "answer": "All three mechanisms divert normal CPU execution to a kernel-level handler, but their origin and synchronicity differ:\n\n1. Hardware Interrupt (Asynchronous / External):\n   - Origin: Generated by external physical hardware devices (keyboard keystroke, NIC packet arrival, disk controller read completion, timer chip tick).\n   - Timing: Asynchronous! Unrelated to the instruction currently being executed by the CPU. Can occur at any random clock cycle.\n   - Handling: CPU checks the interrupt line at the end of every instruction cycle. If raised, CPU pushes registers and jumps to the Interrupt Service Routine (ISR) via the Interrupt Vector Table (IVT).\n\n2. Trap / Software Interrupt (Synchronous / Intentional):\n   - Origin: Generated intentionally by software executing a specific CPU instruction (e.g., `syscall`, `sysenter`, `int 0x80`).\n   - Purpose: Requesting OS kernel services (System Calls).\n   - Timing: Synchronous! Executes at a deterministic, predictable point in code.\n\n3. Exception / Fault / Abort (Synchronous / Unintentional):\n   - Origin: Generated automatically by CPU hardware when executing an erroneous or unexpected instruction.\n   - Examples: Division by zero, page fault (accessing unmapped virtual memory), segmentation violation.\n   - Handling: Kernel resolves it (e.g., swapping in page for page fault) or terminates the offending process (`SIGSEGV`, `SIGFPE`).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Hardware Interrupt: Asynchronous, triggered by external hardware devices (timer, disk, network).",
      "Trap: Synchronous, intentionally triggered by software to execute system calls.",
      "Exception: Synchronous, automatically triggered by CPU on errors (divide by zero, page fault)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nComparison Table:\nEvent Type   | Synchronicity | Trigger Source       | Purpose / Examples\n-------------+---------------+----------------------+-----------------------------------\nInterrupt    | Asynchronous  | Hardware Peripherals | Disk I/O complete, Timer tick\nTrap         | Synchronous   | Software Instruction | System Calls (read, write, fork)\nException    | Synchronous   | CPU Hardware Fault   | Page Fault, Divide-by-Zero, SIGSEGV\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Page Faults are unique exceptions because they are 'recoverable faults': the CPU rolls back the instruction pointer to re-execute the exact instruction after the kernel loads the missing page from disk.",
