    "id": "int-os-003",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Step through the exact hardware and OS execution sequence when a C program executes `read()`.",
    "answer": "Step-by-step sequence of a System Call:\n\n1. User Space Library Wrapper:\n   - Application invokes C standard library function `read(fd, buffer, count)`.\n   - Glibc copies arguments into CPU registers (on x86-64: `rdi`=fd, `rsi`=buf, `rdx`=count) and loads the unique System Call Number for `read` (e.g., `0`) into register `rax`.\n\n2. The Privilege Mode Transition:\n   - Glibc executes the hardware `syscall` instruction (or `int 0x80`).\n   - Hardware atomic actions:\n     - CPU saves User Program Counter (`RIP`) and flags into registers (`RCX`, `R11`).\n     - CPU switches privilege level from User Mode (Ring 3) to Kernel Mode (Ring 0) by setting Mode Bit = 0.\n     - CPU switches from the User Stack to the pre-allocated Kernel Stack.\n     - CPU jumps to the kernel's system call entry point (`system_call_entry`) stored in the Model Specific Register (`MSR_LSTAR`).\n\n3. Kernel Execution:\n   - Kernel saves remaining user registers onto the kernel stack.\n   - Kernel validates user buffer memory pointers (prevents kernel memory overwrites).\n   - Indexes into the System Call Dispatch Table (`sys_call_table[rax]`) to invoke `sys_read()`.\n   - `sys_read()` invokes the VFS (Virtual File System) and device driver.\n\n4. Return to User Space:\n   - Return value (bytes read or error code) is placed in `rax`.\n   - Kernel restores saved user registers, executes `sysret` instruction.\n   - Hardware switches Mode Bit back to Ring 3, restores User Stack, and resumes user code execution.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Glibc places syscall number in RAX and arguments in standard CPU registers.",
      "`syscall` instruction atomically switches Ring 3 -> Ring 0, switches stacks, and jumps to kernel entry.",
      "Kernel validates user pointers and dispatches to handler via `sys_call_table`.",
      "`sysret` restores user registers and drops CPU privilege back to User Mode."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```c\n// Direct raw assembly syscall in C without glibc wrapper:\nlong my_write(int fd, const char *buf, unsigned long count) {\n    long ret;\n    asm volatile (\n        \"mov $1, %%rax\\n\"   // Syscall 1 is sys_write on x86_64\n        \"mov %1, %%rdi\\n\"   // arg 1: fd\n        \"mov %2, %%rsi\\n\"   // arg 2: buffer pointer\n        \"mov %3, %%rdx\\n\"   // arg 3: length\n        \"syscall\\n\"         // Hardware transition to Ring 0!\n        \"mov %%rax, %0\\n\"   // Return value in RAX\n        : \"=r\"(ret)\n        : \"r\"((long)fd), \"r\"(buf), \"r\"(count)\n        : \"rax\", \"rdi\", \"rsi\", \"rdx\", \"rcx\", \"r11\"\n    );\n    return ret;\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Never trust user-space memory pointers in kernel space: the kernel must always use `copy_from_user()` and `copy_to_user()` to verify pointers before dereferencing them.",
