    "id": "int-os-005",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is a Process Control Block (PCB / `task_struct`)? Enumerate its core internal fields.",
    "answer": "A Process Control Block (PCB) is the primary kernel data structure representing an active process. In Linux, it is defined as `struct task_struct` in `<linux/sched.h>`.\n\nCore Internal Fields:\n1. Process Identifier (PID & TGID): Unique process ID, parent PID (PPID), thread group ID.\n2. Process State: Current lifecycle state (`TASK_RUNNING`, `TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE`, `TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE`, `TASK_STOPPED`, `EXIT_ZOMBIE`).\n3. CPU Registers / Context: Saved hardware registers (`RAX`, `RBX`, `RSP` stack pointer, `RIP` instruction pointer, flags) restored during context switching.\n4. CPU Scheduling Information: Process priority, static/dynamic nice value, scheduling policy (`SCHED_NORMAL`, `SCHED_FIFO`), virtual runtime (`vruntime` for CFS).\n5. Memory Management Info (`struct mm_struct *mm`):\n   - Pointers to page directory/tables, Virtual Memory Areas (VMA) for code, data, heap, and stack segments.\n6. File Descriptor Table (`struct files_struct *files`):\n   - Array of pointers to open file descriptions (`fd 0` = stdin, `1` = stdout, `2` = stderr).\n7. Inter-Process Communication & Signals: Pending signals, blocked signals mask, signal handlers table.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "PCB (`task_struct`) is the kernel's central metadata structure for a process.",
      "Stores PID, process state, saved CPU register context, scheduling priority, and vruntime.",
      "Maintains pointers to memory descriptor (`mm_struct`) and open file descriptors (`files_struct`)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```c\n// Conceptual excerpt of Linux task_struct:\nstruct task_struct {\n    volatile long state;             // -1 unrunnable, 0 runnable, >0 stopped\n    pid_t pid;\n    pid_t tgid;                      // Thread Group ID\n    struct task_struct *parent;      // Pointer to parent process\n    struct files_struct *files;      // Open file descriptors table\n    struct mm_struct *mm;            // Virtual memory descriptor\n    u64 vruntime;                    // CFS scheduling virtual runtime\n    struct thread_struct thread;     // Hardware-dependent CPU register context\n};\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In Linux, threads are also represented by `task_struct` instances (cloned with `CLONE_VM | CLONE_FILES`), sharing the same `mm` and `files` pointers as the parent.",
