    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Describe the 5 Process States and their transition triggers. What is the difference between `TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE` and `TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE`?",
    "answer": "Standard 5-State Process Model:\n1. New: The process is being created (allocating PCB and PID).\n2. Ready: The process is loaded into memory, waiting in the ready queue to be allocated CPU time by the scheduler.\n3. Running: The process's instructions are actively executing on a CPU core.\n4. Waiting / Blocked: The process cannot execute until an event completes (waiting for disk I/O, network packet, lock, or timer).\n5. Terminated: The process has finished execution; its resources are released, but exit status is retained in PCB.\n\nState Transitions:\n- Ready $\\to$ Running: Scheduler Dispatch.\n- Running $\\to$ Ready: Timer Interrupt (Preemption) or higher priority task arrives.\n- Running $\\to$ Waiting: I/O request or waiting for child (`wait()`).\n- Waiting $\\to$ Ready: I/O completion interrupt arrives.\n\nDeep Linux Distinction: Interruptible vs Uninterruptible Sleep:\n- `TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE` (State 'S'): Process sleeping waiting for event (e.g., terminal input). Wakes up on event arrival OR if a signal (e.g., `SIGKILL`, `Ctrl+C`) is sent.\n- `TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE` (State 'D'): Process sleeping waiting for critical, fast hardware operations (e.g., raw disk sector I/O). IGNORES ALL SIGNALS! It cannot be killed even with `kill -9`! If hardware hangs, processes in 'D' state become unkillable and accumulate in load average.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Process lifecycle: New -> Ready <-> Running -> Blocked -> Terminated.",
      "`TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE` (S): Sleep state that awakens on I/O completion or signal delivery.",
      "`TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE` (D): Deep sleep state ignoring all signals (including kill -9), waiting for direct device I/O."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nProcess State Diagram:\n             [ New ]\n                | (Admitted)\n                v\n  +-------> [ Ready ] <--------+\n  |             | (Dispatch)   | (I/O Complete)\n  |             v              |\n  | (Preempt) [ Running ] ---> [ Blocked / Waiting ]\n  |             |                (I/O Request)\n  |             v\n  +-------- [ Terminated ]\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "When Linux `load average` is high but CPU usage is 0%, check for processes stuck in 'D' (uninterruptible sleep) state waiting on hung NFS mounts or failing disks.",
