    "id": "int-os-010",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Compare the Memory Layout of a Process versus multiple Threads within that process. What is shared and what is private?",
    "answer": "A Process is an independent execution unit with its own dedicated virtual address space. A Thread is a lightweight dispatchable sub-unit of execution within a process.\n\nMemory Layout Breakdown:\n\n1. Shared Among All Threads in a Process:\n   - Code / Text Segment: The compiled machine instructions (read-only).\n   - Data Segment: Initialized global and static variables.\n   - BSS Segment: Uninitialized global and static variables.\n   - Heap: Dynamically allocated memory (`malloc`, `new`).\n   - Operating System Resources: Open file descriptors table, sockets, environment variables, working directory, and signal handler actions.\n\n2. Private / Unique Per Thread:\n   - Program Counter (PC / RIP): Points to the current instruction the specific thread is executing.\n   - CPU Registers: Local register state holding intermediate calculation values.\n   - Stack: Dedicated thread execution stack storing local variables, function parameters, and return addresses.\n   - Thread-Local Storage (TLS): Variables declared with `__thread` or `thread_local`.\n   - Signal Mask: Which signals are currently blocked for this specific thread.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Shared: Code, Data, BSS, Heap, and open File Descriptors.",
      "Private: Program Counter, CPU registers, Stack, and Thread-Local Storage (TLS).",
      "Stack isolation warning: While stacks are logically private, they reside in the same virtual address space; a rogue pointer can read/corrupt another thread's stack!"
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```c\n#include <pthread.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint global_shared = 100; // Shared across all threads (Data segment)\n\nvoid* thread_func(void* arg) {\n    int local_private = 42; // Allocated on THIS thread's private stack\n    __thread int tls_var = 5; // Thread-Local Storage\n    global_shared += 10;    // Race condition without synchronization!\n    return NULL;\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Because all threads share the identical virtual address space, there is zero hardware memory protection between threads: an out-of-bounds pointer in Thread 1 can overwrite local variables on Thread 2's stack.",
