    "id": "int-os-012",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "How do Test-and-Set and Compare-and-Swap (CAS) work at the CPU hardware level? What is the ABA problem?",
    "answer": "Hardware synchronization primitives provide atomic Read-Modify-Write instructions that execute indivisibly across all CPU cores:\n\n1. Test-and-Set (`TSL`):\n   - Atomically reads a memory location and sets it to `1` in a single clock cycle.\n   - Returns the previous value.\n   - Used to implement basic spinlocks.\n\n2. Compare-and-Swap (`CAS` - `cmpxchg` on x86):\n   - Takes 3 arguments: Memory Location ($V$), Expected Old Value ($A$), New Value ($B$).\n   - Operation: Atomically checks: if $V == A$, set $V = B$ and return `true`; else return `false`.\n   - Foundation of Lock-Free programming, `AtomicInteger`, and atomic reference pointers.\n\nHardware Implementation:\nThe CPU asserts a cache-line lock via the MESI cache coherence protocol (or historical bus lock line), preventing any other core from reading or writing that cache line until the instruction finishes.\n\nThe ABA Problem in CAS:\n1. Thread 1 reads value $A$ from node pointer.\n2. Thread 2 preempts, changes value from $A \\to B$, and then changes it back from $B \\to A$.\n3. Thread 1 resumes, executes `CAS(expected=A, new=C)`.\n4. CAS succeeds because value is $A$! However, the underlying memory structure might have been freed and recycled (e.g., in a lock-free stack), causing memory corruption!\n5. Solution: Tagged pointers / Version stamps (AtomicStampedReference in Java) combining value + integer version counter.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "CAS (`cmpxchg`) atomically compares and updates memory in a single hardware cycle.",
      "Hardware cache coherence (MESI) locks the cache line during atomic operations.",
      "ABA problem: Value changes from A to B and back to A, deceiving naive CAS.",
      "Solved using versioned/tagged pointers (`AtomicStampedReference`)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```c\n// Spinlock implemented via atomic CAS:\n#include <stdatomic.h>\n\ntypedef struct {\n    atomic_int lock_state;\n} spinlock_t;\n\nvoid spin_lock(spinlock_t *lock) {\n    int expected = 0;\n    // Loop until CAS atomically swaps 0 -> 1\n    while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&lock->lock_state, &expected, 1)) {\n        expected = 0; // Reset expected after failed CAS\n        // CPU pause instruction prevents bus starvation:\n        #if defined(__x86_64__)\n        asm volatile(\"pause\");\n        #endif\n    }\n}\n\nvoid spin_unlock(spinlock_t *lock) {\n    atomic_store(&lock->lock_state, 0);\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "The `pause` instruction in x86 spin loops delays execution by ~10-140 cycles to prevent pipeline memory order violations and save CPU power.",
