    "id": "int-os-013",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Compare Mutex, Counting Semaphore, and Spinlock. When is a Spinlock faster than a Mutex?",
    "answer": "1. Mutex (Mutual Exclusion Lock):\n   - Ownership: Owned by the single thread that locked it; only that thread can unlock it.\n   - Behavior: If locked, waiting threads are put to sleep by the OS scheduler (`futex` in Linux) and context-switched out, freeing the CPU core for other tasks.\n\n2. Counting Semaphore:\n   - Ownership: Has NO owner concept. Any thread can post (`sem_post`) to increment count.\n   - Behavior: Initialized with an integer count $N$. Allows up to $N$ threads to access a resource pool simultaneously. Useful for signaling between threads.\n\n3. Spinlock:\n   - Behavior: Waiting threads DO NOT sleep; they spin in an intensive busy-wait CPU loop (`while (locked) {}`), polling the lock continuously.\n\nWhen a Spinlock is FASTER than a Mutex:\n- Condition 1: Multi-core system (on a single-core system, a spinlock is suicidal because the spinning thread blocks the thread holding the lock from running!).\n- Condition 2: The critical section is exceptionally short (e.g., updating a counter or pointer, taking < 1 microsecond).\n- Why: A Mutex sleep incurs a full OS context switch (~5-10 microseconds direct/indirect cost). If the lock is released in 50 nanoseconds, spinning is 100x faster than putting the thread to sleep!\n- Condition 3: Linux Kernel Interrupt Context: Interrupt handlers CANNOT sleep; spinlocks are mandatory.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Mutex: Has an owner thread; puts waiting threads to sleep via OS context switch.",
      "Semaphore: Integer counter without ownership; used for resource pools and thread signaling.",
      "Spinlock: Busy-waits on CPU; fastest for short critical sections on multi-core machines and interrupt handlers."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```c\n// Linux Futex (Fast Userspace Mutex) Hybrid Optimization:\n// Modern Mutexes in glibc do NOT sleep immediately:\n// 1. They spin for a few hundred cycles in user-space hoping the lock frees fast (Spinlock phase).\n// 2. If still locked, they execute a futex() system call to put thread to sleep (Mutex phase).\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Never use spinlocks in user-space code on virtualized cloud VMs (AWS EC2): thread preemption by the hypervisor causes catastrophic 'Lock Holder Preemption' performance collapse.",
