    "id": "int-os-014",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Why must `pthread_cond_wait()` ALWAYS be executed inside a `while` loop instead of an `if` statement? What are Spurious Wakeups?",
    "answer": "A Condition Variable allows threads to suspend execution and wait until another thread signals that a shared predicate is satisfied. It is always paired with a Mutex.\n\nWhy `pthread_cond_wait` requires a `while` loop:\nDevelopers must write:\n`while (!condition) pthread_cond_wait(&cv, &mutex);`\nand NEVER:\n`if (!condition) pthread_cond_wait(&cv, &mutex);`\n\nReasons:\n1. The Mesa Monitor / Signal-and-Continue Semantics:\n   - When Thread A calls `signal()`, the waiting Thread B is moved to the ready queue. Before Thread B actually acquires the mutex and runs, another Thread C can swoop in, acquire the lock, consume the resource, and exit! When Thread B wakes up, the condition is false again!\n2. Spurious Wakeups (Hardware/Kernel Reality):\n   - In multi-processor operating systems, a thread waiting on a condition variable can wake up even though NO thread ever signaled it! This is permitted by the POSIX standard to simplify low-level kernel interrupt and context-switch implementations.\n3. Broadcasts (`cond_broadcast`):\n   - If 10 threads wait on a buffer and 1 item arrives, `broadcast` wakes all 10. Only the first thread gets the item; the other 9 wake up to an empty buffer!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Always enclose `pthread_cond_wait()` in a `while` loop, never an `if` statement.",
      "Spurious wakeups: OS threads can awaken without any explicit signal being fired.",
      "Mesa semantics: Signaling wakes a thread, but does not guarantee the condition remains true when the thread actually runs."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```c\npthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\npthread_cond_t cv = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;\nint items = 0;\n\nvoid consume() {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);\n    // MANDATORY WHILE LOOP:\n    while (items == 0) {\n        // Releases lock and puts thread to sleep atomically;\n        // Re-acquires lock upon waking up!\n        pthread_cond_wait(&cv, &lock);\n    }\n    items--;\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Java's `Object.wait()` documentation explicitly mandates the identical pattern: `while (!condition) wait();` to safeguard against spurious wakeups.",
