    "id": "int-os-018",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Compare FCFS, SJF, and SRTF CPU scheduling algorithms. Walk through calculating average waiting time and describe the Convoy Effect.",
    "answer": "1. First-Come, First-Served (FCFS):\n   - Non-preemptive. Jobs executed in arrival order.\n   - The Convoy Effect: A massive CPU-heavy process (e.g., burst = 100ms) runs first, forcing dozens of short I/O-bound processes (burst = 2ms) to sit idle in the ready queue, dropping I/O and CPU utilization.\n\n2. Shortest Job First (SJF):\n   - Non-preemptive. Schedules the process with the shortest CPU burst next.\n   - Provably Optimal: Guarantees the minimum average waiting time among all non-preemptive algorithms.\n   - Flaw: Requires future knowledge of burst times (must be predicted using exponential smoothing: $\\tau_{n+1} = \\alpha t_n + (1-\\alpha)\\tau_n$). Risk of starvation for long processes.\n\n3. Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF):\n   - Preemptive version of SJF.\n   - If a new process arrives with a remaining CPU burst shorter than the currently running process, the running process is preempted immediately.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "FCFS suffers from the Convoy Effect where short processes queue behind long ones.",
      "SJF is provably optimal for minimizing average waiting time.",
      "SRTF is preemptive SJF; preempts running task if a shorter remaining burst arrives."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nGantt Chart Example:\nProcesses arrive at t=0: P1 (Burst=24), P2 (Burst=3), P3 (Burst=3)\nFCFS:\n[      P1 (24ms)      ][ P2 (3ms) ][ P3 (3ms) ]\nWaiting Times: P1=0, P2=24, P3=27 -> Average Waiting Time = (0+24+27)/3 = 17ms (Convoy!)\n\nSJF:\n[ P2 (3ms) ][ P3 (3ms) ][      P1 (24ms)      ]\nWaiting Times: P2=0, P3=3, P1=6 -> Average Waiting Time = (0+3+6)/3 = 3ms (Optimal!)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Exponential Smoothing Formula for burst prediction: $\\tau_{n+1} = \\alpha t_n + (1-\\alpha)\\tau_n$, where $\\alpha$ weights recent history vs past estimates.",
