    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "How does Round Robin (RR) CPU scheduling work? What happens when Time Quantum $q$ is too small versus too large?",
    "answer": "Round Robin (RR) is designed specifically for time-sharing systems:\n\nMechanics:\n- Preemptive algorithm.\n- The ready queue is treated as a circular FIFO queue.\n- The CPU scheduler iterates through the queue, allocating each process a fixed time slice called a Time Quantum ($q$, typically 10ms - 100ms).\n- If a process executes for $q$ without finishing, a timer interrupt fires, preempting the process and placing it at the tail of the ready queue.\n\nImpact of Quantum Size ($q$):\n1. Quantum $q \\to \\infty$ (Extremely Large):\n   - RR degenerates into FCFS! Short processes suffer high response times (Convoy Effect).\n2. Quantum $q \\to 0$ (Extremely Small - 'Processor Sharing'):\n   - Pure context-switching collapse! If $q = 1\\text{ms}$ and context switch takes $0.5\\text{ms}$, 33% of total CPU time is wasted on context-switch overhead rather than executing user code!\n\nRule of Thumb: Set Time Quantum such that 80% of CPU bursts are shorter than $q$, bounding context-switch overhead to $< 1\\%$ of CPU time.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Round Robin provides fair CPU time-sharing and low response times for interactive apps.",
      "If quantum is too large, it degenerates into FCFS.",
      "If quantum is too small, context-switch overhead devastates CPU throughput.",
      "Thumb rule: 80% of CPU bursts should complete within a single time slice."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nQuantum Size Trade-off:\nQuantum q = 12ms (Normal):\nProcess (Burst 10ms) -> [ Runs 10ms ] -> Finishes! (0 Context switch)\n\nQuantum q = 1ms (Too Small):\nProcess (Burst 10ms) -> [1ms][CS][1ms][CS][1ms][CS]... -> 10 Context Switches!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Unlike SJF, Round Robin is starvation-free: every process is guaranteed a time slice every $(n-1) \\times q$ time units.",
