    "id": "int-os-020",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the Priority Inversion problem? Walk through how it nearly doomed the Mars Pathfinder mission and how Priority Inheritance fixes it.",
    "answer": "Priority Inversion occurs when a lower-priority process indirectly preempts or delays a higher-priority process, violating scheduling priority rules.\n\nThe Scenario (3 Tasks: High $H$, Medium $M$, Low $L$):\n1. Low priority task $L$ acquires a shared mutex lock $R$.\n2. High priority task $H$ preempts $L$ and requests lock $R$. Because $R$ is held by $L$, $H$ blocks!\n3. Medium priority task $M$ (which does NOT need lock $R$) wakes up. Because $M$ has higher priority than $L$, the scheduler preempts $L$ to run $M$!\n4. Disaster: $L$ cannot finish its critical section to release lock $R$ because $M$ is running. And $H$ cannot run because it is waiting for $L$!\n5. Result: Medium task $M$ indirectly blocks High task $H$ indefinitely! Priority is completely inverted!\n\nMars Pathfinder Mission Incident (1997):\nOn Mars, the high-priority attitude control thread blocked on an information bus mutex held by a low-priority meteorological task. Medium-priority communications tasks ran continuously, starving the low-priority task. The watchdog timer triggered repeated system resets on Mars!\n\nSolution: Priority Inheritance Protocol (PIP):\nWhenever a high-priority task $H$ blocks on a lock held by a lower-priority task $L$, task $L$ temporarily INHERITS the high priority of $H$! This prevents medium task $M$ from preempting $L$. As soon as $L$ releases the lock, its priority drops back to normal, and $H$ immediately runs.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Priority Inversion: Medium-priority tasks preempt a low-priority task holding a lock needed by a high-priority task.",
      "Causes high-priority tasks to be indefinitely starved by medium tasks.",
      "Priority Inheritance Protocol temporarily elevates the lock-holder's priority to match the highest waiting task."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nPriority Inversion Sequence:\nTime ->\nTask H:                 [ Requests Lock -> BLOCKED! ] ................ [ Runs ]\nTask M:                       [ Preempts L and runs indefinitely! ]\nTask L: [ Holds Lock ] -------+ (Starved by M, cannot release lock!) -+\n                                                                       ^ Priority Inheritance needed!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In Linux, POSIX mutexes support priority inheritance via `pthread_mutexattr_setprotocol(&attr, PTHREAD_PRIO_INHERIT)`.",
