    "id": "int-os-021",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "How does the Multi-Level Feedback Queue (MLFQ) scheduler work? State its 5 rules and explain how Aging prevents starvation.",
    "answer": "The Multi-Level Feedback Queue (MLFQ) is the classic scheduling algorithm designed to optimize both Turnaround Time (for long batch jobs) and Response Time (for interactive I/O jobs) without requiring prior knowledge of CPU burst times.\n\nThe 5 Canonical Rules of MLFQ (Remzi Arpaci-Dusseau):\n1. Rule 1: If Priority(A) > Priority(B), A runs (B doesn't).\n2. Rule 2: If Priority(A) == Priority(B), A & B run in Round Robin (RR) using the time slice of that priority queue.\n3. Rule 3: When a job enters the system, it is placed at the topmost (highest priority) queue.\n4. Rule 4: Once a job uses up its time allotment at a given priority level (regardless of how many times it yielded CPU for I/O), its priority is reduced (it drops down one queue level).\n5. Rule 5 (Priority Boost / Aging): After some time period $S$, move all jobs in the system to the topmost queue!\n\nWhy Rule 5 (Aging) is Mandatory:\n- Prevents Starvation: Without periodic priority boosting, a continuous stream of interactive jobs would starve CPU-heavy batch jobs indefinitely.\n- Handles Behavior Changes: A CPU-heavy batch job that transitions to an interactive phase gets a fresh chance at the top queue.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "MLFQ dynamically learns process behavior: interactive tasks stay at top, CPU-hogs demote to bottom.",
      "Top queues have short time quantums; bottom queues have long time quantums.",
      "Priority Boost (Aging) resets all tasks to the top queue periodically to eliminate starvation."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nMLFQ Queue Hierarchy:\n[Queue 2 (High Priority, Time Slice = 10ms)]  ---> Interactive I/O tasks run here!\n      | (Demoted if CPU-heavy)\n      v\n[Queue 1 (Medium Priority, Time Slice = 20ms)]\n      | (Demoted if CPU-heavy)\n      v\n[Queue 0 (Low Priority, Time Slice = 40ms / FCFS)] ---> Long batch compute jobs run here!\n      ^\n      +================= Rule 5: Priority Boost resets all to Queue 2! =================+\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Modern operating systems (Windows scheduler, macOS) use variants of MLFQ to keep UI input responsive while background compilation or video encoding runs smoothly.",
