    "id": "int-os-022",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Explain the Linux Completely Fair Scheduler (CFS). How does it use Virtual Runtime (`vruntime`) and Red-Black Trees?",
    "answer": "The Completely Fair Scheduler (CFS), developed by Ingo Molnar, is the default CPU scheduler for normal tasks in the Linux kernel (`SCHED_NORMAL`).\n\nCore Principles of CFS:\n1. Virtual Runtime (`vruntime`):\n   - Tracks the exact amount of CPU time a task has executed, normalized by its nice priority value (-20 to +19).\n   - Formula: $\\Delta vruntime = \\Delta exec\\_time \\times \\frac{\\text{NICE\\_0\\_LOAD}}{\\text{task\\_weight}}$.\n   - A task with a lower nice value (higher priority) has a higher weight, so its `vruntime` advances much more slowly, granting it proportionally more physical CPU time!\n2. The Red-Black Tree ($O(\\log N)$ / $O(1)$):\n   - Runnable tasks are stored in a time-ordered Red-Black self-balancing binary search tree, keyed by `vruntime`.\n   - Leftmost Node: The task with the smallest `vruntime` (the most starved task) sits at the leftmost leaf (`rb_leftmost`).\n   - Picking Next Task ($O(1)$): The scheduler picks the cached leftmost node.\n   - Re-insertion ($O(\\log N)$): After running, the task's `vruntime` increases, and it is re-inserted into the Red-Black tree.\n3. Latency Target (`sched_latency`):\n   - CFS guarantees all active tasks get a turn within a target latency window (e.g., 6ms), dividing the window proportionally based on weights.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "CFS models an 'ideal multi-tasking CPU' on real hardware.",
      "`vruntime` tracks normalized execution time scaled by nice priority weights.",
      "Red-black tree keys tasks by `vruntime`; leftmost node is always selected next in $O(1)$."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```c\n// In Linux kernel (<kernel/sched/fair.c>):\n// Updating vruntime when task runs:\nstatic void update_curr(struct cfs_rq *cfs_rq) {\n    struct sched_entity *curr = cfs_rq->curr;\n    u64 now = rq_clock_task(rq_of(cfs_rq));\n    u64 delta_exec = now - curr->exec_start;\n\n    // Scale delta execution by task priority weight:\n    curr->vruntime += calc_delta_fair(delta_exec, curr);\n    // Rebalance in Red-Black tree:\n    __enqueue_entity(cfs_rq, curr);\n}\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "The nice value range in Linux is -20 (highest priority, weight 88761) to +19 (lowest priority, weight 15). Nice 0 has weight 1024.",
