    "id": "int-os-023",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Compare Rate Monotonic (RM) and Earliest Deadline First (EDF) Real-Time CPU Scheduling. State the Liu and Layland utilization bounds.",
    "answer": "Real-time operating systems (RTOS) must guarantee tasks meet strict deadlines:\n\n1. Rate Monotonic (RM) Scheduling:\n   - Type: Static-priority preemptive scheduling.\n   - Rule: Shorter period ($T$) = Higher static priority (tasks that execute at a higher rate/frequency get higher priority).\n   - Liu & Layland Utilization Bound Theorem:\n     A set of $n$ independent periodic tasks is guaranteed schedulable under RM if total CPU utilization satisfies:\n     $U = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{C_i}{T_i} \\le n(2^{1/n} - 1)$\n     - For $n = 1$: $U \\le 100\\%$\n     - For $n = 2$: $U \\le 82.8\\%$\n     - For $n \\to \\infty$: $U \\le \\ln(2) \\approx 69.3\\%$.\n\n2. Earliest Deadline First (EDF) Scheduling:\n   - Type: Dynamic-priority preemptive scheduling.\n   - Rule: The task whose absolute deadline is closest to the current time gets the highest priority dynamically.\n   - Optimality Bound: Schedulable if and only if total utilization $U = \\sum \\frac{C_i}{T_i} \\le 1.0$ (100% CPU utilization achievable!).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Rate Monotonic: Static priority based on task period (shorter period = higher priority).",
      "RM utilization bound approaches $69.3\\%$ ($\\ln 2$) as $n \\to \\infty$.",
      "Earliest Deadline First: Dynamic priority based on closest deadline; achieves up to $100\\%$ CPU utilization."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nReal-Time Schedulability Comparison:\nAlgorithm        | Priority Assignment | Max Theoretical Utilization | Schedulability Test\n-----------------+---------------------+-----------------------------+--------------------\nRate Monotonic   | Static (1 / Period) | ~69.3% (Liu & Layland)      | Sufficient (not necessary)\nEarliest Deadline| Dynamic (Deadline)  | 100%                        | Necessary & Sufficient\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Automotive and avionics systems often prefer RM over EDF despite lower utilization because static priorities are predictable and easily bounded during transient CPU overloads.",
