    "id": "int-os-027",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Explain Dijkstra's Banker's Algorithm for Deadlock Avoidance. Distinguish between Safe State, Unsafe State, and Deadlock.",
    "answer": "The Banker's Algorithm (developed by Edsger Dijkstra) avoids deadlocks dynamically by simulating resource allocation before granting requests, ensuring the system never enters an Unsafe State.\n\nState Definitions:\n1. Safe State:\n   - A state is Safe if there exists at least one Safe Sequence of processes $\\langle P_1, P_2, ..., P_n \\rangle$ such that for each $P_i$, the resources that $P_i$ can still request can be satisfied by currently available resources plus resources held by all preceding processes $P_j$ ($j < i$).\n   - In a Safe State, the system can guarantee that all processes will eventually finish without deadlock, even in the worst-case scenario where every process suddenly demands its maximum declared needs!\n\n2. Unsafe State:\n   - A state where NO safe sequence exists.\n   - An unsafe state is NOT a deadlock! Rather, an unsafe state is a state that MAY lead to a deadlock if processes demand their maximum resources.\n\n3. Deadlock State:\n   - A subset of the Unsafe state where processes are actually deadlocked in circular wait.\n\nRelationship: $\\text{Deadlock} \\subset \\text{Unsafe State} \\subset \\text{All States}$. Safe State $\\cap$ Deadlock = $\\emptyset$.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Safe State: Guaranteed safe execution sequence exists avoiding deadlock even in worst-case requests.",
      "Unsafe State: No safe sequence exists; deadlock is possible (Unsafe $\\ne$ Deadlock).",
      "Banker's rule: Grant a request IF AND ONLY IF the resulting state remains Safe."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nState Space Venn Diagram:\n+------------------------------------+\n| All Possible States                |\n|  +-------------------------------+ |\n|  | Unsafe States                 | |\n|  |  +--------------------------+ | |\n|  |  | Deadlock States          | | |\n|  |  +--------------------------+ | |\n|  +-------------------------------+ |\n|  +-------------------------------+ |\n|  | Safe States (Deadlock-Free!)  | |\n|  +-------------------------------+ |\n+------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Banker's algorithm requires processes to declare their MAXIMUM resource claims upfront, which is why it is rarely used in general-purpose OS kernels where dynamic allocation needs cannot be known in advance.",
