    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "How does Deadlock Detection and Recovery work? Compare Process Termination vs Resource Preemption.",
    "answer": "Instead of restricting resource requests upfront, Deadlock Detection allows processes to request resources freely, periodically running an algorithm to detect cycles, followed by recovery:\n\n1. Detection Algorithm:\n   - For single-instance resources: Maintain a Wait-For Graph (WFG). Periodically run cycle detection ($O(V + E)$).\n   - For multi-instance resources: Run an algorithm analogous to Banker's safety algorithm to verify whether all processes can finish.\n\n2. Deadlock Recovery Strategies:\n   - Strategy A: Process Termination:\n     - Option 1: Abort all deadlocked processes (brutal, wastes substantial computation).\n     - Option 2: Abort one process at a time until the deadlock cycle is broken (requires re-running detection after each abort).\n   - Strategy B: Resource Preemption:\n     - Victim Selection: Pick the process that minimizes cost (fewest resources held, shortest elapsed time).\n     - Rollback: Roll back the victim process to a safe checkpoint (using application checkpoints).\n     - Starvation Prevention: An unfortunate process might repeatedly be chosen as victim; ensure a counter increments its priority after each abort so it is not picked indefinitely.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Deadlock detection checks Wait-For Graphs periodically rather than gating requests.",
      "Recovery via process termination: Kill all or kill one-by-one until cycle breaks.",
      "Recovery via resource preemption: Pick victim based on cost, rollback to checkpoint, guard against starvation."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```sql\n-- In MySQL InnoDB: Automatic Deadlock Detection and Victim Selection\n-- When InnoDB detects a cycle in transaction locks:\n-- It picks the transaction with the SMALLEST undo log (least modified rows) as victim!\n-- The victim receives: ERROR 1213 (40001): Deadlock found when trying to get lock; try restarting transaction\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In PostgreSQL, deadlock detection is deferred: `deadlock_timeout` (default 1s) waits before waking up the expensive cycle-detection algorithm to avoid wasting CPU on transient lock waits.",
