    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the difference between Logical (Virtual) and Physical Address Space? How does the Memory Management Unit (MMU) translate addresses?",
    "answer": "1. Logical (Virtual) Address:\n   - An address generated by the CPU during program execution (e.g., in user pointers).\n   - Every process sees an identical, contiguous, private address space starting at `0x00000000` up to `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` (on 64-bit OS), completely oblivious to other processes.\n\n2. Physical Address:\n   - The actual physical memory address on the hardware RAM chip (DIMM).\n\n3. Memory Management Unit (MMU) Hardware Translation:\n   - The MMU is a specialized hardware component inside the CPU.\n   - Every time the CPU issues a memory read/write instruction, the MMU intercepts the virtual address and translates it to a physical RAM address before asserting the hardware address bus.\n   - Relocation / Base Register (Simple system): $\\text{Physical Address} = \\text{Virtual Address} + \\text{Base Register}$.\n   - Limit Register: Checks if $\\text{Virtual Address} < \\text{Limit}$. If greater, MMU fires a hardware trap (Segmentation Fault)!\n   - Modern Paging MMU: Traverses hierarchical page tables to map virtual pages to physical frames.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Virtual address is generated by the CPU; physical address points to hardware RAM chips.",
      "MMU hardware intercepts every memory access to translate virtual -> physical addresses.",
      "Provides absolute process isolation and memory protection via Base/Limit checks or page flags."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nMMU Hardware Translation Pipeline:\nCPU --- (Virtual Address) ---> [ MMU ] --- (Physical Address) ---> Physical RAM\n                                  |\n                   If Virtual >= Limit Register\n                                  v\n                   [ Trap: Segmentation Fault! ]\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Virtual memory allows a process to allocate 100GB of memory on a machine with only 8GB of physical RAM, utilizing disk paging to back the unallocated frames.",
