    "title": "Distinguish between Internal Fragmentation and External Fragmentation. How does Compaction address external fragmentation?",
    "answer": "1. Internal Fragmentation:\n   - Occurs when memory is allocated in fixed-size blocks (e.g., 4KB pages in Paging, or fixed block sizes in Slab allocators).\n   - If a process requests 5,000 bytes, the system allocates two 4,096-byte pages (8,192 bytes total).\n   - The leftover $8192 - 5000 = 3192$ bytes inside the second page cannot be used by any other process and are completely wasted!\n   - Definition: Unused memory that resides INSIDE an allocated partition.\n\n2. External Fragmentation:\n   - Occurs in variable-sized contiguous memory allocation.\n   - As processes are allocated, terminated, and freed, memory is broken into a patchwork of small non-contiguous holes.\n   - Total free memory across all holes might be 100MB, but if a new process requests a contiguous block of 50MB and no single hole is $\\ge 50\\text{MB}$, the allocation fails!\n   - Definition: Free memory exists outside allocated partitions, but cannot be used because it is fragmented.\n\nCompaction (Fix for External Fragmentation):\n- Moves all allocated memory blocks to one side of RAM, combining all scattered holes into one single contiguous free block.\n- Prerequisite: Only possible if memory relocation is dynamic at runtime (using base registers or paging). Cannot be done if programs use raw physical pointers!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Internal fragmentation: Wasted space INSIDE allocated fixed-size blocks (e.g., 4KB page padding).",
      "External fragmentation: Free space scattered in non-contiguous chunks OUTSIDE allocations.",
      "Paging completely eliminates external fragmentation, but suffers from minor internal fragmentation on the final page."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nFragmentation Visualized:\nInternal Fragmentation:\n[ Allocated: 5000 bytes | WASTED: 3192 bytes ] <-- 8KB Page boundary\n\nExternal Fragmentation:\n[ Allocated: 20MB ][ Free: 10MB ][ Allocated: 30MB ][ Free: 15MB ][ Free: 25MB ]\nTotal Free = 50MB, but cannot fit a contiguous 35MB process!\n```"
    },
