    "id": "int-os-033",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Compare First-Fit, Best-Fit, and Worst-Fit contiguous memory allocation strategies. Why is Worst-Fit counterintuitively useful?",
    "answer": "When a process requests $N$ bytes of memory from a list of variable-sized free holes:\n\n1. First-Fit:\n   - Algorithm: Scans the free list from the beginning and allocates the FIRST hole that is big enough (size $\\ge N$).\n   - Performance: Fastest allocation time! Excellent overall memory utilization in practice.\n\n2. Best-Fit:\n   - Algorithm: Scans the ENTIRE free list and allocates the SMALLEST hole that is big enough (minimizing leftover hole size: $\\min(\\text{Hole} - N)$).\n   - Flaw: Slowest (scans entire list unless sorted) and produces tiny, useless slivers of leftover memory (< 64 bytes) that can never fit any future process, exacerbating severe external fragmentation.\n\n3. Worst-Fit:\n   - Algorithm: Scans the ENTIRE free list and allocates the LARGEST available hole (maximizing leftover hole size).\n   - Counterintuitive Advantage: Leaves the largest possible leftover hole! The leftover fragment is often large enough to easily accommodate another incoming process, avoiding tiny useless slivers.\n   - Flaw: Rapidly destroys large contiguous blocks needed by large processes.\n\nEmpirical Research: First-Fit and Best-Fit are significantly better than Worst-Fit in speed and storage utilization; First-Fit is generally fastest.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "First-Fit: Allocates first matching hole; fastest execution.",
      "Best-Fit: Allocates closest matching hole; produces tiny unusable memory slivers.",
      "Worst-Fit: Allocates largest hole; leaves usable leftover fragments but destroys large blocks."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nAllocation Example: Holes = [100K, 500K, 200K, 300K, 600K]. Request = 212K.\n- First-Fit:  Allocates in 500K hole (first hole >= 212K). Leftover = 288K.\n- Best-Fit:   Allocates in 300K hole (smallest hole >= 212K). Leftover = 88K.\n- Worst-Fit:  Allocates in 600K hole (largest hole). Leftover = 388K.\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Fifty-Percent Rule: Statistical analysis by Knuth shows that for every $N$ allocated blocks, approximately $0.5 N$ blocks are lost to external fragmentation in First-Fit.",
