    "id": "int-os-035",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB)? Calculate Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with 95% hit ratio, 10ns TLB access, and 100ns RAM access.",
    "answer": "The Problem with Pure Paging:\nEvery memory access requires TWO physical RAM accesses:\n1. Access RAM to read the Page Table entry to get the frame number.\n2. Access RAM to read the actual data instruction.\nThis cuts memory access speed in half!\n\nThe TLB Solution:\nThe Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) is an ultra-fast associative on-chip hardware cache built directly into the CPU MMU. It caches recently translated `(Page Number -> Frame Number)` pairs.\n- TLB Hit: MMU retrieves frame number in ~1 nanosecond directly from TLB, requiring only 1 RAM access.\n- TLB Miss: MMU must walk the page table in RAM (or multi-level page tables), then loads the entry into the TLB.\n\nEffective Memory Access Time (EMAT) Calculation:\nFormula:\n$\\text{EMAT} = \\text{Hit Ratio} \\times (T_{TLB} + T_{RAM}) + (1 - \\text{Hit Ratio}) \\times (T_{TLB} + 2 \\times T_{RAM})$\n\nGiven:\n- Hit Ratio ($\u0007lpha$) = $0.95$\n- TLB Access Time ($T_{TLB}$) = $10\\text{ns}$\n- Main Memory Access Time ($T_{RAM}$) = $100\\text{ns}$\n\nCalculation:\n- Time on TLB Hit = $10\\text{ns} + 100\\text{ns} = 110\\text{ns}$\n- Time on TLB Miss = $10\\text{ns} + 100\\text{ns} (\\text{page table}) + 100\\text{ns} (\\text{data}) = 210\\text{ns}$\n- $\\text{EMAT} = (0.95 \\times 110) + (0.05 \\times 210)$\n- $\\text{EMAT} = 104.5 + 10.5 = 115\\text{ns}$\n\nConclusion: A 95% TLB hit ratio keeps effective access time to 115ns, compared to 200ns without TLB (74% faster!).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "TLB is an on-chip associative cache mapping virtual page numbers to physical frame numbers.",
      "Eliminates the extra memory lookup overhead of page table walking.",
      "EMAT formula: $\\alpha(T_{TLB} + T_{RAM}) + (1-\\alpha)(T_{TLB} + 2T_{RAM})$.",
      "Multi-level page tables increase TLB miss penalty (up to 4 RAM lookups on miss!)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nEMAT Calculation Summary:\n- On Hit (95%):  TLB (10ns) + RAM Data (100ns) = 110ns\n- On Miss (5%):  TLB (10ns) + Page Table (100ns) + RAM Data (100ns) = 210ns\nEMAT = (0.95 * 110) + (0.05 * 210) = 115 ns\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "On x86-64 with 4-level paging, a TLB miss requires FOUR memory accesses to walk the page table before reading the data, making TLB hit rates (>99%) critical for CPU performance.",
