    "id": "int-os-036",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Why is Multi-Level Paging mandatory in 32-bit and 64-bit systems? Calculate the size of a single-level page table for a 64-bit architecture.",
    "answer": "Why Single-Level Page Tables Fail Catastrophically:\n\nIn a 32-bit system with 4KB pages:\n- Number of pages = $2^{20} = 1,048,576$ pages.\n- Each page table entry (PTE) is 4 bytes.\n- Size of page table = $1,048,576 \\times 4\\text{ bytes} = 4\\text{ MB}$ PER PROCESS.\n- If 100 processes run, 400MB of RAM is wasted just storing page tables, and each 4MB page table must be contiguous in physical memory!\n\nIn a 64-bit system (The Mathematical Nightmare):\n- 64-bit address space, 4KB ($2^{12}$) pages.\n- Virtual pages = $2^{64 - 12} = 2^{52}$ pages.\n- If each PTE is 8 bytes, a single-level page table would require:\n  $2^{52} \\times 8\\text{ bytes} = 2^{55}\\text{ bytes} = 32\\text{ PETA-BYTES}$ for ONE single process! Storing this in RAM is physically impossible.\n\nMulti-Level Paging Solution (Hierarchical Paging):\n- Divides the page table itself into smaller 4KB pages.\n- Sparse Allocation: A process rarely uses all 64 bits of address space (it uses a few MBs for code/data/heap and stack). Multi-level paging allocates page tables ONLY for the address ranges the process actually uses!\n- Unused address ranges have `NULL` pointers in the top-level Page Directory, consuming zero physical RAM!\n- x86-64 uses 4-Level Paging (PGD -> P4D -> PUD -> PMD -> PTE) or 5-Level Paging.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Single-level page tables must be contiguous and require 4MB in 32-bit, 32 Petabytes in 64-bit.",
      "Multi-level paging breaks page tables into 4KB chunks, allocating only for active address regions.",
      "x86-64 uses 4-level (48-bit canonical) or 5-level (57-bit) paging hierarchy."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nx86-64 4-Level Paging Address Split (48-bit Virtual Address):\n[ Sign Ext: 16b | PML4: 9b | PDPT: 9b | PD: 9b | PT: 9b | Offset: 12b ]\nCR3 Register ---> Points to PML4 Table in physical RAM\n                     | (Indexes 9 bits -> 512 entries)\n                     v\n                  PDPT Table ---> PD Table ---> Page Table ---> Physical Frame!\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "The top 16 bits in 64-bit x86 pointers are unused sign-extensions (Canonical Addressing: 48-bit addressing gives 256TB of virtual memory).",
