    "id": "int-os-038",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Describe Demand Paging. Step through the exact 6-step kernel sequence when a Page Fault occurs.",
    "answer": "Demand Paging loads pages into physical memory only on-demand when the executing program actually attempts to access them (Lazy Swapper / Pager).\n\nHardware Support:\nEach Page Table Entry contains a Valid-Invalid Bit (`Present Bit` on x86):\n- `Valid` (1): The page is legal and currently loaded in physical RAM.\n- `Invalid` (0): The page is either illegal (out of bounds) OR valid but currently stored on disk swap space.\n\n6-Step Page Fault Handling Sequence:\n1. Trap: CPU checks internal table; references a page with Valid-Invalid Bit = 0. The MMU raises a hardware Page Fault Exception (Interrupt 14 on x86) and pushes the faulting virtual address into register `CR2`.\n2. Validation: OS kernel catches exception, inspects internal process VMA (Virtual Memory Area) tables to verify if the address is legal. (If illegal access $\\to$ kernel sends `SIGSEGV`).\n3. Find Free Frame: Kernel searches for an empty physical frame in the free frame list. If no frame is free, it invokes a Page Replacement Algorithm (LRU) to select a victim frame and writes it to swap if dirty.\n4. Disk I/O: Kernel schedules disk I/O to read the requested page from swap/backing store into the allocated physical frame.\n5. Update Page Table: Once I/O completes, kernel updates the Page Table entry with the new Frame Number and sets the Valid-Invalid Bit to `1` (Valid).\n6. Instruction Restart: Kernel restores saved CPU registers and executes `iret`, restarting the exact CPU instruction that caused the page fault! (CPU reads the now-resident frame seamlessly!).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Demand paging loads pages into RAM only when accessed.",
      "Page fault exception occurs when CPU accesses an entry with Valid bit = 0.",
      "Kernel validates address, finds free frame (or evicts victim), reads page from disk, and sets Valid bit = 1.",
      "The faulting instruction is restarted transparently."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nPage Fault Handling Flow:\nCPU ---> [ Access Page p ] ---> Page Table (Bit = 0) ---> [ 1. Trap: Page Fault ]\n                                                                 |\n[ 6. Restart Instruction ] <--- [ 5. Update Bit=1 ] <--- [ 2. Validate Address ]\n           ^                                                     |\n           |                                                     v\n[ Physical Memory Frame ] <--- [ 4. Read from Disk ] <--- [ 3. Find Free Frame ]\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Effective access time under demand paging is heavily impacted by disk seek latency: if 1 in 1,000 accesses causes a page fault to spinning disk (8ms), memory slows down by 40,000%!",
