    "id": "int-os-041",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Describe the Clock (Second-Chance) Page Replacement Algorithm and the Enhanced Second-Chance Algorithm using Reference and Dirty bits.",
    "answer": "The Clock (Second-Chance) algorithm approximates LRU using the hardware Reference Bit (R) provided by the MMU:\n\n1. Basic Clock Algorithm:\n   - Data Structure: Circular list of frames with a pointer ('clock hand').\n   - Hardware sets $R = 1$ whenever a page is read or written.\n   - When a page fault occurs and replacement is needed:\n     - Check page at clock hand:\n       - If $R == 1$: Give it a 'second chance'! Set $R = 0$, advance hand to next page.\n       - If $R == 0$: VICTIM FOUND! Select this page for replacement, advance hand, and terminate.\n   - In the worst case, the hand sweeps one full revolution clearing all bits and selects the first page (degenerating into FIFO).\n\n2. Enhanced Second-Chance Algorithm (Macintosh / Linux / Windows):\n   - Considers BOTH the Reference Bit ($R$) and the Dirty / Modified Bit ($M$):\n     - $(R=0, M=0)$: Neither recently used nor modified (BEST candidate: can overwrite immediately with zero disk write!).\n     - $(R=0, M=1)$: Not recently used, but modified (must write to disk before evicting).\n     - $(R=1, M=0)$: Recently used, clean.\n     - $(R=1, M=1)$: Recently used and modified.\n   - The clock hand sweeps up to 4 passes searching for the lowest category, prioritizing clean pages to save disk write I/O!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Clock algorithm uses a circular queue and 1-bit hardware Reference flag.",
      "If Reference Bit is 1, clears to 0 and gives second chance; if 0, evicts page.",
      "Enhanced Second-Chance combines Reference (R) and Dirty (M) bits to avoid writing clean pages to disk."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nEnhanced Second Chance Priority Order:\nPriority 1 (Best):   (R=0, M=0) -> Clean, unused. Evict immediately!\nPriority 2:          (R=0, M=1) -> Dirty, unused. Must flush to disk.\nPriority 3:          (R=1, M=0) -> Clean, recently accessed.\nPriority 4 (Worst):  (R=1, M=1) -> Dirty, recently accessed.\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Prioritizing $(R=0, M=0)$ over $(R=0, M=1)$ cuts page replacement disk I/O in half because un-modified pages require zero writeback to swap.",
