    "id": "int-os-042",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is Thrashing in Virtual Memory? Explain Peter Denning's Working Set Model and how it prevents thrashing.",
    "answer": "Thrashing occurs when a computer's virtual memory subsystem is in a constant state of paging: the CPU spends more time swapping pages in and out of disk than executing instructions!\n\nThe Thrashing Spiral:\n1. Multiprogramming degree increases (OS admits more processes into RAM).\n2. Memory becomes over-committed; processes start faulting for pages.\n3. Paging disk queue saturates. Processes block waiting for disk I/O.\n4. CPU utilization DROPS because all processes are waiting for disk!\n5. The naive OS scheduler sees low CPU utilization, assumes the system is idle, and admits EVEN MORE processes into RAM!\n6. Complete system freeze: CPU utilization collapses to near 0%, disk activity hits 100%!\n\nPeter Denning's Working Set Model (Locality of Reference):\n- Locality Principle: As a process executes, it moves through phases, accessing a small, cohesive set of pages (locality) repeatedly.\n- Working Set Window ($\\Delta$): A fixed parameter representing the last $\\Delta$ page references.\n- Working Set $WSS_i$: The set of unique pages referenced in the most recent $\\Delta$ time units.\n- Total Demand for Frames: $D = \\sum WSS_i$.\n\nThrashing Prevention Rule:\n- If total demand $D > \\text{Total Available Physical Frames}$, thrashing is guaranteed to occur!\n- Action: The OS must suspend/swap out one entire process, freeing all its frames to satisfy the working sets of remaining processes.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Thrashing: CPU spends 99% of time swapping pages between RAM and disk, collapsing throughput.",
      "Locality of reference: Programs access a small subset of pages repeatedly in time/space.",
      "Working Set Model: Allocates each process its minimum working set size ($WSS$).",
      "If total demand $D > Frames$, the OS suspends a process to prevent thrashing."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nThe Thrashing Curve:\nCPU Utilization\n   ^\n   |        Peak Performance\n   |           /\\\n   |          /  \\  <--- System collapses into THRASHING!\n   |         /    \\\n   |        /      \\\n   +--------------------------> Degree of Multiprogramming (Number of processes)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Page Fault Frequency (PFF) is a dynamic alternative to the Working Set Model: if a process's page fault rate exceeds an upper threshold, allocate it more frames; if it drops below a lower threshold, remove frames.",
