    "id": "int-os-044",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "How does the Linux Out-Of-Memory (OOM) Killer work? How does it score processes (`oom_score`) for termination?",
    "answer": "Linux uses 'Memory Overcommit': it permits processes to allocate more virtual memory than physically available RAM + Swap combined, banking on the fact that programs rarely use all allocated memory immediately.\n\nWhen RAM and swap are 100% exhausted, the kernel cannot fulfill page faults. To save the operating system from crashing, the kernel triggers the Out-Of-Memory (OOM) Killer (`mm/oom_kill.c`).\n\nHow the OOM Killer Picks a Victim (`oom_badness()`):\nThe kernel evaluates every running task and assigns it an integer `oom_score` (0 to 1000):\n- Basic Points: Proportional to physical RAM footprint (`total_vm / RSS`). Consuming 50% of RAM gives ~500 points.\n- Root & System Daemons: System-critical processes running as root get minor discounts.\n- `oom_score_adj`: Manual administrative adjustment (-1000 to +1000):\n  - Set to `-1000`: Completely immune from OOM killing (used for SSHD, systemd).\n  - Set to `+1000`: Guaranteed first victim.\n\nThe task with the highest `oom_score` is terminated instantly with `SIGKILL` (cannot be caught or ignored) to free its memory pages to the OS!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Memory overcommit allows allocating more virtual memory than physical RAM + Swap.",
      "OOM Killer activates when memory is exhausted to prevent a kernel crash.",
      "Terminates process with highest `oom_score` (largest RAM consumer).",
      "Protect critical daemons by setting `/proc/[pid]/oom_score_adj = -1000`."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```bash\n# Check current OOM score of a process:\ncat /proc/1054/oom_score\n\n# Protect critical database (e.g., PostgreSQL primary) from OOM Killer:\necho -1000 > /proc/$(pgrep -f postgres)/oom_score_adj\n\n# Sacrifice background batch workers first during memory spikes:\necho 1000 > /proc/$(pgrep -f celery_worker)/oom_score_adj\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In Kubernetes, containers hitting their memory limits are terminated by the OOM Killer with exit code 137 (`128 + 9 (SIGKILL)`).",
