    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Explain the Classic Unix Inode Pointer Structure (Direct, Single Indirect, Double Indirect, Triple Indirect). Calculate maximum file size for 4KB blocks.",
    "answer": "The classical Unix Inode contains 15 block pointers (each pointer is 4 bytes):\n- 12 Direct Pointers\n- 1 Single Indirect Pointer\n- 1 Double Indirect Pointer\n- 1 Triple Indirect Pointer\n\nGiven Parameters:\n- Block Size = $4\\text{KB} = 4096\\text{ bytes}$\n- Pointer Size = $4\\text{ bytes}$\n- Pointers per indirect block = $\\frac{4096}{4} = 1024 = 2^{10}$ pointers.\n\nMaximum File Size Derivation:\n1. Direct Pointers (12 pointers):\n   - $12 \\times 4\\text{KB} = 48\\text{ KB}$. (Provides ultra-fast $O(1)$ disk access for small files!).\n2. Single Indirect Pointer (1 pointer):\n   - Points to a disk block filled with 1,024 direct pointers.\n   - Capacity = $1024 \\times 4\\text{KB} = 4,096\\text{ KB} = 4\\text{ MB}$.\n3. Double Indirect Pointer (1 pointer):\n   - Points to a block containing 1,024 single indirect pointers.\n   - Capacity = $1024 \\times 1024 \\times 4\\text{KB} = 1,048,576 \\times 4\\text{KB} = 4\\text{ GB}$.\n4. Triple Indirect Pointer (1 pointer):\n   - Points to a block containing 1,024 double indirect pointers.\n   - Capacity = $1024^3 \\times 4\\text{KB} = 2^{30} \\times 4\\text{KB} = 4\\text{ TB}$.\n\nTotal Maximum File Size = $48\\text{KB} + 4\\text{MB} + 4\\text{GB} + 4\\text{TB} \\approx 4.004\\text{ TB}$!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "12 Direct pointers: Fast $O(1)$ access for small files under 48KB.",
      "Single, Double, and Triple indirect pointers provide hierarchical capacity scaling.",
      "Calculated maximum file size with 4KB blocks is approximately 4 Terabytes.",
      "Modern ext4 replaces indirect pointers with Extents to improve contiguous I/O."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nUnix Inode Block Architecture:\n[ Inode ]\n |-- Direct Pointers (0 - 11) ---------> Data Blocks (12 x 4KB = 48KB)\n |-- Single Indirect (12) -------------> [ Block of 1024 Pointers ] ---> Data Blocks (4MB)\n |-- Double Indirect (13) -------------> [ 1024 Pointers ] ---> [ 1024 Pointers ] ---> Data (4GB)\n |-- Triple Indirect (14) -------------> [ 1024 ] ---> [ 1024 ] ---> [ 1024 ] ---> Data (4TB)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Ext4 replaced the indirect block scheme with 'Extents' (storing a starting block address and length), reducing metadata overhead for large contiguous files.",
