    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Compare Hard Links and Soft (Symbolic) Links under the hood. What happens when the original file is deleted?",
    "answer": "1. Hard Link:\n   - Mechanics: A Hard Link is simply another directory entry (a new filename) pointing directly to the SAME existing Inode number.\n   - Link Count: Increments the inode's `i_links_count` by 1.\n   - Original File Deletion: If `fileA` is deleted, the data and inode ARE NOT DELETED! The kernel simply decrements `i_links_count`. The file contents remain fully accessible via `fileB` until link count hits 0!\n   - Limitations: Cannot cross filesystem partitions (because inode numbers are unique only within a single partition); cannot link to directories (prevents infinite directory loops).\n\n2. Soft Link (Symbolic Link / Symlink):\n   - Mechanics: A Symlink is a completely independent, brand-new file with its OWN unique Inode number. Its data block contains the text string of the target file's pathname (`\"/var/log/app.log\"`).\n   - Link Count: Does NOT increment the target file's link count.\n   - Original File Deletion: If the original file is deleted, the symlink remains, but becomes a 'Dangling / Broken Link' pointing to a non-existent path.\n   - Capabilities: Can cross filesystem boundaries; can link to directories.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Hard link: Additional directory entry pointing to identical Inode number; increments link count.",
      "Soft link: New file with its own Inode storing target pathname string as data.",
      "Deleting original file: Hard links remain valid; Soft links break into dangling links.",
      "Hard links cannot cross filesystem boundaries; Soft links can."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```bash\n# Demonstrating Hard vs Soft Link:\necho \"Hello OS\" > original.txt\n\n# Create Hard link:\nln original.txt hardlink.txt\n# Create Soft link:\nln -s original.txt symlink.txt\n\n# Inspect inodes:\nls -li original.txt hardlink.txt symlink.txt\n# 1054 -rw-r--r-- 2 user user 9 original.txt  (Inode 1054, Link count = 2)\n# 1054 -rw-r--r-- 2 user user 9 hardlink.txt  (Same Inode 1054!)\n# 1055 lrwxrwxrwx 1 user user 12 symlink.txt  (Unique Inode 1055 -> points to original.txt)\n\nrm original.txt\ncat hardlink.txt # Prints \"Hello OS\" (Data preserved!)\ncat symlink.txt  # ERROR: No such file or directory (Broken link!)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Directories always have a hard link count of at least 2 (`.` points to itself; parent's entry points to it) + 1 for every child subdirectory (`..`).",
