    "id": "int-os-048",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Compare SCAN (Elevator), C-SCAN, LOOK, and C-LOOK disk arm scheduling algorithms.",
    "answer": "Traditional mechanical hard disk drives (HDDs) suffer from Seek Time (moving the physical read/write head arm across disk cylinders):\n\n1. SCAN (Elevator Algorithm):\n   - Behavior: The disk arm starts at one end and moves toward the other end, servicing requests along the way. When it reaches the physical end of the disk (cylinder 0 or max), it reverses direction and services requests on the way back.\n   - Flaw: Unfair to cylinders at the far end: requests near the edges wait longer than requests in the center.\n\n2. C-SCAN (Circular SCAN):\n   - Behavior: Moves in ONE direction only (e.g., inside to outside), servicing requests. Upon reaching the end, it immediately returns to the beginning WITHOUT servicing any requests on the return trip, then starts servicing again.\n   - Benefit: Provides a much more uniform waiting time distribution across all cylinders.\n\n3. LOOK and C-LOOK:\n   - Practical Optimization of SCAN / C-SCAN: The arm looks ahead to the final request in the current direction. It reverses or returns immediately as soon as the last pending request is serviced, WITHOUT traveling all the way to the physical edge of the disk platters!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SCAN: Sweeps back and forth servicing requests to physical disk boundaries.",
      "C-SCAN: Sweeps in one direction only; returns to start without servicing to provide uniform wait times.",
      "LOOK / C-LOOK: Reverses at the final pending request rather than traveling to physical platter edges."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nC-LOOK Trajectory:\nRequests: [14, 37, 65, 67, 98, 122, 124, 183], Head starting at 53 moving Right:\n53 -> 65 -> 67 -> 98 -> 122 -> 124 -> 183 (Reverses at 183 without hitting 199!)\n                                       |\n                   (Jumps back to 14 without servicing)\n                                       v\n                                14 -> 37 (Done!)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "For solid-state drives (SSDs / NVMe), mechanical seek time is 0ms; Linux uses the `none` or `kyber` I/O scheduler to bypass disk arm sorting.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Seagate"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
    "is_hidden": false,
