    "id": "int-os-050",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Compare Programmed I/O, Interrupt-Driven I/O, and Direct Memory Access (DMA). Why is DMA mandatory for high-throughput networking and storage?",
    "answer": "Data transfer between peripheral hardware devices (disks, NICs) and RAM evolved through three architectural paradigms:\n\n1. Programmed I/O (Polling / Busy-Wait):\n   - The CPU manually reads and writes every byte/word from device I/O registers in a tight loop.\n   - CPU polls device status bits continuously.\n   - Catastrophe: 100% CPU time is wasted polling; CPU cannot execute other processes during slow disk I/O.\n\n2. Interrupt-Driven I/O:\n   - CPU initiates the I/O transfer and immediately context-switches to execute other processes.\n   - When device data is ready, the device asserts an interrupt line.\n   - CPU interrupts execution, jumps to the ISR, and copies the byte into RAM.\n   - Flaw: An interrupt fires for EVERY SINGLE BYTE or WORD. At 10Gbps network speeds, millions of interrupts per second overwhelm the CPU with interrupt handling overhead!\n\n3. Direct Memory Access (DMA):\n   - A dedicated DMA Controller chip transfers entire blocks of data directly between the peripheral device and RAM over the system bus WITHOUT CPU INVOLVEMENT!\n   - Execution:\n     1. CPU writes buffer address and byte count to DMA controller, then goes back to executing user code.\n     2. DMA controller streams entire 64KB block directly into RAM.\n     3. DMA controller raises ONE single interrupt when the entire block transfer is complete!\n   - Frees the CPU completely from byte-copying duties.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Programmed I/O: CPU polls device continuously; wastes 100% CPU.",
      "Interrupt-Driven: CPU interrupted on every byte/word; high interrupt overhead at speed.",
      "DMA: Hardware controller streams large blocks directly to RAM; fires 1 interrupt per block."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "```text\nDMA Data Flow:\nCPU ---> [ 1. Setup DMA Transfer ]\n             |\n             v\n[ Device ] ===== 2. Direct Block Transfer over Bus =====> [ RAM Memory ]\n             |\n             +---> 3. Interrupt CPU (Only when transfer complete!)\n```"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Zero-Copy networking (`sendfile()` in Linux) combines DMA with kernel buffer forwarding to stream files from disk directly to network sockets without copying bytes into user-space RAM.",
