    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the Virtual File System (VFS) in Linux, and what are its four primary object types?",
    "answer": "The Virtual File System (VFS) is an abstraction layer in the Linux kernel that provides a uniform interface to user-space applications regardless of the underlying concrete filesystem (Ext4, XFS, Btrfs, NFS, FAT32, procfs, sysfs).\n\nWhen user code calls open(), read(), or write(), it interacts with generic VFS functions. VFS then dispatches these calls to filesystem-specific function pointer tables (operations tables).\n\nVFS defines 4 core object types:\n1. superblock: Represents an entire mounted filesystem. Contains filesystem geometry, block size, max file size, and pointers to filesystem operations (`super_operations`).\n2. inode: Represents a specific metadata entity for a file or directory (file size, permissions, owner, timestamps, block pointers). It is uniquely identified by inode number within a superblock. Crucially, an inode contains NO filename.\n3. dentry (Directory Entry): Represents a path component (directory or filename) linking a human-readable name to an inode. Dentries are cached in RAM in the 'dcache' to make path traversal O(1) instead of traversing disk blocks.\n4. file: Represents an open file instance created when a process invokes open(). It tracks per-process state such as current file offset (f_pos), access modes (O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY), and process-specific file operations.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "VFS abstracts disparate filesystems under a uniform POSIX API using C function pointer tables (operations structs).",
      "The 4 core VFS objects are superblock (mounted FS), inode (metadata), dentry (path name to inode link), and file (open file instance).",
      "Dentries are cached in RAM ('dcache') to accelerate hierarchical path lookups without disk reads.",
      "Multiple dentries (hard links) can point to the same inode, but an open file descriptor points to a struct file."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Linux VFS Object Hierarchy */\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n| User Space: open(\"/var/log/app.log\", O_RDONLY)            |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n                             | (syscall)\n                             v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n| VFS Layer:                                                |\n|   dcache lookup: \"/\" -> \"var\" -> \"log\" -> \"app.log\"       |\n|   dentry links name \"app.log\" -> inode #849201            |\n|   creates struct file { f_pos: 0, f_inode, f_op }         |\n|   returns int fd to process file descriptor table          |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n                             |\n         +-------------------+--------------------+\n         v                                        v\n+-----------------------+              +--------------------+\n| ext4_file_operations  |              | nfs_file_operations|\n+-----------------------+              +--------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer favorite: 'What is the difference between an inode, a dentry, and a struct file?' Remember: Inode is metadata on disk/RAM without name; Dentry is directory path cache mapping name to inode; File is process-specific open state holding current offset!",
