    "id": "int-os-052",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "How does Journaling work in filesystems like Ext4, and what are writeback, ordered, and journal modes?",
    "answer": "Journaling is a fault-tolerance mechanism designed to maintain filesystem consistency after a system crash or sudden power failure without requiring a time-consuming filesystem check (fsck) over millions of blocks.\n\nBefore modifying filesystem structures in their permanent on-disk locations, the filesystem writes the intended changes sequentially to a dedicated circular buffer called the journal. Once committed to the journal, the changes are flushed ('checkpointed') to main filesystem blocks asynchronously.\n\nExt4 provides three journaling modes via the JBD2 (Journaling Block Device) layer:\n1. journal (Full Data & Metadata Journaling): Both file data and filesystem metadata are written to the journal before flushing to final disk locations. Highest crash consistency (no stale data), but slowest performance because all data is written twice.\n2. ordered (Default): Only metadata is written to the journal, but all associated file data blocks are flushed to disk BEFORE the metadata transaction is committed in the journal. Prevents reading stale data after a crash while avoiding double-writing data.\n3. writeback: Only metadata is journaled, and there is NO ordering constraint between data blocks and metadata blocks. Fastest throughput, but after a crash, a file's metadata may point to newly allocated blocks that still contain stale unwritten disk data.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Journaling logs filesystem operations to a circular journal prior to updating in-place structures, enabling rapid post-crash recovery.",
      "data=journal writes both data and metadata to journal; highest reliability, double-write throughput penalty.",
      "data=ordered (Ext4 default) writes data to disk first, then metadata to journal, guaranteeing no stale data exposure.",
      "data=writeback writes metadata to journal without ordering data writes, delivering max performance at the expense of data integrity."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Ext4 Journaling Modes Comparison */\n\n1. data=journal:\n   Write Data -> Write Metadata to Journal -> Commit Journal -> Checkpoint to Disk\n   (Safest, slowest - double write penalty)\n\n2. data=ordered (Default):\n   Write Data to Disk -> Write Metadata to Journal -> Commit Journal -> Checkpoint Metadata\n   (Guarantees no stale data in newly allocated blocks)\n\n3. data=writeback:\n   Write Metadata to Journal & Write Data (parallel/unordered) -> Commit Journal\n   (Highest I/O throughput, risk of file pointing to stale garbage data on crash)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If asked how databases configure journaling: PostgreSQL and MySQL typically run best on filesystems mounted with `data=ordered` or `data=writeback` (with write-ahead logging handles database-level atomicity), avoiding the double-write penalty of `data=journal`.",
