    "id": "int-os-053",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Explain Page Cache vs Buffer Cache in Linux and how dirty page writeback works (sync, fsync, fdatasync).",
    "answer": "Historically, UNIX maintained two separate caches: the Page Cache (caching file content pages indexed by inode and offset) and the Buffer Cache (caching raw disk blocks indexed by device and block number). Since Linux 2.4, these are unified: buffer heads point into pages residing within the unified Page Cache.\n\nWhen a process writes data via write(), the kernel copies bytes into Page Cache pages and marks them 'dirty'. The syscall returns immediately (asynchronous write). Kernel flusher threads (e.g., `wb_workfn` / `kswapd`) periodically write dirty pages to persistent storage when `dirty_expire_centisecs` expires or `dirty_background_ratio` memory limit is reached.\n\nTo ensure durability, processes use synchronization syscalls:\n1. sync(): Flushes all dirty pages (data and metadata) across ALL filesystems to storage. Non-blocking/asynchronous or blocking depending on implementation.\n2. fsync(int fd): Flushes all dirty data pages AND modified metadata (timestamps, file size, permissions) of the specific file descriptor to storage, blocking until the storage controller acknowledges persistence.\n3. fdatasync(int fd): Like fsync, but flushes data pages and ONLY the essential metadata required for subsequent reads to succeed (such as file length expansion). Skips flushing non-essential metadata changes like `st_atime` or `st_mtime`, significantly reducing disk head seek latency on HDDs and SSD controller write wear.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Linux unified the Page Cache and Buffer Cache in kernel 2.4; block buffers point directly to pages in the Page Cache.",
      "Regular write() operations return after writing to Page Cache; dirty background flusher threads commit them to disk asynchronously.",
      "fsync(fd) forces file data and all metadata (including timestamps) to persistent storage, blocking until confirmed.",
      "fdatasync(fd) flushes file data and only critical metadata (size changes), skipping cosmetic metadata like mtime to save I/O cycles."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "#include <unistd.h>\n#include <fcntl.h>\n\nint fd = open(\"wal.log\", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0644);\nwrite(fd, wal_entry, len); // 1. Writes only to OS Page Cache (fast, volatile)\n\n// Option A: Full metadata + data flush\nfsync(fd);     // Forces dirty pages + inode metadata (mtime, ctime) to disk\n\n// Option B: Data-only flush (used by high-perf DBs like SQLite, RocksDB)\nfdatasync(fd); // Writes data + essential metadata (file size). Avoids 1 disk seek!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "High-throughput database engines (PostgreSQL WAL, Kafka, RocksDB) rely heavily on `fdatasync()` because updating file modification timestamps requires an extra disk I/O to the inode table, halving write IOPS if `fsync()` were used instead!",
