    "id": "int-os-054",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is Direct I/O (O_DIRECT) vs Buffered I/O vs Memory-Mapped I/O (mmap)?",
    "answer": "Operating systems provide three fundamental mechanisms for accessing files:\n\n1. Buffered I/O (Standard read/write):\nData traverses through the kernel Page Cache. On read, the OS fetches blocks into kernel page cache and copies them to user-space buffer. On write, data is copied to page cache and flushed asynchronously. Provides automatic OS read-ahead and caching, but incurs CPU copy overhead and double-caching if the application has its own cache.\n\n2. Direct I/O (open with O_DIRECT):\nBypasses the kernel Page Cache entirely. DMA transfers data directly between user-space memory buffers and the storage device controller. Requires user memory buffers, file offsets, and I/O sizes to be strictly aligned to filesystem/device block boundaries (typically 512B or 4KB). Ideal for databases (Oracle, MySQL InnoDB, ScyllaDB) that manage their own sophisticated buffer pool and want to avoid double-caching and OS cache pollution.\n\n3. Memory-Mapped I/O (mmap):\nMaps a file's pages directly into the process virtual address space. Reading and writing is performed via pointer dereferencing. Memory pages are faulted into the Page Cache on-demand via minor/major page faults. Eliminates read()/write() syscall overhead and user-kernel copy overhead (zero-copy between page cache and virtual addresses).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Buffered I/O uses the kernel Page Cache; provides readahead but incurs CPU memory copy costs and double buffering.",
      "Direct I/O (`O_DIRECT`) bypasses Page Cache completely via DMA directly into aligned user buffers; preferred by DBMS buffer pools.",
      "mmap maps file blocks directly into process virtual address space, eliminating copy overhead through demand paging.",
      "mmap page faults can block thread execution unpredictably on slow disk reads, which is why multi-threaded DBs often prefer async direct I/O."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Comparison of File I/O Models */\n\n1. Buffered I/O:\n   Disk <---DMA---> Page Cache (Kernel) <---CPU Copy---> User Buffer\n   (High memory overhead, automatic caching)\n\n2. Direct I/O (O_DIRECT):\n   Disk <--------------DMA Direct Transfer-------------> User Buffer\n   (Bypasses Page Cache, no CPU copy, requires aligned memory)\n\n3. Memory-Mapped I/O (mmap):\n   Disk <---DMA---> Page Cache (Kernel)\n                          ^\n                          | (Virtual Memory page table mapping)\n                     Process VAS\n   (Zero-copy, accessed via pointer: char *ptr = mmap(...);)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Why doesn't every high-performance database use `mmap`? While Kafka uses `sendfile` and LMDB uses `mmap`, heavy write databases like MongoDB WiredTiger and Postgres use buffered or direct I/O because handling `SIGBUS` errors during disk full/I/O failure under `mmap` is notoriously complex!",
