    "id": "int-os-057",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the ABA Problem in lock-free concurrency, and how is it solved using Tagged Pointers / Hazard Pointers?",
    "answer": "The ABA Problem is an insidious concurrency bug that occurs in lock-free data structures relying on Compare-And-Swap (CAS).\n\nScenario:\n1. Thread 1 reads pointer value A at memory location M.\n2. Thread 1 gets preempted before performing CAS(M, A, NewValue).\n3. Thread 2 runs, alters M from A to B, and frees the memory occupied by A.\n4. Thread 2 (or another thread) allocates a new object, and the memory allocator happens to reuse the exact same virtual address A!\n5. Thread 2 writes this new object (also at address A) into M.\n6. Thread 1 resumes and executes CAS(M, A, NewValue). Because the address is still A, the CAS succeeds!\n7. Bug: The contents, internal state, and linked nodes of A have completely changed, corrupting the data structure (e.g., in a lock-free stack, node A's `next` pointer now points to freed memory).\n\nSolutions:\n1. Tagged Pointers (Double-Word CAS - DCAS / `CMPXCHG16B`):\nPair each pointer with a monotonic revision counter/tag (e.g., 64-bit address + 64-bit counter). CAS checks both the pointer AND the tag atomically: CAS(ptr+tag, A+1, NewValue+2). Even if address A is reused, tag 1 != tag 3, so CAS fails.\n2. Hazard Pointers:\nThreads record pointers they are currently reading in a global 'hazard list'. A thread attempting to free a node cannot reclaim it if it is listed in any thread's hazard pointer list.\n3. Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR) / RCU:\nDeferred memory reclamation ensures memory is never freed until all active reader epochs have completed.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "The ABA problem occurs when a memory address changes from A to B and back to A, causing CAS to succeed erroneously.",
      "It primarily affects lock-free algorithms where dynamic memory allocation reuses freed memory addresses.",
      "Solved via Tagged Pointers / Versioned References (`AtomicStampedReference` in Java; `CMPXCHG16B` on x86_64).",
      "Also solved using safe memory reclamation schemes: Hazard Pointers or Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* ABA Problem in Lock-Free Stack (Treiber Stack) */\n// Initial stack: Top -> [A] -> [B] -> [C]\n\n// Thread 1 prepares to pop:\nNode *old_top = Top;      // old_top = A\nNode *next_node = old_top->next; // next_node = B\n// Thread 1 PREEMPTED before CAS(Top, old_top, next_node)...\n\n// Thread 2 pops A and B: Stack: Top -> [C]\n// Memory of A is freed.\n// Thread 2 pushes new node. Allocator returns same address A!\n// Stack: Top -> [A] -> [C]\n\n// Thread 1 resumes:\nCAS(&Top, old_top, next_node); // Checks *Top == A. TRUE!\n// Top is set to B (which was freed/corrupted by Thread 2!). Stack corrupted!\n\n// Fix: Tagged Pointer (Double-word CAS)\nstruct TaggedPointer {\n    Node* ptr;\n    uintptr_t tag; // Monotonically incremented counter\n};\n// CAS compares both ptr and tag atomically."
    },
    "pro_tip": "In Java interviews, point immediately to `java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicStampedReference<V>`. It pairs an object reference with an integer stamp to prevent ABA in lock-free Treiber stacks!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Meta",
      "Bloomberg",
      "Two Sigma"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
