    "id": "int-os-058",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is Read-Copy Update (RCU) in the Linux kernel and how does it achieve lockless reads?",
    "answer": "Read-Copy Update (RCU) is an advanced kernel synchronization mechanism optimized for read-heavy workloads (such as routing tables, process lists, and directory lookups) where reads occur orders of magnitude more frequently than writes.\n\nTraditional reader-writer locks incur atomic cache-line bouncing and lock contention on every single read. RCU completely eliminates lock overhead, atomic operations, and memory barriers for readers, allowing readers to traverse shared data structures concurrently with writers with zero overhead.\n\nHow RCU Works:\n1. Read Phase:\nReaders enter an RCU read-side critical section via `rcu_read_lock()` and exit via `rcu_read_unlock()`. On non-preemptible kernels, these are completely NO-OPs (or simple preempt disable). Readers access data lock-free.\n2. Write (Update) Phase:\nWriters do not modify existing data in-place. Instead, a writer copies the object, applies updates to the private copy, and then atomically updates the global pointer to point to the new copy via `rcu_assign_pointer()`.\n3. Defer Phase (Grace Period & Reclamation):\nThe old object cannot be freed immediately because concurrent readers may still be reading it. The writer waits for a 'Grace Period'—the time required for all CPU cores that entered an RCU critical section prior to the pointer update to complete their read sections.\nOnce every CPU passes through a 'quiescent state' (e.g., context switch, idle loop), the writer reclaims the old object via `synchronize_rcu()` or asynchronously via `call_rcu()`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "RCU provides zero-overhead, lock-free reads by decoupling read access from deferred memory reclamation.",
      "Writers publish modifications via pointer updates on copies; readers always see either the old or new version atomically.",
      "A Grace Period ensures memory is only freed after all readers accessing the old version have exited their critical section.",
      "Quiescent states (context switches, user mode execution) mark when a CPU has completed any ongoing RCU read section."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* RCU Read and Update in Linux Kernel */\n\n// Reader Side (Zero lock overhead! No atomics, no cache bouncing):\nrcu_read_lock();\nstruct node *p = rcu_dereference(global_head);\nif (p != NULL) {\n    access(p->data);\n}\nrcu_read_unlock();\n\n// Writer Side:\nstruct node *new_node = kmalloc(sizeof(*new_node), GFP_KERNEL);\n*new_node = *old_node;          // 1. Copy\nnew_node->data = new_value;     // 2. Modify private copy\nrcu_assign_pointer(global_head, new_node); // 3. Atomic pointer swing\n\nsynchronize_rcu();              // 4. Wait for Grace Period (all active readers done)\nkfree(old_node);                // 5. Safely reclaim old memory"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer trap: 'Can RCU have multiple concurrent writers?' No! RCU synchronizes readers with writers. Multiple concurrent writers still require a standard spinlock or mutex to serialize modifications among themselves!",
