    "id": "int-os-059",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is a Futex (Fast Userspace Mutex) in Linux, and how does it avoid kernel transitions?",
    "answer": "A Futex (Fast Userspace Mutex) is the foundational Linux kernel primitive used by POSIX threads (`pthread_mutex_t`), Java monitors, and Go runtimes to implement blazing-fast locks.\n\nBefore futexes, acquiring any mutex required a system call into the kernel (such as `sys_semop`), which cost hundreds of CPU clock cycles due to user-kernel context switching overhead—even when there was ZERO contention (no other thread was holding or trying to hold the lock).\n\nFutex Architecture:\nA futex consists of two parts:\n1. A 32-bit integer in user-space shared memory.\n2. A kernel-space wait queue table (hash table of wait queues indexed by virtual memory address).\n\nExecution Flow:\n- Uncontended Case (95%+ of executions in healthy apps):\nThe thread uses an atomic Compare-And-Swap (CAS) in user space: `atomic_compare_and_swap(&futex_val, 0, 1)`. If successful, the lock is acquired WITHOUT ANY SYSTEM CALL. Cost: ~5-10 nanoseconds!\n- Contended Case (Collision):\nIf CAS reveals the lock is already held, the calling thread invokes the `futex()` syscall with `FUTEX_WAIT`: the kernel verifies the value hasn't changed (avoiding race conditions) and suspends the thread on the kernel wait queue.\n- Unlock:\nThe releasing thread uses atomic decrement in user space. If it detects other threads were queued (`futex_val > 1`), it makes a `futex()` syscall with `FUTEX_WAKE` to awaken waiting threads.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "A futex combines a user-space atomic integer with a kernel-space wait queue.",
      "Uncontended lock acquisition and release require zero syscalls, completing in nanoseconds via atomic CAS.",
      "Syscall transitions (`FUTEX_WAIT` and `FUTEX_WAKE`) occur strictly when contention is detected.",
      "The kernel checks the futex variable value atomically before putting a thread to sleep to prevent missed wakeups."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Simplified Futex Lock / Unlock Concept */\n#include <linux/futex.h>\n#include <sys/syscall.h>\n#include <stdatomic.h>\n\n_Atomic int futex_word = 0; // 0: unlocked, 1: locked, 2: locked + waiters\n\nvoid futex_lock(_Atomic int *f) {\n    int c = 0;\n    // Fast Path: Try to acquire lock in user-space (Zero Syscalls!)\n    if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(f, &c, 1)) return;\n\n    // Slow Path: Contention detected -> switch to state 2 (waiters exist)\n    while (atomic_exchange(f, 2) != 0) {\n        // Sleep in kernel wait queue until FUTEX_WAKE is called\n        syscall(SYS_futex, f, FUTEX_WAIT, 2, NULL, NULL, 0);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid futex_unlock(_Atomic int *f) {\n    // Fast path: if state was 1 (no waiters), atomic dec to 0 and done!\n    if (atomic_fetch_sub(f, 1) != 1) {\n        *f = 0;\n        // Slow path: awaken 1 waiting thread in kernel\n        syscall(SYS_futex, f, FUTEX_WAKE, 1, NULL, NULL, 0);\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If asked how `pthread_mutex_lock` in glibc works: explain that it is an adaptive mutex built on top of futexes—it spins for a few iterations in user space (hoping the lock holder on another core releases it), and only falls back to `FUTEX_WAIT` syscall if spinning times out!",
