    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Why must a kernel thread never sleep while holding a Spinlock?",
    "answer": "In OS kernel programming, a spinlock is designed for extremely brief, low-overhead critical sections where threads actively busy-wait (spin) on a CPU core until the lock becomes available. Holding a spinlock while sleeping (blocking, allocating page-faulting memory, or waiting for I/O) is a catastrophic bug that leads to immediate deadlock or kernel panic.\n\nReasons Sleeping Inside a Spinlock is Prohibited:\n1. Deadlock via Self-Preemption or Priority Inversion:\nWhen Thread A on Core 0 acquires Spinlock S and then calls `msleep()` or waits on I/O, the scheduler runs Thread B on Core 0. If Thread B attempts to acquire Spinlock S, Thread B begins spinning in an infinite loop on Core 0! Since Thread B consumes 100% of Core 0's CPU cycles, Thread A can NEVER be scheduled back onto Core 0 to finish its work and release Spinlock S. System hangs permanently.\n2. Wastage on Multi-Core Systems:\nIf Thread A sleeps while holding Spinlock S, threads on Cores 1, 2, and 3 attempting to acquire Spinlock S will spin continuously at 100% CPU utilization for the entire duration of the sleep (milliseconds/seconds), wasting millions of CPU cycles.\n3. Interrupt Context Incompatibility:\nSpinlocks are often used in interrupt service routines (ISRs). ISRs cannot sleep under any circumstances because they lack a process context (thread struct / PCB) that can be suspended and resumed by the scheduler.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Spinlocks are designed strictly for brief, non-sleeping in-memory critical sections.",
      "If a thread holding a spinlock sleeps, other threads attempting to acquire it will spin indefinitely, starving the sleeping thread of CPU cycles (Deadlock).",
      "Interrupt handlers cannot sleep because they execute outside of any schedulable process context; spinlocks are their only valid lock primitive.",
      "Use `mutex` or `semaphore` if operations inside the critical section might sleep, do I/O, or allocate memory with `GFP_KERNEL`."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Linux Kernel Spinlock Rule */\n\n// WRONG: DISASTER IN KERNEL\nspin_lock(&my_lock);\ncopy_from_user(kernel_buf, user_buf, size); // Page fault can sleep! DEADLOCK!\nkmalloc(size, GFP_KERNEL);                  // GFP_KERNEL can sleep! DEADLOCK!\nspin_unlock(&my_lock);\n\n// CORRECT: Safe operations inside spinlocks\nspin_lock(&my_lock);\n// Modify in-memory atomic linked list or registers only (strictly non-blocking)\nlist_add(&node->list, &head);\nspin_unlock(&my_lock);\n\n// If sleeping is required, use a Mutex or Semaphore instead!\nmutex_lock(&my_mutex);\nkmalloc(size, GFP_KERNEL); // Legal: mutex allows sleeping and context switches\nmutex_unlock(&my_mutex);"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Linux kernel's `CONFIG_DEBUG_SPINLOCK` and `might_sleep()` macros detect this exact bug during development by emitting a kernel stack trace and panic if any blocking function is called while a spinlock is held!",
