    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the difference between Anonymous Pipes and Named Pipes (FIFOs), and what is PIPE_BUF atomicity?",
    "answer": "Pipes provide unidirectional, byte-stream Inter-Process Communication (IPC) managed entirely in kernel memory buffers.\n\n1. Anonymous Pipes (`pipe(int fd[2])`):\n- Temporary kernel buffers that do not exist in the filesystem hierarchy.\n- Created via the `pipe()` system call returning two file descriptors: `fd[0]` (read end) and `fd[1]` (write end).\n- Can ONLY be used between related processes that share file descriptors across `fork()` (e.g., parent and child, or sibling processes in shell command pipelines: `ps aux | grep java`).\n- Disappears automatically when all open file descriptors referencing both ends are closed.\n\n2. Named Pipes (FIFOs - `mkfifo(path, mode)`):\n- Exists as a special FIFO file node in the filesystem directory tree (visible via `ls -l` with file type `p`).\n- Unrelated processes can communicate simply by opening the FIFO path (`open(\"/tmp/my_fifo\", O_RDONLY)` and `open(\"/tmp/my_fifo\", O_WRONLY)`).\n- Even though it has a filesystem path entry, actual data NEVER touches the physical disk; communication occurs strictly through kernel memory.\n\nPIPE_BUF Atomicity:\nWhen multiple processes write to a pipe simultaneously, writes of size <= `PIPE_BUF` (4096 bytes on Linux; 512 bytes on POSIX minimum) are guaranteed to be atomic. The kernel will never interleave bytes from concurrent writers. Writes exceeding `PIPE_BUF` bytes may be split and interleaved with data from other writers.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Anonymous pipes communicate only between related processes sharing file descriptors via fork(); no filesystem presence.",
      "Named pipes (FIFOs) have a filesystem path entry, allowing any arbitrary unrelated processes to open and communicate through kernel memory.",
      "Pipes are unidirectional byte streams; bidirectional communication requires two independent pipes.",
      "Writes smaller than or equal to `PIPE_BUF` (4KB on Linux) are guaranteed atomic and cannot be interleaved by concurrent writers."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Anonymous Pipe Example in C */\n#include <unistd.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint pipefd[2];\npipe(pipefd); // pipefd[0] = read, pipefd[1] = write\n\nif (fork() == 0) {\n    // Child: Reader\n    close(pipefd[1]); // Close unused write end\n    char buf[128];\n    read(pipefd[0], buf, sizeof(buf));\n    printf(\"Child received: %s\\n\", buf);\n    close(pipefd[0]);\n} else {\n    // Parent: Writer\n    close(pipefd[0]); // Close unused read end\n    char msg[] = \"Atomic IPC message\";\n    write(pipefd[1], msg, sizeof(msg)); // Atomic if <= PIPE_BUF (4096 bytes)\n    close(pipefd[1]);\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'What happens when a process writes to a pipe whose read end has been closed?' The kernel immediately delivers a `SIGPIPE` signal to the writer! If unhandled, the process terminates with exit code 141 (`128 + 13`). Robust servers must set `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN)`!",
