    "id": "int-os-062",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Why are UNIX Domain Sockets significantly faster than TCP loopback (127.0.0.1) for local IPC?",
    "answer": "UNIX Domain Sockets (UDS - `AF_UNIX` / `AF_LOCAL`) and TCP loopback (`127.0.0.1:port`) both provide bidirectional stream or datagram socket communication. However, for IPC on the same machine, UNIX Domain Sockets exhibit up to 2x higher throughput and significantly lower latency than TCP loopback.\n\nKey Architectural Reasons:\n1. Bypassing Network Stack Overhead:\nTCP loopback must execute the entire IP and TCP protocol stacks: computing TCP checksums, generating TCP packet headers, sequence number tracking, sliding window flow control, congestion control algorithms (Slow Start, CUBIC), and TCP ACK packet processing. UNIX domain sockets bypass all protocol logic and copy bytes directly between the sender and receiver kernel socket buffers.\n2. Zero Routing and Packetization:\nTCP loopback packets traverse the Linux `lo` (loopback) interface, requiring routing table lookups, ARP emulation, and packet fragmentation/reassembly. UNIX sockets perform no packetization.\n3. Passing File Descriptors (Ancillary Data):\nUNIX domain sockets have a unique capability impossible over TCP: a process can send an open file descriptor (or socket) to another process across process boundaries using `sendmsg()` with `SCM_RIGHTS`. This allows NGINX or Envoy to pass live client connections to worker processes seamlessly without re-opening or transferring data.\n4. Fine-Grained Filesystem Permissions:\nUDS endpoints appear as filesystem socket files (mode `s`), governed by standard UNIX user/group access permissions, whereas TCP loopback ports can be connected to by ANY local user or process.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "UNIX Domain Sockets avoid TCP/IP protocol overhead: no checksums, ACKs, flow control, or congestion control.",
      "UDS copies data directly between kernel socket buffers, yielding ~2x throughput and much lower latency than 127.0.0.1.",
      "UDS supports passing open file descriptors across process boundaries via `sendmsg` and `SCM_RIGHTS`.",
      "Security: UDS endpoints are secured using standard filesystem permissions, preventing unauthorized local users from connecting."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Passing File Descriptors via UNIX Domain Socket (SCM_RIGHTS) */\nstruct msghdr msg = {0};\nstruct cmsghdr *cmsg;\nchar buf[CMSG_SPACE(sizeof(int))]; // Buffer for control message\n\nmsg.msg_control = buf;\nmsg.msg_controllen = sizeof(buf);\n\ncmsg = CMSG_FIRSTHDR(&msg);\ncmsg->cmsg_level = SOL_SOCKET;\ncmsg->cmsg_type = SCM_RIGHTS;       // Passing FD capability\ncmsg->cmsg_len = CMSG_LEN(sizeof(int));\n\nint fd_to_pass = open(\"shared.db\", O_RDWR);\n*((int *) CMSG_DATA(cmsg)) = fd_to_pass;\n\nsendmsg(unix_sock_fd, &msg, 0); // Receiver gets open fd in its own FD table!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Docker, Kubernetes (CSI/CRI plugins), PostgreSQL, and Envoy all use UNIX Domain Sockets for local IPC. When deploying a local reverse proxy (NGINX -> Node.js/Python), always use UNIX domain sockets instead of localhost:8080 for a 15-30% latency reduction!",
