    "id": "int-os-063",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "How does Shared Memory (POSIX shm_open vs System V shmget) work, and why does it require external synchronization?",
    "answer": "Shared Memory is the fastest possible Inter-Process Communication (IPC) mechanism available in operating systems because it provides zero-copy data exchange.\n\nMechanism:\nIn standard IPC (pipes, message queues, sockets), transferring data requires copying bytes twice: once from the sender's user space to kernel memory, and once from kernel memory to the receiver's user space. With Shared Memory, the operating system maps the exact same physical RAM pages into the virtual address spaces of two or more distinct processes. Once mapped, writing to that memory location by Process A is immediately visible to Process B as a standard memory load—without any kernel transitions, system calls, or data copying!\n\nPOSIX vs System V:\n- System V (`shmget`, `shmat`, `shmdt`, `shmctl`): Legacy UNIX standard using numeric IPC keys (`key_t`), rigid kernel limits, and awkward CLI management (`ipcs`/`ipcrm`).\n- POSIX (`shm_open`, `mmap`, `munmap`, `shm_unlink`): Modern standard using path-like names (`/my_shm`), mapped to files in `/dev/shm` (tmpfs in RAM), returning standard file descriptors compatible with `mmap` and `ftruncate`.\n\nWhy External Synchronization is Required:\nThe OS kernel does NOT provide any synchronization, locking, or notification for shared memory. It simply maps physical pages. If Process A writes while Process B reads, data races, torn reads, and memory corruption occur. Applications must synchronize access using external primitives such as POSIX named semaphores (`sem_open`), process-shared mutexes (`pthread_mutexattr_setpshared`), or lock-free atomic rings.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Shared memory is the fastest IPC mechanism because it eliminates kernel data copy overhead entirely.",
      "The OS maps identical physical RAM frames into the virtual memory page tables of multiple processes.",
      "POSIX shared memory (`shm_open`) is preferred over System V (`shmget`) for its clean file descriptor semantics and `/dev/shm` integration.",
      "The OS provides no built-in concurrency control; developers must use process-shared mutexes, semaphores, or atomics."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* POSIX Shared Memory Creation & Mapping */\n#include <sys/mman.h>\n#include <fcntl.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\n// 1. Create shared memory object in /dev/shm\nint fd = shm_open(\"/market_feed\", O_CREAT | O_RDWR, 0666);\nftruncate(fd, 4096); // Set size to 4KB (1 page)\n\n// 2. Map into calling process virtual address space\nchar *shared_buf = mmap(NULL, 4096, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);\n\n// 3. Write directly to RAM (Process B sees it instantly with 0 syscalls)\nsprintf(shared_buf, \"TICKER: AAPL, PRICE: 220.50\");\n\n// Synchronization must be handled via POSIX semaphore or shared mutex!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Shared memory is the backbone of high-frequency trading (HFT) and ultra-low-latency market data feeds. LMAX Disruptor-style ring buffers implemented over `/dev/shm` achieve sub-microsecond IPC latency between market feed handlers and order executors!",
