    "id": "int-os-065",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What are the differences between SIGKILL, SIGSTOP, SIGTERM, and SIGINT?",
    "answer": "UNIX signals notify processes of asynchronous events. Signals differ primarily in whether they can be caught, handled, blocked, or ignored by user space:\n\n1. SIGTERM (Signal 15 - Termination):\n- The default polite request sent to terminate a process (e.g., `kill <pid>` or Kubernetes pod eviction).\n- Can be caught, blocked, or ignored.\n- Allows the process to perform graceful shutdown: completing ongoing requests, flushing disk buffers, releasing distributed locks, and closing database connections.\n\n2. SIGINT (Signal 2 - Interrupt):\n- Sent by the terminal driver when the user presses `Ctrl+C`.\n- Can be caught, blocked, or ignored.\n- Prompts command-line applications to abort the current foreground task gracefully.\n\n3. SIGKILL (Signal 9 - Kill):\n- Forced, immediate termination initiated by the OS kernel.\n- CANNOT be caught, blocked, or ignored. The process NEVER receives or executes code for it; the kernel immediately reclaims all memory and file descriptors and destroys the process.\n- Can leave external state (temporary files, shared locks) in a corrupt state if the app wasn't flushed.\n\n4. SIGSTOP (Signal 19 - Stop):\n- Suspends (pauses) process execution. Sent by `kill -STOP <pid>` or debugger breakpoints.\n- CANNOT be caught, blocked, or ignored. The kernel moves the process from TASK_RUNNING to TASK_STOPPED until a `SIGCONT` (Signal 18) resumes it.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SIGTERM (15) and SIGINT (2) can be caught and handled, enabling clean application shutdown.",
      "SIGKILL (9) and SIGSTOP (19) cannot be caught, blocked, or ignored by any user-space process.",
      "Kubernetes sends SIGTERM first, waits for `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (default 30s), and sends SIGKILL if the container fails to terminate.",
      "SIGSTOP pauses process scheduling until SIGCONT awakens it, forming the foundation of Linux job control and debuggers (`gdb`)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Signal Handling Capabilities Matrix */\n\n+---------+--------+------------------+---------------+-------------------------+\n| Signal  | Number | Can be Caught?   | Can be Ignored| Primary Use Case        |\n+---------+--------+------------------+---------------+-------------------------+\n| SIGINT  | 2      | Yes              | Yes           | Ctrl+C from terminal    |\n| SIGTERM | 15     | Yes              | Yes           | Graceful service stop   |\n| SIGKILL | 9      | NO (Kernel-only) | NO            | Force kill zombie/hung  |\n| SIGSTOP | 19     | NO (Kernel-only) | NO            | Suspend process exec    |\n| SIGCONT | 18     | Yes              | No            | Resume stopped process  |\n+---------+--------+------------------+---------------+-------------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Never use `kill -9` as your first reaction to an unresponsive service! Doing so prevents database buffer pools from writing checkpoints and skips application cleanup hooks, potentially causing data loss or transaction journal recovery delays upon reboot.",
