    "id": "int-os-066",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "Explain I/O Multiplexing evolution: select vs poll vs epoll, and why epoll scales to O(1).",
    "answer": "When building high-concurrency network servers (like NGINX, Redis, Envoy), waiting for network events on thousands of socket connections requires I/O multiplexing.\n\n1. select() (BSD UNIX):\n- State representation: Fixed-size bitmasks (`fd_set`), limited to `FD_SETSIZE` (typically 1024 descriptors).\n- Overhead: On every invocation, the application must copy the entire bitmask from user space to kernel space. The kernel scans all descriptors linearly ($O(N)$). Upon return, user space must scan all 1024 bits again to find which socket is ready.\n\n2. poll() (System V):\n- State representation: Array of `struct pollfd`. Eliminates the 1024 descriptor ceiling.\n- Overhead: Still suffers from $O(N)$ complexity. The entire array must be passed from user space to kernel space and scanned linearly by both kernel and user space on every single poll() call.\n\n3. epoll() (Linux 2.6+):\nSolves both scalability bottlenecks using a stateful kernel data structure split across 3 syscalls:\n- `epoll_create1()`: Creates an epoll instance in the kernel.\n- `epoll_ctl()`: Registers or modifies monitored sockets once. In the kernel, monitored file descriptors are stored in a Red-Black Tree ($O(\\log N)$ lookup, insertion, deletion).\n- `epoll_wait()`: Waits for ready events. The kernel registers a hardware/network interrupt callback on each socket. When data arrives, the callback adds the socket directly to a kernel Ready List (Doubly Linked List).\n\nWhy epoll is $O(1)$ with Respect to Monitored Sockets:\n`epoll_wait()` does NOT scan all monitored sockets! It only inspects the Ready List. If 100,000 connections are idle and only 5 have incoming data, `epoll_wait()` returns immediately with exactly those 5 events in $O(1)$ time relative to active events, copying zero idle descriptors.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "select() is limited to 1024 descriptors and requires copying and scanning full bitmasks ($O(N)$) every cycle.",
      "poll() removes the 1024 limit using an array of `pollfd`, but retains $O(N)$ linear scanning and copy overhead.",
      "epoll uses a Red-Black Tree to persist monitored sockets in the kernel and a Ready List populated by driver callbacks.",
      "epoll_wait() inspects only the Ready List, achieving $O(\\text{active})$ performance independent of the total number of idle connections."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* select/poll vs epoll Internal Architecture */\n\nselect/poll: O(N) per call\nUser Space                   Kernel Space\n+----------+   syscall      +-----------------------------------------+\n| Array of | -------------> | Iterates through all N sockets in loop |\n| N FDs    | <------------- | Returns full array; User iterates again |\n+----------+   (every call) +-----------------------------------------+\n\nepoll: O(1) per event\nUser Space                   Kernel Space\n                            +-----------------------------------------+\n                            | Red-Black Tree (Stores all monitored FDs|\nepoll_ctl(ADD) -----------> | Device driver IRQ callback pushes ready |\n                            | FDs to Ready List                       |\n                            +-----------------------------------------+\n                                                |\nepoll_wait() -------------> Ready List (Linked list of ONLY active FDs)\n             <------------- Returns directly to user space!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'What is the BSD/macOS and Windows equivalent of epoll?' BSD/macOS uses `kqueue`, and Windows uses I/O Completion Ports (IOCP). In fact, IOCP is fully asynchronous proactive I/O, whereas epoll is a readiness notification mechanism!",
