    "id": "int-os-070",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What are Linux Namespaces and what are the eight primary namespace types?",
    "answer": "Linux Namespaces are the core kernel virtualization feature that partitions global system resources into isolated virtual views. A process inside a namespace sees only its own isolated slice of system resources, unaware of other processes on the host.\n\nNamespaces form the foundational building block of Linux containers (Docker, containerd, Podman, LXC).\n\nThe 8 primary Linux namespace types:\n1. PID (Process ID): Isolates process IDs. The container's primary process becomes PID 1 inside its namespace, while retaining its normal host PID in the root namespace.\n2. NET (Network): Isolates network devices, IP addresses, routing tables, port bindings, and firewall (iptables) rules. Allows multiple containers to bind to port 80 simultaneously on the same host.\n3. MNT (Mount): Isolates filesystem mount points. A container can mount and unmount filesystems without affecting the host filesystem.\n4. UTS (UNIX Timesharing System): Isolates the hostname and domain name (e.g., `hostname web-container-01`).\n5. IPC (Inter-Process Communication): Isolates System V IPC objects and POSIX message queues, preventing cross-container IPC access.\n6. USER: Maps user and group IDs. Allows a process to run with root privileges (UID 0) inside the container namespace while mapping to an unprivileged user (e.g., UID 10001) on the host.\n7. CGROUP: Isolates the root cgroup directory view visible to `/proc/self/cgroup`.\n8. TIME (Linux 5.6+): Isolates monotonic and boot clocks, allowing containers to change system time offsets without shifting the host clock.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Linux Namespaces isolate global OS resources into sandboxed virtual views for processes.",
      "The primary namespaces are PID (process tree), NET (IPs/ports), MNT (mounts), UTS (hostnames), IPC, USER (UID mapping), CGROUP, and TIME.",
      "Namespaces control WHAT a container can see (isolation).",
      "USER namespaces enhance security by granting root privileges inside the container while remaining unprivileged on the host."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Creating Isolated Namespaces via unshare(1) or clone(2) */\n\n// Shell example: Run isolated shell with private PID, NET, and Mount namespaces\n$ sudo unshare --pid --net --mount --fork /bin/bash\n\n// C Syscall example:\n#define _GNU_SOURCE\n#include <sched.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\n// Child process runs in isolated PID and Network namespaces\nint child_pid = clone(child_func, child_stack + STACK_SIZE,\n                      CLONE_NEWPID | CLONE_NEWNET | CLONE_NEWNS | SIGCHLD, NULL);"
    },
    "pro_tip": "A classic interview question: 'What is the fundamental difference between Namespaces and Cgroups?' Remember: Namespaces determine WHAT a process can SEE (isolation); Cgroups determine HOW MUCH a process can USE (resource limits)!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Docker",
      "Red Hat",
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
