    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is Linux Swappiness and how does the kernel balance page cache reclaim vs anonymous memory swap?",
    "answer": "When physical RAM becomes constrained, the Linux kernel's page reclamation subsystem (`kswapd`) must reclaim memory. It has two options:\n1. Evict File-Backed Pages (Page Cache): Cached blocks from files on disk. If clean, they are discarded instantly with zero disk write I/O. If dirty, they are flushed to their backing files.\n2. Swap Anonymous Pages: Private application memory (heap, stack, anonymous `mmap`) has no backing file on disk. To reclaim an anonymous page, it must be written out to the dedicated Swap partition/file.\n\nWhat is `vm.swappiness`?\n`swappiness` is a kernel parameter (ranging from 0 to 200, typically 0 to 100) that controls the ratio/weight the kernel assigns to reclaiming anonymous memory vs file-backed page cache memory:\n\nFormula internally:\n$$\\text{reclaim\\_ratio} = \\frac{\\text{scan\\_anon}}{\\text{scan\\_file}} \\approx \\frac{\\text{swappiness}}{200 - \\text{swappiness}}$$\n\n- `swappiness = 60` (Default on most Linux distributions): Balanced scanning between anonymous and page cache pages.\n- `swappiness = 0` or `1`: Aggressively preserves anonymous memory in RAM. The kernel will evict almost all page cache before writing any anonymous pages to swap. (Modern systems recommend `1` over `0` to avoid catastrophic OOMs when page cache is fully evicted).\n- `swappiness = 100`: Kernel scans anonymous pages and page cache pages with equal priority.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Linux reclaims memory either by evicting page cache (file-backed) or paging anonymous memory to swap.",
      "`vm.swappiness` balances the ratio between anonymous page swapping and page cache eviction.",
      "Default value 60 works well for general desktop/server workloads.",
      "High-throughput databases and Kubernetes nodes recommend `swappiness = 1` to prevent swapping application heap memory."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Inspecting and Tuning Swappiness */\n\n# Check current swappiness:\n$ cat /proc/sys/vm/swappiness\n60\n\n# Temporarily set to 1 (Ideal for DBs & Kubernetes worker nodes):\n$ sudo sysctl vm.swappiness=1\n\n# Persist permanently in /etc/sysctl.conf:\necho \"vm.swappiness=1\" >> /etc/sysctl.conf\nsudo sysctl -p"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Common misconception: 'Setting swappiness=0 disables swap.' False! In Linux 3.5+, `swappiness=0` still swaps under severe memory exhaustion to prevent immediate OOM crashes. To completely disable swap, you must run `swapoff -a`!",
