    "id": "int-os-077",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR) and how does it prevent buffer overflow exploits?",
    "answer": "Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR) is an operating system security defense mechanism designed to prevent code injection, Return-Oriented Programming (ROP), and buffer overflow exploitation.\n\nThe Vulnerability Mechanism Without ASLR:\nIn older operating systems without ASLR, an executable was loaded at deterministic, fixed virtual memory addresses every single time it ran. For example, the stack always started at `0x7ffffffde000`, the C standard library (`libc`) always loaded at `0x7ffff7a0d000`, and the `system()` function was always at a known address. An attacker exploiting a buffer overflow could overwrite the function return address with the exact, hardcoded address of `system(\"/bin/sh\")` (a classic return-to-libc attack).\n\nHow ASLR Works:\nEvery time a process is launched via `execve()`, the operating system kernel randomly offsets the base addresses of key virtual memory areas:\n1. Stack: Base address randomized.\n2. Heap: Start of `brk` / `mmap` randomized.\n3. Shared Libraries: Dynamic libraries (libc, libcrypto) loaded at unpredictable random base addresses.\n4. Executable Code: When compiled as a Position-Independent Executable (PIE), the main binary's text segment is also randomized.\n\nBecause the memory addresses change on every execution, attackers cannot reliably jump to specific payload addresses; attempting a blind jump almost always accesses unmapped memory, immediately triggering a `SIGSEGV` and terminating the process.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "ASLR randomly offsets the virtual memory base of stack, heap, shared libraries, and code segments on each execution.",
      "Prevents attackers from using hardcoded memory addresses in buffer overflow and return-to-libc attacks.",
      "Requires binaries to be compiled with `-fPIE -pie` (Position Independent Executable) to randomize code segments.",
      "Blind exploit attempts hit invalid memory addresses, triggering an immediate crash (`SIGSEGV`) rather than code execution."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Inspecting ASLR in Linux */\n\n# ASLR status: /proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space\n# 0 = Disabled\n# 1 = Partial (randomizes stack, mmap, VDSO)\n# 2 = Full (randomizes stack, mmap, VDSO, and heap brk)\n$ cat /proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space\n2\n\n# Demonstrating ASLR across multiple runs of the same binary:\n$ ldd /bin/ls | grep libc\n    libc.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 (0x00007fb18c200000)\n$ ldd /bin/ls | grep libc\n    libc.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 (0x00007f3299400000) # Base changed!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Pair ASLR with Stack Canaries (`-fstack-protector-strong`) and Non-Executable Stack (NX/DEP bit via hardware XD/NX bit) in security discussions. Together, they form the fundamental trio of modern binary exploitation defenses!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Apple",
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Meta"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
