    "id": "int-os-078",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What are Discretionary Access Control (DAC) vs Mandatory Access Control (MAC - SELinux/AppArmor)?",
    "answer": "Operating systems enforce access control to restrict what operations processes can perform on system resources (files, sockets, devices).\n\n1. Discretionary Access Control (DAC - Standard UNIX Permissions):\n- Control is at the discretion of the resource owner.\n- Every file and directory is associated with an Owner (User), a Group, and permissions (`rwxrwxrwx`).\n- Flaw / Limitation: Once a malicious process or compromised daemon gains root privileges (UID 0), DAC becomes completely useless. A compromised web server running as root can delete `/etc/passwd`, overwrite boot loaders, and access any user's private data.\n\n2. Mandatory Access Control (MAC - SELinux, AppArmor):\n- Access decisions are enforced globally by the OS kernel based on strict, centralized security policies. Individual users and owners CANNOT change or override these policies.\n- Even if a process runs as root (UID 0), the kernel enforces MAC policies that restrict its capabilities.\n- SELinux (Security-Enhanced Linux): Uses Type Enforcement. Every process (subject) and file/socket (object) has a security context label (e.g., `httpd_t`, `httpd_sys_content_t`). A rule explicitly states: 'Only subjects labeled httpd_t can read files labeled httpd_sys_content_t'. If an attacker exploits the web server and spawns a root shell, the shell is still confined to `httpd_t` and cannot access `/home/` or `/etc/shadow`!\n- AppArmor: Path-based MAC (used in Ubuntu/Debian). Simpler to configure than SELinux by restricting executables to explicitly whitelisted filesystem paths.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "DAC (standard UNIX permissions) allows file owners to grant permissions, but is bypassed entirely by root (UID 0).",
      "MAC (SELinux, AppArmor) enforces system-wide kernel policies that even root cannot bypass.",
      "SELinux uses label-based Type Enforcement (subjects and objects have security contexts).",
      "AppArmor uses path-based profiles, restricting what files, capabilities, and network ports a specific binary can access."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* DAC vs MAC Enforcement */\n\nDAC Check:\nProcess (UID 0 - root) ---> Checks file permissions ---> Allowed!\n(Root bypasses all DAC checks)\n\nMAC (SELinux) Check:\nProcess (UID 0, Context: httpd_t)\n         |\n         v (Kernel Security Subsystem - LSM)\nDoes policy allow 'httpd_t' to access 'shadow_t' (/etc/shadow)?\n         |\n         +--> NO! Permission Denied (AVC Denied Log)\n(Even root is blocked if policy denies access!)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In production, junior engineers often 'fix' permission bugs by running `setenforce 0` (disabling SELinux). In an interview, NEVER recommend disabling SELinux! Explain that you inspect audit logs via `ausearch -m avc` and generate tailored policies using `audit2allow`.",
