    "id": "int-os-079",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is Seccomp (Secure Computing Mode) and how do container runtimes use Seccomp-BPF?",
    "answer": "The Linux kernel exposes over 450 distinct system calls. Most user-space applications and microservices require only a small fraction (around 40-70 syscalls) during their entire operational lifecycle. However, dangerous or legacy system calls (such as `ptrace`, `reboot`, `kexec_load`, `mount`) remain accessible by default, expanding the kernel attack surface if an application is compromised.\n\nSeccomp (Secure Computing Mode):\nA Linux kernel security facility that restricts the system calls a process can make:\n1. Strict Mode: Allows only 4 system calls: `read()`, `write()`, `_exit()`, and `sigreturn()`. Any other syscall results in the kernel terminating the process with `SIGKILL`.\n2. Seccomp-BPF (Filter Mode):\nUses Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) bytecode programs attached to the process via `prctl(PR_SET_SECCOMP)`. Instead of a hardcoded whitelist of 4 calls, developers define fine-grained rules evaluating syscall numbers and their arguments.\n\nContainer Runtime Usage:\nEvery Docker and Kubernetes container runs under a default Seccomp profile (unless explicitly disabled). Docker's default Seccomp profile blocks approximately 44 out of 450+ system calls (including `sys_chroot`, `acct`, `add_key`, `ptrace`, `process_vm_writev`). Even if an attacker achieves a container escape vulnerability or acquires root privileges inside the container, attempting to invoke blocked system calls results in the kernel immediately terminating the thread or returning `EPERM`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Seccomp restricts the system calls a process can execute, shrinking the kernel attack surface.",
      "Seccomp-BPF allows writing programmable BPF filter rules inspecting syscall numbers and parameter values.",
      "Disallowed syscalls can trigger immediate termination (`SCMP_ACT_KILL`) or return an error (`SCMP_ACT_ERRNO`).",
      "Docker and Kubernetes apply default Seccomp profiles blocking dozens of dangerous kernel syscalls."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Example Docker Default Seccomp JSON Profile Fragment */\n{\n  \"defaultAction\": \"SCMP_ACT_ERRNO\",\n  \"architectures\": [\"SCMP_ARCH_X86_64\"],\n  \"syscalls\": [\n    {\n      \"names\": [\"read\", \"write\", \"openat\", \"close\", \"epoll_wait\", \"futex\"],\n      \"action\": \"SCMP_ACT_ALLOW\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"names\": [\"reboot\", \"swapon\", \"swapoff\", \"kexec_load\", \"ptrace\"],\n      \"action\": \"SCMP_ACT_KILL\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If asked how Google Chrome sandbox isolates browser tabs from executing malicious malware on the OS: Chrome applies Seccomp-BPF filters to render processes immediately after startup, locking them into an unprivileged sandbox that cannot make unauthorized syscalls!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Docker",
      "Google",
      "Cloudflare",
      "Apple"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
