    "id": "int-os-083",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is an Inverted Page Table and why is it used in 64-bit architectures?",
    "answer": "In traditional multi-level hierarchical paging, each process has its own page table indexed by virtual page number (VPN). In 64-bit systems with huge address spaces ($2^{64}$ bytes), multi-level page tables can consume gigabytes of physical memory just to store page translation structures.\n\nWhat is an Inverted Page Table (IPT)?\nInstead of indexing by Virtual Page Number per process, an Inverted Page Table maintains a SINGLE global table indexed by Physical Frame Number (PFN). Each entry in the IPT corresponds to exactly one physical frame of RAM:\n\n$$\\text{IPT Entry} = \\langle \\text{Process ID (PID)}, \\text{Virtual Page Number (VPN)}, \\text{Protection Bits}, \\text{Chain Pointer} \\rangle$$\n\nKey Advantages:\n1. Fixed, Minimal Memory Overhead: The size of the page table is strictly proportional to the amount of PHYSICAL RAM installed on the machine, completely independent of the number of processes or the size of their virtual address spaces!\n\nDisadvantages & Solutions:\n1. Linear Lookup Time: When a process issues virtual address $V$, finding the matching entry requires searching the entire table ($O(N)$), which is unacceptable for every memory reference.\n2. Solution (Hashing): Modern IPTs use a Hashed Page Table. The virtual page number and PID are hashed to index into a Hash Anchor Table (HAT), which points to a short collision chain in the IPT. Furthermore, aggressive hardware TLBs ensure 99%+ of lookups bypass the IPT entirely.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Inverted Page Tables (IPT) index entries by physical frame number rather than virtual page number.",
      "Only ONE global page table exists for the entire operating system, sized strictly to installed physical RAM.",
      "Drastically reduces page table memory footprint in 64-bit architectures.",
      "Lookup speed is optimized using Hash Anchor Tables and hardware TLB caching to prevent $O(N)$ frame searches."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Traditional Paging vs Inverted Page Table (IPT) */\n\nTraditional Multi-Level Paging:\nVirtual Page Number (VPN) ---> [P1] -> [P2] -> [P3] -> [P4] ---> Physical Frame\n(Separate page table per process! Consumes massive RAM for sparse 64-bit VAS)\n\nInverted Page Table (IPT):\nVirtual Address (PID, VPN)\n            |\n            v\n     Hash Function\n            |\n            v\n+-----------------------+     +-----------------------------------------+\n| Hash Anchor Table     | --> | IPT Entry [Frame #]:                     |\n| (Indices to IPT)      |     | Matches (PID, VPN)? Yes -> Frame # found!|\n+-----------------------+     +-----------------------------------------+\n(1 Global table sized strictly to physical RAM!)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "IBM PowerPC and UltraSPARC architectures heavily utilized Inverted Page Tables. x86_64, however, chose 4-level (and now 5-level) hierarchical paging because hardware page-table walkers can traverse hierarchical radix trees faster in silicon.",
