    "id": "int-os-087",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the Linux Completely Fair Scheduler (CFS) and how does virtual runtime (vruntime) ensure fairness?",
    "answer": "The Completely Fair Scheduler (CFS), designed by Ingo Molnar, has been the default Linux process scheduler for standard tasks (SCHED_OTHER / SCHED_NORMAL) since kernel 2.6.23.\n\nCore Concept: Virtual Runtime (`vruntime`):\nCFS models an 'ideal multi-tasking CPU' where $N$ processes run simultaneously on 1 CPU, each advancing at a speed of $1/N$. In reality, the CPU can only run one thread at a time.\nTo track execution fairness, CFS maintains a 64-bit value called `vruntime` for each thread: the amount of CPU execution time the thread has consumed, scaled by its priority (nice level):\n\n$$\\Delta \\text{vruntime} = \\Delta \\text{actual\\_time} \\times \\frac{\\text{NICE\\_0\\_LOAD}}{\\text{thread\\_weight}}$$\n\n- Threads with higher priority (lower nice value) have greater weight; their `vruntime` advances slower for each physical millisecond of CPU time.\n- Threads with lower priority (higher nice value) have lesser weight; their `vruntime` advances faster.\n\nScheduling Algorithm:\n1. The scheduler organizes all runnable tasks in a time-ordered Red-Black Tree (`rb_node`), keyed strictly on `vruntime`.\n2. The thread with the smallest `vruntime` is stored at the leftmost node of the Red-Black tree (`rb_leftmost`).\n3. Picking Next Task: The scheduler picks `rb_leftmost` in $O(1)$ time (cached pointer).\n4. After running for its time slice, its `vruntime` increases, and it is re-inserted into the Red-Black Tree in $O(\\log N)$ time.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "CFS models an ideal multitasking CPU where runnable processes share CPU time proportionally to their weight.",
      "`vruntime` tracks CPU time weighted by priority; higher-priority threads accrue `vruntime` slower.",
      "Runnable tasks are stored in a Red-Black Tree indexed by `vruntime`.",
      "Task selection is $O(1)$ via the cached leftmost pointer; reinsertion is $O(\\log N)$."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* CFS Red-Black Tree Scheduling */\n\n           Red-Black Tree of Runnable Tasks\n                     [vruntime: 50ms]\n                     /              \\\n         [vruntime: 25ms]        [vruntime: 85ms]\n             /\n    [vruntime: 10ms]  <--- rb_leftmost (Smallest vruntime)\n          |\n          v\nSelected to run NEXT by CFS in O(1) time!\nAfter running for 5ms:\nNew vruntime = 10ms + 5ms = 15ms -> Re-inserted into tree in O(log N)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Note: In Linux 6.6 (late 2023), CFS was succeeded by the EEVDF (Earliest Eligible Virtual Deadline First) scheduler by Peter Zijlstra, which separates fairness from latency! However, CFS remains the golden standard question in 99% of interviews.",
