    "id": "int-os-089",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the difference between Preemptive and Cooperative Multitasking?",
    "answer": "Multitasking allows an operating system to execute multiple tasks concurrently on limited CPU cores.\n\n1. Cooperative Multitasking (Windows 3.1, Classic Mac OS, Go Goroutines runtime, Node.js event loop):\n- The currently running task retains control of the CPU until it explicitly and voluntarily yields control back to the scheduler (via `yield()`, blocking I/O, or sleep).\n- Vulnerability: If a buggy or malicious process enters an infinite loop (`while(true);`) without yielding, the ENTIRE operating system freezes! No other task or user interface can run.\n- Simpler concurrency model: Shared data structures rarely need locking because context switches only happen at explicit yield points.\n\n2. Preemptive Multitasking (Modern OS: Linux, Windows NT/11, macOS, Android, iOS):\n- The operating system kernel enforces control using hardware timer interrupts (typically firing every 1ms to 10ms).\n- When a thread's allocated time slice expires, the timer interrupt triggers, switching the CPU from user mode to kernel mode. The OS scheduler preempts (pauses) the running thread, saves its state to its PCB, and schedules another thread.\n- Robustness: A rogue thread stuck in an infinite loop cannot freeze the system; the OS preempts it automatically.\n- Complexity: Requires synchronization primitives (mutexes, semaphores, atomics) to protect shared data against preemptive race conditions.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Cooperative multitasking relies on processes voluntarily yielding the CPU.",
      "A single non-yielding or hanging process in cooperative multitasking freezes the entire system.",
      "Preemptive multitasking uses hardware timer interrupts to forcefully switch tasks when their time slice ends.",
      "Modern operating systems are strictly preemptive, guaranteeing system responsiveness and isolation."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Preemptive vs Cooperative Context Switching */\n\nCooperative Multitasking:\nTask A runs -----> Calls yield() -----> Scheduler runs Task B\n(If Task A never yields, System HANGS!)\n\nPreemptive Multitasking:\nTask A runs ----------------------------------------->\n                     | (Hardware Timer Interrupt fires!)\n                     v\n                OS Scheduler preempts Task A, runs Task B\n(System remains responsive even if Task A has an infinite loop!)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Note that while modern operating systems are preemptive, modern programming language runtimes (Go goroutines, Python asyncio, Kotlin coroutines) implement user-space cooperative multitasking on top of preemptive OS threads for lightweight concurrency!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Microsoft",
      "Apple",
      "Google"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "EASY",
    "is_hidden": false,
