    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the role of the init / systemd process (PID 1) in Linux?",
    "answer": "When the Linux kernel finishes booting and mounts the root filesystem, it launches the very first user-space process: PID 1 (traditionally `/sbin/init`, modernized as `systemd` in modern distributions).\n\nTwo Fundamental Responsibilities of PID 1:\n1. System Bootstrapping & Service Supervision:\nPID 1 starts, manages, and monitors all user-space system daemons (networking, sshd, logging, cron) and manages system state transitions (runlevels or systemd targets like `multi-user.target`, `reboot.target`).\n\n2. Adopting and Reaping Orphan Processes:\nWhen a parent process terminates before its child processes, the children become 'orphan processes'. In Linux, the kernel automatically re-parents all orphan processes to PID 1 (or a subreaper process defined via `PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER`).\nWhen an orphan process eventually exits, it enters the ZOMBIE state. It is PID 1's non-negotiable duty to continuously call `wait()` or `waitpid()` in an event loop to read their exit status codes and reap them from the kernel process table.\n\nWhy Containers Break Without Proper PID 1:\nIf a container's entrypoint is a custom application binary (like Node.js or Python) that does not implement signal forwarding or `wait()` reaping, child processes spawned inside the container will accumulate as zombie processes indefinitely until the host runs out of PIDs! Tools like `tini` or `dumb-init` act as minimal PID 1 init systems inside Docker containers.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "PID 1 is the ancestor of all user-space processes, launched directly by the kernel at boot.",
      "Adopts orphaned processes whose original parents terminated prematurely.",
      "Must continuously invoke waitpid() to reap zombie processes and free their process table entries.",
      "In containers, using `tini` or `dumb-init` as PID 1 prevents zombie process accumulation and ensures signal propagation."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Docker Container Zombie Leak Without Init */\n\n# Bad Container Dockerfile:\nENTRYPOINT [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n# If Node spawns child processes that exit, Node does not reap them.\n# The container process table fills with <defunct> zombie processes!\n\n# Correct Container with tini as PID 1:\nENTRYPOINT [\"/usr/bin/tini\", \"--\", \"node\", \"server.js\"]\n# tini runs as PID 1, forwards SIGTERM properly, and reaps all zombie orphans!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer trap: 'What happens if you run kill -9 1 on Linux?' Nothing! The Linux kernel explicitly blocks `SIGKILL` and `SIGTERM` from being delivered to PID 1 to protect the operating system from crashing!",
