    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the difference between Synchronous vs Asynchronous I/O and Blocking vs Non-Blocking I/O?",
    "answer": "These two orthogonal dimensions define how software interacts with I/O devices:\n\n1. Blocking vs Non-Blocking (How the caller behaves while waiting for I/O readiness):\n- Blocking: The calling thread is put to sleep (suspended) by the OS scheduler until data is available in the kernel buffer or the operation completes. Zero CPU cycles wasted while waiting, but requires 1 thread per connection.\n- Non-Blocking (`O_NONBLOCK`): The syscall returns immediately. If data is ready, it returns bytes; if no data is available, it returns immediately with an error (`EAGAIN` or `EWOULDBLOCK`). The thread does not sleep and can perform other work or poll again.\n\n2. Synchronous vs Asynchronous (Who actually transfers the data between user and kernel space):\n- Synchronous I/O: The application process initiates the I/O transfer and executes the transfer itself. Even with I/O multiplexing (`select`, `poll`, `epoll`), the model is SYNCHRONOUS: epoll only notifies that the socket is *ready*; the application thread must still call `read()` and block while bytes are copied from kernel buffers to user space.\n- Asynchronous I/O (Proactive I/O - Windows IOCP, Linux io_uring): The application asks the OS kernel: 'Read 4KB into this user buffer and notify me when you are completely DONE'. The application thread proceeds immediately. The kernel DMA engine or background kernel workers transfer data directly into user memory and signal a completion event when finished.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Blocking puts the thread to sleep until data arrives; Non-blocking returns immediately with `EAGAIN` if not ready.",
      "Synchronous requires the application thread to perform the data copy from kernel space to user space.",
      "Asynchronous lets the OS kernel perform the complete data transfer, notifying the app only upon completion.",
      "epoll is NOT Asynchronous I/O; it is a Synchronous I/O readiness multiplexer. io_uring and IOCP are true Asynchronous I/O."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* The 4 I/O Models Matrix */\n\n+------------------------+------------------------------------+----------------------------------+\n|                        | Synchronous (Caller transfers data)| Asynchronous (OS transfers data) |\n+------------------------+------------------------------------+----------------------------------+\n| Blocking               | Traditional read()/write()         | N/A                              |\n|                        | Thread sleeps until data arrives   |                                  |\n+------------------------+------------------------------------+----------------------------------+\n| Non-Blocking / Ready   | epoll / select / poll              | Windows IOCP / Linux io_uring    |\n|                        | Syscall returns EAGAIN; app reads  | OS transfers data to user buffer |\n|                        | when notified of readiness         | and signals completion           |\n+------------------------+------------------------------------+----------------------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer trick: 'Is Node.js asynchronous I/O at the OS level on Linux?' Answer: No! libuv in Node.js uses epoll on Linux, which is synchronous non-blocking I/O readiness. For disk file I/O, libuv uses a thread pool of blocking threads because Linux lacked true async disk I/O until io_uring!",
