    "id": "int-os-095",
    "topic_id": "topic-os",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the difference between Write-Through and Write-Back caching in CPU and OS memory?",
    "answer": "Caching systems (CPU L1/L2 caches, OS page caches, and RAID controller caches) must synchronize cached modifications with underlying slower storage (RAM or disk).\n\n1. Write-Through Caching:\n- When a write occurs, data is written simultaneously to the cache AND to the backing store (RAM or persistent storage).\n- Advantages: Simplest design. Cache and backing storage are always strictly consistent. On a sudden crash or power outage, no data is lost.\n- Disadvantages: Slowest write performance. Every write is bound by the latency and bandwidth of the slowest underlying storage tier.\n\n2. Write-Back Caching (Copy-Back):\n- When a write occurs, data is written ONLY to the high-speed cache. The modified cache line/page is marked 'dirty'.\n- The write operation returns immediately to the caller.\n- Dirty data is written back to the slower backing storage asynchronously in batches when the cache line is evicted, periodically by background threads, or during explicit flushes.\n- Advantages: Extremely fast write performance and reduces write traffic by coalescing multiple consecutive writes to the same block.\n- Disadvantages: Complexity and risk. If power fails before dirty blocks are flushed, uncommitted data is lost unless battery-backed cache (BBU) or journaling is present.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Write-Through writes to both cache and backing storage simultaneously; safe, but latency-bound by slower storage.",
      "Write-Back writes only to cache and marks blocks dirty, flushing asynchronously for peak write throughput.",
      "Modern CPU L1/L2 data caches and OS Page Caches use Write-Back caching.",
      "Hardware RAID controllers use Write-Back caching paired with Battery Backup Units (BBU) or non-volatile flash to ensure crash safety."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Write-Through vs Write-Back */\n\nWrite-Through:\nCPU Write ---> Cache [Update]\n           ---> Backing RAM [Update simultaneously!] (High latency)\n\nWrite-Back:\nCPU Write ---> Cache [Update & mark DIRTY] ---> Returns immediately! (Fast!)\n                     |\n                     v (Later, on eviction or flush)\n               Backing RAM [Updated asynchronously in background]"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Database administrators often check: 'Is the RAID controller cache in Write-Through or Write-Back mode?' If the RAID controller's battery backup dies, it automatically degrades from Write-Back to Write-Through mode, instantly tanking database write IOPS by 90%!",
