    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What is the difference between Thread-Safe, Reentrant, and Async-Signal-Safe functions?",
    "answer": "These three terms describe concurrency safety across different execution dimensions:\n\n1. Thread-Safe:\n- A function is thread-safe if it can be called concurrently by multiple threads without data races or corruption.\n- Can achieve thread safety using locks/mutexes around shared global state.\n- Example: `malloc()` in glibc is thread-safe because it acquires internal per-arena heap mutexes.\n\n2. Reentrant:\n- A function is reentrant if its execution can be interrupted in the middle (e.g., by a recursive call, context switch, or hardware interrupt), re-entered by another execution path, and safely resume without corrupting state.\n- Rule: A reentrant function must NOT use global or static non-const variables, must NOT return pointers to static internal buffers, and must NOT call non-reentrant functions.\n- A reentrant function is ALWAYS thread-safe, but a thread-safe function is NOT necessarily reentrant! (e.g., `malloc()` uses a mutex; if interrupted by a signal that calls `malloc()`, it self-deadlocks).\n\n3. Async-Signal-Safe:\n- A function that can be safely called from an asynchronous signal handler.\n- Because a signal can interrupt a thread while it holds an internal library mutex, async-signal-safe functions cannot acquire mutexes or modify shared global state. Only a strict POSIX whitelist (e.g., raw `write`, `_exit`, `read`) are async-signal-safe.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Thread-Safe: Safe for concurrent threads; often uses mutexes.",
      "Reentrant: Safe to interrupt and re-enter mid-execution; avoids global state and mutexes.",
      "All reentrant functions are thread-safe, but not all thread-safe functions are reentrant.",
      "Async-Signal-Safe: Safe to call from signal handlers; cannot use locks that might already be held by the interrupted thread."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Thread-Safe vs Reentrant Example */\n\n// 1. Thread-Safe but NOT Reentrant (uses mutex):\nint counter = 0;\npthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\nint thread_safe_increment() {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);\n    counter++; // Safe across threads, but if interrupted by a signal\n               // handler that calls this -> SELF-DEADLOCK!\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);\n    return counter;\n}\n\n// 2. Reentrant AND Thread-Safe (zero global state, stack-only):\nint reentrant_add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b; // Uses strictly CPU registers & stack. 100% Reentrant!\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Notice the `_r` suffix in legacy POSIX functions like `strtok_r()`, `localtime_r()`, and `gethostbyname_r()`. The `_r` stands for REENTRANT! They replace static internal buffers with user-supplied buffer arguments, making them reentrant and thread-safe.",
