    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "OPERATING_SYSTEMS",
    "subject_label": "Operating Systems",
    "title": "What are eBPF (Extended Berkeley Packet Filter) programs and how are they revolutionizing Linux OS observability and networking?",
    "answer": "Historically, adding new functionality, observability hooks, or networking logic to the Linux kernel required either modifying kernel source code (and waiting years for upstream distribution releases) or writing out-of-tree Kernel Modules (`.ko`), which risked crashing the kernel on any memory bug.\n\neBPF (Extended Berkeley Packet Filter):\neBPF is a revolutionary technology that allows developers to run custom, sandboxed bytecode directly inside the Linux kernel dynamically without modifying kernel source code or rebooting the machine.\n\nHow eBPF Works Safely:\n1. Writing Code: Developers write restricted C programs compiled via LLVM/Clang into eBPF bytecode.\n2. Loading via `bpf()` Syscall: The bytecode is loaded into the kernel.\n3. The In-Kernel Verifier: Before execution, the kernel Verifier performs rigorous static analysis to guarantee safety: ensures the program has no infinite loops, cannot access arbitrary memory, cannot cause kernel panics, and always terminates.\n4. JIT Compilation: Safe bytecode is Just-In-Time (JIT) compiled into native machine instructions for bare-metal execution speed.\n5. Event Attachment: Programs attach to kernel probe points: kprobes/kretprobes (kernel functions), uprobes (user space functions), tracepoints, socket filters, or XDP (eXpress Data Path at network driver level).\n\nModern Industry Impact:\n- Networking: Cilium uses eBPF to replace `iptables` and `kube-proxy`, routing network packets directly at the network driver level ($O(1)$ lookup instead of $O(N)$ iptables chains).\n- Observability: Tools like BCC, bpftrace, and Datadog trace function execution latency across the entire OS stack with zero overhead.\n- Security: Tetragon monitors real-time process execution and file access, blocking malicious actions before syscalls execute.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "eBPF runs sandboxed, user-defined bytecode directly inside the Linux kernel at native speed.",
      "The in-kernel Verifier guarantees safety, preventing kernel crashes, memory corruption, and infinite loops.",
      "Attaches dynamically to tracepoints, kprobes, uprobes, and network interfaces (XDP) without kernel reboots.",
      "Powers modern cloud-native networking (Cilium), observability (bpftrace, Datadog), and security (Tetragon)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "c",
      "code": "/* Conceptual eBPF Lifecycle */\n\nUser Space:\n[ C code ] ---> [ Clang/LLVM ] ---> [ eBPF Bytecode ]\n                                              |\n                                              | sys_bpf(BPF_PROG_LOAD)\nKernel Space:                                 v\n+-----------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. Static Verifier: Checks safety & termination     |\n| 2. JIT Compiler: Converts to native x86/ARM code    |\n| 3. Attach to Hook: e.g. sys_enter_execve or XDP     |\n+-----------------------------------------------------+\n                          |\n              Hook triggered on event!\n                          |\n                          v\n         Runs at native speed, writes metrics\n         to lockless eBPF Maps (shared with User Space)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If an interviewer asks about the future of Linux systems engineering, mention eBPF! Highlighting how Cilium uses eBPF to bypass TCP/IP stacks for pod-to-pod communication in Kubernetes shows cutting-edge cloud infrastructure expertise.",
