    "title": "Explain the OSI 7-Layer Model vs TCP/IP 4-Layer / 5-Layer Model with Protocol Data Units (PDUs).",
    "answer": "The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) reference model is a theoretical 7-layer framework developed by ISO to standardize network communication. The TCP/IP model is the pragmatic, real-world protocol suite that powers the Internet.\n\nOSI 7 Layers vs TCP/IP & their Protocol Data Units (PDUs):\n1. Physical Layer (Bits): Transmits raw bit streams over physical media (cables, optical fiber, radio frequencies). Repeaters, hubs, physical connectors (RJ-45).\n2. Data Link Layer (Frames): Node-to-node hop delivery, framing, physical MAC addressing, error detection (CRC). Switches, Ethernet (802.3), Wi-Fi (802.11).\n3. Network Layer (Packets): Host-to-host end-to-end delivery, logical IP addressing, routing across internetworks. Routers, IPv4, IPv6, ICMP, OSPF, BGP.\n4. Transport Layer (Segments for TCP / Datagrams for UDP): Process-to-process communication, port numbers, flow control, congestion control, reliability. TCP, UDP.\n5. Session Layer: Session establishment, maintenance, and synchronization (RPC, NetBIOS).\n6. Presentation Layer: Data syntax translation, character encoding (ASCII, UTF-8), compression, encryption/decryption (TLS/SSL).\n7. Application Layer (Data): User-facing network services and high-level application protocols (HTTP, DNS, SMTP, SSH, FTP).\n\nIn TCP/IP 4-Layer Model: Layers 5, 6, and 7 are collapsed into a single Application Layer. The Physical and Data Link layers are combined into the Network Access (Link) Layer.\nIn TCP/IP 5-Layer Model: Physical and Data Link remain distinct.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "OSI is a 7-layer theoretical conceptual model; TCP/IP is the practical 4/5-layer industry standard.",
      "Each layer adds its own header (encapsulation) on transmission and strips it (decapsulation) on reception.",
      "PDUs: Application = Data/Message; Transport = Segment/Datagram; Network = Packet; Data Link = Frame; Physical = Bits.",
      "TCP/IP merges OSI Session and Presentation layers directly into the Application layer."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* OSI 7-Layer vs TCP/IP Model & Encapsulation */\n\nOSI Model               TCP/IP Model     PDU          Protocols / Devices\n+-------------------+   +-------------+  +----------+ +--------------------+\n| 7. Application    |---| Application |  | Data     | | HTTP, DNS, SSH, TLS|\n| 6. Presentation   |   |             |  |          | | (Session/Format)   |\n| 5. Session        |---|             |  |          | |                    |\n+-------------------+   +-------------+  +----------+ +--------------------+\n| 4. Transport      |---| Transport   |  | Segment  | | TCP, UDP (Ports)   |\n+-------------------+   +-------------+  +----------+ +--------------------+\n| 3. Network        |---| Internet    |  | Packet   | | IP, ICMP, Routers  |\n+-------------------+   +-------------+  +----------+ +--------------------+\n| 2. Data Link      |---| Link /      |  | Frame    | | Ethernet, Switches |\n+-------------------+   | Net Access  |  +----------+ +--------------------+\n| 1. Physical       |---|             |  | Bits     | | Cables, Fiber, Hubs|\n+-------------------+   +-------------+  +----------+ +--------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer trick: 'At which OSI layer does TLS/SSL operate?' Technically, TLS provides Presentation/Session layer services (encryption/session management), but in the TCP/IP stack it runs directly above Transport (Layer 4) and below Application (Layer 7) as Layer 4.5/5!",
