    "id": "int-cn-003",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Compare Circuit Switching vs Packet Switching: Virtual Circuit vs Datagram networks.",
    "answer": "Networks switch data between nodes using two primary paradigms:\n\n1. Circuit Switching (Traditional Telephone Network - PSTN, Optical TDM):\n- A dedicated physical or logical communication path (circuit) is established between sender and receiver before any data can be transferred (Call Setup Phase).\n- Guaranteed bandwidth, constant transmission rate, and zero queuing delay once established.\n- Wasteful: If callers are silent, the allocated circuit bandwidth remains idle and cannot be utilized by other users (No Statistical Multiplexing).\n- High setup latency; failure of any intermediate link abruptly drops the active circuit.\n\n2. Packet Switching (The Modern Internet - IP):\n- Data is divided into discrete chunks called packets, each carrying header metadata (source/destination IP, sequence numbers).\n- Packets share communication links on-demand (Statistical Multiplexing).\n- Store-and-Forward: Routers receive the entire packet, verify CRC checksum, inspect destination address, and forward it to the next hop.\n- Dynamic resource sharing: Maximizes link utilization; however, heavy congestion causes queuing delay and packet drops.\n\nTwo Packet Switching Approaches:\n- Datagram Networks (Internet IP): Connectionless. Each packet is routed independently; packets can take different paths and arrive out-of-order.\n- Virtual Circuit Networks (ATM, Frame Relay, MPLS): Connection-oriented. A virtual path is pre-established; packets carry a small Virtual Circuit Identifier (VCI) instead of full destination IPs and follow the same route sequentially.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Circuit switching reserves dedicated end-to-end channels, delivering guaranteed bandwidth but poor link efficiency.",
      "Packet switching breaks data into packets that share links dynamically using statistical multiplexing.",
      "Datagram packet switching (IP) routes packets independently with no setup phase.",
      "Virtual Circuit packet switching establishes a pre-planned route using VC identifiers (MPLS, ATM)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Circuit Switching vs Packet Switching Comparison */\n\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Feature             | Circuit Switching (PSTN)      | Packet Switching (Internet IP)|\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Dedicated Path      | Yes (Reserved for call)       | No (Dynamic store-and-forward)|\n| Bandwidth           | Guaranteed, fixed             | Dynamic / Best-effort         |\n| Resource Waste      | High during idle periods      | Low (Statistical Multiplexing)|\n| Queuing Delays      | Zero                          | Variable (Buffer queueing)    |\n| Fault Tolerance     | Low (Broken link drops call)  | High (Packets re-routed)      |\n| Packet Ordering     | Strictly in-order             | Can arrive out-of-order       |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'If packet switching is best-effort, how do modern ISPs guarantee SLAs for voice and video?' Mention MPLS (Multi-Protocol Label Switching)! MPLS brings virtual circuit behavior to IP networks by assigning short labels to packets, enabling traffic engineering and deterministic Quality of Service (QoS).",
