    "id": "int-cn-005",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain Nyquist Theorem vs Shannon Capacity Theorem for maximum channel data rate.",
    "answer": "These two foundational physical layer theorems define theoretical limits on data transmission over communication channels:\n\n1. Nyquist Bit Rate (Noiseless Channel - 1928):\nHarry Nyquist proved that for an ideal, noiseless channel of bandwidth $B$ Hertz, the maximum theoretical symbol transmission rate is $2B$ baud. If each signal element carries $M$ discrete voltage/signal levels (where $M = 2^n$, encoding $n = \\log_2 M$ bits per symbol):\n\n$$\\text{Max Data Rate} = 2B \\log_2 M \\quad (\\text{bps})$$\n\n- Example: A noiseless 3000 Hz channel with 8 discrete signal levels ($M = 8$, $\\log_2 8 = 3$ bits):\n$$\\text{Data Rate} = 2 \\times 3000 \\times 3 = 18,000 \\text{ bps}$$\n- Limitation: Assumes zero thermal noise, which is physically impossible in the real world.\n\n2. Shannon Capacity Theorem (Noisy Channel - 1948):\nClaude Shannon extended Nyquist's work to real-world channels afflicted by thermal white noise. Given bandwidth $B$ and Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR), the Shannon Capacity $C$ is the absolute upper bound on error-free data transmission, regardless of how many signal levels ($M$) are used:\n\n$$C = B \\log_2 \\left(1 + \\frac{S}{N}\\right) \\quad (\\text{bps})$$\n\nWhere $\\text{SNR} = S/N$ (linear power ratio). In decibels:\n$$\\text{SNR}_{\\text{dB}} = 10 \\log_{10} \\left(\\frac{S}{N}\\right)$$\n\n- Example: A telephone line with $B = 3000 \\text{ Hz}$ and $\\text{SNR} = 30 \\text{ dB}$ (linear ratio $S/N = 1000$):\n$$C = 3000 \\times \\log_2(1 + 1000) \\approx 3000 \\times 9.97 \\approx 29,900 \\text{ bps} \\approx 30 \\text{ kbps}$$",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Nyquist formula ($2B \\log_2 M$) calculates maximum data rate for noiseless channels with $M$ signal levels.",
      "Shannon capacity ($B \\log_2(1 + S/N)$) defines the fundamental upper limit for real-world channels with noise.",
      "Shannon's law proves you cannot achieve infinite bit rates simply by increasing modulation levels ($M$).",
      "SNR is expressed as linear power ratio ($S/N$) in Shannon's formula, converted from decibels ($10 \\log_{10}(S/N)$)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Nyquist vs Shannon Summary */\n\nNyquist (Ideal Noiseless):\nData Rate = 2 * B * log2(M)\n- Governed by Bandwidth (B) and Modulation Levels (M).\n- Suggests infinite data rate if M is made arbitrarily large.\n\nShannon (Real-World Noisy):\nCapacity C = B * log2(1 + S/N)\n- Governed by Bandwidth (B) and Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR).\n- Imposes an absolute fundamental physical ceiling; increasing M\n  beyond Shannon capacity results in undetectable bit errors!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer puzzle: 'How do you increase channel capacity according to Shannon?' Answer: Either increase channel bandwidth ($B$) via fiber optics/wider spectrum, or increase signal power ($S$) relative to noise ($N$), or use error-correcting codes (LDPC/Turbo codes) to operate closer to the Shannon limit!",
