    "id": "int-cn-009",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain CSMA/CD in Ethernet: Collision Detection, Minimum Frame Size calculation, and Exponential Backoff.",
    "answer": "Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection (CSMA/CD - IEEE 802.3) is the legacy MAC protocol developed for half-duplex shared Ethernet buses:\n\n1. Carrier Sense: Listen Before Talk (LBT). If the bus is busy, defer transmission.\n2. Collision Detection: Listen While Talk (LWT). The sender continues monitoring voltage levels while transmitting. If two stations transmit simultaneously, signals collide, causing a voltage spike. The detecting station aborts immediately and broadcasts a 32-bit Jam Signal.\n\nWhy Minimum Frame Size is Mandatory:\nA station must continue transmitting long enough to detect a collision from the furthest station across the entire cable length before its transmission finishes. If a frame is too small, transmission finishes before the collision signal returns, and the sender falsely assumes successful delivery!\n\nMathematical Condition:\n$$T_{\\text{trans}} \\ge 2 \\times T_{\\text{prop}}$$\n$$\\frac{L_{\\text{min}}}{R} \\ge 2 \\times \\frac{d}{v} \\implies L_{\\text{min}} = 2 \\times R \\times \\frac{d}{v}$$\n\nWhere $2 \\times T_{\\text{prop}}$ is the Slot Time (Round-Trip Propagation Time). In classic 10 Mbps Ethernet over 2.5 km coaxial cable: Slot Time = $51.2 \\,\\mu\\text{s}$.\n$$L_{\\text{min}} = 10 \\times 10^6 \\text{ bps} \\times 51.2 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{ s} = 512 \\text{ bits} = 64 \\text{ bytes}$$\nHence, the mandatory minimum Ethernet frame size is 64 bytes (padded if payload is smaller).\n\nBinary Exponential Backoff Algorithm:\nAfter collision $k$ ($k \\le 10$):\n1. Pick a random integer $r$ from range $[0, 2^k - 1]$.\n2. Back off (wait) for $r \\times \\text{Slot Time}$ before attempting retransmission.\n3. After 16 consecutive collisions, transmission is aborted and an error reported.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "CSMA/CD uses Listen Before Talk and Listen While Talk to detect shared bus collisions.",
      "Minimum frame size condition: $T_{\\text{trans}} \\ge 2 \\times T_{\\text{prop}}$ ensures sender detects collisions before finishing transmission.",
      "Standard Ethernet minimum frame size is 64 bytes (512 bits) based on 10 Mbps over 2.5 km maximum diameter.",
      "Binary Exponential Backoff picks random slot delays from $[0, 2^k - 1]$, reducing collision probabilities dynamically."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Worst-Case Collision Detection Scenario */\n\nTime t = 0: Station A begins transmitting frame.\nStation A [==========>                     ] Station B\n                                               |\nTime t = T_prop - epsilon:                     v\nStation B senses medium (still quiet!), begins transmitting!\nStation A [====================><==========] Station B (COLLISION at B!)\n\nCollision signal travels back towards A:\nArrives at Station A at Time t = 2 * T_prop!\n\nConclusion: Station A MUST still be transmitting at t = 2 * T_prop\nto realize that its own frame collided!\nTherefore: Transmission Time >= 2 * Propagation Time!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'Do modern full-duplex Gigabit/10G Ethernet switches still use CSMA/CD?' NO! Modern Ethernet uses dedicated full-duplex twisted-pair or fiber links with switch buffers. There are zero collisions, so CSMA/CD is completely disabled in full-duplex mode!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Cisco",
      "Broadcom",
      "Intel",
      "Amazon"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "is_hidden": false,
