    "id": "int-cn-025",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol): Ping, Traceroute mechanics, and ICMP Type/Code error reporting.",
    "answer": "ICMP (RFC 792) is a network-layer error-reporting and diagnostic protocol encapsulated directly within IP datagrams (IP Protocol `1`). It does not transport user data; it conveys operational status about network reachability and errors.\n\nKey ICMP Types and Codes:\n- Type 8, Code 0: Echo Request (Ping)\n- Type 0, Code 0: Echo Reply (Ping response)\n- Type 3: Destination Unreachable (Code 0: Net Unreachable, Code 1: Host Unreachable, Code 3: Port Unreachable, Code 4: Fragmentation Needed & DF set)\n- Type 11, Code 0: Time Exceeded (TTL expired in transit)\n- Type 5: Redirect (Informs host of a better first-hop router)\n\nHow Ping Works:\n`ping <target>` transmits an ICMP Echo Request (Type 8) with an identifier and sequence number. The destination replies with an ICMP Echo Reply (Type 0). The sender computes Round-Trip Time (RTT) and packet loss percentage.\n\nHow Traceroute Works (Ingenious abuse of TTL!):\nTraceroute discovers every intermediate router along the network path:\n1. Probe 1: Transmits a packet with TTL = 1 (UDP to high-numbered port on Linux/macOS, or ICMP Echo on Windows `tracert`).\n2. Router 1 decrements TTL to 0, drops the packet, and sends back an ICMP Time Exceeded (Type 11 Code 0). The source records Router 1's IP and RTT.\n3. Probe 2: Transmits packet with TTL = 2. Router 1 decrements to 1; Router 2 decrements to 0, drops it, and replies with ICMP Type 11. Source records Router 2.\n4. Process repeats incrementing TTL by 1 on each step.\n5. Destination Reached: When the packet reaches the target destination host, the host does not drop it for TTL! On Linux, because the target port was a random high port (e.g., 33434) with no listening service, the destination replies with ICMP Destination Unreachable / Port Unreachable (Type 3 Code 3). Traceroute detects this and terminates!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "ICMP provides network-layer diagnostics and error reporting encapsulated in IP datagrams.",
      "Ping uses Echo Request (Type 8) and Echo Reply (Type 0) to measure reachability and RTT.",
      "Traceroute discovers path routers by incrementing IP TTL from 1 upwards, collecting ICMP Type 11 replies.",
      "Traceroute terminates when the destination replies with ICMP Type 3 Code 3 (Port Unreachable)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Traceroute Mechanics */\n\nHost A                          Router 1        Router 2        Target Host B\n  |                                |               |                  |\n  |--- Packet (TTL=1) ------------>| (TTL=0! Drop!)                   |\n  |<-- ICMP Type 11 (Time Exceeded)|                                  |\n  |                                                                   |\n  |--- Packet (TTL=2) ------------>|--------------->| (TTL=0! Drop!)  |\n  |<-- ICMP Type 11 (Time Exceeded)-----------------|                 |\n  |                                                                   |\n  |--- Packet (TTL=3) ----------------------------------------------->| (Arrived!)\n  |<-- ICMP Type 3 Code 3 (Port Unreachable)--------------------------| (Done!)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer trick: 'Why do some hops in traceroute output appear as * * *?' Answer: The intermediate router has disabled ICMP Time Exceeded generation, rate-limits ICMP responses, or a firewall is blocking inbound/outbound ICMP traffic!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Cisco"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
