    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain Anycast Routing: How do Anycast DNS (8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1) and CDNs route requests to the nearest edge server?",
    "answer": "Anycast is a network addressing and routing technique where the exact same IP address (e.g., Cloudflare's 1.1.1.1 or Google's 8.8.8.8) is assigned to hundreds of geographically distributed server nodes (Points of Presence - POPs) across the planet.\n\nHow Anycast Works via BGP:\n1. Every edge datacenter (POP in London, Tokyo, New York, Sydney) advertises the identical IP prefix (e.g., 1.1.1.0/24) to its local transit Internet Service Providers via BGP.\n2. Global BGP routers propagate these routes. Because BGP selects paths with the shortest AS-Path and lowest transit metrics, a client in London querying 1.1.1.1 is automatically routed to Cloudflare's London POP (shortest BGP path).\n3. Simultaneously, a client in Tokyo querying 1.1.1.1 is routed to Cloudflare's Tokyo POP.\n4. Neither client nor DNS resolver needs any geolocation logic; standard BGP routing delivers packets to the network-topological 'closest' server.\n\nDDoS Mitigation Advantage:\nIn a distributed denial of service (DDoS) attack involving millions of botnet nodes, Anycast geographically segments the attack traffic. Attackers in Europe hit European POPs, while Asian bots hit Asian POPs. No single central server is overwhelmed, absorbing terabits of malicious volumetric traffic naturally at the edge.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Anycast assigns the identical IP address to multiple physical servers across the globe.",
      "BGP automatically routes user traffic to the topologically closest datacenter.",
      "Powers global public DNS resolvers (8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1) and Content Delivery Networks (CDNs).",
      "Naturally localizes and mitigates DDoS attacks by dispersing botnet traffic across edge POPs."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Anycast Architecture via BGP */\n\n                 [ Client in London ]                     [ Client in Tokyo ]\n                          |                                        |\n                 (Shortest BGP Path)                      (Shortest BGP Path)\n                          v                                        v\n               [ POP London: 1.1.1.1 ]                  [ POP Tokyo: 1.1.1.1 ]\n                          ^                                        ^\n                          |                                        |\n                          +------- Same Public IP (1.1.1.1) -------+\n                          |   Advertised by BGP in both locations  |"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'Why was Anycast historically avoided for stateful TCP connections?' Because if a BGP route flaps mid-connection, subsequent TCP packets route to a different POP that has no record of the TCP connection state, causing an immediate TCP RST! Modern Anycast CDNs solve this using BGP route dampening and Maglev/Katran consistent hashing load balancers.",
