    "id": "int-cn-032",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain Multicast Routing: IGMP vs PIM (Dense Mode vs Sparse Mode).",
    "answer": "IP Multicast delivers a single stream of packets simultaneously to a group of interested receivers (Class D address range: 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255) without duplicating packets over shared backbone links. Widely used in financial market data tickers (NASDAQ/NYSE feeds) and IPTV broadcasts.\n\n1. Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP):\n- Host-to-Router protocol operating at Layer 3.\n- End hosts use IGMP Join and Leave messages to notify their directly attached local router that they want to receive traffic for a specific multicast group (e.g., 239.1.1.1).\n- IGMP Snooping: Switches listen to IGMP messages to forward multicast traffic only to specific interested switch ports, preventing multicast from flooding the entire switch segment like broadcast.\n\n2. Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM):\n- Router-to-Router multicast routing protocol that builds multicast distribution trees:\n- PIM Dense Mode (PIM-DM - 'Flood and Prune'): Assumes almost all subnets have interested receivers. It broadcasts multicast traffic everywhere; routers with no interested downstream receivers send 'Prune' messages back upstream. Inefficient for sparse groups.\n- PIM Sparse Mode (PIM-SM - 'Explicit Join'): Assumes very few subnets want the stream. Routers with interested receivers must explicitly send 'Join' messages toward a central Rendezvous Point (RP) router, building a Shared Distribution Tree (*, G) or Source-Specific Tree (S, G). Dominant standard in enterprise networks.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Multicast transmits a single packet stream to an arbitrary group of receivers using Class D IPs.",
      "IGMP operates between local hosts and their first-hop router to report group membership.",
      "PIM Dense Mode uses 'flood and prune', suitable only for networks where most nodes want data.",
      "PIM Sparse Mode uses explicit joins toward a Rendezvous Point (RP) to build on-demand distribution trees."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Multicast PIM-SM Distribution Tree */\n\n[ Market Data Publisher ] (Source S)\n            |\n    [ First-Hop Router ]\n            |\n    [ Rendezvous Point (RP) ] <--- Receives explicit PIM Join (*, G)\n            |\n    [ Last-Hop Router ]\n            | (IGMP Join Report from Host)\n    [ Financial Trader PC ] (Receiver)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "High-Frequency Trading (HFT) firms rely entirely on IP Multicast over UDP to receive stock market order book feeds simultaneously with microsecond latency, using kernel bypass (Solarflare OpenOnload) to process packets directly in user memory!",
