    "id": "int-cn-034",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain Transport Layer Ports: Well-Known, Registered, Dynamic/Ephemeral ranges, and the 5-Tuple Socket Pair.",
    "answer": "While the Network Layer (IP) is responsible for host-to-host delivery, the Transport Layer (TCP/UDP) is responsible for process-to-process communication. Port numbers (16-bit integers from 0 to 65,535) enable multiplexing multiple application processes on the same IP address.\n\nIANA Port Number Ranges:\n1. Well-Known Ports (0 to 1023):\n- Assigned to standard system services and core Internet protocols.\n- On UNIX-like systems, binding to a well-known port requires root/administrative privileges.\n- Examples: HTTP (80), HTTPS (443), SSH (22), DNS (53), SMTP (25), DHCP (67/68).\n2. Registered Ports (1024 to 49151):\n- Registered with IANA for specific third-party applications and vendor services; unprivileged users can bind to them.\n- Examples: MySQL (3306), PostgreSQL (5432), Redis (6379), MongoDB (27017), Kafka (9092).\n3. Dynamic / Private / Ephemeral Ports (49152 to 65535):\n- Assigned temporarily by the client operating system when establishing outbound network connections.\n- Automatically recycled when the socket connection closes. (Linux default ephemeral range: 32768 to 60999).\n\nThe 5-Tuple (Socket Pair):\nEvery network connection in an operating system is uniquely identified by a 5-tuple:\n$$\\text{Connection} = \\langle \\text{Source IP}, \\text{Source Port}, \\text{Destination IP}, \\text{Destination Port}, \\text{Protocol} \\rangle$$\nA web server listening on a single port (e.g., 443) can handle millions of simultaneous TCP connections because each client connection has a distinct client IP or ephemeral client port!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Port numbers are 16-bit integers (0 to 65535) enabling process-level multiplexing.",
      "Well-known ports (0-1023) require root privileges; Registered ports span 1024-49151.",
      "Ephemeral ports (49152-65535) are assigned dynamically by client operating systems.",
      "Connections are uniquely identified by the 5-tuple: (Src IP, Src Port, Dst IP, Dst Port, Protocol)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* The 5-Tuple Unique Connection Identifier */\n\nClient A: 192.168.1.10:52411 ---\\\n                                  +--> Server: 142.250.190.46:443 (TCP)\nClient B: 192.168.1.20:52411 ---/     (Unique by Source IP!)\n\nClient A: 192.168.1.10:52412 --------> Server: 142.250.190.46:443 (TCP)\n                                      (Unique by Source Port!)\n\nBoth 5-tuples are completely distinct in the OS TCP connection table!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer puzzle: 'Can a client establish more than 65,535 TCP connections to a single server IP:Port?' No, because ephemeral ports are limited to 65,535! BUT if the server has multiple IP addresses or the client binds to multiple local IPs, the 5-tuple changes, allowing millions of connections!",
