    "id": "int-cn-035",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Compare TCP vs UDP: Fundamental differences, header size, reliability, flow control, and trade-offs.",
    "answer": "TCP (Transmission Control Protocol - RFC 793) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol - RFC 768) represent two opposing design philosophies at the Transport Layer:\n\n1. TCP (Reliable, Connection-Oriented, Byte-Stream):\n- Connection: Requires explicit connection establishment (3-way handshake) and teardown (4-way handshake).\n- Header Overhead: 20 bytes minimum (up to 60 bytes with options).\n- Reliability: 100% guaranteed delivery, in-order packet sequencing, duplicate packet detection, and retransmission of lost segments using sequence numbers and ACKs.\n- Flow Control: Sliding window dynamically matches sender rate to receiver buffer capacity.\n- Congestion Control: Algorithms (CUBIC, BBR) prevent the sender from overwhelming intermediate network links.\n- Data Boundary: Stream-oriented. Does NOT preserve message boundaries (a sender writing 100 bytes three times may be received as one 300-byte read).\n\n2. UDP (Lightweight, Connectionless, Message-Oriented):\n- Connection: Stateless. No handshake, no teardown; packets sent immediately.\n- Header Overhead: Strictly 8 bytes fixed. Minimal latency and bandwidth waste.\n- Reliability: Best-effort. Packets can be lost, duplicated, or arrive out of order; zero OS retransmissions.\n- Flow & Congestion Control: None. Sends as fast as the application desires.\n- Data Boundary: Datagram-oriented. Preserves discrete message boundaries (one write = one discrete packet on wire).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "TCP is connection-oriented, reliable, in-order, and features flow and congestion control.",
      "UDP is connectionless, lightweight (8-byte header), best-effort, with zero retransmission latency.",
      "TCP treats data as an unstructured byte stream; UDP preserves discrete datagram boundaries.",
      "TCP handles web, financial, and database traffic; UDP powers real-time streaming, gaming, and HTTP/3."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* TCP vs UDP Comparison Matrix */\n\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Feature             | TCP (RFC 793)                 | UDP (RFC 768)                 |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Connection State    | Connection-oriented (Handshake| Connectionless (Stateless)   |\n| Reliability         | Guaranteed In-Order Delivery  | Best-effort (Loss possible)   |\n| Header Size         | 20 - 60 Bytes                 | Strictly 8 Bytes              |\n| Transmission Model  | Byte Stream (Continuous)      | Datagrams (Discrete messages) |\n| Flow Control        | Sliding Window (rwnd)         | None                          |\n| Congestion Control  | AIMD, CUBIC, BBR (cwnd)       | None                          |\n| Speed / Latency     | Higher latency (Retransmit)   | Blazing fast, minimal jitter  |\n| Typical Use Cases   | HTTP/HTTPS, SSH, FTP, DBs     | DNS, VoIP, Video, Gaming, QUIC|\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer trick: 'Why does multiplayer game state run on UDP instead of TCP?' In fast-paced games (FPS/racing), late data is completely useless data! If a player's coordinate packet from 100ms ago is lost, TCP stalls subsequent incoming packets (Head-of-Line Blocking) waiting for retransmission. UDP simply discards it and applies the freshest coordinate!",
