    "id": "int-cn-039",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain TCP Connection Teardown (Four-Way Handshake): Active vs Passive Close and Half-Closed Connections.",
    "answer": "Because a TCP connection is full-duplex (two independent simplex byte streams flowing in opposite directions), each direction must be closed independently. This results in a 4-Way Handshake (or 3-way if FIN and ACK are piggbacked).\n\nFour-Way Teardown Steps:\n1. Active Close: Host A finishes sending data and calls `close()` or `shutdown()`. Host A transmits a segment with `FIN = 1`, `Seq = U`. Host A enters `FIN_WAIT_1`.\n2. Passive Close: Host B receives FIN. Host B transmits `ACK = 1`, `Ack = U + 1`. Host B enters `CLOSE_WAIT`. Host A receives ACK and enters `FIN_WAIT_2`.\n- Half-Closed Connection State: The connection is now half-closed! Host A can no longer transmit any data, but Host B CAN STILL SEND remaining data bytes to Host A, and Host A will acknowledge them!\n3. Host B finishes sending its remaining data: Host B transmits a segment with `FIN = 1`, `Seq = V`. Host B enters `LAST_ACK`.\n4. Host A receives FIN: Host A transmits `ACK = 1`, `Ack = V + 1`. Host A enters `TIME_WAIT` state for $2 \\times \\text{MSL}$ (Maximum Segment Lifetime). Host B receives the final ACK and transitions directly to `CLOSED`!\n\nclose() vs shutdown(SHUT_WR):\n- `close(fd)`: Closes both read and write channels. Any unread data is discarded, and if the remote end sends more data, the OS sends an immediate `RST`.\n- `shutdown(fd, SHUT_WR)`: Closes only the write channel (sends FIN) while leaving the read channel open, cleanly implementing half-closed connections.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "TCP teardown requires 4 steps because each half-duplex communication stream closes independently.",
      "Half-closed connections allow the passive closer to transmit remaining data after receiving a FIN.",
      "Active close initiator transitions: FIN_WAIT_1 -> FIN_WAIT_2 -> TIME_WAIT -> CLOSED.",
      "Passive close responder transitions: CLOSE_WAIT -> LAST_ACK -> CLOSED."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* TCP Four-Way Teardown Exchange */\n\nHost A (Active Close)                                Host B (Passive Close)\n  |                                                            |\n  |--- [FIN, Seq=U] ------------------------------------------>| (Enters CLOSE_WAIT)\n  |    (Enters FIN_WAIT_1)                                     |\n  |<-- [ACK, Ack=U+1] -----------------------------------------| (Kernel auto-ACKs)\n  |    (Enters FIN_WAIT_2)                                     |\n  |                                                            |\n  |    <==== Half-Closed: B can still send data to A ====>     |\n  |                                                            |\n  |<-- [FIN, Seq=V] -------------------------------------------| (Calls close())\n  |                                                            | (Enters LAST_ACK)\n  |--- [ACK, Ack=V+1] ---------------------------------------->|\n  |    (Enters TIME_WAIT: waits 2*MSL)                         | (Enters CLOSED)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer trick: 'Can a TCP connection teardown be 3 packets instead of 4?' Yes! If Host B has no pending data to send when it receives FIN, its TCP stack can combine its ACK and its own FIN into a single [FIN, ACK] segment!",
