    "id": "int-cn-040",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Walk through the complete TCP State Machine: Transitions from LISTEN to CLOSED.",
    "answer": "The TCP finite state machine (FSM - RFC 793) defines 11 distinct operational states:\n\n1. CLOSED: Fictional base state; no connection exists.\n2. LISTEN: Server waiting for incoming connection requests on a bound port.\n3. SYN_SENT: Client has sent a SYN segment and is awaiting SYN-ACK.\n4. SYN_RECEIVED: Server received a SYN, sent a SYN-ACK, and is awaiting the final ACK.\n5. ESTABLISHED: Connection fully open; bidirectional data transfer underway.\n\nTeardown States (Active Close Path):\n6. FIN_WAIT_1: Initiator sent FIN, waiting for ACK or FIN from peer.\n7. FIN_WAIT_2: Initiator received ACK for its FIN; waiting for remote peer's FIN.\n8. CLOSING: Rare simultaneous close where both sides sent FIN at the same instant.\n9. TIME_WAIT: Initiator received peer's FIN and sent final ACK; waits $2 \\times \\text{MSL}$ before transitioning to CLOSED.\n\nTeardown States (Passive Close Path):\n10. CLOSE_WAIT: Receiver received FIN from peer, sent ACK; waiting for local application to close the socket.\n11. LAST_ACK: Receiver sent its own FIN, waiting for peer's final ACK before entering CLOSED.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "TCP finite state machine defines 11 distinct states managing connection lifecycle.",
      "ESTABLISHED represents an open, active bidirectional communication channel.",
      "Active close transitions through FIN_WAIT_1, FIN_WAIT_2, and TIME_WAIT.",
      "Passive close transitions through CLOSE_WAIT and LAST_ACK."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* TCP State Machine Lifecycle Transitions */\n\nServer:  CLOSED ---> LISTEN ---> SYN_RECEIVED ---> ESTABLISHED\nClient:  CLOSED ---> SYN_SENT -------------------> ESTABLISHED\n\nTeardown:\nActive Closer:  ESTABLISHED ---> FIN_WAIT_1 ---> FIN_WAIT_2 ---> TIME_WAIT ---> CLOSED\nPassive Closer: ESTABLISHED ---> CLOSE_WAIT --------------> LAST_ACK   ---> CLOSED"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Run `netstat -nat` or `ss -tan` on a live Linux server! Being able to inspect connection states (`SYN_RECV`, `ESTAB`, `TIME_WAIT`, `CLOSE_WAIT`) in terminal commands shows practical system debugging skills.",
