    "id": "int-cn-043",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain TCP Flow Control: Sliding Window protocol, Zero Window Probe (ZWP), and Window Scaling (RFC 1323).",
    "answer": "Flow Control prevents a fast sender from overwhelming a slow receiver's memory buffer.\n\nSliding Window Flow Control:\nThe receiver advertises its available buffer space in the 16-bit `Window Size` field (`rwnd` - Receive Window) of every outgoing ACK packet:\n$$\\text{Max Unacknowledged Data Allowed} \\le \\text{rwnd}$$\nAs the receiver's application consumes data, `rwnd` slides forward. If the application stops reading, the buffer fills and `rwnd` shrinks.\n\nZero Window & Zero Window Probe (ZWP):\nIf the receiver's buffer fills completely, it advertises `rwnd = 0` (Zero Window). The sender MUST cease data transmission immediately.\nDeadlock Hazard:\nWhen the receiver's application finally reads data and frees buffer space, it sends an ACK advertising `rwnd > 0`. What if this ACK packet is lost on the network?\nThe sender is waiting for the window update; the receiver is waiting for data. Deadlock!\nSolution (Zero Window Probe):\nWhen `rwnd = 0`, the sender starts a Persist Timer. When the timer expires, the sender transmits a 1-byte Zero Window Probe (ZWP) segment. The receiver is forced to reply with an ACK re-advertising its current `rwnd`, safely breaking any lost-ACK deadlocks.\n\nWindow Scaling (RFC 1323):\nThe original TCP header allocates strictly 16 bits for Window Size, capping the maximum window at $2^{16} - 1 = 65,535$ bytes (64 KB).\nOn modern Gigabit and 100G networks with high Bandwidth-Delay Products (BDP), a 64 KB window throttles throughput to a tiny fraction of link capacity!\nSolution: The Window Scale option negotiated during the 3-way handshake defines a scale factor bit-shift (up to 14 bits), expanding the effective maximum receive window to $65,535 \\times 2^{14} \\approx 1 \\text{ Gigabyte}$!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "TCP flow control matches transmission rate to receiver buffer capacity via the `rwnd` advertisement.",
      "Zero Window (`rwnd = 0`) halts sender transmission when receiver buffers fill.",
      "Zero Window Probes (ZWP) periodically poll the receiver using persist timers to prevent lost-ACK deadlocks.",
      "Window Scaling (RFC 1323) shifts the 16-bit window up to 14 bits, supporting up to 1GB receive windows."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* TCP Window Scale Calculation */\n\nWithout Window Scale (16-bit header limit):\nMax Window Size = 65,535 bytes = 64 KB\n\nWith Window Scale (Shift Count = 7 in SYN option):\nHeader Window value: 32,768\nActual Effective Window = 32,768 * 2^7 = 32,768 * 128 = 4,194,304 bytes (4 MB!)\n\nEnables full link saturation across high-latency cross-country links!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer question: 'Can Window Scaling be negotiated mid-connection?' NO! The Window Scale option can ONLY be sent during the initial 3-way handshake (SYN and SYN-ACK packets). If omitted in SYN, neither side can use window scaling for the entire lifetime of that connection!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Apple",
      "Microsoft"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
