    "id": "int-cn-044",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "What is Silly Window Syndrome and how do Clark's Solution and Nagle's Algorithm eliminate it?",
    "answer": "Silly Window Syndrome (SWS) is a catastrophic degradation in TCP efficiency where data is transmitted in tiny, inefficient dribbles (e.g., 1 byte of payload encapsulated inside a 40-byte TCP/IP header, yielding a ridiculous 97.5% protocol overhead!).\n\nCauses & Solutions:\n1. Receiver-Induced Silly Window Syndrome:\n- Cause: The receiver's buffer is completely full (`rwnd = 0`). The application consumes a tiny amount (e.g., 1 byte via `read()`). The naive receiver immediately advertises `rwnd = 1`! The sender eagerly transmits a 1-byte segment. The buffer fills again (`rwnd = 0`). The cycle repeats indefinitely!\n- Clark's Solution (Receiver Side):\nThe receiver is forbidden from advertising small window increments. The receiver MUST advertise `rwnd = 0` until it can open a window of at least:\n$$\\text{Advertised Window} \\ge \\min(\\text{Full Buffer Capacity} / 2, \\text{MSS})$$\n\n2. Sender-Induced Silly Window Syndrome:\n- Cause: An application generates data byte-by-byte (e.g., an interactive typing console writing 1 character at a time). The naive sender immediately wraps each individual byte into a 41-byte packet.\n- Nagle's Algorithm (Sender Side - RFC 896):\nIf there is unacknowledged in-flight data on the connection, buffer newly generated small writes until:\n1. A full Maximum Segment Size (MSS) chunk has accumulated, OR\n2. An ACK arrives acknowledging all previously sent unacknowledged segments.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Silly Window Syndrome causes data to transmit in tiny 1-byte chunks with 97%+ header overhead.",
      "Receiver-induced SWS is solved by Clark's Solution: advertise window only when at least $\text{MSS}$ or half-buffer is free.",
      "Sender-induced SWS is solved by Nagle's Algorithm: buffer small data until an ACK arrives or an MSS accumulates.",
      "Combined, they guarantee that TCP streams exchange large, bandwidth-efficient segments."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Nagle's Algorithm Logic (Sender Side) */\n\nif (data_to_send >= MSS) {\n    send_segment_immediately(); // Full-sized segment: send!\n} else {\n    if (all_previous_data_acked()) {\n        send_segment_immediately(); // No in-flight data: send small segment!\n    } else {\n        buffer_data_in_socket_until_ack_or_mss(); // Coalesce data!\n    }\n}"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Remember: Clark's solution fixes the receiver; Nagle's algorithm fixes the sender!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Cisco",
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
