    "id": "int-cn-055",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain HTTP/1.0 vs HTTP/1.1: Persistent Connections (Keep-Alive), Pipelining, Chunked Transfer Encoding, and the Host header.",
    "answer": "HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is the foundational application-layer protocol of the World Wide Web.\n\nHTTP/1.0 (RFC 1945 - 1996):\n- Short-Lived Connections: Every single HTTP request required opening a brand-new TCP connection (3-way handshake) and closing it immediately upon response delivery (4-way teardown).\n- Extreme Overhead: Loading a webpage with 50 images required 50 separate TCP handshakes and slow-start warmups!\n- No mandatory Host header: Virtual hosting (multiple domains on 1 IP) was impossible.\n\nHTTP/1.1 (RFC 2616 / 7230 - 1999) Major Innovations:\n1. Persistent Connections (Default Keep-Alive):\nConnections remain open by default (`Connection: keep-alive`). Multiple consecutive HTTP requests and responses can reuse the SAME established TCP connection, eliminating handshake latency and preserving TCP congestion window scale.\n2. Mandatory `Host` Header:\nClients MUST include `Host: example.com` in every request, enabling Web servers to host thousands of distinct websites on a single public IP address (Virtual Hosting).\n3. Chunked Transfer Encoding (`Transfer-Encoding: chunked`):\nAllows servers to stream dynamically generated responses of unknown size in sequential chunks (`<hex-chunk-size>\\r\\n<data>\\r\\n...0\\r\\n\\r\\n`) without having to calculate a `Content-Length` header upfront.\n4. HTTP Pipelining:\nAllowed clients to send multiple HTTP requests without waiting for individual responses. However, responses were strictly required to return in the EXACT order requested, leading to severe Head-of-Line blocking; consequently, all major browsers disabled pipelining by default!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "HTTP/1.0 opened and closed a new TCP connection for every single resource.",
      "HTTP/1.1 introduced persistent connections (`keep-alive`) by default to reuse TCP sockets.",
      "Mandatory `Host` header enabled virtual hosting of multiple domains on one IP address.",
      "Chunked Transfer Encoding allowed streaming dynamic content without pre-calculating Content-Length."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* HTTP/1.1 Chunked Transfer Encoding Response */\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: text/plain\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\n\n7\\r\\n         (Chunk size = 7 bytes)\nMozilla\\r\\n\n9\\r\\n         (Chunk size = 9 bytes)\nDeveloper\\r\\n\n0\\r\\n         (Terminating chunk: size 0)\n\\r\\n"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'What happens in HTTP/1.1 if a request omits the Host header?' The server MUST reject the request immediately with a `400 Bad Request` status code, as mandated by RFC 7230!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Meta"
    ],
