    "id": "int-cn-064",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Compare Real-Time Web Communication: Short Polling vs Long Polling vs Server-Sent Events (SSE) vs WebSockets.",
    "answer": "Web applications requiring real-time updates (chat, dashboards, live GPS) choose from four primary communication architectures:\n\n1. Short Polling:\n- Client repeatedly sends HTTP GET requests at fixed intervals (e.g., every 2 seconds).\n- Flaw: Extreme waste. 95%+ of requests return empty responses, saturating server CPU with connection overhead and TCP handshakes.\n\n2. Long Polling (Comet):\n- Client issues an HTTP request. The server does NOT reply immediately; it holds the request open until new data arrives or a timeout occurs.\n- Once data arrives, the server replies, and the client immediately issues another long-poll request.\n- Better efficiency than short polling, but still incurs repeated HTTP header overhead on each message.\n\n3. Server-Sent Events (SSE - RFC 8895):\n- Unidirectional (Server -> Client only) streaming over a single long-lived HTTP connection (`Content-Type: text/event-stream`).\n- Built-in automatic reconnection, event IDs, and reconnection retry handling in browser `EventSource` API.\n- Runs over standard HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 (multiplexed natively without extra TCP connections), passing easily through corporate firewalls.\n- Ideal for stock tickers, sports scores, and AI LLM streaming completions (OpenAI ChatGPT API).\n\n4. WebSockets (RFC 6455):\n- Full-Duplex, bidirectional, persistent TCP connection over a single socket (`ws://` or `wss://`).\n- Starts with an HTTP Upgrade handshake, then switches to a lightweight binary framing protocol (2 to 10 bytes overhead per message).\n- Minimal latency and overhead; ideal for collaborative whiteboards, multiplayer gaming, and bidirectional chat.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Short Polling wastes bandwidth with periodic empty GETs; Long Polling holds connections open until updates occur.",
      "Server-Sent Events (SSE) provides lightweight unidirectional server-to-client streaming over HTTP.",
      "WebSockets provides full-duplex bidirectional communication with minimal 2-byte frame overhead.",
      "OpenAI ChatGPT uses SSE (`text/event-stream`) for streaming tokens because the flow is strictly server-to-client."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Real-Time Protocols Comparison Matrix */\n\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Feature           | Short Polling      | Long Polling       | Server-Sent Events | WebSockets         |\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Directionality    | Half-Duplex (Req)  | Half-Duplex (Hold) | Unidirectional S->C| Full-Duplex (Both) |\n| Protocol          | Standard HTTP      | Standard HTTP      | HTTP (event-stream)| WS (TCP Upgrade)   |\n| Overhead          | Enormous (Headers) | High per message   | Low (HTTP/2 multi) | Ultra-Low (2-10B)  |\n| Browser API       | setInterval/fetch  | Recursive fetch    | EventSource        | WebSocket          |\n| Auto-Reconnect    | Manual             | Manual             | Native Built-in    | Manual             |\n| Best Use Case     | Legacy low-churn   | Chat fallback      | LLM stream/Dashbrd | Gaming / Trading   |\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If an interviewer asks: 'Would you use WebSockets or SSE for an AI streaming chatbot interface?' Answer SSE! WebSockets is bidirectional overkill; SSE works natively over HTTP/2, supports built-in reconnection, handles corporate proxy traversal cleanly, and requires no custom heartbeat management!",
