    "id": "int-cn-065",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain the WebSocket Protocol: The HTTP 101 Switching Protocols Handshake, Frame Masking, and Heartbeats.",
    "answer": "The WebSocket Protocol (RFC 6455) provides persistent, full-duplex communication channels over a single TCP connection.\n\n1. The HTTP 101 Upgrade Handshake:\nTo guarantee compatibility with standard web infrastructure and port 80/443 firewalls, WebSocket connections begin as standard HTTP GET requests:\n- Client Request:\n  - `GET /chat HTTP/1.1`\n  - `Host: server.example.com`\n  - `Upgrade: websocket`\n  - `Connection: Upgrade`\n  - `Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==` (16-byte random nonce base64-encoded)\n  - `Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13`\n- Server Response:\n  - `HTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols`\n  - `Upgrade: websocket`\n  - `Connection: Upgrade`\n  - `Sec-WebSocket-Accept: s3pPLMBiTxaQ9kYGzzhZRbK+xOo=`\n  - Calculation: Server appends the magic GUID string `258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-C5AB0DC85B11` to the client's key, computes SHA-1, and base64-encodes the result! Proves the server speaks the WebSocket protocol.\n\n2. Frame Masking (Client-to-Server Security):\nAll frames sent from CLIENT to SERVER MUST be masked with a 32-bit random masking key (XOR encryption).\nWhy? To prevent Cache Poisoning Attacks on legacy intermediate HTTP proxies! If frames were unmasked, a malicious script could craft a WebSocket payload mimicking an HTTP GET request, tricking a caching proxy into poisoning its web cache. Server-to-client frames are unmasked.\n\n3. Heartbeats & Control Frames:\nWebSockets define lightweight control frames:\n- Ping (`0x9`) and Pong (`0xA`): Used for keep-alive heartbeats and detecting dead TCP connections.\n- Close (`0x8`): Graceful bidirectional teardown handshake.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "WebSockets start as an HTTP request and upgrade to raw TCP binary framing via `101 Switching Protocols`.",
      "`Sec-WebSocket-Accept` hashes the client key with a standardized GUID to confirm protocol support.",
      "Client-to-server frames MUST be XOR-masked to prevent proxy cache poisoning exploits.",
      "Built-in Ping (`0x9`) and Pong (`0xA`) control frames handle connection heartbeats and dead-peer detection."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* WebSocket Handshake & Frame Structure */\n\nClient Handshake:\nGET /chat HTTP/1.1\nHost: example.com\nUpgrade: websocket\nConnection: Upgrade\nSec-WebSocket-Key: x3JJHMbDL1EzLkh9GBhXDw==\nSec-WebSocket-Version: 13\n\nServer Response:\nHTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols\nUpgrade: websocket\nConnection: Upgrade\nSec-WebSocket-Accept: HSmrc0sMlYUkAGmm5OPpG2HaGWk=\n\n[ Connection switched from HTTP to raw WebSocket binary framing! ]\nFrame: [FIN, Opcode: 4b] [Masked: 1b, Length: 7b] [Masking Key: 4B] [Payload XORed]"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'What happens if a client sends an unmasked frame to a compliant WebSocket server?' The server MUST immediately abort the connection and send a close frame with status code 1002 (Protocol Error)!",
