    "id": "int-cn-081",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain DDoS Attack Classifications: Volumetric (NTP/DNS amplification) vs Protocol (SYN Flood) vs Application Layer (HTTP Flood, Slowloris).",
    "answer": "Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks overwhelm targets by coordinating millions of compromised botnet nodes across three distinct layers:\n\n1. Volumetric Attacks (Layer 3/4 - Saturates Link Bandwidth):\n- Goal: Consume all incoming Internet bandwidth (e.g., 500 Gbps to 3+ Tbps), clogging the target's ISP transit pipes.\n- Amplification & Reflection Attacks (UDP):\n  Attacker sends tiny spoofed UDP requests with the VICTIM'S IP as the source address to thousands of publicly accessible servers:\n  - DNS Amplification: Small 50-byte query generates a 3,000-byte DNSSEC response (60x amplification!).\n  - NTP Amplification: Sending `monlist` command (234 bytes) returns a 48 KB response containing the last 600 connected clients (206x amplification factor!).\n  - Memcached Amplification: Can achieve an incredible 50,000x amplification factor!\n  - Mitigation: BGP Anycast scrubbing centers, Anycast dispersion, and ISP egress source IP verification (BCP 38).\n\n2. Protocol Attacks (Layer 3/4 - Exhausts Network Equipment State Tables):\n- Goal: Exhaust finite connection state tables in intermediate stateful devices (firewalls, load balancers, router NAT tables).\n- Examples: SYN Floods (fills SYN queue), Ping of Death, Smurf attacks.\n- Measured in: Packets per second (Mpps).\n- Mitigation: Stateless SYN Cookies, hardware anti-DDoS firewalls.\n\n3. Application Layer Attacks (Layer 7 - Exhausts Application CPU/RAM):\n- Goal: Overwhelm web servers and backend database pools with seemingly legitimate HTTP requests.\n- Examples:\n  - HTTP Flood: Thousands of bots hammering expensive database search endpoints (e.g., `POST /api/search?q=*`).\n  - Slowloris: Holding thousands of HTTP connections open indefinitely using trickle headers.\n- Measured in: Requests per second (RPS).\n- Mitigation: Web Application Firewalls (WAF), CAPTCHAs, behavioral rate limiting, and CDN edge challenge pages.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Volumetric attacks (NTP/DNS amplification) saturate physical bandwidth using UDP spoofing.",
      "Protocol attacks (SYN floods) exhaust connection state tables in firewalls and operating systems.",
      "Application attacks (HTTP floods, Slowloris) exhaust backend CPU and database connection pools.",
      "Defense requires multi-layer mitigation: Anycast scrubbing, SYN cookies, and Layer 7 WAF rate limiting."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* UDP Amplification Attack Architecture */\n\nAttacker (Botnet)\n    |\n    | 1. Sends 50-byte UDP Query (Spoofed Source IP = Victim's IP!)\n    v\n[ Open DNS Resolvers / NTP Servers ]\n    |\n    | 2. Replicating & Amplifying by 50x - 200x!\n    v\n[ Victim Network Gateway ] <=== Flodded with 1 Tbps of Unsolicited Traffic!\n(Transit pipe saturated! Legitimate users blocked!)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Why are UDP protocols vulnerable to reflection attacks while TCP is not? Because UDP is connectionless and does not verify source IPs! In TCP, a spoofed SYN packet receives a SYN-ACK sent to the spoofed IP, which immediately sends an RST, aborting the connection before any amplification can occur!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Cloudflare",
      "AWS Shield",
      "Google",
      "Akamai"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
