    "id": "int-cn-095",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "What causes TCP Reset (RST) packets, and how do attackers perform TCP Reset Attacks?",
    "answer": "A TCP segment with the `RST` (Reset) flag set is an abrupt, ungraceful abort mechanism indicating: 'This connection has suffered an unrecoverable error or does not exist; tear down all socket state immediately!'. Zero handshakes or acknowledgments are exchanged.\n\nLegitimate Scenarios That Trigger a TCP RST:\n1. Connection to an Inactive Port: Client sends `SYN` to Port 9999. The server has no process listening on 9999. The server kernel immediately replies with `[RST, ACK]`, causing the client's `connect()` call to fail with `Connection refused` (ECONNREFUSED).\n2. Host Crash and Reboot: Server crashes abruptly and reboots. Client sends a data packet on its existing connection. The newly rebooted server has no memory of this connection in its TCB table, replying with `RST`.\n3. Closing Socket with Unread Data: An application calls `close()` while unread bytes remain in the socket receive buffer. Glibc abruptly sends an `RST` instead of a graceful `FIN` to signal data loss.\n4. Firewall / Load Balancer Timeout: An idle connection is purged from a stateful firewall table; subsequent packets trigger an RST.\n\nTCP Reset Attack (Censorship & Hijacking):\n- An in-path or on-path eavesdropper (e.g., the Great Firewall of China or a malicious Wi-Fi attacker) monitors unencrypted TCP traffic.\n- When it detects forbidden keywords (e.g., banned domain names or search queries), the censor injects a forged TCP packet with `RST = 1`, spoofed to appear as if it came from the server.\n- The client receives the spoofed RST and immediately tears down the connection!\n- Defense: End-to-end TLS encryption and TCP Authentication Option (TCP-AO - RFC 5925) or modern QUIC.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "TCP RST forcefully aborts a connection without graceful teardown handshakes.",
      "Triggered when connecting to closed ports (`Connection refused`) or sending to crashed hosts.",
      "Closing sockets with unread data in receive buffers emits an RST.",
      "TCP Reset Attacks inject forged RST packets with valid sequence numbers to censor connections."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* TCP RST Injection Attack */\n\nClient                               Attacker (In-Path Censor)               Web Server\n  |--- HTTP GET /banned-topic -------->|------------------------------------>|\n  |                                    | (Inspects packet payload)           |\n  |<-- Forged TCP [RST] ---------------|                                     |\n  |    (Spoofed Src IP = Web Server)   |                                     |\n  |                                    |                                     |\nClient tears down connection!          |                                     |\n\"Connection Reset by Peer!\"            |                                     |"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer puzzle: 'Why does an injected TCP RST packet need a valid sequence number?' A TCP stack silently drops any RST packet whose sequence number falls outside the current receive window! To successfully kill a connection, an attacker must sniff or predict an in-window sequence number.",
