    "id": "int-cn-098",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "Explain WebRTC Internals: SDP Offer/Answer, SRTP encryption, and SCTP Data Channels.",
    "answer": "WebRTC (Web Real-Time Communication - W3C / RFC 8825) enables peer-to-peer audio, video, and arbitrary data streaming directly between browser clients with sub-100ms latency without installing plugins or routing media through central servers.\n\nCore Architectural Subsystems:\n1. Signaling (Out-of-Band):\nWebRTC does NOT specify a signaling protocol! Peers use an external communication channel (WebSockets, SSE, HTTP) to discover each other and exchange metadata.\n2. SDP Offer / Answer Exchange (Session Description Protocol - RFC 8866):\n- Peer A generates an SDP Offer containing supported audio/video codecs (Opus, VP9, H.264), media resolutions, and ICE candidate network paths.\n- Peer B receives the Offer and replies with an SDP Answer confirming agreeable codecs.\n3. NAT Traversal via ICE, STUN, and TURN:\nPeers gather local IPs, STUN server-mapped public IPs, and TURN relay candidates to punch holes through NAT routers and establish direct UDP connectivity.\n4. SRTP (Secure Real-Time Transport Protocol):\nAll audio and video media streams in WebRTC are MANDATORILY encrypted end-to-end using SRTP, with keys negotiated using DTLS (Datagram Transport Layer Security).\n5. SCTP over DTLS (Data Channels):\nArbitrary non-media data (chat messages, multiplayer game state, file transfers) uses Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) running on top of encrypted DTLS over UDP. Supports both reliable in-order delivery (like TCP) and unreliable out-of-order delivery (like UDP) on a per-message basis!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "WebRTC provides peer-to-peer real-time audio, video, and data streaming between browsers.",
      "Signaling is handled out-of-band (WebSockets) to exchange SDP Offer and Answer codec metadata.",
      "ICE, STUN, and TURN negotiate peer-to-peer UDP connectivity across NAT routers.",
      "Media is encrypted via SRTP; Data Channels use SCTP over DTLS for configurable reliable/unreliable messaging."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* WebRTC Architecture Stack */\n\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n| WebRTC JavaScript API (getUserMedia, RTCPeerConnection)   |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n| Voice Engine (Opus) | Video Engine (VP8/VP9/AV1) | Data   |\n+---------------------+----------------------------+--------+\n| SRTP (Media Encryption)                          | SCTP   |\n+--------------------------------------------------+--------+\n| DTLS (Key Exchange & Security)                            |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n| ICE, STUN, TURN (NAT Traversal)                           |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n| UDP Transport Layer                                       |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'Why doesn't Zoom use pure WebRTC peer-to-peer for large group calls?' Peer-to-peer mesh requires each participant to upload their video stream to EVERY other participant ($N \\times (N-1)$ connections), saturating home uplink bandwidth! Group calls use an SFU (Selective Forwarding Unit) media server in the cloud that receives 1 video upload per user and fans it out efficiently.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Zoom",
      "Discord",
      "Twilio",
      "Meta"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "is_hidden": false,
