    "id": "int-cn-099",
    "topic_id": "topic-cn",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "COMPUTER_NETWORKS",
    "subject_label": "Computer Networks",
    "title": "What is Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP): MIB, OID, Traps, and why SNMPv3 is mandatory?",
    "answer": "SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol - RFC 1157 / 3411 - UDP Ports 161/162) is the industry standard protocol used by network monitoring systems (Prometheus, Datadog, Zabbix) to monitor and configure routers, switches, and servers.\n\nCore SNMP Architecture:\n1. SNMP Manager (NMS - Network Management Station): Central monitoring server that polls devices and receives alert notifications.\n2. SNMP Agent: Software daemon running on the managed network device (switch, router) exposing metrics.\n3. Management Information Base (MIB): A hierarchical, tree-structured database of all manageable parameters on the device.\n4. Object Identifier (OID): A dot-separated string of integers identifying a specific metric in the MIB tree (e.g., `1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.10` = incoming octets on network interface).\n\nSNMP Operations:\n- `GetRequest` / `GetNextRequest` / `GetBulkRequest`: Manager queries agent for specific OID values over UDP 161.\n- `SetRequest`: Manager updates a device configuration parameter remotely.\n- `SNMP Trap` / `InformRequest`: Unsolicited alert sent by the agent to the Manager over UDP 162 when a critical event occurs (e.g., port link down, power supply failure, temperature threshold exceeded).\n\nWhy SNMPv3 is Strictly Mandatory (The Flaw of v1/v2c):\n- SNMPv1 and SNMPv2c used plaintext 'Community Strings' (e.g., `public` or `private`) as passwords with ZERO encryption or authentication! Any network sniffer could intercept the string and reconfigure core routers.\n- SNMPv3 introduced USM (User-Based Security Model): Provides strong SHA-2 cryptographic user authentication AND full AES-128/256 payload encryption, protecting network management channels from eavesdropping and tampering.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SNMP monitors and configures network hardware via Managers and local Agents over UDP 161/162.",
      "Metrics are structured in a hierarchical MIB database and addressed via Object Identifiers (OIDs).",
      "SNMP Traps are asynchronous alerts pushed by devices to the monitoring manager on hardware events.",
      "SNMPv1/v2c transmitted plaintext community strings; SNMPv3 provides mandatory SHA authentication and AES encryption."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "bash",
      "code": "/* SNMP MIB OID Hierarchy */\n\n. (Root)\n+-- 1 (iso)\n    +-- 3 (org)\n        +-- 6 (dod)\n            +-- 1 (internet)\n                +-- 2 (mgmt)\n                    +-- 1 (mib-2)\n                        +-- 1 (system)   -> 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1 (sysDescr)\n                        +-- 2 (interfaces)-> 1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.10 (ifInOctets)\n\nSNMP Polling: Manager sends GetRequest(1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.10.1) -> Switch returns byte count!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In network infrastructure interviews, mention that high-performance cloud networks (Google, Meta, Arista) are transitioning from pull-based SNMP polling to Streaming Network Telemetry via gNMI (gRPC Network Management Interface) over HTTP/2, streaming telemetry metrics continuously with sub-second resolution!",
