    "id": "int-sql-002",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain WHERE vs HAVING: Filtering rows vs groups and query performance implications.",
    "answer": "Both `WHERE` and `HAVING` filter data, but operate at fundamentally different stages of logical query execution:\n\n1. WHERE Clause:\n- Filters individual raw rows BEFORE any grouping or aggregation takes place.\n- Can utilize database B-Tree indexes on filtered columns to rapidly prune index scans.\n- CANNOT reference aggregate functions (e.g., `WHERE SUM(sales) > 1000` causes a syntax error).\n- Operates on individual row values.\n\n2. HAVING Clause:\n- Filters aggregated groups AFTER the `GROUP BY` clause has organized rows into groups.\n- Can evaluate aggregate functions (e.g., `HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3` or `HAVING AVG(price) < 50`).\n- Cannot take advantage of standard row-level indexes directly because it operates on post-aggregation group summaries.\n\nPerformance Optimization Rule:\nAlways filter as many rows as possible in the `WHERE` clause rather than waiting to filter in `HAVING`! Filtering in `WHERE` reduces the volume of rows that must be sorted and held in memory during the `GROUP BY` operation, dramatically improving query performance.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.",
      "WHERE cannot contain aggregate functions; HAVING is designed specifically for aggregate conditions.",
      "WHERE can use B-Tree indexes to eliminate rows early; HAVING operates on computed group summaries.",
      "Always push non-aggregate filters into WHERE to reduce GROUP BY memory and CPU overhead."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- POOR PERFORMANCE (Aggregates 10 million rows, then filters in HAVING):\nSELECT department_id, COUNT(*) AS emp_count\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY department_id, status\nHAVING status = 'ACTIVE' AND COUNT(*) > 10;\n\n-- OPTIMAL PERFORMANCE (Filters in WHERE first using index, aggregates 500k rows):\nSELECT department_id, COUNT(*) AS emp_count\nFROM employees\nWHERE status = 'ACTIVE'   -- Row-level filter executes BEFORE Group By!\nGROUP BY department_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 10;      -- Group-level filter executes AFTER Group By!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer trick: 'Can you have a HAVING clause without a GROUP BY clause in SQL?' YES! In standard SQL, a query with aggregates like `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders HAVING COUNT(*) > 0;` treats the entire table as a single aggregate group!",
