    "id": "int-sql-007",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain Self Joins and Non-Equi Joins with practical business use cases.",
    "answer": "1. Self Join:\nA Self Join is a regular join in which a table is joined with ITSELF. It requires assigning distinct table aliases (e.g., `employees e` and `employees m`).\nPrimary Use Cases:\n- Hierarchical Data: Finding an employee and their direct manager from the same `employees` table (`e.manager_id = m.id`).\n- Finding Pairs: Finding all pairs of customers living in the same city (`a.city = b.city AND a.id < b.id`).\n- Sequence Comparison: Comparing a user's current purchase with their previous purchase.\n\n2. Non-Equi Join:\nA join that uses comparison operators other than equality (`=`), such as `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`, `<>`, or `BETWEEN`.\nPrimary Use Cases:\n- Salary Bands / Tax Brackets: Matching an employee's salary to a tax bracket defined by lower and upper limits (`e.salary BETWEEN t.min_salary AND t.max_salary`).\n- Overlapping Time Intervals: Finding overlapping hotel room bookings or schedule collisions (`b1.start_date < b2.end_date AND b1.end_date > b2.start_date`).\n- Rolling Ranges: Joining transactions within a 7-day rolling window of each other.\n- Performance Caution: Relational databases cannot use high-speed Hash Joins on non-equi joins! They must fall back to Nested Loop joins or Range Index Scans, which can be computationally expensive on large tables.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Self Joins join a table with itself using aliases to query hierarchies and relationships.",
      "Non-Equi Joins use inequality operators (`<`, `>`, `BETWEEN`) instead of `=`. ",
      "Non-Equi Joins power range matching (tax brackets) and interval overlap detection.",
      "Non-Equi Joins cannot use Hash Joins, often requiring Nested Loops with index scans."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Self Join & Non-Equi Join Examples */\n\n-- 1. Self Join: Manager Hierarchy\nSELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager\nFROM employees e\nLEFT JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id;\n\n-- 2. Non-Equi Join: Salary Tax Brackets\nSELECT e.name, e.salary, t.bracket_name, t.tax_rate\nFROM employees e\nJOIN tax_brackets t \n  ON e.salary BETWEEN t.min_salary AND t.max_salary;\n\n-- 3. Non-Equi Self Join: Overlapping Reservations\nSELECT r1.room_id, r1.id AS res1, r2.id AS res2\nFROM reservations r1\nJOIN reservations r2 \n  ON r1.room_id = r2.room_id \n AND r1.id < r2.id                     -- Avoid matching self & duplicate pairs\n AND r1.start_date < r2.end_date \n AND r1.end_date > r2.start_date;     -- Overlap condition!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "When finding pairs with a Self Join, always use `a.id < b.id` rather than `a.id <> b.id`! Using `<` prevents pairing an entity with itself AND prevents duplicate symmetric pairs (`(A, B)` and `(B, A)`), cutting result volume in half!",
