    "id": "int-sql-008",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain Semi-Joins vs Anti-Joins: How query planners optimize EXISTS vs IN vs LEFT JOIN.",
    "answer": "Semi-Joins and Anti-Joins are relational algebra operators that do NOT exist as standalone SQL keywords, but are executed internally by database query planners:\n\n1. Semi-Join:\n- Definition: Returns rows from Table A where at least ONE matching row exists in Table B.\n- Difference from INNER JOIN: In an INNER JOIN, if a row in Table A matches 5 rows in Table B, the row in Table A is DUPLICATED 5 times in the output. In a Semi-Join, the row in Table A is returned EXACTLY ONCE, regardless of how many matches exist in Table B!\n- Written in SQL via: `WHERE EXISTS (...)` or `WHERE id IN (SELECT ...)`.\n- Query Planner Optimization: The database uses Hash Semi-Join or Merge Semi-Join. As soon as the first match is found in Table B, it short-circuits and moves to the next row of Table A without scanning further!\n\n2. Anti-Join:\n- Definition: Returns rows from Table A where NO matching row exists in Table B.\n- Written in SQL via: `WHERE NOT EXISTS (...)` or `LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right.id IS NULL`.\n- Query Planner Optimization: The database uses Hash Anti-Join or Nested Loop Anti-Join, rapidly discarding rows as soon as a match is encountered.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Semi-Join returns left rows that have at least one match in the right table without duplicating rows.",
      "Anti-Join returns left rows that have zero matches in the right table.",
      "Semi-joins execute faster than INNER JOIN + DISTINCT because they short-circuit on the first match.",
      "Query optimizers map `EXISTS` and `IN` directly to internal Hash Semi-Join operators."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Semi-Join and Anti-Join Equivalences */\n\n-- SEMI-JOIN (Returns customers who have made orders, with ZERO row duplicates):\n-- Syntax 1: EXISTS (Best practice)\nSELECT c.* FROM customers c\nWHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id);\n\n-- ANTI-JOIN (Returns customers with zero orders):\n-- Syntax 1: NOT EXISTS\nSELECT c.* FROM customers c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id);\n\n-- Syntax 2: LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL (Anti-Join Pattern)\nSELECT c.* FROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.customer_id IS NULL;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Never write `SELECT DISTINCT c.* FROM customers c JOIN orders o ...` when you just want customers who have ordered! Using `JOIN + DISTINCT` materializes a massive intermediate Cartesian set and performs an expensive sort to deduplicate. Writing `WHERE EXISTS` triggers a Semi-Join with zero duplication and instant short-circuiting!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Oracle",
      "PostgreSQL",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
