    "id": "int-sql-009",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain GROUPING SETS, ROLLUP, and CUBE for multi-dimensional aggregation.",
    "answer": "In traditional SQL, computing subtotals across multiple grouping dimensions required writing multiple queries with different `GROUP BY` clauses and combining them with `UNION ALL`. This read the source table multiple times, resulting in poor performance.\n\nModern SQL provides extensions to compute multi-level aggregations in a single pass:\n\n1. GROUPING SETS:\n- Allows explicitly declaring multiple distinct grouping dimensions in one query:\n  `GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((year, region), (year), ())`\n- Evaluates: Subtotals by (year, region), subtotals by (year), and the Grand Total `()`.\n\n2. ROLLUP (Hierarchical Subtotals):\n- Generates subtotals rolling up from the most detailed level to the grand total:\n  `GROUP BY ROLLUP (year, quarter, month)`\n- Computes aggregations for:\n  1. `(year, quarter, month)`\n  2. `(year, quarter)`\n  3. `(year)`\n  4. `()` (Grand Total)\n- Ideal for hierarchical time or geographic dimensions (Country -> State -> City).\n\n3. CUBE (Cross-Tabulation / All Combinations):\n- Generates all possible permutations of the grouping columns ($2^N$ grouping sets for $N$ columns):\n  `GROUP BY CUBE (region, product)`\n- Computes:\n  1. `(region, product)`\n  2. `(region)`\n  3. `(product)`\n  4. `()` (Grand Total)\n\nThe `GROUPING()` Function:\nReturns `1` if a column's `NULL` value represents an aggregated subtotal row, or `0` if it is a real row value, preventing confusion with genuine NULLs.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "`GROUPING SETS` defines arbitrary multiple group dimensions in a single table scan.",
      "`ROLLUP` produces hierarchical subtotals and a grand total ($N+1$ grouping levels).",
      "`CUBE` computes all $2^N$ combinations of grouping dimensions for multidimensional analysis.",
      "`GROUPING()` function distinguishes calculated subtotal NULLs from genuine data NULLs."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* ROLLUP and GROUPING() Example */\n\nSELECT \n    COALESCE(year, 'All Years') AS year,\n    COALESCE(region, 'All Regions') AS region,\n    SUM(sales) AS total_sales,\n    GROUPING(year) AS is_year_subtotal,\n    GROUPING(region) AS is_region_subtotal\nFROM sales_data\nGROUP BY ROLLUP (year, region);\n\n/* Output Sample:\nyear       | region       | total_sales | is_year_sub | is_reg_sub\n-----------+--------------+-------------+-------------+------------\n2025       | North        | 50000       | 0           | 0\n2025       | South        | 70000       | 0           | 0\n2025       | All Regions  | 120000      | 0           | 1  (Year Subtotal)\nAll Years  | All Regions  | 350000      | 1           | 1  (Grand Total)\n*/"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In data warehousing (Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift), BI tools like Tableau generate `GROUPING SETS` and `ROLLUP` under the hood to populate interactive pivot tables and drill-down dashboards!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Oracle",
      "Snowflake",
      "Databricks",
      "Amazon"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
