    "id": "int-sql-011",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain String Aggregation: STRING_AGG (Postgres), GROUP_CONCAT (MySQL), and LISTAGG (Oracle).",
    "answer": "String aggregation concatenates values from multiple rows into a single delimited string per group. Different database engines use different function names:\n\n1. PostgreSQL: `STRING_AGG(expression, delimiter [ORDER BY ...])`\n- Supports explicit ordering inside the function: `STRING_AGG(name, ', ' ORDER BY name ASC)`.\n\n2. MySQL / SQLite: `GROUP_CONCAT([DISTINCT] expression [ORDER BY ...] [SEPARATOR str])`\n- Default separator is comma (`,`).\n- Supports deduplication: `GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT tag SEPARATOR '; ')`.\n- Buffer limit: Capped by `group_concat_max_len` (default 1024 bytes in older MySQL; must be increased for large outputs).\n\n3. Oracle: `LISTAGG(expression, delimiter) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY ...)`\n- Mandatory `WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY ...)` clause.\n- Overflow handling: `ON OVERFLOW TRUNCATE` prevents errors if string exceeds 4000 characters.\n\n4. SQL Server (2017+): `STRING_AGG(expression, delimiter) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY ...)`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "String aggregation rolls up multiple row values into a single delimited text string.",
      "PostgreSQL uses `STRING_AGG`, MySQL uses `GROUP_CONCAT`, Oracle uses `LISTAGG`.",
      "Most engines support sorting items within the concatenated string via `ORDER BY`.",
      "Beware buffer truncation limits (e.g. MySQL `group_concat_max_len`)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* String Aggregation Syntax Across Engines */\n\n-- PostgreSQL:\nSELECT department_id, STRING_AGG(name, ', ' ORDER BY salary DESC) AS employees\nFROM employees GROUP BY department_id;\n\n-- MySQL:\nSELECT department_id, GROUP_CONCAT(name ORDER BY salary DESC SEPARATOR ', ') AS employees\nFROM employees GROUP BY department_id;\n\n-- Oracle:\nSELECT department_id, LISTAGG(name, ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS employees\nFROM employees GROUP BY department_id;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In modern REST and GraphQL APIs, combining `JSON_AGG(JSON_BUILD_OBJECT(...))` in PostgreSQL allows returning entire nested object graphs directly from the database in a single round-trip, outperforming ORMs!",
