    "id": "int-sql-015",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Compare Set Operations: UNION vs UNION ALL, INTERSECT, and EXCEPT / MINUS with performance differences.",
    "answer": "Set operations combine the result sets of two or more independent `SELECT` queries vertically. All queries must have the same number of columns with compatible data types:\n\n1. UNION vs UNION ALL (The Performance Penalty):\n- `UNION ALL`: Concatenates result sets directly. Retains all rows, INCLUDING DUPLICATES.\n  - Performance: Blazing fast ($O(1)$ concatenation). Zero memory sort or deduplication overhead.\n- `UNION`: Concatenates result sets AND removes duplicate rows.\n  - Performance Penalty: The database engine MUST perform an expensive unique sort (`Sort (Unique)`) or Hash Aggregate across the entire combined dataset in memory/tempdb to eliminate duplicates!\n  - Rule: ALWAYS use `UNION ALL` unless duplicate removal is strictly required!\n\n2. INTERSECT:\n- Returns ONLY rows that exist in BOTH queries (set intersection). Removes duplicates.\n- Alternative: Can also be written as an `INNER JOIN` or `WHERE EXISTS`.\n\n3. EXCEPT (SQL Standard / Postgres / SQL Server) / MINUS (Oracle):\n- Returns rows that exist in the FIRST query but do NOT exist in the second query (set difference).\n- Order matters: `Query A EXCEPT Query B` $\\ne$ `Query B EXCEPT Query A`.\n- Alternative: Can also be written as `WHERE NOT EXISTS`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "`UNION ALL` combines results retaining duplicates; executes in $O(1)$ without sorting.",
      "`UNION` removes duplicate rows by forcing an expensive unique sort or hash aggregate.",
      "`INTERSECT` returns mutual rows common to both result sets.",
      "`EXCEPT` (or `MINUS` in Oracle) returns rows from the first query that are absent in the second."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Set Operations Execution Comparison */\n\n-- Fast: UNION ALL (Concatenates with zero sorting)\nSELECT user_id, email FROM active_users\nUNION ALL\nSELECT user_id, email FROM archived_users;\n\n-- Slower: UNION (Forces Sort Unique across millions of rows)\nSELECT user_id, email FROM active_users\nUNION\nSELECT user_id, email FROM archived_users;\n\n-- INTERSECT: Users in both tables\nSELECT user_id FROM beta_testers\nINTERSECT\nSELECT user_id FROM paying_customers;\n\n-- EXCEPT: Beta testers who are NOT paying customers\nSELECT user_id FROM beta_testers\nEXCEPT\nSELECT user_id FROM paying_customers;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In 95% of real-world queries where developers write `UNION`, the two datasets are already mutually exclusive (e.g. `2024_archive` and `2025_active`). Using `UNION` forces a completely useless sort of millions of rows! Replacing it with `UNION ALL` can speed up queries by 10x!",
