    "id": "int-sql-022",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Write a query to find the Nth Highest Salary in SQL handling ties, duplicates, and NULLs.",
    "answer": "The 'Nth Highest Salary' (e.g. 2nd highest, 5th highest) is the single most frequently asked SQL interview question.\n\nCommon Traps to Avoid:\n1. Using `LIMIT 1 OFFSET N-1` with `ORDER BY salary DESC`: Fails if multiple employees tie for the highest salary! If two people earn \\$100k, `OFFSET 1` returns \\$100k again instead of the true 2nd highest salary.\n2. Using `DISTINCT + LIMIT/OFFSET`: Fails if the table contains fewer than $N$ distinct salaries; returns empty set instead of NULL.\n3. Using `RANK()`: Skips numbers on ties (1, 1, 3), returning zero rows for rank 2.\n\nThe Robust Solution: `DENSE_RANK()`:\n1. `DENSE_RANK()` assigns the identical rank to tied salaries and never skips numbers (`$100k -> 1, $100k -> 1, $90k -> 2`).\n2. Wrap in a CTE and select the distinct salary where `rank = N`.\n3. Wrap in an outer scalar subquery to guarantee returning `NULL` if no $N$-th highest salary exists.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "`DENSE_RANK()` correctly handles ties by assigning identical ranks without skipping numbers.",
      "Wrapping in an outer `SELECT (...) AS Alias` guarantees returning `NULL` if fewer than $N$ records exist.",
      "Do NOT use simple `LIMIT 1 OFFSET N-1` without `DISTINCT` due to duplicate tie values.",
      "Can also be solved using correlated subqueries: `COUNT(DISTINCT s2.salary) = N - 1`."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Finding the N-th Highest Salary (e.g., N = 2) */\n\n-- Option 1: DENSE_RANK() (Most robust, handles ties & returns NULL if missing)\nSELECT (\n    SELECT DISTINCT salary \n    FROM (\n        SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER(ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk \n        FROM employees\n    ) ranked\n    WHERE rnk = 2 -- N = 2\n) AS SecondHighestSalary;\n\n-- Option 2: MySQL / PostgreSQL LIMIT with DISTINCT (Quick & clean)\nSELECT (\n    SELECT DISTINCT salary \n    FROM employees \n    ORDER BY salary DESC \n    LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1 -- OFFSET N - 1\n) AS SecondHighestSalary;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Notice the outer wrapper `SELECT ( ... ) AS SecondHighestSalary`! On LeetCode / HackerRank, tests check for `NULL` when $N$ exceeds table rows. Raw queries return empty sets (`0 rows`); wrapping in `SELECT (...)` converts empty sets to a single `NULL` row!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Google",
      "Amazon",
      "Meta",
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle"
    ],
