    "id": "int-sql-023",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Solve the Gaps and Islands Problem: Finding Consecutive Active Days / Logins.",
    "answer": "The Gaps and Islands problem is a classic advanced SQL puzzle:\n- Islands: Sequences of consecutive data points (e.g., consecutive days a user logged in).\n- Gaps: Missing data points separating consecutive sequences.\nProblem: Find all users who logged in for at least 3 consecutive days.\n\nThe Ingenious Mathematical Solution (The Difference Technique):\n1. Take a distinct list of user login dates.\n2. Number the dates sequentially using `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date)`.\n3. Subtract the `ROW_NUMBER` (as an integer number of days) from the `login_date`:\n$$\\\\text{Group Key} = \\\\text{login\\\\_date} - (\\\\text{ROW\\\\_NUMBER} \\\\times \\\\text{1 day})$$\n4. The Magic Principle:\n   - If dates are consecutive (`2025-01-01, 2025-01-02, 2025-01-03`), both the date AND the row number advance by 1 simultaneously!\n   - Therefore: `Date - RowNumber` produces the EXACT SAME CONSTANT DATE for every element in that consecutive island!\n   - As soon as a gap occurs, the row number increases by 1, but the date skips ahead, shifting `Date - RowNumber` to a new constant value!\n5. Now, simply `GROUP BY user_id, (login_date - rn)` and filter `HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3`!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Gaps and Islands identifies consecutive data streaks separated by missing periods.",
      "Subtracting `ROW_NUMBER()` from consecutive dates produces an identical constant date.",
      "Consecutive dates share the same calculated island key; gaps create new keys.",
      "Group by the calculated island key and filter `HAVING COUNT(*) >= N` to find streaks."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Solving Gaps & Islands via Difference Method */\n-- Find users with >= 3 consecutive login days\n\nWITH DistinctLogins AS (\n    SELECT DISTINCT user_id, CAST(login_time AS DATE) AS login_date\n    FROM user_logins\n),\nGroupedIslands AS (\n    SELECT \n        user_id,\n        login_date,\n        -- Magic subtraction: login_date - row_number days\n        login_date - CAST(ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date) * INTERVAL '1 day' AS DATE) AS island_group\n    FROM DistinctLogins\n)\nSELECT \n    user_id,\n    MIN(login_date) AS streak_start,\n    MAX(login_date) AS streak_end,\n    COUNT(*) AS streak_length\nFROM GroupedIslands\nGROUP BY user_id, island_group\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 3\nORDER BY streak_length DESC;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Always deduplicate logins by day first using `SELECT DISTINCT user_id, CAST(login_time AS DATE)`! If a user logs in 5 times on the same day, raw row numbers will advance while dates stay fixed, destroying the consecutive streak math!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Meta",
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Stripe",
      "Spotify"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
