    "id": "int-sql-025",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Write a query to calculate Day 1, Day 7, and Day 30 User Retention (Cohort Retention Matrix).",
    "answer": "Cohort Retention measures what percentage of users who joined on Day 0 return to use the product on subsequent days (Day 1, Day 7, Day 30). This is the single most important product metric asked at Meta, Uber, and consumer tech companies.\n\nSteps to Build a Retention Matrix in SQL:\n1. Define User Cohort (Day 0): Find each user's initial signup date: `MIN(event_date) AS signup_date`.\n2. Match Activity Days: Join user activity logs back to their signup cohort on `user_id`, calculating the day difference:\n$$\\\\text{day\\\\_diff} = \\\\text{activity\\\\_date} - \\\\text{signup\\\\_date}$$\n3. Aggregate Cohort Matrix: Group by `signup_date` and calculate percentages using conditional aggregation (`CASE WHEN day_diff = 1 THEN 1 END`).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Cohort retention tracks return activity relative to initial signup dates.",
      "Step 1 identifies initial signup date (`MIN(date)`); Step 2 calculates day differences.",
      "Conditional aggregation (`CASE WHEN day_diff = 7`) computes retention percentages per cohort.",
      "Essential product metric evaluated in analytics and product engineering interviews."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Cohort Retention Calculation */\n\nWITH UserCohorts AS (\n    -- Step 1: Find user signup/first active date\n    SELECT user_id, MIN(activity_date) AS signup_date\n    FROM user_activity\n    GROUP BY user_id\n),\nUserDays AS (\n    -- Step 2: Compute day difference for each subsequent login\n    SELECT \n        c.user_id,\n        c.signup_date,\n        a.activity_date - c.signup_date AS day_diff\n    FROM UserCohorts c\n    JOIN user_activity a ON c.user_id = a.user_id\n)\n-- Step 3: Aggregate cohort retention percentages\nSELECT \n    signup_date,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS cohort_size,\n    ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN day_diff = 1 THEN user_id END) / COUNT(DISTINCT user_id), 1) AS day_1_retention,\n    ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN day_diff = 7 THEN user_id END) / COUNT(DISTINCT user_id), 1) AS day_7_retention,\n    ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN day_diff = 30 THEN user_id END) / COUNT(DISTINCT user_id), 1) AS day_30_retention\nFROM UserDays\nGROUP BY signup_date\nORDER BY signup_date;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Notice `COUNT(DISTINCT user_id)` inside the conditional aggregation! If a user logs in 4 times on Day 7, `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)` ensures they are counted exactly once, preventing retention percentages from erroneously exceeding 100%!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Meta",
      "TikTok",
      "Uber",
      "Spotify",
      "Netflix"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
