    "id": "int-sql-028",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain Pivoting and Unpivoting Data in SQL without native PIVOT/UNPIVOT commands.",
    "answer": "Pivoting transforms rows into columns (cross-tabulation); Unpivoting transforms columns back into rows (normalizing). While SQL Server and Oracle provide proprietary `PIVOT` and `UNPIVOT` keywords, ANSI SQL provides universal techniques that work across ALL databases:\n\n1. Pivoting (Rows to Columns) via `MAX(CASE WHEN ...)`:\n- Group by the row identifier (e.g., `year` or `product_id`).\n- Wrap conditional expressions inside `MAX()` or `SUM()` for each target column:\n  `MAX(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q1' THEN revenue END) AS q1_rev`\n\n2. Unpivoting (Columns to Rows) via `CROSS JOIN LATERAL` / `CROSS JOIN VALUES`:\n- Given a wide table with columns `(id, q1, q2, q3, q4)`:\n- Cross join with a virtual table of values mapping each column name to a row!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Pivoting converts distinct row values into columnar attributes using `SUM(CASE WHEN...)`.",
      "Unpivoting normalizes wide multi-column tables into normalized key-value rows.",
      "`CROSS JOIN LATERAL (VALUES ...)` in Postgres and SQL Server unpivots in a single table pass.",
      "Universal ANSI CASE expressions work across all database engines without vendor-specific extensions."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Pivoting and Unpivoting in Universal ANSI SQL */\n\n-- 1. PIVOT: Rows to Columns (Quarterly sales by department)\nSELECT \n    department_id,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q1' THEN sales ELSE 0 END) AS Q1_Sales,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q2' THEN sales ELSE 0 END) AS Q2_Sales,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q3' THEN sales ELSE 0 END) AS Q3_Sales,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q4' THEN sales ELSE 0 END) AS Q4_Sales\nFROM department_sales\nGROUP BY department_id;\n\n-- 2. UNPIVOT: Columns to Rows (Normalizing wide metrics table)\n-- Given table WideMetrics(company_id, rev_2023, rev_2024, rev_2025):\nSELECT \n    w.company_id,\n    u.year,\n    u.revenue\nFROM WideMetrics w\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    VALUES \n        ('2023', w.rev_2023),\n        ('2024', w.rev_2024),\n        ('2025', w.rev_2025)\n) AS u(year, revenue);"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Why `CROSS JOIN LATERAL (VALUES ...)` instead of `UNION ALL` for unpivoting? Because `UNION ALL` scans the original table 4 separate times (once for each column)! `CROSS JOIN LATERAL` scans the underlying table ONCE, generating the unpivoted rows in-memory with a 4x performance boost!",
