    "id": "int-sql-029",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Write a query to find users who purchased Product A AND Product B, but NEVER purchased Product C (Relational Division).",
    "answer": "This is a classic Relational Division and Set Difference problem:\n\nThree Ways to Solve It:\n\nOption 1: Group By & HAVING with Conditional Aggregation (Fastest & cleanest):\n1. Group by `user_id`.\n2. Ensure the user bought Product A: `COUNT(CASE WHEN product = 'A' THEN 1 END) > 0`.\n3. Ensure the user bought Product B: `COUNT(CASE WHEN product = 'B' THEN 1 END) > 0`.\n4. Ensure the user NEVER bought Product C: `COUNT(CASE WHEN product = 'C' THEN 1 END) = 0`.\nEvaluates the entire condition in a single table scan!\n\nOption 2: INTERSECT and EXCEPT:\n`(SELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE product = 'A' INTERSECT SELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE product = 'B') EXCEPT SELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE product = 'C'`\n\nOption 3: Multiple EXISTS and NOT EXISTS subqueries.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Conditional aggregation in HAVING checks for presence of A & B and absence of C in one scan.",
      "Set operations (`INTERSECT` and `EXCEPT`) provide an intuitive set-theoretic solution.",
      "`COUNT(CASE WHEN product = 'C' THEN 1 END) = 0` guarantees absolute exclusion.",
      "Single-pass GROUP BY avoids multi-query materialization penalties."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Relational Division: Has A AND B, but NOT C */\n\n-- Method 1: Single-Pass GROUP BY & HAVING (Recommended)\nSELECT user_id\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY user_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN product_name = 'Product A' THEN product_name END) > 0\n   AND COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN product_name = 'Product B' THEN product_name END) > 0\n   AND COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN product_name = 'Product C' THEN product_name END) = 0;\n\n-- Method 2: Set Operations (INTERSECT / EXCEPT)\nSELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE product_name = 'Product A'\nINTERSECT\nSELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE product_name = 'Product B'\nEXCEPT\nSELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE product_name = 'Product C';"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Interviewer follow-up: 'What if you need users who purchased ALL products in a dynamic category containing N products?' Use Relational Division: `HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM category_products)`!",
