    "title": "Solve Trips and Users (LeetCode 262 Hard): Calculating unbanned cancellation rates.",
    "answer": "Problem (LeetCode 262 Hard):\nGiven tables:\n- `Trips(id, client_id, driver_id, city_id, status, request_at)`\n- `Users(users_id, banned, role)`\nCalculate the cancellation rate of requests made by unbanned clients AND unbanned drivers between '2013-10-01' and '2013-10-03', rounded to 2 decimal places.\n\nSolution Steps:\n1. Join `Trips` to `Users` TWICE: once for `client_id` and once for `driver_id`.\n2. Filter out banned participants: `c.banned = 'No' AND d.banned = 'No'`.\n3. Filter date range: `request_at BETWEEN '2013-10-01' AND '2013-10-03'`.\n4. Calculate Cancellation Rate:\n$$\\text{Cancellation Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Count of Cancelled Trips (by client or driver)}}{\\text{Total Trips}}$$\nUse conditional aggregation: `ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN status <> 'completed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)::NUMERIC / COUNT(*), 2)`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Dual-table join against Users ensures both client AND driver are unbanned.",
      "Restricts analysis to the specified three-day window in the WHERE clause.",
      "Conditional aggregation counts cancelled trips divided by total trip rows.",
      "`ROUND(..., 2)` formats the rate to the required two decimal places."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Trips and Users Solution */\n\nSELECT \n    t.request_at AS \"Day\",\n    ROUND(\n        SUM(CASE WHEN t.status IN ('cancelled_by_driver', 'cancelled_by_client') THEN 1.0 ELSE 0.0 END) / \n        COUNT(*), \n        2\n    ) AS \"Cancellation Rate\"\nFROM Trips t\nJOIN Users c ON t.client_id = c.users_id AND c.banned = 'No'\nJOIN Users d ON t.driver_id = d.users_id AND d.banned = 'No'\nWHERE t.request_at BETWEEN '2013-10-01' AND '2013-10-03'\nGROUP BY t.request_at\nORDER BY t.request_at;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Why `JOIN Users c ... JOIN Users d` instead of `WHERE client_id NOT IN (SELECT users_id FROM Users WHERE banned = 'Yes')`? Standard INNER JOINs on indexed user IDs allow the database to use fast Hash Joins and index seeks, easily outperforming multi-level subqueries on large rideshare datasets!",
