    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Solve Human Traffic of Stadium (LeetCode 601 Hard): Finding 3 or more consecutive rows meeting a condition.",
    "answer": "Problem (LeetCode 601 Hard):\nTable `Stadium(id, visit_date, people)`. Display records with 3 or more consecutive IDs where `people >= 100`.\n\nThe Gaps and Islands Difference Method:\n1. Filter rows where `people >= 100`.\n2. Compute `id - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY id) AS island_id`.\n   - For consecutive IDs ($5, 6, 7$), row numbers are $1, 2, 3$.\n   - $5 - 1 = 4$, $6 - 2 = 4$, $7 - 3 = 4$ -> Constant island key `4`!\n3. Count group size using a window function: `COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY island_id) AS group_size`.\n4. Select all rows where `group_size >= 3`!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Filters candidates first (`people >= 100`) to expose gaps in IDs.",
      "`id - ROW_NUMBER()` produces an identical constant value for consecutive sequences.",
      "`COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY island_id)` evaluates streak length across the group.",
      "Returns all rows belonging to streaks of 3 or more consecutive days."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Human Traffic of Stadium Solution */\n\nWITH QualifiedDays AS (\n    SELECT \n        id,\n        visit_date,\n        people,\n        -- Group key for consecutive sequences\n        id - ROW_NUMBER() OVER(ORDER BY id) AS island_id\n    FROM Stadium\n    WHERE people >= 100\n),\nIslandCounts AS (\n    SELECT *,\n        -- Count how many days belong to this specific consecutive streak\n        COUNT(*) OVER(PARTITION BY island_id) AS streak_count\n    FROM QualifiedDays\n)\nSELECT id, visit_date, people\nFROM IslandCounts\nWHERE streak_count >= 3\nORDER BY visit_date ASC;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Traditional solutions use messy 3-way self-joins (`s1.id = s2.id-1 AND s1.id = s3.id-2`). The `id - ROW_NUMBER()` difference method scales effortlessly to N consecutive rows (e.g., 5 or 10 days) without adding any extra joins!",
