    "id": "int-sql-042",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "What are SARGable Queries and why do functions on indexed columns destroy Index Seeks?",
    "answer": "SARGable stands for Search Argument Able. A query predicate is SARGable if the database query engine can use a B-Tree index to perform an ultra-fast Index Seek ($O(\\log N)$) to locate matching rows directly.\n\nNon-SARGable Anti-Pattern (Wrapping Columns in Functions):\nWhen a column is wrapped inside a scalar function, mathematical operation, or string manipulation:\n`WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2025`\n`WHERE SUBSTRING(phone, 1, 3) = '415'`\n`WHERE salary * 1.1 > 100000`\n\nWhy Non-SARGable Predicates Destroy Performance:\nA B-Tree index on `order_date` stores sorted raw dates (`2025-01-01`, `2025-01-02`). The index does NOT store the evaluated result of `YEAR(order_date)`.\nTherefore, the database engine CANNOT traverse the B-Tree! It is forced to perform a full Table Scan or full Index Scan ($O(N)$), invoking the `YEAR()` function on EVERY SINGLE ROW in the table! If the table has 50 million rows, it executes 50 million function calls!\n\nHow to Rewrite Queries to be SARGable:\nIsolate the indexed column completely on one side of the operator:\n- Instead of `WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2025`:\n  `WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2026-01-01'`\n- Instead of `WHERE salary * 1.1 > 100000`:\n  `WHERE salary > 100000 / 1.1`",
    "bullet_points": [
      "SARGable predicates allow the query optimizer to perform B-Tree index seeks ($O(\\log N)$).",
      "Wrapping indexed columns in functions (`YEAR(d)`, `LOWER(s)`) forces full table scans ($O(N)$).",
      "Rewrite range queries using open-ended bounds: `>= 'start' AND < 'end'`.",
      "Always isolate the raw indexed column alone on one side of the comparison operator."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Non-SARGable vs SARGable Rewrites */\n\n-- 1. Date Filtering\n-- NON-SARGABLE (Full Table Scan! Function on column):\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE DATE(created_at) = '2025-03-15';\n\n-- SARGABLE (Index Seek! Raw column isolated):\nSELECT * FROM orders \nWHERE created_at >= '2025-03-15 00:00:00' \n  AND created_at <  '2025-03-16 00:00:00';\n\n-- 2. String Prefix Matching\n-- NON-SARGABLE:\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE LEFT(username, 4) = 'john';\n\n-- SARGABLE:\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE username LIKE 'john%'; -- Uses B-Tree index seek!\n\n-- 3. Arithmetic Operations\n-- NON-SARGABLE:\nSELECT * FROM products WHERE price - discount > 50;\n\n-- SARGABLE (Move literal/expression to other side):\nSELECT * FROM products WHERE price > 50 + discount;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If business requirements strictly necessitate querying on a function (e.g. case-insensitive lookups on email), create a Function-Based Index (or Expression Index in PostgreSQL): `CREATE INDEX idx_users_lower_email ON users (LOWER(email));`!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "is_hidden": false,
