    "id": "int-sql-043",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Why do Implicit Data Type Conversions silently invalidate Index Seeks?",
    "answer": "Implicit Data Type Conversion is a silent performance killer where the database automatically converts one data type to another to satisfy an equality comparison.\n\nData Type Precedence Rules:\nSQL standards define strict data type precedence: Numbers (INTEGER, NUMERIC) have higher precedence than Strings (VARCHAR, CHAR).\nWhen a number and a string are compared:\n$$\\text{VARCHAR Column} = 12345$$\nThe database converts the lower-precedence type to the higher-precedence type!\nIt rewrites your query internally to:\n$$\\text{CAST}(\\text{VARCHAR Column AS INTEGER}) = 12345$$\n\nThe Performance Disaster:\nBecause the database applies the `CAST()` function to the column, the predicate instantly becomes NON-SARGABLE!\nEven if you have a perfect B-Tree index on that column, the index is rendered completely USELESS! The engine falls back to a Full Table Scan, converting every string to a number row-by-row.\n\nReal-World Production Example:\nStoring National IDs, Phone Numbers, or SKU Codes as `VARCHAR(20)`. An application ORM passes an unquoted integer:\n`SELECT * FROM users WHERE phone_number = 1234567890;`\nA query that should take 0.5 milliseconds suddenly takes 30 seconds!\n\nFix: Always match parameter types:\n`SELECT * FROM users WHERE phone_number = '1234567890';`",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Database type precedence converts strings to integers when comparing mixed types.",
      "Wraps the column in an implicit `CAST()` function, destroying index seeks.",
      "Forces a full table scan across millions of rows, spiking CPU and query latency.",
      "Always match application parameter types to the underlying database column types."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Implicit Conversion Disaster */\n-- Schema: users(id INT, national_id VARCHAR(50))\n-- Index exists on national_id!\n\n-- BAD: Application passes unquoted integer:\nEXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM users WHERE national_id = 987654321;\n-- Plan: Seq Scan on users! Filter: (CAST(national_id AS integer) = 987654321)\n-- Execution Time: 4,500 ms (Table Scan across 10 million rows!)\n\n-- GOOD: Correct data type passed as string literal:\nEXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM users WHERE national_id = '987654321';\n-- Plan: Index Scan using idx_national_id on users!\n-- Execution Time: 0.12 ms (Index Seek!)"
    },
    "pro_tip": "In SQL Server execution plans, look for the warning yellow exclamation mark! Hovering over it reveals: `Type conversion in expression (CONVERT_IMPLICIT) may affect 'SeekPlan' in query choice`.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Microsoft",
      "Amazon",
      "Oracle",
      "Google"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "is_hidden": false,
