    "id": "int-sql-044",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain Cardinality Estimation, Database Statistics, and why Stale Statistics ruin query plans.",
    "answer": "Cost-Based Query Optimizers (CBO) do not execute queries blindly; they evaluate dozens of possible execution plans (e.g., Hash Join vs Nested Loop, Index Scan vs Seq Scan) and pick the plan with the lowest estimated cost.\n\nCardinality Estimation:\nCardinality is the estimated number of rows that will satisfy a given filter or join predicate.\nThe query optimizer calculates cardinality estimates using Database Statistics:\n- Total row count ($N$).\n- Distinct values count (n_distinct).\n- Fraction of NULL values.\n- Histograms (Equi-width and Equi-depth distribution buckets) showing data skew.\n- Most Common Values (MCV) lists.\n\nHow Stale Statistics Ruin Query Plans:\nSuppose a table originally had 1,000 rows. Statistics were generated. Over the next month, 50,000,000 new rows were inserted via batch jobs, but statistics were NOT updated.\n1. The Disaster:\n   The query optimizer still thinks the table has only 1,000 rows!\n2. Catastrophic Plan Selection:\n   - For a 1,000-row table, the optimizer chooses a Nested Loop Join (believing it will iterate only 1,000 times).\n   - In reality, the table has 50,000,000 rows! The Nested Loop join executes 50 million index seeks, freezing the server for hours!\n   - Had statistics been accurate, the optimizer would have chosen a Hash Join, finishing in 3 seconds!\n\nSolution: Keep statistics fresh via automated autovacuum/auto-analyze, or manual `ANALYZE table_name;` (Postgres) / `UPDATE STATISTICS table_name;` (SQL Server).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Query optimizers rely on statistics (histograms, distinct counts) to estimate cardinality.",
      "Cardinality estimates dictate join algorithms (Nested Loop vs Hash Join) and index usage.",
      "Stale statistics lead to disastrous execution plans (e.g. Nested Loops on 50M rows).",
      "Run `ANALYZE` or `UPDATE STATISTICS` immediately following large batch insert/delete operations."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Inspecting and Updating Statistics in PostgreSQL */\n\n-- Inspect table statistics:\nSELECT tablename, attname, null_frac, n_distinct, most_common_vals \nFROM pg_stats \nWHERE tablename = 'orders';\n\n-- Update statistics manually after massive data load:\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- SQL Server: Update statistics with full scan:\nUPDATE STATISTICS orders WITH FULLSCAN;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "When reading an `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` output in PostgreSQL, compare `rows=X` (Estimated rows) with `actual rows=Y`! If estimated is 10 and actual is 5,000,000, your statistics are horribly stale, and running `ANALYZE` will fix your query instantly!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Oracle",
      "PostgreSQL",
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
