    "id": "int-sql-047",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain Composite Indexes and the Leftmost Prefix Rule.",
    "answer": "A Composite Index (Multi-Column Index) is an index built on two or more columns:\n`CREATE INDEX idx_name ON employees (last_name, first_name, department_id);`\n\nThe Leftmost Prefix Rule (The Phonebook Analogy):\nA composite B-Tree index is sorted hierarchically from left to right:\n- First, rows are sorted strictly by `last_name`.\n- Within identical `last_name`s, rows are sorted by `first_name`.\n- Within identical `(last_name, first_name)` pairs, rows are sorted by `department_id`.\n\nThink of a physical telephone book sorted by `(LastName, FirstName)`:\n- Can you find 'Smith, John'? YES (Index Seek on both columns).\n- Can you find all people with LastName = 'Smith'? YES (Index Seek on leftmost column).\n- Can you find all people with FirstName = 'John'? NO! The phonebook is not sorted by first name! To find all 'Johns', you would have to read the entire telephone book from page 1 to the end!\n\nQueries That CAN Use the Index:\n1. `WHERE last_name = 'Smith'` (Uses prefix column 1)\n2. `WHERE last_name = 'Smith' AND first_name = 'John'` (Uses prefix columns 1 & 2)\n3. `WHERE last_name = 'Smith' AND first_name = 'John' AND department_id = 5` (Uses all 3)\n\nQueries That CANNOT Use the Index Seek:\n1. `WHERE first_name = 'John'` (Skips leftmost column! Full Scan!)\n2. `WHERE department_id = 5` (Skips leftmost columns! Full Scan!)\n3. `WHERE first_name = 'John' AND department_id = 5` (Skips leftmost column! Full Scan!)",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Composite indexes sort data hierarchically from the leftmost column to the right.",
      "Queries can use an index seek only if the filter contains a contiguous leading subset of columns.",
      "Omitting the leftmost column in a query prevents B-Tree index seeks, forcing full scans.",
      "Column ordering rule: Place equality columns first, followed by range/sort columns."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Leftmost Prefix Evaluation */\n-- Composite Index: (colA, colB, colC)\n\n-- FULL SEEK (Uses entire index):\nSELECT * FROM t WHERE colA = 10 AND colB = 'XYZ' AND colC = 50;\n\n-- PARTIAL SEEK (Uses colA & colB; scans colC):\nSELECT * FROM t WHERE colA = 10 AND colB = 'XYZ';\n\n-- PARTIAL SEEK (Uses colA seek; colC filtered as predicate):\nSELECT * FROM t WHERE colA = 10 AND colC = 50; -- Skips colB!\n\n-- ZERO SEEK (FULL TABLE SCAN! Violates leftmost prefix):\nSELECT * FROM t WHERE colB = 'XYZ' AND colC = 50;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "Golden ordering rule: If you have a query with equality on Column A and a range filter on Column B (`WHERE status = 'ACTIVE' AND created_at >= '2025-01-01'`), ALWAYS index `(status, created_at)`! If you index `(created_at, status)`, the range filter on created_at prevents the index from seeking on status!",
