    "id": "int-sql-048",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain Partial (Filtered) Indexes: Use cases, space savings, and write performance benefits.",
    "answer": "A Partial Index (or Filtered Index in SQL Server) is an index built over a subset of rows in a table defined by a `WHERE` clause:\n`CREATE INDEX idx_unprocessed_orders ON orders (created_at) WHERE status = 'PENDING';`\n\nThe Skewed Data Problem:\nIn many production workflows, data distribution is heavily skewed:\n- An `orders` table has 100,000,000 total rows.\n- 99,500,000 rows have `status = 'COMPLETED'` or `'DELIVERED'`.\n- Only 500,000 rows have `status = 'PENDING'` (active queue).\nIf you build a standard index on `status`, the index contains 100 million entries, consuming 5 GB of RAM and disk!\n\nMassive Advantages of Partial Indexes:\n1. 95%+ Disk & RAM Savings:\nThe partial index stores ONLY the 500,000 pending rows! Index size shrinks from 5 GB down to 25 MB, fitting entirely in high-speed CPU L3 cache or RAM buffer pool.\n2. Dramatic Write Performance Improvement:\nWhen updating or inserting the 99.5% of completed orders, the database does NOT touch the partial index! It only updates the index when a pending order is created or modified, saving millions of disk writes per day.\n3. Enforcing Partial Uniqueness:\nYou can enforce unique constraints on a subset of data (e.g., allowing multiple soft-deleted accounts with the same email, while enforcing that active accounts must have unique emails!):\n`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_unique_active_email ON users (email) WHERE is_deleted = FALSE;`",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Partial indexes index only rows matching a specific `WHERE` predicate condition.",
      "Saves 90%+ of RAM and disk space on skewed data distributions (queues, flags).",
      "Eliminates index maintenance overhead on rows that don't match the filter.",
      "Enables partial uniqueness (e.g. unique active emails while allowing duplicate deleted accounts)."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Partial Index Implementation */\n\n-- 1. Partial Index for Task Queues:\nCREATE INDEX idx_pending_jobs ON jobs (priority, scheduled_at)\nWHERE status = 'QUEUED';\n\n-- Query automatically uses the tiny 25MB partial index:\nEXPLAIN SELECT * FROM jobs \nWHERE status = 'QUEUED' AND priority > 5 \nORDER BY scheduled_at;\n\n-- 2. Partial Unique Index (Enforcing uniqueness only for active records):\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_unique_active_user ON users (username) \nWHERE is_active = TRUE;\n-- Allows 100 inactive users named 'john_doe', but strictly ONE active 'john_doe'!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "MySQL does not support partial indexes natively! In MySQL, developers emulate partial indexes using generated columns with `CASE WHEN status = 'QUEUED' THEN id ELSE NULL END`, relying on the fact that B-Tree indexes ignore NULLs!",
