    "id": "int-sql-049",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain Join Algorithms: Nested Loop Join vs Hash Join vs Sort-Merge Join (When does the engine pick each?).",
    "answer": "Query engines use three primary physical join algorithms to execute relational joins:\n\n1. Nested Loop Join:\n- Algorithm: For each row in the Outer (driving) table, scan or seek matching rows in the Inner table.\n- Best When: One table is tiny (e.g., 10 rows), and the other table has a B-Tree index on the join key ($O(M \\log N)$).\n- Worst When: Both tables are large and unindexed ($O(M \\times N)$ Cartesian scan disaster!).\n- Sub-type (Index Nested Loop): Uses index seek on inner table for blazing sub-millisecond execution on OLTP queries.\n\n2. Hash Join:\n- Algorithm: Two-phase algorithm:\n  1. Build Phase: Reads the smaller table into memory and builds an in-memory Hash Table on the join key.\n  2. Probe Phase: Scans the larger table row-by-row, hashes its join key, and probes the in-memory hash table for matches.\n- Best When: Joining large, unsorted, unindexed tables on EQUALITY conditions (`=`).\n- Memory Management: If the hash table exceeds `work_mem` (Postgres) or memory grant, it spills to temporary disk files (Grace Hash Join / Hybrid Hash Join), degrading performance.\n- Limitation: Works ONLY on equi-joins (`=`); CANNOT be used on non-equi joins (`<`, `>`, `BETWEEN`).\n\n3. Sort-Merge Join:\n- Algorithm: Both tables are sorted by the join key (if not already sorted by an index). Then, two pointers walk down both tables in parallel like the merge step in MergeSort ($O(M + N)$).\n- Best When: Joining very large tables that are ALREADY sorted by a clustering index or where the query includes an `ORDER BY` matching the join key. Supports non-equi inequality joins (`<`, `>`).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Nested Loop joins iterate through outer rows and seek into indexed inner rows (ideal for OLTP).",
      "Hash Joins build an in-memory hash table from the smaller input and probe with the larger (ideal for OLAP).",
      "Hash joins operate strictly on equality (`=`) conditions.",
      "Sort-Merge joins sort both inputs and merge sequentially; ideal for pre-sorted indexed data."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "/* Physical Join Algorithms Comparison */\n\n+-------------------+----------------------------+-----------------------+---------------------+\n| Join Algorithm    | Ideal Workload             | Requires Index?       | Supported Operators |\n+-------------------+----------------------------+-----------------------+---------------------+\n| Nested Loop       | Small outer + indexed inner| YES (on inner table)  | All (=, <, >, etc.) |\n| Hash Join         | Large unindexed tables     | NO                    | Equi-join (=) ONLY  |\n| Sort-Merge Join   | Pre-sorted large tables    | Beneficial (Sorted)   | Equi and Non-equi   |\n+-------------------+----------------------------+-----------------------+---------------------+"
    },
    "pro_tip": "If a query with a Hash Join is spilling to disk (`Batch: 16, Disk: 45MB` in EXPLAIN), increasing the session memory parameter (`SET work_mem = '256MB'` in Postgres) allows the entire hash table to reside in RAM, speeding up joins by 10x!",
