    "id": "int-sql-051",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Explain Dirty Reads, Non-Repeatable Reads, Phantom Reads, and Write Skew with SQL Scenarios and ANSI Isolation Levels",
    "answer": "### Concurrency Phenomena & ANSI Isolation Levels\n\nWhen multiple concurrent database transactions execute simultaneously without proper isolation, specific data anomalies arise:\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------+-----------------+-----------------------+--------------+------------+\n| Isolation Level                   | Dirty Read (G1) | Non-Repeatable (G2a)  | Phantom (A3) | Write Skew |\n+-----------------------------------+-----------------+-----------------------+--------------+------------+\n| Read Uncommitted                  | Allowed         | Allowed               | Allowed      | Allowed    |\n| Read Committed                    | Prevented       | Allowed               | Allowed      | Allowed    |\n| Repeatable Read                   | Prevented       | Prevented             | Prev.* (PG)  | Allowed    |\n| Serializable                      | Prevented       | Prevented             | Prevented    | Prevented  |\n+-----------------------------------+-----------------+-----------------------+--------------+------------+\n* PostgreSQL's Repeatable Read uses snapshot isolation, preventing phantom reads as well, but permits Write Skew.\n```\n\n#### 1. Dirty Read (G1)\nTransaction T1 modifies a row without committing. Transaction T2 reads this uncommitted row. If T1 issues a `ROLLBACK`, T2 has operated on data that never officially existed in the database.\n\n#### 2. Non-Repeatable Read (Fuzzy Read - G2a)\nTransaction T1 reads row X. Transaction T2 updates row X and commits (`COMMIT`). T1 re-reads row X and observes different values within the same transaction.\n\n#### 3. Phantom Read (A3)\nTransaction T1 executes a range query: `SELECT count(*) FROM employees WHERE dept_id = 10` (returns 5). Transaction T2 inserts a new employee in `dept_id = 10` and commits. T1 re-executes the identical range query and observes 6 rows. The new row is a \"phantom\".\n\n#### 4. Write Skew (Snapshot Isolation Anomaly)\nWrite skew occurs when two concurrent transactions read overlapping datasets, make disjoint updates based on shared business constraints, and commit.\n- **Example Constraint**: \"At least one doctor must be on call at all times.\"\n- Doctors Alice and Bob are both on call (`is_on_call = true`).\n- T1 (Alice) checks if other doctors are on call (`SELECT count(*) FROM doctors WHERE is_on_call = true`). Count is 2. Alice sets her status to `false`.\n- Concurrently, T2 (Bob) checks the same query. Count is 2. Bob sets his status to `false`.\n- Both transactions commit under standard Repeatable Read/Snapshot Isolation because they updated different rows. However, 0 doctors are now on call, violating the business invariant!\n\n```sql\n-- Doctor On-Call Write Skew Scenario\n-- Setup\nCREATE TABLE doctor_on_call (\n    doctor_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(50),\n    is_on_call BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE\n);\nINSERT INTO doctor_on_call VALUES (1, 'Alice', true), (2, 'Bob', true);\n\n-- Transaction 1 (Alice wants to go off call)\nBEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM doctor_on_call WHERE is_on_call = true; -- Returns 2\nUPDATE doctor_on_call SET is_on_call = false WHERE doctor_id = 1;\n\n-- Transaction 2 (Bob concurrently wants to go off call)\nBEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM doctor_on_call WHERE is_on_call = true; -- Returns 2 (snapshot)\nUPDATE doctor_on_call SET is_on_call = false WHERE doctor_id = 2;\nCOMMIT; -- T2 succeeds!\n\n-- In T1:\nCOMMIT; -- T1 succeeds! Both rows are now FALSE! Invariant violated.\n\n-- Serializable solution:\n-- Under SERIALIZABLE, T1 or T2 will abort with error:\n-- ERROR: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- **Dirty Reads** involve reading uncommitted modifications from another transaction; eliminated in Read Committed.\n- **Non-Repeatable Reads** alter existing rows previously read; prevented by row-level shared locks or MVCC snapshots in Repeatable Read.\n- **Phantom Reads** introduce new matching rows to a range query; prevented by predicate locking, next-key locks, or Serializable isolation.\n- **Write Skew** is the primary weakness of snapshot isolation where two transactions read the same predicate and write to distinct rows violating a global invariant.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Many candidates falsely claim PostgreSQL's `REPEATABLE READ` suffers from Phantom Reads because ANSI SQL allows phantoms in Repeatable Read. In PostgreSQL, `REPEATABLE READ` is implemented via MVCC Snapshot Isolation, which **does** prevent Phantom Reads! To observe Write Skew vs Serializability, you must use a relational invariant spanning multiple rows.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Dirty Reads: involve reading uncommitted modifications from another transaction; eliminated in Read Committed.",
      "Non-Repeatable Reads: alter existing rows previously read; prevented by row-level shared locks or MVCC snapshots in Repeatable Read.",
      "Phantom Reads: introduce new matching rows to a range query; prevented by predicate locking, next-key locks, or Serializable isolation.",
      "Write Skew: is the primary weakness of snapshot isolation where two transactions read the same predicate and write to distinct rows violating a global invariant."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- How to prevent Write Skew in PostgreSQL without full Serializable isolation:\n-- Option 1: Explicit Locking with SELECT FOR UPDATE (Locks the evaluated rows)\nBEGIN;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM doctor_on_call WHERE is_on_call = true FOR UPDATE;\n-- If count > 1, safely update:\nUPDATE doctor_on_call SET is_on_call = false WHERE doctor_id = 1;\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- Option 2: True Serializable Isolation Level\nBEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM doctor_on_call WHERE is_on_call = true;\nUPDATE doctor_on_call SET is_on_call = false WHERE doctor_id = 1;\nCOMMIT; -- Database throws 40001 (serialization_failure) if concurrent conflict detected"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Many candidates falsely claim PostgreSQL's `REPEATABLE READ` suffers from Phantom Reads because ANSI SQL allows phantoms in Repeatable Read. In PostgreSQL, `REPEATABLE READ` is implemented via MVCC Snapshot Isolation, which **does** prevent Phantom Reads! To observe Write Skew vs Serializability, you must use a relational invariant spanning multiple rows.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Transactions",
      "ACID",
      "Isolation Levels",
      "Concurrency Anomalies",
      "ANSI SQL"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "is_hidden": false,
    "is_deleted": false,
    "sort_order": 51
