    "id": "int-sql-053",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "SELECT FOR UPDATE vs SKIP LOCKED vs NOWAIT: Building High-Throughput Job Queues in SQL",
    "answer": "### Concurrency Lock Modifiers: WAIT, NOWAIT, and SKIP LOCKED\n\nWhen multiple worker processes pull jobs from a relational database table, standard `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` causes workers to serialize and block behind one another, destroying queue throughput.\n\n```\nWorker 1: SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE status = 'PENDING' LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE; -> Locks Job #1\nWorker 2: SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE status = 'PENDING' LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE; -> BLOCKS until Worker 1 commits!\nWorker 3: SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE status = 'PENDING' LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE; -> BLOCKS until Worker 2 finishes!\n```\n\nModern SQL engines (PostgreSQL 9.5+, MySQL 8.0+, Oracle 11g+) introduce **`NOWAIT`** and **`SKIP LOCKED`** modifiers to eliminate this bottleneck.\n\n```\n+-------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Clause                  | Behavior when matching row is already locked by another transaction             |\n+-------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| FOR UPDATE              | Blocks/sleeps until the lock is released or statement timeout occurs            |\n| FOR UPDATE NOWAIT       | Immediately fails and raises error `55P03: lock_not_available`                   |\n| FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED  | Skips the locked row entirely and locks the next available matching row         |\n| FOR SHARE               | Acquires shared read lock; allows other readers, blocks concurrent writers      |\n+-------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### Building an Industrial Job Queue with `SKIP LOCKED`\nUsing a CTE with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` enables hundreds of parallel workers to claim disjoint jobs concurrently with zero blocking and zero race conditions:\n\n```sql\n-- Worker poll query (Atomic dequeue)\nWITH next_job AS (\n    SELECT id \n    FROM job_queue\n    WHERE status = 'QUEUED'\n    ORDER BY priority DESC, created_at ASC\n    LIMIT 1\n    FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED\n)\nUPDATE job_queue jq\nSET status = 'PROCESSING',\n    locked_by = 'worker-node-04',\n    started_at = NOW()\nFROM next_job\nWHERE jq.id = next_job.id\nRETURNING jq.id, jq.payload;\n```\n\n#### Why Not Standard Message Queues (Kafka / RabbitMQ)?\nWhile Kafka/RabbitMQ offer dedicated pub/sub, database job queues with `SKIP LOCKED` provide:\n1. **Transactional Enqueueing**: A job is created inside the exact same atomic database transaction as the business entity (guaranteeing the Outbox pattern with zero two-phase commit overhead).\n2. **Ad-hoc Querying & Inspection**: Jobs can be filtered, paused, retried, and queried using standard SQL `WHERE` clauses.\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- `FOR UPDATE` serializes concurrent readers; workers block waiting for prior workers to commit.\n- `FOR UPDATE NOWAIT` immediately errors out instead of waiting, useful for strict fail-fast timeout requirements.\n- `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` provides lock-free concurrency for queue workers by skipping rows currently locked by peers.\n- Combined with a CTE and `RETURNING`, `SKIP LOCKED` delivers safe, ACID-compliant multi-consumer job queues.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> When using `ORDER BY priority DESC LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, ensure you have an index matching `(status, priority DESC, created_at ASC)`. Without an index, the database engine must scan and check locks on every row in the table, degrading queue polling performance to `O(N)`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "`FOR UPDATE` serializes concurrent readers; workers block waiting for prior workers to commit.",
      "`FOR UPDATE NOWAIT` immediately errors out instead of waiting, useful for strict fail-fast timeout requirements.",
      "`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` provides lock-free concurrency for queue workers by skipping rows currently locked by peers.",
      "Combined with a CTE and `RETURNING`, `SKIP LOCKED` delivers safe, ACID-compliant multi-consumer job queues."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Production Job Queue Table Definition & Worker Query\nCREATE TABLE job_queue (\n    id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    payload JSONB NOT NULL,\n    status VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'QUEUED',\n    priority INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    attempts INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    locked_by VARCHAR(50),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),\n    started_at TIMESTAMPTZ\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_jobs_poll ON job_queue (priority DESC, created_at ASC) \nWHERE status = 'QUEUED';\n\n-- Safe Worker Claim Query\nWITH claimed_job AS (\n    SELECT id FROM job_queue\n    WHERE status = 'QUEUED'\n    ORDER BY priority DESC, created_at ASC\n    LIMIT 1\n    FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED\n)\nUPDATE job_queue\nSET status = 'PROCESSING',\n    attempts = attempts + 1,\n    started_at = clock_timestamp()\nWHERE id = (SELECT id FROM claimed_job)\nRETURNING id, payload;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> When using `ORDER BY priority DESC LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, ensure you have an index matching `(status, priority DESC, created_at ASC)`. Without an index, the database engine must scan and check locks on every row in the table, degrading queue polling performance to `O(N)`.",
