    "id": "int-sql-057",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Two-Phase Commit (2PC) Protocol in Distributed SQL: PREPARE TRANSACTION and Failure Recovery",
    "answer": "### Distributed Atomic Commitment: Two-Phase Commit (2PC)\n\nIn a microservice or sharded database architecture, a single business action may span multiple independent database nodes (e.g., deducting balance on Node A and creating an order on Node B). Standard single-node `COMMIT` cannot guarantee atomic atomicity across both.\n\n**Two-Phase Commit (2PC)** ensures that either all participant nodes commit the transaction or all abort.\n\n```\nCoordinator                  Participant Node 1             Participant Node 2\n    |                                |                              |\n    |--- Phase 1: PREPARE ---------->|                              |\n    |--- Phase 1: PREPARE ----------------------------------------->|\n    |                                |                              |\n    |<-- VOTE_COMMIT (Prepared) -----|                              |\n    |<-- VOTE_COMMIT (Prepared) ------------------------------------|\n    |                                |                              |\n    | (Coordinator writes commit log to stable storage)             |\n    |                                |                              |\n    |--- Phase 2: COMMIT ----------->|                              |\n    |--- Phase 2: COMMIT ------------------------------------------>|\n    |<-- ACK ------------------------|                              |\n    |<-- ACK -------------------------------------------------------|\n```\n\n#### 1. SQL Standard 2PC Syntax in PostgreSQL\n```sql\n-- Step 1: On Participant Node 1\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 500 WHERE id = 10;\nPREPARE TRANSACTION 'tx_transfer_step1_node1'; \n-- Transaction state is durably flushed to disk; locks remain held!\n\n-- Step 2: On Participant Node 2\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO orders (user_id, item_id, price) VALUES (10, 888, 500);\nPREPARE TRANSACTION 'tx_transfer_step1_node2';\n\n-- Step 3: If both nodes succeed PREPARE, Coordinator issues:\nCOMMIT PREPARED 'tx_transfer_step1_node1';\nCOMMIT PREPARED 'tx_transfer_step1_node2';\n\n-- If ANY node fails the PREPARE phase, Coordinator issues rollback on all:\nROLLBACK PREPARED 'tx_transfer_step1_node1';\n```\n\n#### 2. Failure Modes & The Blocking Problem\nThe fundamental vulnerability of 2PC is that **it is a blocking protocol**:\n- If the Coordinator crashes after participants vote `PREPARED` but before sending `COMMIT`, participant nodes are left in **in-doubt** state.\n- Participants cannot unilaterally abort or commit because they do not know the coordinator's global decision.\n- All database row locks acquired during the transaction remain held indefinitely until the coordinator recovers or an administrator manually resolves the in-doubt transaction!\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- 2PC guarantees distributed ACID atomicity across disparate database instances.\n- **Phase 1 (Prepare)**: Nodes validate constraints, write WAL records, and lock rows.\n- **Phase 2 (Commit)**: Coordinator orders all nodes to finalize changes once all vote yes.\n- 2PC is synchronous and blocking; in modern distributed microservices, asynchronous **Saga patterns** with compensating transactions are often preferred over 2PC.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Prepared transactions persist across database crashes and restarts! If an orphaned prepared transaction is forgotten, it will hold table locks and freeze `xmin` progress indefinitely, eventually causing autovacuum failure and database shutdown. Query `pg_prepared_xacts` to detect orphans.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "2PC guarantees distributed ACID atomicity across disparate database instances.",
      "Phase 1 (Prepare): Nodes validate constraints, write WAL records, and lock rows.",
      "Phase 2 (Commit): Coordinator orders all nodes to finalize changes once all vote yes.",
      "2PC is synchronous and blocking; in modern distributed microservices, asynchronous **Saga patterns** with compensating transactions are often preferred over 2PC."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Identifying and Resolving Orphaned Prepared Transactions in PostgreSQL\nSELECT \n    gid,            -- Global transaction identifier string\n    prepared,       -- Timestamp when prepared\n    owner, \n    database \nFROM pg_prepared_xacts;\n\n-- Manually resolving a stuck prepared transaction:\nROLLBACK PREPARED 'tx_transfer_step1_node1';"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Prepared transactions persist across database crashes and restarts! If an orphaned prepared transaction is forgotten, it will hold table locks and freeze `xmin` progress indefinitely, eventually causing autovacuum failure and database shutdown. Query `pg_prepared_xacts` to detect orphans.",
