    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Savepoints and Partial Rollbacks in SQL: Error Handling Without Aborting Entire Transactions",
    "answer": "### Handling Errors Inside SQL Transactions: The Problem\n\nIn PostgreSQL and ANSI SQL, when an individual statement encounters an error inside an active transaction (e.g., unique constraint violation, syntax error, division by zero), the entire transaction transitions into an **aborted error state**:\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO customers (id, name) VALUES (1, 'Alice'); -- Success\nINSERT INTO customers (id, name) VALUES (1, 'Duplicate'); -- ERROR: duplicate key!\nSELECT * FROM customers; -- ERROR: current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block\nCOMMIT; -- Results in ROLLBACK! All prior valid inserts are lost!\n```\n\n#### The Solution: `SAVEPOINT`\nA **Savepoint** establishes a named checkpoint within an existing transaction. If a statement fails or specific logic branch requires reversal, the transaction can roll back to the savepoint without discarding prior operations:\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n\nINSERT INTO audit_log (event) VALUES ('batch_start');\n\n-- Create Savepoint A\nSAVEPOINT savepoint_item_1;\n\nINSERT INTO orders (id, user_id, amount) VALUES (101, 1, 50.00);\n\n-- Create Savepoint B before risky insert\nSAVEPOINT savepoint_item_2;\n\n-- Suppose this insert violates a foreign key constraint\nINSERT INTO orders (id, user_id, amount) VALUES (102, 9999, 100.00);\n-- In application catch block, roll back ONLY the failed order:\nROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT savepoint_item_2;\n\n-- Release savepoint memory once no longer needed\nRELEASE SAVEPOINT savepoint_item_1;\n\nINSERT INTO audit_log (event) VALUES ('batch_completed_with_partial_success');\n\nCOMMIT; -- Order 101 and both audit logs are safely committed!\n```\n\n#### How ORMs Implement Nested Transactions\nRelational databases do not support genuine nested physical transactions (`BEGIN; BEGIN;`). ORMs like Hibernate, Prisma, and SQLAlchemy simulate nested transactions by translating inner `@Transactional` boundaries into database `SAVEPOINT`s under the hood.\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- A single SQL error inside a transaction invalidates the entire transaction block unless shielded by a savepoint.\n- `SAVEPOINT name`: Marks a rollback boundary.\n- `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT name`: Undoes changes made after the savepoint while retaining locks and changes made prior to it.\n- `RELEASE SAVEPOINT name`: Destroys the savepoint marker without rolling back, freeing transaction manager tracking overhead.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Creating thousands of savepoints inside a single loop generates severe performance degradation in PostgreSQL. Each savepoint allocates a subtransaction ID (SubXID). When SubXIDs overflow the shared memory cache (64 entries), PostgreSQL falls back to scanning `pg_subtrans` on disk, slowing down visibility checks across the entire cluster!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "A single SQL error inside a transaction invalidates the entire transaction block unless shielded by a savepoint.",
      "`SAVEPOINT name`: Marks a rollback boundary.",
      "`ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT name`: Undoes changes made after the savepoint while retaining locks and changes made prior to it.",
      "`RELEASE SAVEPOINT name`: Destroys the savepoint marker without rolling back, freeing transaction manager tracking overhead."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- PostgreSQL PL/pgSQL Exception Blocks Use Savepoints Internally\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION process_user_batch(user_ids INT[])\nRETURNS VOID LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$\nDECLARE\n    uid INT;\nBEGIN\n    FOREACH uid IN ARRAY user_ids LOOP\n        BEGIN\n            -- PL/pgSQL implicitly creates a SAVEPOINT here\n            INSERT INTO processed_users (user_id) VALUES (uid);\n        EXCEPTION WHEN unique_violation THEN\n            -- PL/pgSQL rolls back to the savepoint and continues the loop!\n            RAISE NOTICE 'User % already processed, skipping', uid;\n        END;\n    END LOOP;\nEND;\n$$;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Creating thousands of savepoints inside a single loop generates severe performance degradation in PostgreSQL. Each savepoint allocates a subtransaction ID (SubXID). When SubXIDs overflow the shared memory cache (64 entries), PostgreSQL falls back to scanning `pg_subtrans` on disk, slowing down visibility checks across the entire cluster!",
