    "id": "int-sql-061",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "B+ Tree Index Internals: Fan-Out, Height Calculation, and Disk I/O Efficiency",
    "answer": "### Why B+ Trees Dominate Database Storage Engines\n\nRelational databases store terabytes of data on disk. While Binary Search Trees (BST), AVL, or Red-Black trees require \\(O(\\log_2 N)\\) pointer dereferences, each disk I/O seek takes milliseconds. A B+ Tree optimizes for **block-oriented disk storage** by maximizing **fan-out**.\n\n```\n                      [ Root Node: 8KB Page ]\n                     /           |           \\\n           [ Internal 1 ]  [ Internal 2 ]  [ Internal 3 ]\n              /     \\         /     \\         /     \\\n           [ Leaf 1 ] <---> [ Leaf 2 ] <---> [ Leaf 3 ]  (Doubly Linked List)\n          (Row Data/Pointers stored ONLY in Leaf Nodes)\n```\n\n#### 1. Fundamental Properties of B+ Trees\n- **All Data in Leaf Nodes**: Interior nodes store only search keys and child page pointers; leaf nodes store actual keys and data row locators.\n- **High Fan-Out**: A single 8KB or 16KB interior page can hold hundreds or thousands of keys and child pointers.\n- **Doubly Linked Leaf Level**: Leaf nodes form a bidirectional linked list, enabling \\(O(1)\\) progression for range scans (`BETWEEN`, `>`, `<`).\n- **Balanced Depth**: Every leaf node is at the exact same depth from the root.\n\n#### 2. Height & Capacity Calculation Formula\nGiven:\n- Page size \\(B = 8192\\text{ bytes}\\)\n- Key size = 8 bytes (e.g., `BIGINT`)\n- Pointer size = 8 bytes\n- Leaf entry size = 32 bytes (Key + Heap Pointer / PK)\n\nInterior node entry size = \\(8 + 8 = 16\\text{ bytes}\\).\nAssuming 70% average page fill factor:\n$$\\text{Fan-Out } F = \\frac{8192 \\times 0.70}{16} \\approx 358\\text{ child pointers per interior page}$$\n\nNumber of records supported per Tree Height \\(H\\):\n$$\\text{Leaf capacity } L = \\frac{8192 \\times 0.70}{32} \\approx 179\\text{ rows per leaf page}$$\n- **Height 1 (Root only)**: \\(179\\) rows\n- **Height 2 (Root + Leaves)**: \\(358 \\times 179 \\approx 64,000\\) rows\n- **Height 3 (Root + 1 Interior Level + Leaves)**: \\(358^2 \\times 179 \\approx 22.9\\text{ million}\\) rows!\n- **Height 4**: \\(358^3 \\times 179 \\approx 8.2\\text{ billion}\\) rows!\n\nBecause the Root and Level-1 interior pages are permanently pinned in RAM (Buffer Pool), finding any record in an 8-billion row table requires at most **1 or 2 physical disk I/O reads**!\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- B+ Trees maximize fan-out, keeping tree height extremely low (\\(H = 3\\) to \\(4\\)) even for billions of rows.\n- Interior nodes store only keys and child page pointers; row data exists exclusively in leaf nodes.\n- Leaf nodes are linked via a doubly linked list, providing high performance for range scans and pagination.\n- Binary search trees fail in databases because low fan-out requires dozens of random disk seeks for every lookup.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Why not use a standard B-Tree where interior nodes also store data records? In a B-Tree, data records consume page space inside interior nodes, reducing the number of child pointers (fan-out) dramatically! This increases the overall tree height and forces more disk I/Os.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "B+ Trees maximize fan-out, keeping tree height extremely low (\\(H = 3\\) to \\(4\\)) even for billions of rows.",
      "Interior nodes store only keys and child page pointers; row data exists exclusively in leaf nodes.",
      "Leaf nodes are linked via a doubly linked list, providing high performance for range scans and pagination.",
      "Binary search trees fail in databases because low fan-out requires dozens of random disk seeks for every lookup."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Inspecting PostgreSQL B+ Tree Index Depth and Leaf Statistics:\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pageinspect;\n\n-- Inspect B-Tree Index Metadata (Height, Root page, Number of tuples)\nSELECT \n    level AS tree_height,\n    root,\n    leaf_pages,\n    empty_pages\nFROM bt_metap('idx_users_email');\n\n-- Inspect leaf page details\nSELECT \n    itemoffset, \n    ctid, \n    data \nFROM bt_page_items('idx_users_email', 1) \nLIMIT 5;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Why not use a standard B-Tree where interior nodes also store data records? In a B-Tree, data records consume page space inside interior nodes, reducing the number of child pointers (fan-out) dramatically! This increases the overall tree height and forces more disk I/Os.",
    "company_tags": [
      "Indexing",
      "B+ Tree",
      "Data Structures",
      "Fan-Out",
      "Storage Engine"
    ],
