    "id": "int-sql-062",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "GIN vs GiST Indexes in PostgreSQL: Full-Text Search, JSONB Arrays, and Geospatial Queries",
    "answer": "### Specialized Index Types: GIN vs GiST\n\nWhile standard B+ Tree indexes excel at scalar 1-to-1 comparisons (`=`, `<`, `>`), modern databases manage composite, multi-valued, and non-scalar structures (e.g., full-text documents, JSON arrays, GIS spatial polygons). For these, PostgreSQL provides **GIN** and **GiST** indexes.\n\n```\n+------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+\n| Dimension              | GIN (Generalized Inverted Index)                | GiST (Generalized Search Tree)               |\n+------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+\n| Internal Structure     | Inverted index (Token -> List of Row IDs / TIDs)| Balanced tree of bounding predicates / boxes |\n| Value Multiplicity     | 1 row maps to MULTIPLE index keys (array/words) | 1 row maps to 1 hierarchical bounding shape  |\n| Best For               | JSONB `@>`, Full-Text Search (`tsvector`), Array| PostGIS geometry, IP ranges, overlaps (`&&`) |\n| Write Speed            | Slower (updates multiple inverted postings)     | Faster updates than GIN                      |\n| Read Speed             | Extremely fast exact match / contains lookups   | Fast for nearest-neighbor (k-NN), overlaps   |\n| Index Size             | Larger (stores postings lists)                  | Compact                                      |\n+------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### 1. GIN: The Powerhouse for JSONB and Tag Arrays\nA GIN index splits compound values into individual elements and creates an inverted lookup table:\n\n```sql\n-- Tag filtering table\nCREATE TABLE articles (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    title TEXT,\n    tags TEXT[],\n    attributes JSONB\n);\n\n-- Create GIN index on array and jsonb\nCREATE INDEX idx_articles_tags ON articles USING GIN (tags);\nCREATE INDEX idx_articles_attrs ON articles USING GIN (attributes jsonb_path_ops);\n\n-- Lightning-fast query execution via GIN index:\nSELECT * FROM articles \nWHERE tags @> ARRAY['database', 'postgres'];\n\nSELECT * FROM articles \nWHERE attributes @> '{\"status\": \"published\", \"author\": \"Alice\"}';\n```\n\n#### 2. GiST: The Engine for Range Types and Geospatial\nGiST structures arbitrary hierarchical search trees. For example, in PostGIS or range queries, a bounding box covers child bounding boxes:\n\n```sql\n-- Room reservation table preventing overlapping bookings\nCREATE TABLE room_bookings (\n    room_id INT,\n    during TSRANGE,\n    EXCLUDE USING GIST (room_id WITH =, during WITH &&)\n);\n-- The GiST index enforces exclusion: No two rows can have the same room_id\n-- with overlapping time ranges (&& operator)!\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- **GIN (Inverted Index)** maps individual tokens or elements to lists of matching rows; ideal for JSONB containment (`@>`), arrays, and full-text search.\n- **GiST (Search Tree)** is a balanced tree indexing bounding shapes; ideal for spatial coordinates (PostGIS), IP address ranges, and date ranges.\n- `jsonb_path_ops` produces smaller, faster GIN indexes than default `jsonb_ops` if queries only perform containment searches (`@>`).\n- GIN write overhead can be mitigated using the `fastupdate = on` pending list.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> By default, GIN indexes have `fastupdate = on`, which buffers new index entries in a temporary pending list. While this speeds up `INSERT`s, it causes random read query latency spikes whenever the pending list exceeds `gin_pending_list_limit` and triggers an inline flush!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "GIN (Inverted Index): maps individual tokens or elements to lists of matching rows; ideal for JSONB containment (`@>`), arrays, and full-text search.",
      "GiST (Search Tree): is a balanced tree indexing bounding shapes; ideal for spatial coordinates (PostGIS), IP address ranges, and date ranges.",
      "`jsonb_path_ops` produces smaller, faster GIN indexes than default `jsonb_ops` if queries only perform containment searches (`@>`).",
      "GIN write overhead can be mitigated using the `fastupdate = on` pending list."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Full-Text Search with GIN Index\nALTER TABLE articles ADD COLUMN tsv_content tsvector \nGENERATED ALWAYS AS (to_tsvector('english', coalesce(title, ''))) STORED;\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_articles_fts ON articles USING GIN (tsv_content);\n\n-- Querying using full-text search operators:\nSELECT title \nFROM articles \nWHERE tsv_content @@ to_tsquery('english', 'distributed & database');"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> By default, GIN indexes have `fastupdate = on`, which buffers new index entries in a temporary pending list. While this speeds up `INSERT`s, it causes random read query latency spikes whenever the pending list exceeds `gin_pending_list_limit` and triggers an inline flush!",
    "company_tags": [
      "PostgreSQL",
      "GIN",
      "GiST",
      "JSONB",
      "Full Text Search"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
