    "id": "int-sql-066",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Deconstructing EXPLAIN ANALYZE: Cost, Actual Time, Buffers, Loops, and Estimation Skew",
    "answer": "### Reading Query Execution Plans: EXPLAIN vs EXPLAIN ANALYZE\n\n- **`EXPLAIN`**: Shows the optimizer's estimated execution plan based on table statistics without actually running the query.\n- **`EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`**: Actually executes the query, measures real CPU time, records buffer cache hits/reads, and compares estimates to reality.\n\n```\n                                                     QUERY PLAN\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n Hash Join  (cost=3.25..35.50 rows=100 width=72) (actual time=0.045..0.820 rows=120 loops=1)\n   Hash Cond: (o.customer_id = c.id)\n   Buffers: shared hit=14 read=2\n   ->  Seq Scan on orders o  (cost=0.00..28.50 rows=800 width=40) (actual time=0.012..0.450 rows=800 loops=1)\n         Buffers: shared hit=10\n   ->  Hash  (cost=2.00..2.00 rows=100 width=36) (actual time=0.025..0.025 rows=100 loops=1)\n         Buckets: 1024  Batches: 1  Memory Usage: 15kB\n         Buffers: shared hit=4\n         ->  Seq Scan on customers c  (cost=0.00..2.00 rows=100 width=36) (actual time=0.005..0.015 rows=100 loops=1)\n```\n\n#### Anatomical Breakdown of Plan Metrics\n\n##### 1. `cost=3.25..35.50`\n- `3.25` = **Startup cost** (units of arbitrary disk page fetch cost): Cost to return the very first row (e.g., time spent building the hash table).\n- `35.50` = **Total cost**: Cost to process and return all matching rows.\n\n##### 2. `actual time=0.045..0.820 rows=120 loops=1`\n- `0.045` ms = Time taken to output the first row.\n- `0.820` ms = Total elapsed time for this node.\n- **`loops=N`**: If a node executes multiple times (common in Nested Loop joins), the `actual time` and `rows` values shown are **averages per loop**! You must multiply by `loops` to compute total time.\n\n##### 3. `Buffers: shared hit=14 read=2`\n- `shared hit`: 8KB data pages fetched directly from RAM (Buffer Pool).\n- `read`: 8KB pages read from physical disk (or OS filesystem page cache).\n- `dirtied / written`: Pages modified and flushed.\n\n##### 4. Estimation Skew: The #1 Indicator of Query Problems\nCompare `rows=100` (Optimizer estimate) against `actual rows=120` (Reality):\n- When estimated rows differ from actual rows by an order of magnitude (e.g., estimated 1, actual 500,000), the optimizer chooses terrible join strategies (e.g., choosing Nested Loop instead of Hash Join).\n- **Cause**: Stale statistics, skewed data distributions, or unindexed correlated expressions.\n- **Fix**: Run `ANALYZE table_name;` or increase `default_statistics_target`.\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- `EXPLAIN` estimates without running; `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` executes the query and measures actual runtime.\n- Always include `BUFFERS` (`EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`) to see memory vs disk I/O cache behavior.\n- In nested loops, multiply node `actual time` and `rows` by `loops` count to get total runtime.\n- Huge discrepancies between estimated `rows` and `actual rows` signal outdated statistics or un-analyzed tables.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Be careful running `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on `DELETE` or `UPDATE` statements in production! Because `ANALYZE` actually executes the query, it will modify or delete production rows. Always wrap it inside a transaction rollback: `BEGIN; EXPLAIN ANALYZE DELETE ...; ROLLBACK;`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "`EXPLAIN` estimates without running; `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` executes the query and measures actual runtime.",
      "Always include `BUFFERS` (`EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`) to see memory vs disk I/O cache behavior.",
      "In nested loops, multiply node `actual time` and `rows` by `loops` count to get total runtime.",
      "Huge discrepancies between estimated `rows` and `actual rows` signal outdated statistics or un-analyzed tables."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Production Best Practice: Running EXPLAIN ANALYZE safely on mutating queries\nBEGIN;\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING, COSTS, VERBOSE)\nDELETE FROM sessions WHERE last_active < NOW() - INTERVAL '30 days';\nROLLBACK; -- Ensures no data is permanently deleted during profiling!"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Be careful running `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on `DELETE` or `UPDATE` statements in production! Because `ANALYZE` actually executes the query, it will modify or delete production rows. Always wrap it inside a transaction rollback: `BEGIN; EXPLAIN ANALYZE DELETE ...; ROLLBACK;`.",
