    "id": "int-sql-068",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "The Implicit Type Casting Trap: Why VARCHAR = INT Destroys Index Usage",
    "answer": "### The Hidden Cost of Type Coercion\n\nIn relational databases, when a query compares two operands of differing data types (e.g., `VARCHAR` column vs `INT` literal), the database must implicitly convert one operand to match the other according to **type precedence rules**.\n\nIf the type precedence rules dictate that the **indexed table column** must be cast to match the literal, the index is instantly invalidated!\n\n```\nComparison: phone_number (VARCHAR with B+ Tree Index) = 123456789 (INT Literal)\n\nMySQL Type Precedence: String is converted to Double/Integer!\nEngine rewrites query to:\n  WHERE CAST(phone_number AS SIGNED) = 123456789;\n  --> SARGability destroyed! Performs FULL TABLE SCAN on millions of rows!\n\nPostgreSQL Behavior:\n  PostgreSQL has strict typing; it throws an error:\n  ERROR: operator does not exist: character varying = integer\n  HINT: No operator matches the given name and argument types. You might need to add explicit type casts.\n```\n\n#### What About the Reverse?\n```sql\n-- Comparison: user_id (INT column with B+ Tree Index) = '42' (VARCHAR Literal)\n```\nIn both MySQL and PostgreSQL, the string `'42'` is converted to integer `42`.\nThe query is rewritten to:\n```sql\nWHERE user_id = CAST('42' AS INT); -- i.e. WHERE user_id = 42\n```\nBecause the function was applied to the **constant literal** and NOT to the indexed column, the index remains fully SARGable!\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Implicit type conversion on an indexed column turns an index seek into a full table scan.\n- In MySQL, comparing a `VARCHAR` column to a numeric literal casts the column, destroying index usage.\n- In PostgreSQL, strict type safety prevents implicit string-to-number coercions, surfacing errors early.\n- Always pass query parameters matching the exact schema data types in application ORM mappings.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> ORM frameworks (like Spring Data JPA or Hibernate) frequently cause this bug when a Java `String` property is mapped to a column, but a developer writes a repository method passing a `Long` or `Integer` argument. The generated JDBC statement sends `?` typed as `BIGINT`, triggering silent full table scans in MySQL!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Implicit type conversion on an indexed column turns an index seek into a full table scan.",
      "In MySQL, comparing a `VARCHAR` column to a numeric literal casts the column, destroying index usage.",
      "In PostgreSQL, strict type safety prevents implicit string-to-number coercions, surfacing errors early.",
      "Always pass query parameters matching the exact schema data types in application ORM mappings."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Reproducing and Fixing the Implicit Cast Trap in MySQL:\n-- Table with indexed phone number string:\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,\n    phone VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\n    KEY idx_phone (phone)\n);\n\n-- WRONG: Casts phone column to number -> FULL TABLE SCAN\nEXPLAIN SELECT * FROM users WHERE phone = 9876543210;\n-- Type: ALL, Possible Keys: NULL\n\n-- CORRECT: Matches exact data type -> FAST INDEX SEEK\nEXPLAIN SELECT * FROM users WHERE phone = '9876543210';\n-- Type: ref, Key: idx_phone"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> ORM frameworks (like Spring Data JPA or Hibernate) frequently cause this bug when a Java `String` property is mapped to a column, but a developer writes a repository method passing a `Long` or `Integer` argument. The generated JDBC statement sends `?` typed as `BIGINT`, triggering silent full table scans in MySQL!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Indexing",
      "Type Casting",
      "Performance",
      "Gotchas",
      "Optimizer"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
