    "title": "SQL Upsert Patterns: INSERT ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE vs ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE vs MERGE",
    "answer": "### The Upsert Problem: Atomic Insert or Update\n\nIn high-concurrency applications, checking if a record exists with `SELECT`, followed by either `INSERT` or `UPDATE`, introduces a race condition (Time-Of-Check to Time-Of-Use / TOCTOU): two concurrent requests can both see the record is absent and both attempt `INSERT`, causing a duplicate key error.\n\nModern SQL engines resolve this via atomic **Upsert** statements.\n\n```\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Database System                    | Native Syntax                                                          |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PostgreSQL (9.5+), SQLite (3.24+)  | INSERT INTO ... ON CONFLICT (col) DO UPDATE SET ...                   |\n| MySQL (5.7+ / 8.0+)                | INSERT INTO ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE ...                           |\n| SQL Standard / Oracle / PG 15+     | MERGE INTO target USING source ON (...) WHEN MATCHED ...              |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### 1. PostgreSQL: `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT`\nRequires specifying the conflicting target column/constraint:\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO page_views (page_id, view_count, last_viewed_at)\nVALUES (42, 1, NOW())\nON CONFLICT (page_id) \nDO UPDATE SET \n    view_count = page_views.view_count + EXCLUDED.view_count,\n    last_viewed_at = EXCLUDED.last_viewed_at\nRETURNING id, view_count;\n```\n- `EXCLUDED`: A pseudo-record representing the values proposed in the `INSERT` clause.\n- Can also specify `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` for idempotent inserts.\n\n#### 2. MySQL: `INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE`\n```sql\nINSERT INTO page_views (page_id, view_count, last_viewed_at)\nVALUES (42, 1, NOW())\nON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE \n    view_count = view_count + VALUES(view_count),\n    last_viewed_at = VALUES(last_viewed_at);\n```\n\n#### 3. ANSI SQL `MERGE` Statement (PostgreSQL 15+, Oracle, SQL Server)\n```sql\nMERGE INTO customer_balance cb\nUSING (SELECT 101 AS cust_id, 250.00 AS payment) src\nON cb.customer_id = src.cust_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN\n    UPDATE SET balance = cb.balance + src.payment\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN\n    INSERT (customer_id, balance) VALUES (src.cust_id, src.payment);\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Never implement upserts via application-level `SELECT` followed by `INSERT/UPDATE`; concurrent requests will fail with unique violations.\n- PostgreSQL uses `ON CONFLICT (target) DO UPDATE SET ...` utilizing the `EXCLUDED` record.\n- PostgreSQL `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` provides safe, idempotent batch ingestion.\n- ANSI `MERGE` provides unified syntax across engines and supports conditional deletes in addition to updates.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> In PostgreSQL, `ON CONFLICT` still burns through sequence numbers (`BIGSERIAL`) even when an update occurs instead of an insert! If you perform millions of upserts on existing rows, your sequence ID will advance rapidly, potentially exhausting 32-bit `SERIAL` IDs. Always use `BIGINT` / `BIGSERIAL`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Never implement upserts via application-level `SELECT` followed by `INSERT/UPDATE`; concurrent requests will fail with unique violations.",
      "PostgreSQL uses `ON CONFLICT (target) DO UPDATE SET ...` utilizing the `EXCLUDED` record.",
      "PostgreSQL `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` provides safe, idempotent batch ingestion.",
      "ANSI `MERGE` provides unified syntax across engines and supports conditional deletes in addition to updates."
    ],
