    "title": "Finding Missing Numbers and Sequence Gaps: GENERATE_SERIES vs Window Differences",
    "answer": "### The Gap Detection Problem in SQL\n\nIn accounting, invoice tracking, or order processing, gaps in sequentially generated numbers (e.g., Missing Invoice #4 and #7 in sequence `1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10`) indicate lost data, skipped transactions, or fraud.\n\n#### Method 1: Using `LEAD()` Window Function (Detects Start and End of Gaps)\nBy comparing each record's ID to the next record's ID using `LEAD()`, any difference greater than 1 represents a missing gap:\n\n```sql\nWITH sequence_check AS (\n    SELECT \n        invoice_id,\n        LEAD(invoice_id) OVER (ORDER BY invoice_id) AS next_id\n    FROM invoices\n)\nSELECT \n    invoice_id + 1 AS gap_start,\n    next_id - 1 AS gap_end,\n    (next_id - invoice_id - 1) AS missing_count\nFROM sequence_check\nWHERE next_id - invoice_id > 1;\n```\n\n#### Method 2: Generating Expected Sequence with `GENERATE_SERIES`\nTo list **every individual missing number**:\n```sql\nSELECT expected.id AS missing_id\nFROM generate_series(\n    (SELECT MIN(invoice_id) FROM invoices),\n    (SELECT MAX(invoice_id) FROM invoices)\n) AS expected(id)\nLEFT JOIN invoices i ON expected.id = i.invoice_id\nWHERE i.invoice_id IS NULL\nORDER BY missing_id;\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- The `LEAD()` method is optimal when you want to report contiguous gap ranges (`gap_start` to `gap_end`) efficiently in \\(O(N \\log N)\\).\n- The `generate_series()` method is ideal when you need to enumerate every individual missing integer.\n- The `generate_series()` approach requires an anti-join (`LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right.id IS NULL`) or `NOT EXISTS`.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> The `LEAD()` approach fails to detect gaps at the very beginning of the range (e.g., if the sequence should start at 1, but the lowest ID present in the table is 5). To guard against this, `UNION ALL` an artificial boundary record `0` before evaluating window functions.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "The `LEAD()` method is optimal when you want to report contiguous gap ranges (`gap_start` to `gap_end`) efficiently in \\(O(N \\log N)\\).",
      "The `generate_series()` method is ideal when you need to enumerate every individual missing integer.",
      "The `generate_series()` approach requires an anti-join (`LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right.id IS NULL`) or `NOT EXISTS`."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Detecting Gaps and Islands with Full Gap Boundaries\nWITH bounds AS (\n    SELECT 0 AS invoice_id UNION ALL SELECT invoice_id FROM invoices\n),\ngap_calc AS (\n    SELECT \n        invoice_id,\n        LEAD(invoice_id) OVER (ORDER BY invoice_id) AS next_id\n    FROM bounds\n)\nSELECT \n    invoice_id + 1 AS gap_start,\n    next_id - 1 AS gap_end\nFROM gap_calc\nWHERE next_id - invoice_id > 1;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> The `LEAD()` approach fails to detect gaps at the very beginning of the range (e.g., if the sequence should start at 1, but the lowest ID present in the table is 5). To guard against this, `UNION ALL` an artificial boundary record `0` before evaluating window functions.",
