    "title": "Calculating the Exact Median in SQL: PERCENTILE_CONT vs Window Numbering",
    "answer": "### The Median Challenge in SQL\n\nUnlike `AVG()`, standard ANSI SQL historically lacked a native aggregate `MEDIAN()` function because computing the median requires ordering the entire dataset and finding the middle element (or averaging the two middle elements if the count is even).\n\n#### Method 1: Using `PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5)` (ANSI Standard & PostgreSQL)\nThe standard modern mechanism utilizes the `WITHIN GROUP` ordered-set aggregate:\n\n```sql\n-- Continuous median (interpolates middle values if count is even):\nSELECT \n    dept_id,\n    PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) AS median_salary\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY dept_id;\n\n-- Discrete median (returns an actual existing row value):\nSELECT \n    dept_id,\n    PERCENTILE_DISC(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) AS median_salary\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY dept_id;\n```\n\n#### Method 2: Pure Window Functions (Universal - Works in MySQL 8.0+, SQLite, SQL Server)\nIf `PERCENTILE_CONT` is unavailable, compute the median using `ROW_NUMBER()` and `COUNT()`:\n\n```sql\nWITH ranked_data AS (\n    SELECT \n        salary,\n        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY salary) AS row_asc,\n        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS row_desc\n    FROM employees\n)\nSELECT AVG(salary * 1.0) AS median_salary\nFROM ranked_data\nWHERE row_asc IN (row_desc, row_desc - 1, row_desc + 1);\n```\n**Why this works**: For an odd count of 5 elements, the 3rd element has `row_asc = 3` and `row_desc = 3`. For an even count of 6 elements, the middle two elements have `(3, 4)` and `(4, 3)`, which satisfy the condition `row_asc IN (row_desc - 1, row_desc + 1)`. Taking the `AVG()` yields the exact mathematical median!\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- `PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5)` computes the interpolated mathematical median.\n- `PERCENTILE_DISC(0.5)` returns the nearest actual value from the dataset.\n- The dual `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ... ASC)` and `DESC` trick calculates the median on any SQL database supporting window functions.\n- Median is resilient to extreme outliers compared to `AVG()`, making it essential for latency and salary analysis.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> On billion-row datasets, computing an exact median requires a full sort (\\(O(N \\log N)\\)) which cannot be parallelized easily. In big data engines (ClickHouse, BigQuery, Presto), use approximate percentile algorithms like `APPROX_QUANTILES(salary, 100)[OFFSET(50)]` or T-Digest / HyperLogLog sketches for \\(O(N)\\) computation.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "`PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5)` computes the interpolated mathematical median.",
      "`PERCENTILE_DISC(0.5)` returns the nearest actual value from the dataset.",
      "The dual `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ... ASC)` and `DESC` trick calculates the median on any SQL database supporting window functions.",
      "Median is resilient to extreme outliers compared to `AVG()`, making it essential for latency and salary analysis."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Comparing Mean vs Median to Detect Skew in Compensation:\nSELECT \n    department,\n    COUNT(*) AS employee_count,\n    ROUND(AVG(salary), 2) AS mean_salary,\n    PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) AS median_salary,\n    ROUND(AVG(salary) - PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary), 2) AS skew\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY department\nORDER BY skew DESC;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> On billion-row datasets, computing an exact median requires a full sort (\\(O(N \\log N)\\)) which cannot be parallelized easily. In big data engines (ClickHouse, BigQuery, Presto), use approximate percentile algorithms like `APPROX_QUANTILES(salary, 100)[OFFSET(50)]` or T-Digest / HyperLogLog sketches for \\(O(N)\\) computation.",
