    "id": "int-sql-074",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Pivoting Rows to Columns Without Vendor Extensions: The Cross-Tab Conditional Aggregation Technique",
    "answer": "### Pivoting in SQL: Transforming Tall Data into Wide Data\n\nRelational databases store data normalized in \"tall\" format (many rows), but business dashboards require \"wide\" cross-tabulated reports (rows converted to column headers).\n\n```\nTall Normalized Input:\n+---------+----------+--------+\n| year    | quarter  | sales  |\n+---------+----------+--------+\n| 2023    | Q1       | 1000   |\n| 2023    | Q2       | 1500   |\n| 2023    | Q3       | 1200   |\n| 2023    | Q4       | 1800   |\n| 2024    | Q1       | 2000   |\n+---------+----------+--------+\n\nPivoted Wide Output:\n+---------+--------+--------+--------+--------+-------------+\n| year    | Q1     | Q2     | Q3     | Q4     | Total_Sales |\n+---------+--------+--------+--------+--------+-------------+\n| 2023    | 1000   | 1500   | 1200   | 1800   | 5500        |\n| 2024    | 2000   | 0      | 0      | 0      | 2000        |\n+---------+--------+--------+--------+--------+-------------+\n```\n\n#### The Universal Standard: Conditional Aggregation (`CASE WHEN`)\nWhile SQL Server and Oracle offer proprietary `PIVOT` keywords, conditional aggregation with `SUM(CASE WHEN ...)` is 100% portable across PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, Snowflake, and BigQuery:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n    year,\n    COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q1' THEN sales END), 0) AS Q1,\n    COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q2' THEN sales END), 0) AS Q2,\n    COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q3' THEN sales END), 0) AS Q3,\n    COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN quarter = 'Q4' THEN sales END), 0) AS Q4,\n    SUM(sales) AS total_sales\nFROM quarterly_sales\nGROUP BY year\nORDER BY year;\n```\n\n#### Filtering Aggregates in PostgreSQL: `FILTER (WHERE ...)`\nPostgreSQL offers the cleaner ANSI standard `FILTER` clause:\n```sql\nSELECT \n    year,\n    COALESCE(SUM(sales) FILTER (WHERE quarter = 'Q1'), 0) AS Q1,\n    COALESCE(SUM(sales) FILTER (WHERE quarter = 'Q2'), 0) AS Q2,\n    COALESCE(SUM(sales) FILTER (WHERE quarter = 'Q3'), 0) AS Q3,\n    COALESCE(SUM(sales) FILTER (WHERE quarter = 'Q4'), 0) AS Q4\nFROM quarterly_sales\nGROUP BY year;\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Pivoting rotates distinct row values into individual column headers.\n- Conditional aggregation (`SUM(CASE WHEN col = 'val' THEN metric END)`) is the universal standard.\n- Always wrap conditional sums in `COALESCE(..., 0)` to prevent NULLs when a period has zero activity.\n- In PostgreSQL, `FILTER (WHERE ...)` offers clean syntax that is semantically equivalent to `CASE WHEN`.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Can SQL dynamically generate pivot columns when the distinct values are unknown at query time (e.g., dynamic product names)? **No!** Relational SQL requires fixed, predefined result schema columns before query compilation. To pivot on dynamic values, you must construct dynamic SQL in a stored procedure or pivot in your application layer.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Pivoting rotates distinct row values into individual column headers.",
      "Conditional aggregation (`SUM(CASE WHEN col = 'val' THEN metric END)`) is the universal standard.",
      "Always wrap conditional sums in `COALESCE(..., 0)` to prevent NULLs when a period has zero activity.",
      "In PostgreSQL, `FILTER (WHERE ...)` offers clean syntax that is semantically equivalent to `CASE WHEN`."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Multi-Metric Pivot: Pivoting Both Count and Volume Simultaneously\nSELECT \n    merchant_id,\n    COUNT(CASE WHEN status = 'SUCCESS' THEN 1 END) AS successful_orders,\n    COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'SUCCESS' THEN amount END), 0) AS successful_volume,\n    COUNT(CASE WHEN status = 'REFUNDED' THEN 1 END) AS refunded_orders,\n    COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'REFUNDED' THEN amount END), 0) AS refunded_volume\nFROM transactions\nGROUP BY merchant_id;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Can SQL dynamically generate pivot columns when the distinct values are unknown at query time (e.g., dynamic product names)? **No!** Relational SQL requires fixed, predefined result schema columns before query compilation. To pivot on dynamic values, you must construct dynamic SQL in a stored procedure or pivot in your application layer.",
