    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Unpivoting Columns to Rows: Normalizing Wide Tables Using CROSS JOIN LATERAL and VALUES",
    "answer": "### Unpivoting in SQL: Wide to Tall Transformation\n\n**Unpivoting** is the reverse of pivoting: converting multiple static columns into multiple rows (normalizing data for relational operations and group aggregations).\n\n```\nWide Input:\n+------------+-----------+-----------+-----------+\n| product_id | q1_sales  | q2_sales  | q3_sales  |\n+------------+-----------+-----------+-----------+\n| 101        | 500       | 750       | 900       |\n+------------+-----------+-----------+-----------+\n\nUnpivoted Tall Output:\n+------------+----------+--------+\n| product_id | quarter  | sales  |\n+------------+----------+--------+\n| 101        | Q1       | 500    |\n| 101        | Q2       | 750    |\n| 101        | Q3       | 900    |\n+------------+----------+--------+\n```\n\n#### Method 1: The Modern Optimal Way (`CROSS JOIN LATERAL` / `VALUES`)\nUsing `CROSS JOIN LATERAL` with row values constructors generates new rows per source record in a single table scan:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n    p.product_id,\n    u.quarter,\n    u.sales\nFROM product_sales p\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    VALUES \n        ('Q1', p.q1_sales),\n        ('Q2', p.q2_sales),\n        ('Q3', p.q3_sales)\n) AS u(quarter, sales);\n```\n\n#### Method 2: The Legacy `UNION ALL` Approach\n```sql\nSELECT product_id, 'Q1' AS quarter, q1_sales AS sales FROM product_sales\nUNION ALL\nSELECT product_id, 'Q2' AS quarter, q2_sales AS sales FROM product_sales\nUNION ALL\nSELECT product_id, 'Q3' AS quarter, q3_sales AS sales FROM product_sales;\n```\n**Why `CROSS JOIN LATERAL` is Superior**:\n`UNION ALL` scans the underlying table 3 distinct times (once for each branch). `CROSS JOIN LATERAL` scans the underlying table **exactly once**, unrolling the columns in CPU memory!\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Unpivoting transforms columns into rows, normalizing wide spreadsheets for relational analysis.\n- `CROSS JOIN LATERAL (VALUES ...)` is the highest-performing unpivoting technique because it scans the source table only once.\n- Legacy `UNION ALL` unpivoting triggers multiple table scans, degrading performance on large tables.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> What happens if a column value is NULL? In standard unpivoting, missing or NULL quarters should usually be excluded. Simply append `WHERE u.sales IS NOT NULL` to the `CROSS JOIN LATERAL` query to automatically filter empty cells.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Unpivoting transforms columns into rows, normalizing wide spreadsheets for relational analysis.",
      "`CROSS JOIN LATERAL (VALUES ...)` is the highest-performing unpivoting technique because it scans the source table only once.",
      "Legacy `UNION ALL` unpivoting triggers multiple table scans, degrading performance on large tables."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Unpivoting Multi-Language Columns in Product Catalog:\nSELECT \n    p.id,\n    l.lang_code,\n    l.description\nFROM products p\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    VALUES \n        ('en', p.desc_en),\n        ('es', p.desc_es),\n        ('fr', p.desc_fr),\n        ('de', p.desc_de)\n) AS l(lang_code, description)\nWHERE l.description IS NOT NULL;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> What happens if a column value is NULL? In standard unpivoting, missing or NULL quarters should usually be excluded. Simply append `WHERE u.sales IS NOT NULL` to the `CROSS JOIN LATERAL` query to automatically filter empty cells.",
