    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Hierarchical Data & Path Reconstruction: Full Breadcrumb Paths Using Recursive CTEs",
    "answer": "### Tree Traversal & Path Generation in SQL\n\nAdjacency list models store hierarchical data using a `parent_id` foreign key referencing the table's own primary key (e.g., E-commerce Category Trees or Organizational Charts).\n\n```\nElectronics (id=1, parent=NULL)\n   └── Computers (id=2, parent=1)\n          └── Laptops (id=3, parent=2)\n                 └── Gaming Laptops (id=4, parent=3)\n```\n\n#### Generating Full Breadcrumb Paths and Depth Levels\nTo build a breadcrumb string (`Electronics > Computers > Laptops > Gaming Laptops`) and compute tree depth, use a **Recursive Common Table Expression (CTE)**:\n\n```sql\nWITH RECURSIVE category_tree AS (\n    -- Anchor Member: Find all Root Categories (parent_id IS NULL)\n    SELECT \n        id,\n        name,\n        parent_id,\n        1 AS depth,\n        CAST(name AS TEXT) AS breadcrumb_path,\n        ARRAY[id] AS path_array\n    FROM categories\n    WHERE parent_id IS NULL\n\n    UNION ALL\n\n    -- Recursive Member: Join children to their parents\n    SELECT \n        c.id,\n        c.name,\n        c.parent_id,\n        ct.depth + 1 AS depth,\n        ct.breadcrumb_path || ' > ' || c.name AS breadcrumb_path,\n        ct.path_array || c.id AS path_array\n    FROM categories c\n    JOIN category_tree ct ON c.parent_id = ct.id\n)\nSELECT \n    id,\n    name,\n    depth,\n    breadcrumb_path\nFROM category_tree\nORDER BY path_array; -- Orders hierarchically!\n```\n\n#### Key Components:\n1. **Anchor Query**: Identifies root nodes (`parent_id IS NULL`), initializes `depth = 1`, and seeds breadcrumb text.\n2. **Recursive Query**: Joins child rows `c.parent_id` to the prior iteration `ct.id`. Runs iteratively until no children remain.\n3. **`path_array`**: Accumulates the lineage of IDs (`{1, 2, 3, 4}`). Ordering by `path_array` outputs the tree in exact **Depth-First Search (DFS)** pre-order!\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Recursive CTEs consist of an **Anchor Member**, `UNION ALL`, and a **Recursive Member** referencing the CTE itself.\n- Tracking path arrays (`ARRAY[id]`) enables hierarchical sorting and prevents infinite loops from circular references.\n- Breadcrumbs are constructed by concatenating the parent's accumulated path with the current node's name.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> If dirty data introduces a circular loop (e.g., Category 1 is parent of 2, but Category 2 is parent of 1), a standard recursive CTE will loop infinitely until memory exhausts! In PostgreSQL 14+, use the `CYCLE` clause (`CYCLE id SET is_cycle USING path`) to guard against infinite loops automatically.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Recursive CTEs consist of an **Anchor Member**, `UNION ALL`, and a **Recursive Member** referencing the CTE itself.",
      "Tracking path arrays (`ARRAY[id]`) enables hierarchical sorting and prevents infinite loops from circular references.",
      "Breadcrumbs are constructed by concatenating the parent's accumulated path with the current node's name."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Preventing Infinite Loops in Hierarchical CTEs (PostgreSQL 14+):\nWITH RECURSIVE org_chart AS (\n    SELECT emp_id, manager_id, name, 1 AS level\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE manager_id IS NULL\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT e.emp_id, e.manager_id, e.name, oc.level + 1\n    FROM employees e\n    JOIN org_chart oc ON e.manager_id = oc.emp_id\n)\nCYCLE emp_id SET is_cycle USING path -- Halts recursion immediately if a cycle is detected!\nSELECT * FROM org_chart WHERE NOT is_cycle;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> If dirty data introduces a circular loop (e.g., Category 1 is parent of 2, but Category 2 is parent of 1), a standard recursive CTE will loop infinitely until memory exhausts! In PostgreSQL 14+, use the `CYCLE` clause (`CYCLE id SET is_cycle USING path`) to guard against infinite loops automatically.",
