    "id": "int-sql-080",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Market Basket Analysis in SQL: Product Association Pairs and Co-Purchase Frequency",
    "answer": "### Market Basket Analysis: \"Frequently Bought Together\"\n\nAmazon's recommendation widget (\"Customers who bought this also bought...\") is built on **Co-Occurrence Frequency**: identifying pairs of items that appear together in the same shopping cart or transaction.\n\n```\nOrder 1: Milk, Bread, Butter\nOrder 2: Bread, Butter, Diapers\nOrder 3: Milk, Bread, Beer\nResulting Pair: (Bread, Butter) appears in Order 1 and Order 2 -> Frequency = 2\n```\n\n#### Finding Top Product Pairs Using a Self-Join\nTo generate item pairs without duplicates or self-pairings, perform a `SELF JOIN` on the order items table with a strict inequality (`p1.product_id < p2.product_id`):\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n    p1.product_id AS product_a,\n    p2.product_id AS product_b,\n    COUNT(*) AS co_purchase_count\nFROM order_items p1\nJOIN order_items p2 \n  ON p1.order_id = p2.order_id \n AND p1.product_id < p2.product_id -- Prevents (A, A) and eliminates mirror (B, A)\nGROUP BY p1.product_id, p2.product_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 5 -- Support threshold\nORDER BY co_purchase_count DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\n#### Why `p1.product_id < p2.product_id` is Critical:\n1. **Eliminates Self-Pairs**: If you used `=`, an item would pair with itself (`Milk, Milk`).\n2. **Eliminates Commutative Mirrors**: If you used `!=`, the output would contain both `(Milk, Bread)` and `(Bread, Milk)`. Using `<` enforces canonical ordering, cutting intermediate join volume by 50%!\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Market Basket Analysis identifies affinity between products purchased in the same transaction.\n- Self-joins on `order_id` pair items together.\n- The condition `item_a < item_b` guarantees each undirected pair is counted exactly once.\n- The `HAVING` clause filters out statistical noise by requiring a minimum co-purchase support threshold.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> On huge e-commerce tables, an unconstrained self-join explodes into billions of rows for large orders. Always filter out bulk orders (e.g., `WHERE order_id NOT IN (SELECT order_id FROM order_items GROUP BY order_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 50)`) before running market basket analysis!",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Market Basket Analysis identifies affinity between products purchased in the same transaction.",
      "Self-joins on `order_id` pair items together.",
      "The condition `item_a < item_b` guarantees each undirected pair is counted exactly once.",
      "The `HAVING` clause filters out statistical noise by requiring a minimum co-purchase support threshold."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Computing Confidence Metric: P(Product B | Product A)\nWITH pair_counts AS (\n    SELECT \n        p1.product_id AS prod_a,\n        p2.product_id AS prod_b,\n        COUNT(*) AS co_purchases\n    FROM order_items p1\n    JOIN order_items p2 ON p1.order_id = p2.order_id AND p1.product_id != p2.product_id\n    GROUP BY p1.product_id, p2.product_id\n),\nprod_totals AS (\n    SELECT product_id, COUNT(*) AS total_orders\n    FROM order_items\n    GROUP BY product_id\n)\nSELECT \n    pc.prod_a,\n    pc.prod_b,\n    pc.co_purchases,\n    ROUND(100.0 * pc.co_purchases / pt.total_orders, 2) AS confidence_pct\nFROM pair_counts pc\nJOIN prod_totals pt ON pc.prod_a = pt.product_id\nWHERE pc.co_purchases >= 10\nORDER BY confidence_pct DESC;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> On huge e-commerce tables, an unconstrained self-join explodes into billions of rows for large orders. Always filter out bulk orders (e.g., `WHERE order_id NOT IN (SELECT order_id FROM order_items GROUP BY order_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 50)`) before running market basket analysis!",
    "company_tags": [
      "Self Join",
      "Market Basket",
      "Analytics",
      "Recommendation Engine"
    ],
    "frequency": "HIGH",
