    "id": "int-sql-086",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Swapping Adjacent Seats: Bitwise Arithmetic and CASE Conditional Transformations",
    "answer": "### Exchange Seats (LeetCode 626)\n\nSwap the seat IDs of every two consecutive students. If the total number of students is odd, the ID of the last student is not swapped.\n\n```\nInput Seat Table:\n+----+---------+\n| id | student |\n+----+---------+\n| 1  | Abbot   |\n| 2  | Doris   |\n| 3  | Emerson |\n| 4  | Green   |\n| 5  | Jeames  |\n+----+---------+\nOutput:\n+----+---------+\n| id | student |\n+----+---------+\n| 1  | Doris   |\n| 2  | Abbot   |\n| 3  | Green   |\n| 4  | Emerson |\n| 5  | Jeames  |\n+----+---------+\n```\n\n#### Approach 1: Mathematical `CASE WHEN` Transformation\nTransform each student's `id`:\n- If `id` is odd AND it is the final row (`id = total_count`) -> Keep `id` unchanged.\n- If `id` is odd -> Map to `id + 1`.\n- If `id` is even -> Map to `id - 1`.\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n    CASE \n        WHEN id % 2 != 0 AND id = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Seat) THEN id\n        WHEN id % 2 != 0 THEN id + 1\n        ELSE id - 1\n    END AS id,\n    student\nFROM Seat\nORDER BY id ASC;\n```\n\n#### Approach 2: Window Function `LEAD()` and `LAG()`\nInstead of modifying IDs, swap the student names:\n```sql\nSELECT \n    id,\n    CASE \n        WHEN id % 2 != 0 THEN COALESCE(LEAD(student) OVER (ORDER BY id), student)\n        ELSE LAG(student) OVER (ORDER BY id)\n    END AS student\nFROM Seat\nORDER BY id ASC;\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Modifying IDs with `id + 1` (odds) and `id - 1` (evens) followed by `ORDER BY id` is the standard mathematical solution.\n- The boundary condition for an odd number of total seats must preserve the final student's ID unchanged.\n- Swapping the string column (`student`) via `LEAD`/`LAG` keeps the primary key ID sequence untouched.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> In write-heavy production systems, never update primary key IDs in-place to swap positions! Swapping PKs causes index fragmentation, foreign key cascade overhead, and potential temporary unique constraint violations. Always swap the payload column or use view presentation logic.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Modifying IDs with `id + 1` (odds) and `id - 1` (evens) followed by `ORDER BY id` is the standard mathematical solution.",
      "The boundary condition for an odd number of total seats must preserve the final student's ID unchanged.",
      "Swapping the string column (`student`) via `LEAD`/`LAG` keeps the primary key ID sequence untouched."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Swapping via Bitwise XOR Operator (Elegant trick for even/odd swap):\n-- 1 XOR 1 = 0, 2 XOR 1 = 3, 3 XOR 1 = 2, 4 XOR 1 = 5\nSELECT \n    CASE \n        WHEN id = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Seat) AND id % 2 != 0 THEN id\n        WHEN id % 2 != 0 THEN (id + 1)\n        ELSE (id - 1)\n    END AS id,\n    student\nFROM Seat\nORDER BY id;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> In write-heavy production systems, never update primary key IDs in-place to swap positions! Swapping PKs causes index fragmentation, foreign key cascade overhead, and potential temporary unique constraint violations. Always swap the payload column or use view presentation logic.",
