    "id": "int-sql-090",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Row-Level Security (RLS) in PostgreSQL: Building Bulletproof Multi-Tenant Architectures",
    "answer": "### Multi-Tenancy: Shared Database, Shared Schema\n\nIn modern SaaS applications, hosting a separate database or schema per tenant introduces enormous operational overhead. A shared database with a shared schema isolates tenant data via a `tenant_id` column.\n\nHowever, relying entirely on application developers to remember `WHERE tenant_id = current_tenant` in every single SQL query inevitably leads to catastrophic **cross-tenant data leaks**.\n\n**PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS)** enforces tenant isolation directly inside the database kernel.\n\n```\n                      [ Application Connection ]\n                                  |\n               SET LOCAL app.current_tenant = 'tenant_123';\n                                  |\n                                  v\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  SELECT * FROM customers;                                         |\n|  PostgreSQL Query Engine automatically rewrites query to:         |\n|  SELECT * FROM customers WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant_123';          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step RLS Configuration\n\n##### 1. Enable RLS on Table\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    tenant_id VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\n    title TEXT NOT NULL,\n    body TEXT\n);\n\n-- Enable RLS (Defaults to denying all access!)\nALTER TABLE documents ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n```\n\n##### 2. Define Tenant Isolation Policy\n```sql\nCREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_policy ON documents\n    FOR ALL\n    USING (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_tenant', true), ''))\n    WITH CHECK (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_tenant', true), ''));\n```\n- `USING`: Predicate applied to `SELECT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE` rows.\n- `WITH CHECK`: Predicate applied to incoming `INSERT` and `UPDATE` data.\n\n##### 3. Application Session Setting\nBefore executing tenant queries:\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n-- Set transaction-local tenant context\nSET LOCAL app.current_tenant = 'acme_corp';\n\n-- Transparent query: Returns strictly acme_corp documents!\nSELECT * FROM documents;\n\nCOMMIT; -- Automatically clears setting at end of transaction!\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- RLS rewrites SQL queries transparently at the database engine level, enforcing access policies.\n- Table owners and superusers bypass RLS by default; specify `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` to subject table owners to policies.\n- `SET LOCAL variable = value` scopes the session configuration to the active transaction block.\n- RLS eliminates accidental cross-tenant data leaks caused by forgotten `WHERE` clauses in application code.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Does RLS hurt query performance? Yes, if not indexed properly! Because the RLS policy injects `tenant_id = ...` into every query, you **must** ensure `tenant_id` is the leading column in composite indexes (e.g., `CREATE INDEX idx_docs_tenant ON documents (tenant_id, id)`).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "RLS rewrites SQL queries transparently at the database engine level, enforcing access policies.",
      "Table owners and superusers bypass RLS by default; specify `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` to subject table owners to policies.",
      "`SET LOCAL variable = value` scopes the session configuration to the active transaction block.",
      "RLS eliminates accidental cross-tenant data leaks caused by forgotten `WHERE` clauses in application code."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Enforcing RLS Even for Table Owners and Superusers:\nALTER TABLE documents FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n\n-- Creating a Bypass Role for Background Analytics / Migrations:\nCREATE ROLE reporting_admin BYPASSRLS;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Does RLS hurt query performance? Yes, if not indexed properly! Because the RLS policy injects `tenant_id = ...` into every query, you **must** ensure `tenant_id` is the leading column in composite indexes (e.g., `CREATE INDEX idx_docs_tenant ON documents (tenant_id, id)`).",
    "company_tags": [
      "Security",
      "RLS",
      "Multi-Tenancy",
      "PostgreSQL",
      "Architecture"
    ],
    "frequency": "VERY_HIGH",
