    "id": "int-sql-092",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Zero-Downtime Schema Migrations: Safe Column Additions and CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY",
    "answer": "### High-Availability Schema Changes: Zero-Downtime Rules\n\nIn high-traffic production databases, executing naive DDL statements (`ALTER TABLE`, `CREATE INDEX`) acquires aggressive table locks (`AccessExclusiveLock`), blocking all incoming read and write transactions and causing cascading connection exhaustion.\n\n#### 1. Building Indexes Online: `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`\nStandard `CREATE INDEX` locks the entire table against all writes for the full duration of the index build:\n\n```sql\n-- DANGEROUS: Locks table against writes for 45 minutes on a 50M-row table!\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user ON orders(user_id);\n\n-- SAFE: Builds index concurrently without blocking reads or writes!\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user ON orders(user_id);\n```\n- **Mechanism**: Scans the table twice across two separate transactions without taking exclusive locks.\n- **Gotcha**: If a concurrent index build fails (e.g., duplicate key in unique index), it leaves an `INVALID` index that consumes disk space and degrades write speed. You must drop it: `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user;`.\n\n#### 2. Adding Columns with Default Values\n- **PostgreSQL 11+**: `ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT 'ACTIVE';` is **instantaneous**! It updates catalog metadata without rewriting the physical heap table.\n- **PostgreSQL 10 and older / MySQL**: Adding a column with a default value rewrites the entire multi-gigabyte table to disk under an exclusive lock.\n\n#### 3. Setting Strict Lock Timeouts\nAlways set a tight `lock_timeout` before executing any DDL migration. If the migration cannot acquire the table lock within 2 seconds, it aborts rather than queueing up and blocking client queries:\n\n```sql\nSET lock_timeout = '2s';\nALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN age INT;\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Never run standard `CREATE INDEX` in production; always use `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`.\n- Concurrent index creation cannot run inside an explicit transaction block (`BEGIN ... COMMIT`).\n- Guard all DDL operations with `SET lock_timeout = '2s'` to prevent queuing behind slow queries.\n- Clean up failed concurrent indexes marked as `INVALID` in `pg_index`.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Dropping a column with `ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN bio;` is instant in PostgreSQL (metadata change). However, the column's physical disk space is **not reclaimed** until the table is rewritten! Use `pg_repack` to reclaim disk space safely without locking the table.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Never run standard `CREATE INDEX` in production; always use `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`.",
      "Concurrent index creation cannot run inside an explicit transaction block (`BEGIN ... COMMIT`).",
      "Guard all DDL operations with `SET lock_timeout = '2s'` to prevent queuing behind slow queries.",
      "Clean up failed concurrent indexes marked as `INVALID` in `pg_index`."
    ],
