    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Temporary Tables vs CTEs vs Materialized Views: Storage, Scope, and Lifecycle",
    "answer": "### Comparing Intermediate Query Structures\n\nWhen decomposing complex SQL transformations, developers can choose between **CTEs**, **Temporary Tables**, and **Materialized Views**.\n\n```\n+------------------------+----------------------+--------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| Feature                | Common Table Expr    | Temporary Table    | Materialized View                         |\n+------------------------+----------------------+--------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| Physical Storage       | In-memory / inline   | Disk/temp buffer   | Persistent physical table on disk         |\n| Lifecycle / Scope      | Single statement     | Database session   | Permanent until dropped                   |\n| Can Add Indexes?       | No                   | Yes (B-Tree, etc.) | Yes (Full index support)                  |\n| Statistics Generated?  | No                   | Yes (via ANALYZE)  | Yes (Full statistics)                     |\n| Concurrency / Sharing  | Private to query     | Private to session | Shared globally across all connections    |\n| Recomputation          | Evaluated per query  | Once per session   | Only on explicit REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW|\n+------------------------+----------------------+--------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### When to Use Which?\n1. **CTE (`WITH ... AS`)**:\n   - Best for query readability and one-off subquery refactoring.\n   - Ideal when data volume is small (\\(< 10,000\\) rows) and immediately consumed.\n2. **Temporary Table (`CREATE TEMP TABLE`)**:\n   - Best for heavy multi-step batch transformations.\n   - You can create indexes on temporary tables and run `ANALYZE` so the optimizer has accurate cardinality statistics for downstream joins.\n3. **Materialized View**:\n   - Best for expensive analytical aggregations executed repeatedly by hundreds of concurrent users.\n   - Precomputes and caches results on disk.\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- CTEs are ephemeral and live only for the duration of a single SQL query statement.\n- Temporary tables exist for the lifetime of the database connection session and support indexes and statistics.\n- Materialized views store query results permanently on disk and must be refreshed periodically.\n- In PostgreSQL 12+, CTEs are inlined by default unless explicitly marked `WITH cte AS MATERIALIZED (...)`.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> If a CTE is referenced multiple times in the same query in older PostgreSQL (pre-12), it acted as an optimization fence (materialized in memory without index support). In modern versions, CTEs are inlined like subqueries unless explicitly forced with `AS MATERIALIZED`.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "CTEs are ephemeral and live only for the duration of a single SQL query statement.",
      "Temporary tables exist for the lifetime of the database connection session and support indexes and statistics.",
      "Materialized views store query results permanently on disk and must be refreshed periodically.",
      "In PostgreSQL 12+, CTEs are inlined by default unless explicitly marked `WITH cte AS MATERIALIZED (...)`."
    ],
