    "id": "int-sql-096",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Time-Series Data Optimization: TimescaleDB Hypertables, Chunk Sizing, and Columnar Compression",
    "answer": "### Time-Series at Scale: The B+ Tree Cliff\n\nWhen storing billions of metrics (IoT sensors, server telemetry, financial ticks), standard PostgreSQL tables hit a **performance cliff**:\n- As table size exceeds RAM (Buffer Pool), B+ Tree indexes fall out of memory.\n- Every new insert must read random B+ Tree leaf pages from physical disk, causing insertion throughput to collapse by 90%!\n\n```\nStandard Postgres:\n  RAM Capacity: 16 GB -> Table Size: 100 GB -> Inserts: Collapses from 50k/s to 2k/s!\nTimescaleDB Hypertables:\n  Maintains steady 100k+ inserts/sec indefinitely by partitioning into small memory-sized Chunks!\n```\n\n#### 1. What is a Hypertable?\nA **Hypertable** presents itself as a standard SQL table, but TimescaleDB automatically partitions it into two-dimensional **chunks** (by time intervals and optionally by space/device):\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS timescaledb;\n\nCREATE TABLE cpu_metrics (\n    recorded_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    host_id INT NOT NULL,\n    usage_pct NUMERIC(5, 2)\n);\n\n-- Convert to Hypertable with 1-day chunk intervals:\nSELECT create_hypertable('cpu_metrics', 'recorded_at', chunk_time_interval => INTERVAL '1 day');\n```\n\n#### 2. Native Columnar Compression (90%+ Disk Savings)\nTime-series data is predominantly read-only after a few hours. TimescaleDB converts older chunks from row-oriented storage to **columnar compressed format**:\n\n```sql\n-- Enable compression on chunks older than 7 days:\nALTER TABLE cpu_metrics SET (\n    timescaledb.compress,\n    timescaledb.compress_segmentby = 'host_id',\n    timescaledb.compress_orderby = 'recorded_at DESC'\n);\n\nSELECT add_compression_policy('cpu_metrics', INTERVAL '7 days');\n```\n\n#### 3. Continuous Aggregates (Real-Time Rollups)\nPre-aggregates hourly or daily summaries automatically in the background without batch Cron jobs:\n\n```sql\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW cpu_hourly_summary\nWITH (timescaledb.continuous) AS\nSELECT \n    time_bucket('1 hour', recorded_at) AS bucket,\n    host_id,\n    AVG(usage_pct) AS avg_usage,\n    MAX(usage_pct) AS max_usage\nFROM cpu_metrics\nGROUP BY bucket, host_id;\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Hypertables partition data into small time-bounded chunks so current writes fit entirely within RAM buffer cache.\n- Chunk sizing must ensure the active chunk's table and indexes fit comfortably in memory.\n- Columnar compression reduces disk usage by 90% and accelerates analytical scans.\n- **Continuous Aggregates** maintain incremental rollups automatically, serving analytical dashboards with millisecond response times.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> When defining compression, selecting the right `compress_segmentby` column is vital! Segmenting by low-cardinality foreign keys (`host_id`, `device_type`) allows TimescaleDB to group similar values together, achieving maximum gzip/delta-of-delta compression ratios.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Hypertables partition data into small time-bounded chunks so current writes fit entirely within RAM buffer cache.",
      "Chunk sizing must ensure the active chunk's table and indexes fit comfortably in memory.",
      "Columnar compression reduces disk usage by 90% and accelerates analytical scans.",
      "Continuous Aggregates: maintain incremental rollups automatically, serving analytical dashboards with millisecond response times."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Automated Data Retention Policy in TimescaleDB:\n-- Automatically drops chunks older than 90 days in O(1) time without vacuum bloat:\nSELECT add_retention_policy('cpu_metrics', INTERVAL '90 days');"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> When defining compression, selecting the right `compress_segmentby` column is vital! Segmenting by low-cardinality foreign keys (`host_id`, `device_type`) allows TimescaleDB to group similar values together, achieving maximum gzip/delta-of-delta compression ratios.",
