    "id": "int-sql-097",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Materialized Views: Zero-Downtime Concurrent Refresh and Incremental Maintenance",
    "answer": "### Accelerating Heavy Queries with Materialized Views\n\nA standard SQL `VIEW` is a saved query that re-executes against underlying tables every single time it is queried. A **Materialized View (MV)** computes the query once and persists the results physically on disk as a concrete table.\n\n#### The Problem with Standard `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW`\n```sql\n-- Re-executes the view query and replaces table data\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW mv_executive_kpis;\n```\n- **The Lock Trap**: Standard `REFRESH` acquires an `AccessExclusiveLock` on the materialized view! While refreshing (which may take 10 minutes on big data), **all concurrent `SELECT` queries on the view are completely blocked**!\n\n#### The Zero-Downtime Fix: `CONCURRENTLY`\nPostgreSQL provides `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY`, which allows ongoing reads without blocking:\n\n```sql\n-- Step 1: Must create at least one UNIQUE index on the materialized view!\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_mv_kpis_unique_day \nON mv_executive_kpis (metric_date, department_id);\n\n-- Step 2: Refresh online without acquiring exclusive read locks:\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY mv_executive_kpis;\n```\n\n#### How `CONCURRENTLY` Operates Internally:\n1. Executes the view query and writes the complete result set into a temporary table.\n2. Compares the temporary table against the current materialized view using the **UNIQUE index**.\n3. Issues `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` diffs to bring the view up to date while readers continue scanning the existing data pages.\n- **Trade-off**: Requires slightly more temporary disk space and runs slightly slower than standard refresh, but guarantees zero read downtime!\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- Standard views compute on-demand; materialized views cache computed results on disk.\n- Standard `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW` takes an exclusive lock, blocking all readers.\n- `CONCURRENTLY` requires a **UNIQUE index** across columns that uniquely identify each row.\n- `CONCURRENTLY` updates the view via diffing, providing uninterrupted read availability for analytics dashboards.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Does a newly created materialized view populate data immediately? By default, yes. However, if you add `WITH NO DATA` (`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW ... WITH NO DATA;`), it creates the schema structure without running the query, allowing you to build indexes before the initial load.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "Standard views compute on-demand; materialized views cache computed results on disk.",
      "Standard `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW` takes an exclusive lock, blocking all readers.",
      "`CONCURRENTLY` requires a **UNIQUE index** across columns that uniquely identify each row.",
      "`CONCURRENTLY` updates the view via diffing, providing uninterrupted read availability for analytics dashboards."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Production Materialized View with Concurrent Refresh Capability:\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW mv_user_order_stats AS\nSELECT \n    user_id,\n    COUNT(*) AS total_orders,\n    SUM(amount) AS total_spent,\n    MAX(created_at) AS last_order_at\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY user_id\nWITH DATA;\n\n-- Mandatory unique index for concurrent refreshes:\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_mv_user_order_uid ON mv_user_order_stats (user_id);\n\n-- Scheduled refresh command:\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY mv_user_order_stats;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Does a newly created materialized view populate data immediately? By default, yes. However, if you add `WITH NO DATA` (`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW ... WITH NO DATA;`), it creates the schema structure without running the query, allowing you to build indexes before the initial load.",
