    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Window Frame Specifications: ROWS vs RANGE vs GROUPS and Frame Exclusion Rules",
    "answer": "### Mastering Window Frame Specifications\n\nWhen using window functions (`SUM()`, `AVG()`, `COUNT()`) with `OVER (ORDER BY ...)`, an invisible default window frame is applied if not explicitly written:\n\n$$\\text{Default Frame} = \\text{RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW}$$\n\nThis subtle default causes major performance penalties and logical bugs when duplicate values exist in the `ORDER BY` column!\n\n```\n+----------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Mode           | Framing Behavior                                                                               |\n+----------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| ROWS           | Physical row offsets (e.g., strictly 3 physical rows before current row)                       |\n| RANGE          | Logical value offsets based on the values in the ORDER BY expression (includes peers!)         |\n| GROUPS         | Groups of equal-valued rows (peers) treated as atomic window units                             |\n+----------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### The `ROWS` vs `RANGE` Gotcha: Running Sum with Ties\nSuppose two transactions occur on the same date:\n\n```sql\n-- Table: Transactions\n-- Date: 2024-01-01, Amount: 100\n-- Date: 2024-01-01, Amount: 200\n-- Date: 2024-01-02, Amount: 300\n\n-- Query A: Default (RANGE)\nSELECT date, amount, \n       SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY date) AS running_total \nFROM transactions;\n-- Output:\n-- 2024-01-01 | 100 | 300  <-- SURPRISE! Evaluates ALL rows with date '2024-01-01' together!\n-- 2024-01-01 | 200 | 300\n-- 2024-01-02 | 300 | 600\n\n-- Query B: Explicit (ROWS)\nSELECT date, amount, \n       SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY date ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS running_total \nFROM transactions;\n-- Output:\n-- 2024-01-01 | 100 | 100  <-- Strict progressive cumulative sum!\n-- 2024-01-01 | 200 | 300\n-- 2024-01-02 | 300 | 600\n```\n\n#### Frame Exclusion Rules (`EXCLUDE`)\nModern SQL (PostgreSQL 11+) supports excluding specific rows from the frame:\n- `EXCLUDE CURRENT ROW`: Excludes the current row from the frame computation.\n- `EXCLUDE GROUP`: Excludes the current row and all its peers.\n- `EXCLUDE TIES`: Retains the current row, but excludes all other rows sharing the same value.\n\n```sql\n-- Compute average of peers excluding myself:\nSELECT \n    dept_id, \n    emp_id, \n    salary,\n    AVG(salary) OVER (\n        PARTITION BY dept_id \n        ORDER BY salary \n        ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND 2 FOLLOWING \n        EXCLUDE CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS peer_avg_salary\nFROM employees;\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- The default window frame is `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`.\n- Default `RANGE` groups rows with identical sorting values as peers, inflating cumulative sums prematurely.\n- `ROWS` evaluates physical records sequentially and executes significantly faster because it streams in-memory without buffering peers.\n- `EXCLUDE CURRENT ROW` computes metrics across neighbors while omitting the focal row.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Always specify `ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` for cumulative running aggregates! Besides guaranteeing correct single-row increments, `ROWS` executes up to **10x faster** than `RANGE` in PostgreSQL and MySQL by avoiding duplicate value peer group checks.",
    "bullet_points": [
      "The default window frame is `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`.",
      "Default `RANGE` groups rows with identical sorting values as peers, inflating cumulative sums prematurely.",
      "`ROWS` evaluates physical records sequentially and executes significantly faster because it streams in-memory without buffering peers.",
      "`EXCLUDE CURRENT ROW` computes metrics across neighbors while omitting the focal row."
    ],
