    "id": "int-sql-099",
    "topic_id": "topic-sql",
    "category": "CORE_CS",
    "subject": "SQL_QUERIES",
    "subject_label": "SQL Queries",
    "title": "Computing Departmental Median Salary Without Built-in MEDIAN Aggregates",
    "answer": "### Departmental Median Salary (LeetCode 569)\n\nFind the median salary for each department. If a department has an even number of employees, the median is defined as the two middle values (or their average depending on the problem specification).\n\n`\nEmployee Table:\n+----+--------------+--------+\n| id | company      | salary |\n+----+--------------+--------+\n| 1  | A            | 2341   |\n| 2  | A            | 341    |\n| 3  | A            | 15     |\n| 4  | A            | 15314  |\n| 5  | A            | 451    |\n| 6  | A            | 513    |\n+----+--------------+--------+\n`\n\n#### The Universal Mathematical Solution with ROW_NUMBER() and COUNT()\nBy numbering each employee's salary ascending within their department and comparing the row number against the total department count \\(N\\):\n\\text{Median Indices} = \\left[\\frac{N}{2}, \\frac{N}{2} + 1\\right] \\quad (\\text{even } N) \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\left[\\frac{N + 1}{2}\\right] \\quad (\\text{odd } N)\n\n`sql\nWITH ranked_employees AS (\n    SELECT \n        id,\n        company,\n        salary,\n        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY salary ASC, id ASC) AS rn,\n        COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY company) AS total_count\n    FROM Employee\n)\nSELECT id, company, salary\nFROM ranked_employees\nWHERE rn BETWEEN total_count / 2.0 AND (total_count / 2.0) + 1\nORDER BY company, salary;\n`\n\n### Key Takeaways for Quick Revision\n- ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY salary) assigns sequential positions.\n- In integer / float division, \nn BETWEEN total_count / 2.0 AND (total_count / 2.0) + 1 mathematically isolates the middle 1 or 2 median rows for both odd and even counts.\n- Adding id ASC as a secondary tie-breaker in ORDER BY guarantees deterministic row numbering when salaries are identical.\n\n> **Pro Tip / Interviewer Trap**:\n> Clarify whether the interviewer wants the actual row records of the median employees (LeetCode 569 requirement) or a single scalar median salary number per department (achieved via AVG(salary) over the median rows or PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5)).",
    "bullet_points": [
      "ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY salary) assigns sequential positions.",
      "In integer / float division,",
      "Adding id ASC as a secondary tie-breaker in ORDER BY guarantees deterministic row numbering when salaries are identical."
    ],
    "code_snippet": {
      "language": "sql",
      "code": "-- Scalar Median Salary Per Department:\nWITH median_rows AS (\n    SELECT \n        company,\n        salary,\n        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY salary, id) AS rn,\n        COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY company) AS total_cnt\n    FROM Employee\n)\nSELECT \n    company,\n    ROUND(AVG(salary), 2) AS median_salary\nFROM median_rows\nWHERE rn BETWEEN total_cnt / 2.0 AND (total_cnt / 2.0) + 1\nGROUP BY company;"
    },
    "pro_tip": "> Clarify whether the interviewer wants the actual row records of the median employees (LeetCode 569 requirement) or a single scalar median salary number per department (achieved via AVG(salary) over the median rows or PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5)).",
